hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"5741","(5741) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; ʿAmr ibn Khālid informed us; Ibn Lahīʿah reported from Muḥammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhājir, that is, ibn Qunfudh al-Taymī, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir from Jābir, who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ascended the minbar, he would give salām.\" [Ḥasan through other chains. Ibn Mājah 1109]"
"5742","(5742) Abū Saʿid al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿArūbah informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn al-Ḥakkām informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad informed us; al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah informed us. Both said: al-Walīd ibn Muslim reported from ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī — and al-Walīd said: ʿĪsā ibn Abī ʿAwn al-Qurashī narrated to me — from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) approached his minbar on the day of Jumuʿah, he would give salām to those sitting near him. Then when he ascended the minbar, he would face the people and then give salām.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil 5/253]"
"5743","(5743) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Ibn Abī ʿĀṣim informed us; ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān informed us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim reported from a man who narrated to him from Nāfiʿ, and he mentioned it with the same meaning, except that he said: \"When he ascended the minbar, he would give salām to the people before sitting down.\" This was narrated uniquely by ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥakam ibn al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, Abū Mūsā al-Anṣārī. Abū Saʿid said: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī said: \"Most of what he narrates is not corroborated.\" The Shaykh said: Something to that effect has also been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās, Ibn al-Zubayr, and then from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"5744","(5744) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Sayyārī in Marw informed us; Abū al-Muwajjih informed us; ʿAbdān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Yūnus ibn Yazīd reported from al-Zuhrī, who said: al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd informed me that the first adhān on the day of Jumuʿah was initially given when the imām sat upon the minbar during the era of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), the era of Abū Bakr, and the era of ʿUmar. Then during the caliphate of ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with them all), when the people increased in number, he ordered the third adhān, which was called at al-Zawrāʾ, and the matter remained established upon that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See 5681]"
"5745","(5745) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Ḥibbān reported from Ibn al-Mubārak from Yūnus, and he mentioned it with the same wording, except that he said: \"The adhān on the day of Jumuʿah — its first (call) was when the imām sat upon the minbar on the day of Jumuʿah.\" And he said: \"ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered the third adhān on the day of Jumuʿah.\" The rest is the same. al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muqātil from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See 5681]"
"5746","(5746) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qāḍī informed us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā ibn al-Ṭabbāʿ informed us; Muṣʿab ibn Salām reported from Hishām ibn al-Ghāz from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) would, when he came out on the day of Jumuʿah and sat upon the minbar, have Bilāl call the adhān.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. al-Ḥākim 1/420]"
"5747","(5747) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, i.e. ibn ʿAṭāʾ, narrated to us, from al-ʿUmarī, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) used to deliver two sermons. He would sit when he ascended the minbar until the muʾadhdhin finished, then he would stand and deliver the sermon, then he would sit and not speak, then he would stand and deliver the sermon.\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us in the ḥadīth of Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Anas ibn Mālik, in the story of the minbar, who said: So a minbar was made for him with two steps, and he would sit on the third. When the Prophet (ﷺ) sat upon it, the trunk [of the palm tree] cried.\n\n[Ṣaḥīh in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 1092]"
"5748","(5748) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mihrān narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) ascended the minbar on Friday and said to the people: \"Sit down.\" Ibn Masʿūd heard him while he was at the door of the mosque, so he sat down. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: \"Come forward, O Ibn Masʿūd.\"\n\nThus he said. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1091]"
"5749","(5749) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Kaʿb al-Anṭākī narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Muhammad al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Kaʿb al-Ḥalabī narrated to us; Makhlad ibn Yazīd narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ascended the minbar on Friday, he said: \"Sit down.\" Ibn Masʿūd heard and sat down at the door of the mosque. The Prophet (ﷺ) saw him and said: \"Come forward, O ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd.\"\n\nAnd similarly it was narrated from Muʿādh ibn Muʿād, from Ibn Jurayj, and it was said from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) saw Ibn Masʿūd outside the mosque while the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon, and he said: \"Come forward, O ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd.\"\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1091]"
"5750","(5750) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Walīd ibn Ḥammād ibn Jābir al-Zayyāt narrated to us in al-Ramlah; Yazīd ibn Khālid ibn Mursal ibn Yazīd ibn Numayr al-Qurashī narrated to us; Shihāb ibn Khirāsh narrated to us; Shuʿayb ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, from al-Ḥakam ibn Ḥazn al-Kulafī, who said: We came to him and he began narrating to us from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as a delegation of seven, or nine, and he gave us permission to enter upon him. We entered upon him and greeted him, and we said: \"We have visited you, O Messenger of Allah, so that you may supplicate to Allah for us, or supplicate for good for us.\" He said: So he supplicated good for us and commanded that we be hosted, and he ordered some dates for us, and the matter at that time was modest. He said: I stayed with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for some days during which I witnessed Friday. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood, leaning on a bow—or he said, on a staff—and praised Allah and extolled Him with words that were light, pure, and blessed. Then he said: \"O people, you will not be able to bear, or you will not be able to do, everything you have been commanded with. But aim for what is right, seek the middle path, and give glad tidings.\"\n\nAnd similarly Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr and others narrated it from Shihāb ibn Khirāsh. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1096]"
"5751","(5751) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us; Abū Yaḥyā Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd al-Ḥarrānī al-Dimashqī narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd ibn ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd, the muʾadhdhin of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), said: My father narrated to me from his fathers that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he delivered a sermon during battle, would lean on a bow, and when he delivered the Friday sermon, he would lean on a staff.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Mājah 1107]"
"5752","(5752) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"Would the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stand when delivering the sermon, leaning on a staff?\" He said: \"Yes, and he would lean upon it heavily.\"\n\n[Ḥasan in other than this chain. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5246]"
"5753","(5753) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shīruwayh narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Walīd informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; they said: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivered the sermon, his eyes would redden, his voice would rise, and his anger would intensify, as though he were a warner to an army, saying: \"You are being approached in the morning and in the evening.\" And he would say: \"I have been sent and the Hour [of Judgment] like these two.\" And he would separate between his index finger and middle finger, and he would say: \"To proceed: the best speech is the Book of Allah, and the best guidance is the guidance of Muḥammad, and the worst of matters are their innovations, and every innovation is misguidance.\" Then he would say: \"I am closer to every believer than his own self. Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his family. Whoever leaves a debt or dependents, then it is upon me and incumbent on me.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, and thus ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad said it, from Jaʿfar: \"When he delivered the sermon, his eyes would redden, his voice would rise, and his anger would intensify.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 867]"
"5754","(5754) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed him; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated it with its meaning. And the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wahhāb is more complete."
"5755","(5755) And Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Jaʿfar with his chain, and he said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned the Hour, his anger would intensify, his voice would rise, and his cheeks would redden, as though he were a warner to an army: \"You have been approached in the morning, you have been approached in the evening.\"\n\nʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated it in abridged form.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī in al-Kubrā 5892]"
"5756","(5756) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivered a sermon and said: \"I have warned you of the Fire,\" until he said: \"If someone were in my position, he would hear what is in the market,\" until a thin cloth that was on his shoulder fell down.\n\n[Jayyid. al-Dārimī 2812]"
"5757","(5757) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, in dictation: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʪsā narrated to us: Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Usāmah ibn Zayd from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not speak in a rapid, continuous manner as you do now. Rather, his speech was distinct and clear, such that everyone who heard it would memorize it.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. Tirmidhī 3639]"
"5758","(5758) Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Kisāʾī al-Miṣrī, resident in Makkah — may Allah, the Most High, protect it — in the Sacred Mosque, informed us: Ibn al-Wann ʿAlī ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Ghaffār al-Azdī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād ibn Abī Masrah narrated to us: Khallād ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Usāmah ibn Zayd from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \"The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) did not speak in a rapid, continuous manner as you do now. Rather, whenever he spoke, he spoke in a distinct and clear manner, such that everyone who heard it would memorize it.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5759","(5759) This chain of narration was also narrated by Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ and Abū Usāmah from al-Thawrī.\n\nAbū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, saying: Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī said: Usāmah from al-Qāsim and al-Zuhrī are both authentic together.\n\nThe Shaykh said: The hadith has been established in its meaning from Yūnus ibn Yazīd and others from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah, and it has already been mentioned in the Kitāb al-Madkhal."
"5760","(5760) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us: Ibn Bishr narrated from Misʿar, who said: I heard a shaykh in the mosque saying: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: \"In the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) there was a measured, deliberate pace.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. Abū Dāwūd 4838]"
"5761","(5761) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us: Simāk narrated from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: \"I used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and his prayer was of moderate length and his sermon was of moderate length.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 766]"
"5762","(5762) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Khālid narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Khālid al-Dimashqī narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us: Shaybān Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb from Jābir ibn Samurah al-Suwāʾī, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would not prolong the sermon on Friday; it was merely a few brief words.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1107]"
"5763","(5763) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Faḍl al-Faḥḥām narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us: Abū ʿĀṣim al-Bajalī, from the descendants of Mālik ibn Miḥwal, narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Saʿīd ibn Ḥayyān ibn al-Abjaranī al-Kinānī narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — and the wording is his: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Surayj ibn Yūnus narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abjaranī narrated from his father from Wāṣil ibn Ḥayyān al-Aḥdab from Abū Wāʾil, who said: \"ʿAmmār (may Allah be pleased with him) delivered a sermon to us, being eloquent and concise. When he came down, we said: 'O Abū al-Yaqẓān, you were indeed eloquent and concise — if only you had spoken at greater length.' He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: \"Indeed, the length of a man's prayer and the brevity of his sermon are a sign of his understanding. So lengthen the prayer and shorten the sermon, and indeed some eloquence is akin to magic.\"'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Surayj ibn Yūnus, and it has also been narrated as a statement of Ibn Masʿūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 689]"
"5764","(5764) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr al-Faḥḥām narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Sufyān narrated from al-Aʿmash from Abū Wāʾil from ʿAmr ibn Sharḥbīl, who said: ʿAbdullāh said: \"Indeed, the length of the prayer and the brevity of the sermon are a sign of a man's understanding\" — meaning, an indication. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 9994]"
"5765","(5765) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid narrated from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: \"Lengthen this prayer and shorten this sermon\" — meaning the Friday prayer. And it has also been narrated through another chain from ʿAmmār, in a marfūʿ (elevated) abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥākim 2/530]"
"5766","(5766) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Sulaymān al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated to us: My father narrated to us: al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from ʿAdīy ibn Thābit from Abū Rāshid from ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us to keep the sermons brief.\""
"5767","In the established hadith from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on Friday would begin with praising Allah and glorifying Him, and then he would proceed with the remainder of the hadith.\n\nAbū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us — Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us — Ibn Abī Uways and al-Farwiyy both narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us from Jaʿfar, from his father, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, that he heard him saying that. And Muslim has recorded it in his Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 867]"
"5768","And Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us — Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us — ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Tarqafī informed us — Abū al-Mughīrah informed us — al-Awzāʿī informed us — Qurrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Every affair of importance that does not begin with 'Al-ḥamdu lillāh' (All praise is due to Allah) is cut off (i.e., devoid of blessing).\"\n\nQurrah alone transmitted it with this chain. And Yūnus ibn Yazīd, ʿUqayl ibn Khālid, Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah, and Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated it from al-Zuhrī from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal (disconnected) hadith. [Munkar. Ibn Mājah 1894]"
"5769","And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us — my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us — Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar al-Bakrāwī informed us — ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād informed us — ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb informed us, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Every sermon in which there is no testimony (shahādah) is like the severed hand.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 4841]"
"5770","And Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us — my grandfather said: Abū al-Faḍl, meaning Aḥmad ibn Salamah, said: I heard Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj saying: \"No one transmitted this hadith from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb except ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād.\" So I said to him: Abū Hishām al-Rifāʿī narrated to us — Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Every sermon in which there is no testimony is like the severed hand.\" Muslim said: \"Indeed, Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn only criticized Abū Hishām regarding what he narrated from Ibn Fuḍayl.\" The Shaykh said: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād is among the trustworthy narrators whose solitary transmissions are accepted. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi]"
"5771","Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Ibn ʿUyaynah informed us, from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, regarding the statement of Allah, the Most High: \"And We raised high for you your mention\" (al-Sharḥ, 94:4). He said: \"I will not mention (you) except that I will be mentioned — I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah.\" And a similar narration is reported from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 31689]"
"5772","Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us — Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firyābī informed us — Sufyān informed us, from Ṣāliḥ, the freed slave of al-Taʾūmah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"No people sit in a gathering in which they do not remember their Lord and do not send blessings upon their Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) except that it will be a source of regret for them on the Day of Resurrection — if Allah wills, He will take them to account, and if He wills, He will pardon them.\" [Jayyid. al-Tirmidhī 3380]"
"5773","Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us — Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl informed us — Musaddad informed us — Abū al-Aḥwaṣ informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us — Abū al-Aḥwaṣ informed us, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Jābir ibn Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would deliver two sermons, sitting between them, reciting the Qurʾān and reminding the people.\n\nIn the narration of Musaddad, it is \"he would recite\" without the conjunction \"wāw.\" Muslim recorded it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 862]"
"5774","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me — Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Idrīs informed us, from Ḥuṣayn, from ʿUmārah ibn Ruwaybah, who said: He saw Bishr ibn Marwān raising his hands and said: \"May Allah curse these two hands! I have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) doing no more than this\" — and he indicated with his index finger (al-musabbiḥah).\n\nMuslim recorded it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 874]"
"5775","And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us — Shuʿbah informed us, from Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿUmārah ibn Ruwaybah, that he saw Bishr ibn Marwān on Friday raising his hands in supplication while on the pulpit. He said: \"Look at this!\" — and he reviled him. He said: \"I have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) doing no more than this\" — and he indicated with his index finger (al-sabbābah). [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith]"
"5776","Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Musaddad informed us — Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, meaning Ibn Isḥāq, informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muʿāwiyah informed us, from Ibn Abī Dhubāb, from Sahl ibn Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) raising his hands at all while supplicating on his pulpit or elsewhere. Rather, I saw him doing like this\" — and he indicated with his index finger and joined the middle finger with the thumb.\n\nThe purpose of these two hadiths is to establish the practice of supplication during the sermon, and that the Sunnah therein is that one should not raise one's hands while supplicating during the sermon, but rather restrict oneself to pointing with the finger.\n\nIt is also established from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he extended his hands and supplicated when he sought rain during the Friday sermon.\n\nWe have received from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he would not raise his hands in any of his supplications except during the prayer for rain, to the extent that the whiteness of his armpits would become visible.\n\nAnd we have received from al-Zuhrī that he said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would deliver the sermon on Friday, he would supplicate, point with his finger, and the people would say 'Āmīn.'\"\n\nAnd Qurrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, though it is not authentic. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1105]"
"5777","(5777) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Shaybānī narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qabbānī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from a sister of ʿAmrah, who said: \"I memorized {Qāf. By the glorious Qurʾān} [Qāf: 1] from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on Friday, as he used to recite it from the pulpit every Friday.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dārimī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 873]"
"5778","(5778) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Khubayb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Maʿn from a daughter of Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān, who said: \"I did not memorize {Qāf} [Qāf: 1] except from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), as he used to recite it every Friday.\" She said: \"And our oven and the oven of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) were one and the same.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār."
"5779","(5779) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us: My father narrated from Ibn Isḥāq.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Ḥammād al-Maʿnā al-Shaykh al-Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated to me from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd ibn Zurārah from Umm Hishām bint Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stayed with us in our homes, and our oven and his oven were one and the same for two years, or one year and part of another. And I did not take {Qāf. By the glorious Qurʾān} except from the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him); he used to recite it every Friday to the people when he delivered the sermon to them.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm. And Umm Hishām bint Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān is the sister of ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from the same mother. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains]"
"5780","(5780) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Abū Muslim narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us: Sufyān narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAṭāʾ from Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā, meaning Ibn Umayyah, from his father, who said: \"I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) reciting from the pulpit: {And they will call, 'O Mālik! Let your Lord put an end to us!'} [al-Zukhruf: 77].\"\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl. And Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 3058]"
"5781","(5781) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed me: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥalḥalah narrated to me from Abū Nuʿaym Wahb ibn Kaysān from Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābl (may Allah be pleased with him) used to recite in his Friday sermon {When the sun is wrapped up} [al-Takwīr: 1] until he reached {A soul will know what it has brought forth} [al-Takwīr: 14], and then he would stop.\n\n[Very weak. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī 425]"
"5782","(5782) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Isfarāyinī narrated to us: Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Barbarī narrated to us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr al-Kāhilī narrated to us from Miswār ibn Yazīd al-Asadī, who said: \"I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) reciting in the prayer and he omitted something that he did not recite. A man said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, you left out such-and-such a verse.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Why did you not remind me of it?'\" He said: \"I thought it had been abrogated.\"\n\nAbū Dāwūd narrated it in the book of Sunan. [Ḥasan through other chains. Abū Dāwūd 907]"
"5783","(5783) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us: ʿAbdān narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh — I think he was Ibn al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Zabr — from Sālim from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed a recitation in which he became confused. When he finished, he said to Ubayy ibn Kaʿb: \"Did you pray with us?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then what prevented you from prompting me?\"\n\nAbū Dāwūd narrated it in the book of Sunan. And it was narrated by Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) in a mursal narration regarding the incident of Ubayy. And it was narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 907]"
"5784","(5784) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAlī ibn Mukram in Baghdād narrated to us: al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ghaylān narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Buzayʿ narrated to us: Ḥumayd narrated from Anas, who said: \"We used to prompt the imams during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).\"\n\n[Weak. al-Ḥākim 1/410]"
"5785","(5785) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Ziyād ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Jāriyah ibn Harim narrated to us: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: \"The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to prompt one another in the prayer.\"\n\n[Very weak. al-Ḥākim 1/411]"
"5786","(5786) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Rūḥ narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Isḥāq from ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd, who said: \"I was sitting in Makkah when a man with a pleasant fragrance was praying near the Maqām, and a man was seated behind him prompting him. It was ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him).\"\n\n[Ḥasan. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 2825]"
"5787","(5787) Abu al-Qasim — ʿAbd al-ʿAziz ibn ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Tajir al-Isbahani in Rayy — informed us; Abu Hatim Muhammad ibn ʿIsa al-Wasqandi informed us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari informed us on the authority of ʿAbd al-Razzaq, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj; Nafiʿ informed me, saying: I used to prompt Ibn ʿUmar in the prayer, and he would not say anything to me.\n\nAnd on the authority of ʿAbd al-Razzaq, on the authority of Maʿmar, on the authority of Ayyub, on the authority of Nafiʿ: that Ibn ʿUmar prayed Maghrib, and when he recited {Not of those who have earned [Your] anger, nor of those who are astray} [al-Fatihah: 1], he began reciting {In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful} repeatedly, reiterating it. So I said to him: \"{When the earth is shaken}\" [al-Zalzalah: 1], and he recited it. When he finished, he did not reproach me for that. [Sahih. ʿAbd al-Razzaq 2826]"
"5788","(5788) Abu Saʿid ibn Abi ʿAmr informed us; Abu ʿAbd Allah al-Saffar narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Birti narrated to us; Abu Nuʿaym narrated to us; ʿIsa ibn Tahman said: I heard Thabit al-Bunani saying: When Anas stood to pray, a boy would stand behind him with a mushaf (copy of the Qur'an), and if he faltered over something, he would open it for him (i.e., prompt him). [Sahih]"
"5789","(5789) Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us; Abu al-Hasan al-Karizi narrated to us; ʿAli ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAziz narrated to us; Abu ʿUbayd narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; Muhammad ibn ʿAbd al-Rahman informed us, on the authority of Abu Jaʿfar al-Qari, who said: I saw Abu Hurayrah opening (i.e., prompting) Marwan in the prayer. [Daʿif]"
"5790","(5790) As for the hadith: Abu Muhammad ʿAbd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us; Abu Saʿid ibn al-Aʿrabi narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Zaʿfarani narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Israʾil narrated to us; Abu Ishaq narrated to us, on the authority of al-Harith, on the authority of ʿAli (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"O ʿAli, I love for you what I love for myself, and I dislike for you what I dislike for yourself. Do not recite [the Qur'an] while you are in rukūʿ (bowing), nor while you are in sajdah (prostration). Do not pray with your hair braided, for that is the portion of Satan. Do not sit between the two prostrations (in iqʿāʾ). Do not play with the pebbles. Do not spread your forearms (on the ground in prostration). Do not prompt the Imam. Do not wear a gold ring. Do not wear silk garments (al-qisiyy). And do not ride upon silk-covered saddles (al-mayathir).\" [Daʿif. Tirmidhi 282]"
"5791","(5791) Abu ʿAli al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah said: Abu Dawud said: Abu Ishaq did not hear from al-Harith except four hadiths, and this is not among them.\n\nThe Shaykh said: And al-Harith is not used as a basis for argument (i.e., his narration is not relied upon).\n\nAnd it has been narrated from ʿAli (may Allah be pleased with him) that which indicates the permissibility of prompting the Imam. [Sahih]"
"5792","(5792) Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us; Abu al-Hasan al-Karizi narrated to us; ʿAli ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAziz narrated to us; Abu ʿUbayd narrated to us; Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us, on the authority of Layth, on the authority of ʿAbd al-Aʿla, on the authority of Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman — Ismaʿil said: I think he attributed it to ʿAli (may Allah be pleased with him) — who said: Abu ʿUbayd said: Thus I memorized it from him. Then it reached me afterward from him, without doubt, that when the Imam seeks your help (istiṭʿāmakum), then feed him (i.e., prompt him).\n\nAnd Sufyan al-Thawri narrated it from ʿAbd al-Aʿla, from Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman, as his own statement, similar to the first, and he added: We said: What is his seeking help (istiṭʿām)? He said: When he falters and falls silent, then prompt him. [Hasan li-ghayrihi. al-Daraqutni 1/455]"
"5793","(5793) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Isbahani narrated to us; Ibn Jamil narrated to us; Ibn Maniʿ narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Mayyasar narrated to us; Israʾil narrated to us, on the authority of ʿAbd al-Aʿla, on the authority of Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman, who said: ʿAli (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"It is from the Sunnah that you prompt the Imam when he seeks your help.\" I said to Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman: What is the Imam's seeking help? He said: When he falls silent. [Hasan li-ghayrihi]"
"5794","(5794) And Abu ʿAbd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-ʿAbbas Muhammad ibn Yaʿqub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Khalid narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us; al-Hasan — that is, ibn ʿUmarah — narrated to us, on the authority of ʿAtaʾ ibn al-Saʾib, on the authority of Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Sulami, on the authority of ʿAli (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"When the Imam seeks your help, then feed him (i.e., prompt him).\" [Hasan li-ghayrihi]"
"5795","(5795) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us; ʿAli ibn ʿUmar al-Hafiz narrated to us; ʿAbd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAziz narrated to us; Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us; Abu Hafs — meaning al-Abbar — narrated to us, on the authority of ʿAtaʾ ibn al-Saʾib, on the authority of Abu ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Sulami, who I think narrated from ʿAli (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"When the Imam seeks your help, then feed him (i.e., prompt him).\" [Hasan li-ghayrihi]"
"5796","(5796) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Malik narrated to us, on the authority of Hisham ibn ʿUrwah, on the authority of his father: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) recited the verse of prostration (āyat al-sajdah) while he was on the pulpit on Friday. He came down and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him. Then on the following Friday he recited it again, and the people prepared to prostrate. But ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"Stay where you are. Allah has not made it obligatory upon us, unless we will (to do so).\" So he recited it and did not prostrate, and he prevented the people from prostrating. [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Malik 484]"
"5797","(5797) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: Ayyūb, meaning ibn Suwayd, narrated to me: Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim from Zirr: that ʿAmr ibn ʿAmmār (may Allah be pleased with him) recited upon the minbar {When the sky is split apart} (al-Inshiqāq) on the day of Jumuʿah, then he descended and prostrated. [Ḥasan. Ibn Abī Shaybah 4251]"
"5798","(5798) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Uways and al-Farwiyy both said: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Jaʿfar, meaning ibn Muḥammad, narrated from his father from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh: that he heard him say: \"The sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the day of Jumuʿah — he would praise Allah and extol Him, then he would say thereafter, and his voice would rise, and his anger would intensify, and his two cheeks would redden, as though he were a warner to an army, saying: 'It has reached you this morning or this evening.' Then he would say: 'I was sent and the Hour like these two,' and he indicated with his middle finger and the one next to the thumb. Then he would say: 'Indeed, the best speech is the Book of Allah, and the best guidance is the guidance of Muhammad, and the worst of matters are their innovations, and every innovation is misguidance. Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his family, and whoever leaves a debt or dependents, then it is upon me and incumbent upon me.'\" The wording is from Ibn Abī Uways. [Ṣaḥīh. See 5753]"
"5799","(5799) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir narrated to us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated from his father, who said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh say: \"The sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the day of Jumuʿah…\" and he mentioned it similarly in full. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from Khālid ibn Makhlad. [Ṣaḥīh. See 5753]"
"5800","(5800) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAmr narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from Jābir, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would address the people, praising Allah and extolling Him as is His due, and he would say: 'Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He misguides, none can guide. And the best speech is the Book of Allah, and the best guidance is the guidance of Muhammad, and the worst of matters are their innovations, and every innovation is misguidance.' And when he mentioned the Hour, his voice would rise, his two cheeks would redden, and his anger would intensify, as though he were a warner to an army, saying: 'It has reached you this morning or this evening.' Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs, and whoever leaves a debt or dependents, then it is upon me and incumbent upon me — I am the guardian of the believers.'\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīh. See 5753]"
"5801","(5801) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us. And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — and the wording is his — he said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: my father and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad said: my father narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to me: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind narrated from ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās: that Ḍimād came to Makkah, and he was from the Azd Shanūʾah, and he used to perform incantations for this type of affliction. He heard some foolish people of Makkah saying: \"Muḥammad is mad.\" He said: \"If only I could see this man, perhaps Allah would cure him at my hands.\" He met him and said: \"O Muḥammad, I perform incantations for this affliction, and Allah cures at my hands whom He wills. Do you have need of this?\" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Indeed, all praise is due to Allah — we praise Him and seek His help. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He misguides, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, with no partner, and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. To proceed…\" Ḍimād said: \"Repeat these words of yours for me.\" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) repeated them to him three times. Ḍimād said: \"I have heard the words of the soothsayers, the words of the sorcerers, and the words of the poets, but I have never heard the like of these words of yours. Indeed, they have reached the depths of the sea.\" He said: \"Give me your hand — I pledge allegiance to you upon Islam.\" So he pledged allegiance. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"And upon your people?\" He said: \"And upon my people.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent out a raiding party, and they passed by his people. The commander of the raiding party said to the army: 'Have you taken anything from these people?' A man from the people said: 'I took a water vessel from them.' He said: 'Return it, for these are the people of Ḍimād.'\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. [Ṣaḥīh. Muslim 868]"
"5802","(5802) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓafar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Qādim al-Khuzāʿī narrated to us: al-Masʿūdī narrated from Abī Isḥāq from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) taught us the sermon of need: 'All praise is due to Allah — we praise Him, seek His help, and seek His forgiveness. We seek refuge in Allah from the evils of our souls. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He misguides, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. {Fear Allah as He ought to be feared, and do not die except as Muslims} [Āl ʿImrān: 102]. {Fear Allah, through whom you ask one another, and the wombs. Indeed, Allah is ever over you a Watcher} [al-Nisāʾ: 1]. {Fear Allah and speak a word of appropriate justice. He will amend for you your deeds and forgive you your sins. And whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger has certainly attained a great triumph} [al-Aḥzāb: 70–71].'\" [Ṣaḥīh in other than this chain. al-Nasāʾī 1404]"
"5803","(5803) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: al-Ḍaḥḥākh ibn Makhlad — Abū ʿĀṣim — narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAwwām narrated from Qatādah from ʿAbd Rabbih from Abī ʿIyāḍ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would say in his sermon: 'All praise is due to Allah — we praise Him and seek His help. We seek refuge in Allah from the evils of our souls. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He misguides, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. He sent him with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner before the Hour. Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger has indeed been guided, and whoever disobeys them harms none but himself, and does not harm Allah at all.'\" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1097]"
"5804","(5804) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: Yūnus informed me that he asked Ibn Shihāb about the sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the day of Jumuʿah. Ibn Shihāb said: \"Indeed, all praise is due to Allah — we praise Him, seek His help, and seek His forgiveness. We seek refuge in Him from the evils of our souls. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He misguides, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. He sent him with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner before the Hour. Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger has indeed been guided, and whoever disobeys them has indeed gone astray. We ask Allah, our Lord, to make us among those who obey Him and obey His Messenger, seek His pleasure, and avoid His wrath. Indeed, we are for Him and unto Him.\" Ibn Shihāb said: \"And it has reached us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) that he would say when he delivered the sermon: 'Everything that is coming is near. There is no distance for what is coming. Allah does not hasten for the haste of anyone, nor does He neglect the affairs of the people as He wills — not as the people wills. The people will a thing and Allah wills a thing. What Allah wills comes to pass, even if the people dislike it. There is no one to remove what Allah has brought near, and no one to bring near what Allah has put far. Nothing occurs except by the permission of Allah.'\" Ibn Shihāb said: \"And ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) would say in his sermon: 'Successful among you is he who is protected from desire, greed, and anger. There is no good in speech other than truthfulness. Whoever lies acts wickedly, and whoever acts wickedly is destroyed. Beware of wickedness — what wickedness is it that a man is created from dust, returns to dust, is alive today and will be dead tomorrow? Work for each day its deeds. Avoid the supplication of the oppressed, and count yourselves among the dead.'\" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1098]"
"5805","(5805) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Tamīm al-Ḥanẓalī narrated to us in Baghdād: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Uwaysī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us: the nephew of Ibn Shihāb narrated from his uncle from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) would say in his sermon: 'Successful among you is he who is protected from desire, anger, and greed, and is granted truthfulness in speech, for it leads him to goodness. Whoever lies acts wickedly,' and he mentioned what follows.\""
"5806","(5806) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us: Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us: ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Abū Ghassān Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl al-Nahdī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī narrated from Nubayṭ ibn Sharīṭ, who said: \"I was riding behind my father on the back of the mount, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was delivering the sermon at the Jamrah. He said: 'All praise is due to Allah — we praise Him, seek His help, and seek His forgiveness. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. I enjoin you to fear of Allah. Which day is the most sacred?' They said: 'This one.' He said: 'And which month is the most sacred?' They said: 'This one.' He said: 'And which land is the most sacred?' They said: 'This land.' He said: 'Indeed, your blood and your wealth are sacred among you, like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours.'\""
"5807","(5807) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥammāmiy al-Muqriʾ informed us in Baghdad: Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us, he said: it was read to Ibrāhīm ibn al-Haytham while I heard: ʿAlī ibn ʿAyyāsh informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Sinān informed us, from Abū al-Zāhiriyyah, from Kathīr ibn Murrah al-Ḥaḍramī, from Shaddād ibn Aws, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"O people, the world is a present enjoyment, from which both the righteous and the wicked eat. The Hereafter is a true promise, in which a just King shall judge, establishing truth and nullifying falsehood.\""
"5808","(5808) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Bajaliy al-Muqriʾ informed us in Kūfah: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Aḥmasī informed us: ʿUbayd ibn Kathīr Abū Saʿīd al-ʿĀmiriy al-Tammār informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayd informed us: ʿIyāḍ ibn Saʿīd al-Thumālī informed us, from Huraym ibn Sufyān al-Bajalī, from Layth ibn Abī Sulaym, from Zubayd ibn al-Ḥārith, from Shaddād ibn Aws, who said: The sermon of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was: \"Indeed, the world is a present enjoyment, from which both the righteous and the wicked eat. Indeed, the Hereafter is a true promise, in which a powerful King shall decree. Beware! All of goodness, in its entirety, lies at its farthest reaches in Paradise. Beware! All of evil, in its entirety, lies at its farthest reaches in the Fire. Act, while you are in fear of Allah. Know that you shall be presented with your deeds, and that you shall inevitably meet your Lord: 'Whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil shall see it' [al-Zalzalah: 7–8].\""
"5809","(5809) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd al-ʿAdanī informed us: Sufyān informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ informed us: Sufyān informed us, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, from Tamīm ibn Ṭarafah, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, who said: A man addressed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: \"Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger has been guided, and whoever disobeys them has gone astray.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"What a poor speaker you are! Say instead: 'And whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger has gone astray.'\" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Wakīʿ, and al-ʿAdanī did not mention his saying: \"Say: 'And whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger has gone astray.'\""
"5810","(5810) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us: Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from Manṣūr, who said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yasār, from Ḥudhayfah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Do not say: 'What Allah wills and so-and-so wills.' Rather say: 'What Allah wills, then so-and-so wills.'\""
"5811","(5811) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us: al-Masʿūdī informed us, from Maʿbad ibn Khālid, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yasār, from Qutaylah bint Ṣayfī al-Juhanī, who said: A learned man from among the Rabbis came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: \"O Muḥammad, what a good people you were, were it not that you commit shirk.\" He said: \"Glory be to Allah! And what is that?\" He said: \"You say, when you swear, 'By the Kaʿbah.'\" The Prophet (ﷺ) paused, then said: \"Whoever swears an oath, let him swear by the Lord of the Kaʿbah.\" Then he said: \"What a good people you were, were it not that you say: 'What Allah wills and so-and-so wills.'\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) paused, then said: \"Whoever says 'What Allah wills,' let him insert between the two: 'then what you will.'\""
"5812","(5812) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: al-Ajlaḥ Abū Ḥujayjah informed us, from Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and spoke to him about a certain matter. The man said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): \"What Allah wills and you will.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Have you made me equal to Allah? Rather say: 'What Allah wills alone.'\""
"5813","(5813) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: ʿAbd al-Majīd informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"That which I see the people supplicating with in the sermon these days—did it reach you from the Prophet (ﷺ) or from someone after the Prophet (ﷺ)?\" He said: \"No, it is merely an innovation. The sermon was only a reminder.\" And the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn Samurah and others regarding the sermon of the Prophet (ﷺ) has already preceded."
"5814","(5814) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh informed us: Ibn ʿAwn informed us, saying: I was informed that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote: \"Let no one be named in supplication.\""
"5815","(5815) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār informed us in Baghdad: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Abū al-Ashʿath Aḥmad ibn al-Miqdām al-ʿIjlī informed us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us orally: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl, ʿĀrim, and ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us. They said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd informed us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir, who said: A man came on Friday while the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. He said to him: \"Have you prayed?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Stand and pray.\""
"5816","(5816) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān informed us, from Ibn ʿAjlān, from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ, who said: I saw Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī come while Marwān was delivering the sermon. He stood and prayed two rakʿahs. The guards came to him to make him sit, but he refused to sit until he had prayed two rakʿahs. When we finished the prayer, we came to him and said: \"O Abū Saʿīd, those men nearly did something to you!\" He said: \"I would never abandon it for anything after something I saw from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) when a man came to him while he was delivering the sermon, entering the mosque in a disheveled state. He said: 'Have you prayed?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Pray two rakʿahs.'\" He said: \"Then the Prophet (ﷺ) urged the people to give charity, and they cast garments. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave the man two garments from among them. When the next Friday came, a man came while the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: 'Have you prayed?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Pray two rakʿahs.' Then he urged the people to give charity, and the man cast one of his two garments. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called out and said: 'Take it!' So he took it. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Look at this one! He came last Friday in a disheveled state, so I commanded the people to give charity, and they cast garments, and I gave him two garments from among them. When this Friday came, I commanded the people to give charity, and he came and cast one of his two garments.'\""
"5817","(5817) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Khuza'i in Makkah narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Zakariyya al-Makki narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us — Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated from Humayd ibn Hilal — from Abu Rifa'ah al-'Adawi, who said: I came upon the Prophet ﷺ while he was delivering a sermon. I said: \"O Messenger of Allah, a stranger has come asking about his religion, not knowing what his religion is.\" He ﷺ turned to me and left his sermon. A chair was brought to me — I thought its legs were made of iron — and he began to teach me what Allah had taught him. Then he returned to his sermon and completed its end. [Sahih. Muslim 876]"
"5818","(5818) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Ibrahim ibn Isma'il al-Qari' narrated to me — al-Hasan ibn Sufyan in Naysabur narrated to us — Shayban ibn Farukh narrated to us — Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated it with the same meaning. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Shayban ibn Farukh. (5818) also."
"5819","(5819) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi informed us verbally — Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Marwazi narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Hilal al-Marwazi narrated to us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq informed us — al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, who said: I heard my father Buraydah saying: The Prophet ﷺ was delivering a sermon to us when al-Hasan and al-Husayn came wearing red shirts, walking and stumbling. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended and picked them up, placing them before him, and then said: \"Allah has spoken truly: {Your wealth and your children are only a trial} [al-Taghabun: 15]. I looked at these two boys walking and stumbling, and I could not bear it, so I interrupted my speech and picked them up.\" Zayd ibn al-Hubab also narrated it from al-Husayn ibn Waqid with the same meaning. [Sahih. al-Tirmidhi 3774]"
"5820","(5820) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: My father stood in the sun while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering a sermon. He ﷺ gave a command concerning him, and he was brought into the shade. [Sahih. Ibn Khuzaymah 1453]"
"5821","(5821) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Yahya narrated from Isma'il — Qays narrated from his father, that he came while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering a sermon. He stood in the sun, so he ﷺ gave a command concerning him, and he was moved into the shade. [Sahih. Abu Dawud 4822]"
"5822","(5822) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us — Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh informed us — Ibn Jurayj narrated from 'Ata' — from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, that when the Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit on Friday, he said: \"Sit down.\" Ibn Mas'ud heard that and sat down. The Prophet ﷺ saw him and said: \"Come forward, O 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud.\" [Da'if. Previously mentioned, 5749]"
"5823","(5823) And 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated from 'Ata', who sent it — Abu Sa'id Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Khatib informed us — Abu Bahr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Barbahari narrated to us — Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us — al-Humaydi narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, who said: The Prophet ﷺ saw 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud outside the mosque while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering a sermon, and he said: \"Come forward, O 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud.\" [Da'if. Previously mentioned, 5748]"
"5824","(5824) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salman ibn al-Hasan al-Faqih in Baghdad narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Milhan narrated to us — Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us — al-Layth narrated from 'Uqayl — from Ibn Shihab, who said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib informed me that Abu Hurayrah informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When you say to your companion on Friday, 'Be quiet,' you have indeed spoken idle words.\" [Sahih. al-Bukhari 892]"
"5825","(5825) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir informed us — my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us — Qutaybah narrated to us — al-Layth narrated from 'Uqayl — from al-Zuhri — from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib — from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever says to his companion on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon, 'Be quiet,' has indeed spoken idle words.\" al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah ibn Sa'id. [Sahih. See the previous hadith]"
"5826","(5826) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us — 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us — Ibn Jurayj narrated from al-Zuhri — from Ibn al-Musayyib — from Abu Hurayrah. Ibn Shihab said: And 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to me from Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Qariz — from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When a man says to his companion, 'Be quiet,' while the imam is delivering the sermon, he has indeed spoken idle words.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Meaning previously stated]"
"5827","(5827) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakka and others narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Malik narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When you say to your companion, 'Be quiet,' while the Imam is delivering the sermon on Friday, you have indeed spoken idle words (laghawta).\" [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5828","(5828) He said: and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), with the same meaning, except that he said: \"laghaïta.\" Ibn Uyaynah said: \"Laghaïta is the dialect of Abu Hurayrah.\" [Sahih. Al-Shafi'i narrated it as 292.]"
"5829","(5829) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated it to us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sahl ibn Bahr narrated to us: Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us: Sufyan narrated it, mentioning something similar to it, except that he said: Abu al-Zinad said: \"It is only the dialect of Abu Hurayrah; the correct form is laghawta.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Abi Umar, and Ibn Ajlan narrated it from Abu al-Zinad with an additional wording in it."
"5830","(5830) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn Ishaq al-Bazzaz narrated to us in Baghdad: Abu Muhammad Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Fakihi narrated to us in Makkah: Abu Yahya ibn Abi Masrrah narrated to us: al-Muqri' narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"When you say to your companion, 'Be quiet,' on Friday, you have indeed spoken idle words (laghawta) against yourself.\" [Sahih]"
"5831","(5831) Abu Ali al-Rudhbari narrated to us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad and Abu Kamil narrated to us, they said: Yazid narrated to us, from Habib al-Mu'allim, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Three types of people attend Friday prayer: one who attends it and engages in idle talk (yalghu) — that is his share of it; one who attends it with supplication — he is a man who supplicates to Allah; if He wills, He grants him, and if He wills, He withholds from him; and one who attends it in silence and attentiveness, and does not step over the neck of a Muslim nor harm anyone — it will be an expiation until the following Friday plus an additional three days, because Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: {Whoever comes with a good deed will have ten times the like thereof} [al-An'am:16].\" [Hasan. Abu Dawud 1113]"
"5832","(5832) Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ja'far, meaning Ibn Abi Kathir, narrated to me: Sharik, meaning Ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir, narrated to me, from Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Dharr, who said: I entered the mosque on Friday while the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. I sat close to Ubayy ibn Ka'b. The Prophet (ﷺ) recited Surah Bara'ah. I said to Ubayy: \"When was this Surah revealed?\" He remained silent and did not speak to me. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) finished his prayer, I said to Ubayy: \"I asked you a question and you rebuffed me and did not speak to me.\" Ubayy said: \"You will have nothing from your prayer except what you spoke idly (laghawta).\" I went to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: \"O Prophet of Allah, I was beside Ubayy while you were reciting Bara'ah, and I asked him when this Surah was revealed, and he rebuffed me and did not speak to me. Then he said: 'You will have nothing from your prayer except what you spoke idly.'\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Ubayy has spoken the truth.\"\n\nAbd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated it from Sharik, from Ata', from Abu al-Darda' or Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and he placed the incident between the two of them. Harb ibn Qays narrated it from Abu al-Darda' and placed the incident between him and Ubayy. Isa ibn Jarirah narrated it from Jabir ibn Abd Allah and mentioned the meaning of this incident between Ibn Mas'ud and Ubayy ibn Ka'b. al-Hakim ibn Aban narrated it from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, and placed the meaning of this incident between an unnamed man and Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, and made the one who was correct Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud instead of Ubayy. There is nothing in this chapter more authentic than the hadith whose chain we have mentioned, and Allah knows best. Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated it in a mursal form between Abu Dharr and Ubayy ibn Ka'b regarding something he asked him about. Muhammad ibn Amr narrated it in a musnad form from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah. [Jayyid. Ibn Khuzaymah 1807]"
"5833","(5833) Abu Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Hammad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: While the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering the Friday sermon, Abu Dharr said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b: \"When was this Surah revealed?\" He did not answer him. When he finished his prayer, he said to him: \"You will have nothing from your prayer except what you spoke idly (laghawta).\" Abu Dharr came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and mentioned that to him, and he said: \"Ubayy has spoken the truth (sadaqa Ubayy).\"\n\n[Sahih in view of other narrations. Al-Tayalisi narrated it as 2365.]"
"5834","(5834) Abu Sa'id Muhammad ibn Musa ibn al-Fadl narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazid narrated to me: my father narrated to us: Ibn Jabir narrated to me: Ata' al-Khurasani narrated to me, from a freed slave of his wife, Uthman, who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) say from the pulpit: \"When Friday comes, the devils go out with their banners to the markets, deterring people with distractions, reminding them of their needs, and discouraging them from Friday prayer. The angels go out with their banners to the doors of the mosques, writing down the time at which each man came — so-and-so came at such-and-such an hour, so-and-so came at two hours. When a man sits in a position where he can listen and see clearly, and he is attentive and does not speak idly, he will have two portions of reward. If he sits far away and is attentive and does not speak idly, he will have one portion of reward. If he sits in a position where he can listen and see clearly, but speaks idly and is not attentive, he will bear two portions — or he said, one portion — of sin. Whoever says to his brother on Friday, 'Hush (sih),' has indeed spoken idle words. Whoever speaks idly has nothing from his Friday prayer.\" Then he said at the end of that: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say that.\"\n\nAbu Dawud narrated it in the book of Sunan. [Da'if. Ahmad 1/93]"
"5835","(5835) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakka and others narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Malik narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu Ahmad al-Mihranjani narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Abdi narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, the freed slave of Ubayd Allah ibn Umar ibn Ubayd Allah, from Malik ibn Abi Amir, that Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say in his sermon — he rarely omitted this when he delivered a sermon: \"When the Imam stands to deliver the sermon on Friday, listen and be silent, for the one who is silent and does not hear has the same share of reward as the one who listens and is silent. When the prayer commences, straighten the rows and align the shoulders, for the straightening of the rows is part of the completeness of the prayer.\" Then he would not say the Takbir until men whom he had entrusted with straightening the rows came to him and informed him that the rows had been straightened, and then he would say the Takbir. [Sahih. Malik 229]"
"5836","(5836) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas narrated to us: al-Rabi' narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Ibrahim narrated to us, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, that he saw no harm in mentioning Allah to oneself — with Takbir, Tahlil, and Tasbih.\n\nHe said: and al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: I do not know except that Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir narrated to me that he asked Ibrahim: \"May I recite while the Imam is delivering the sermon on Friday, when I cannot hear the sermon?\" He said: \"Perhaps it will not harm you.\" [Very Da'if]"
"5837","(5837) Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Zakariyya informed us — Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us — my grandfather narrated to us — 'Ali ibn Hujr narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Sharik narrated that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: A man entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was upon the pulpit on the day of Jumu'ah. He said: \"O Messenger of Allah, when is the Hour?\" The people gestured to him to be silent. He asked three times, and each time they gestured to him to be silent. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him on the third occasion: \"Woe to you! What have you prepared for it?\" And he mentioned the hadith. [Good. Ibn Khuzaymah 796]"
"5838","(5838) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us — 'Arim, Sulayman, and Musaddad said — Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, who said: A man came while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon to the people on the day of Jumu'ah. He said: \"Have you prayed, O so-and-so?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Stand and pray.\" The wording is that of 'Arim.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from 'Arim, and Muslim narrated it from Abu al-Rabi' from Hammad. The hadith of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri and others in the chapter of the Imam speaking during the sermon has preceded, and the hadith of the man who requested the prayer for rain is recorded in the book of Istisqa'. [Sahih. Preceded at 5689]"
"5839","(5839) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu 'Abdullah Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Susi both said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazyad narrated to us — my father narrated to us — al-Awza'i narrated to us — Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us — Anas ibn Malik narrated to us, saying: A year of drought afflicted the people during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). While the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was upon the pulpit on the day of Jumu'ah delivering the sermon to the people, a Bedouin came to him and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the livestock have perished and the families are starving. Supplicate to Allah for us.\" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raised his hands, and we did not see any patch of cloud in the sky. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, he had not lowered them before clouds like mountains had risen up. Then he did not come down from the pulpit until I saw the rain dripping from his beard. So we received rain that day, the following day, the day after that, and the one after that, until the next Jumu'ah. Then that Bedouin — or he said another man — stood and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the buildings have collapsed and the families are starving. Supplicate to Allah for us.\" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raised his hands and said: \"O Allah, around us and not upon us.\" He said: \"He would not point with his hand toward any direction of the clouds except that they parted, until Madinah became like an open space, and the valley of Qanat flowed for a month. No one came from any of the surrounding areas except that he reported abundant rain.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari and Muslim recorded it in the Sahih from the hadith of al-Awza'i. [Sahih. Bukhari 890]"
"5840","(5840) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Shadil ibn 'Ali narrated to us — Abu Marwan, meaning al-'Uthmani, narrated to us — Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Sa'd, narrated from al-Zuhri from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b that the group whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent to Ibn Abi al-Huquq in Khaybar to kill him — they killed him and came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was standing upon the pulpit on the day of Jumu'ah. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw them, he said: \"Prosperous are the faces!\" They said: \"May your face be prosperous, O Messenger of Allah!\" He said: \"Did you kill him?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He asked for the sword with which he had been killed while he was standing upon the pulpit, and he unsheathed it. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, this is his nourishment in the scabbard of the sword.\" The group consisted of 'Abdullah ibn 'Atik, 'Abdullah ibn Unays, and Aswad ibn Khuza'i, an ally among them, and Abu Qatadah — according to what al-Zuhri thought. Al-Zuhri did not preserve the name of the fifth. Although this is a mursal narration, it is a good mursal narration, and this is a well-known story among the scholars of military expeditions.\n\nIt has been narrated through another chain from al-Zuhri, and it has been narrated from Abu al-Aswad from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, and they both mentioned this story and included among them Mas'ud ibn Sinan alongside those mentioned. [Sahih through other chains. 'Abd al-Razzaq 9747]"
"5841","(5841) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad narrated it to us — 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to me — Hassan ibn 'Abdullah narrated to me — Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me — Abu al-Aswad narrated from 'Urwah.\n\n(Additionally) Ya'qub said — Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated from Musa ibn 'Uqbah from Ibn Shihab, and they both mentioned this story. It has also been narrated through another chain, connected and abbreviated."
"5842","(5842) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith al-Faqih al-Isbahani informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Amr, meaning Ibn 'Abd al-Khaliq, narrated to us — Ibrahim al-Jawhari narrated to us — Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr from Ibn 'Abdullah ibn Unays from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me to Ibn Abi al-Huquq. When I returned, he was delivering the sermon on the day of Jumu'ah. He said: \"Prosperous is the face!\" I said: \"And your face, O Messenger of Allah — may it prosper!\"\n\nThat has been narrated in full from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b from 'Abdullah ibn Unays, connected. [Hasan through other chains]"
"5843","(5843) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Nasr al-Marwazi narrated to us — Abu al-Muwajjah narrated to us — Abu 'Ammar.\n\n(Additionally) Abu Hazim al-'Abdawi al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — Abu 'Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth narrated to us — al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq from al-Mughirah ibn Shubayl from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah, who said: When I approached the city of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), I halted my mount, unpacked my saddlebag, and put on my robe. I entered while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) greeted me, and the people stared at me with their eyes. I said to my companion: \"O 'Abdullah, did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mention anything about me?\" He said: \"Yes, he mentioned you in the best of terms.\" While he was delivering the sermon, something occurred to him during his sermon, and he said: \"Soon there will enter upon you through this door or through this path one of the best of the people of Yemen, and upon his face is the touch of an angel.\" So I praised Allah for what He had tested me with. [Hasan. Ahmad 4/359]"
"5844","(5844) Abu Sa'id Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Khatib informed us — Abu Bahr al-Barbahari narrated to us — Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us — al-Humaydi narrated to us — 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated from Ma'mar from al-Zuhri from Salim ibn 'Abdullah from his father, who said: 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) came to 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was upon the pulpit delivering the sermon on the day of Jumu'ah. He said: \"What time is this?\" 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"It is nothing but the time for wudu'.\" He said: \"And wudu' as well? Yet you know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command us with ghusl.\" [Sahih. Its meaning is extracted in the book of Ghusl]"
"5845","(5845) He said — al-Humaydi narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — Mis'ar narrated from 'Imran ibn Musa from his father, who said: 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) said while he was upon the pulpit on the day of Jumu'ah delivering the sermon to a man: \"Have you purchased this for our family?\" And he pointed with the tip of his finger, meaning wheat. [Da'if]"
"5846","(5846) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — al-Baghandi narrated to us — Qabisa narrated to us — Sufyan narrated from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha' from Mu'awiyah ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us with seven things and forbade us from seven things. He commanded us to visit the sick, to follow funeral processions, to return the greeting of peace, to accept the invitation of the host, to fulfill oaths, to respond to the one who sneezes, and to help the oppressed. And he forbade us from gold rings, from drinking from silver vessels, from silk, brocade, silk brocade (istabraq), qassi cloth, and the silk cushion (mitharah).\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Qabisa ibn 'Uqbah, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Sufyan. [Sahih. Bukhari 1182]"
"5847","(5847) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Quraysh informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Fayyāḍ ibn Zuhayr informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated on the authority of al-Zuhrī, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Five rights of a Muslim over his fellow Muslim are: returning the greeting of salām, responding to the one who sneezes, visiting the ill, following the funeral procession, and accepting the invitation.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and al-Bukhārī alluded to it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1183]"
"5848","(5848) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm narrated on the authority of Hishām, from al-Ḥasan, from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"When a man sneaks and the imām is delivering the khutbah on the day of Jumuʿah, respond to his sneeze.\"\n\nAnd this is a mursal narration.\n\nIt has also been narrated from al-Ḥasan as his own statement, and from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh regarding returning the salām, and from Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī regarding responding to the sneezer and returning the salām — and it has been narrated from him that he disliked it. It is mentioned that Ibn al-Musayyab said regarding the salām: \"He should return it to himself (silently).\" And he was asked about responding to the sneezer, and he forbade it. And from Ibn Sīrīn regarding the salām: \"He used to return it by gesture and would not speak.\""
"5849","(5849) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated on the authority of al-Aʿmash, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever performs wuḍūʾ and does it well, then comes to the Jumuʿah prayer and sits near (the imām), listens attentively, and pays close heed, he will be forgiven from one Jumuʿah to the next plus an additional three days. But if he touches the pebbles, he has engaged in idle talk (laghā).\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others from Abū Muʿāwiyah. And in it there is evidence that wuḍūʾ suffices in place of the ghusl of Jumuʿah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 857]"
"5850","(5850) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh in Rayy informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq informed us: Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated on the authority of Ibn Jurayj: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed me, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you breaks his wuḍūʾ during the prayer, let him hold his nose and then leave.\"\n\nSimilarly, al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā al-Sīnānī and ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī al-Muqaddamī narrated it from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. And a group narrated it from Hishām as a mursal narration without mentioning ʿĀʾishah in it. And al-Thawrī narrated it from Hishām as a mursal narration, saying: \"When one of you breaks his wuḍūʾ on the day of Jumuʿah, let him hold his nose and then leave.\""
"5851","(5851) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us: Abū Usāmah narrated on the authority of Jarīr, meaning Ibn Ḥāzim, who said: I heard Thābit al-Bunānī mention, on the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, who said: A man would present himself to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) after the prayer had been commenced and after he had descended from the pulpit, and he would stand with him until he fulfilled his need, then he would come forward to lead the prayer.\n\nAbū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, saying: Abū Dāwūd said regarding this ḥadīth: \"It is not well-known from Thābit, and it is among the narrations in which Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim was alone (in reporting it).\"\n\nThe Shaykh said: And al-Bukhārī (may Allah have mercy on him) mentioned something to that effect.\n\nAnd the well-known narration from Thābit is what… [Shādh. Abū Dāwūd 1120]"
"5852","(5852) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Abū Muslim informed us: Ḥajjāj, meaning Ibn Minhāl, informed us: Ḥammād, meaning Ibn Salamah, narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad informed us: Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us: Ḥibbān informed us: Ḥammād narrated on the authority of Thābit, from Anas, that he said: The ʿIshāʾ prayer was commenced, and a man said to me, \"I have a need.\" So the Prophet (ﷺ) stood conversing with him until the people fell asleep, or some of the people fell asleep, then they prayed.\n\nThis is the wording of Ḥibbān's ḥadīth. And in the narration of Ḥajjāj, he said: \"The prayer — the last ʿIshāʾ — was commenced, and a man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have a need.' So he stood with him conversing until some of the people became drowsy. Then he came and led the prayer, and he did not mention that they performed wuḍūʾ.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 617]"
"5853","(5853) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Ḥusayn ibn Muʿādh informed us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated on the authority of Ḥumayd, who said: I asked Thābit al-Bunānī about a man who speaks after the prayer has been commenced. He narrated to me on the authority of Anas, who said: \"The prayer was commenced, and a man presented himself to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he detained him after the prayer had been commenced.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAyysh al-Raqqām from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā. And with the same meaning, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb narrated it from Anas, and its mention has passed previously. And al-Zuhrī narrated it from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal narration with the meaning of the narration of Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 201]"
"5854","(5854) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs narrated on the authority of Shuʿbah, from Abī ʿImrān al-Jawnī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ṣāmit, from Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), that he went out to al-Rabadhah, and at the water source there was an Ethiopian slave. The prayer was commenced, and it was said, \"Abū Dharr!\" So the slave stepped back. Abū Dharr said to him: \"Come forward! Indeed, my beloved (ﷺ) instructed me to listen and obey, even if the one in charge is a mutilated-limbed Ethiopian slave.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned, 5119]"
"5855","(5855) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq informed us in Baghdād: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Qurashī al-Kūfī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us: Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb informed us: Mālik ibn Anas informed me: al-Zuhrī narrated on the authority of Abī ʿUbayd, who said: \"I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him). Then I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him). Then I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), while ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) was under siege.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned, 5310]"
"5856","(5856) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Khālid ibn Khalī informed us: Bishr ibn Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated on the authority of his father, from al-Zuhrī, who said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAdī ibn al-Khiyār informed him that he entered upon the Commander of the Faithful ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) in his house while he was under siege, and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) was leading the people in prayer. He said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, I feel uneasy about praying with these people while you are under siege, and you are the imām. What do you think about praying with them?\" ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: \"The prayer is the best of what the people do. When they do good, do good with them, and when they do evil, avoid their evil.\"\n\nAnd the rest of the narrations on this meaning have passed in the chapter on Imāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned, 5311]"
"5857","(5857) Al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Ibn 'Uthman informed us: 'Abdullah informed us: Harmalah ibn 'Imran narrated to me: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Mulyayl al-Salihi narrated to me, tracing his lineage up to Quda'ah, saying: My father narrated to me, saying: I was sitting with 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir near the minbar on the day of Jumu'ah. Muhammad ibn Abi Hudhayfah came out and ascended the minbar. He addressed the people and then recited a surah from the Qur'an to them. He was among the most proficient of people in recitation. 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir said: Allah and His Messenger have spoken truly, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"There will recite the Qur'an men whose recitation will not pass beyond their collarbones, and they will pass through the religion as the arrow passes through the game.\" Ibn Abi Hudhayfah heard this and said: By Allah, if you are truthful—and as far as I know you are a liar—you are indeed one of them. 'Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, said: The import of this hadith is that they will gather with them and say to them these words. [Weak. Ahmad 4/145]"
"5858","(5858) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Yahya, from Dawud ibn Husayn, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A boy should not lead the prayer until he reaches puberty. [Absolutely weak. 'Abd al-Razzaq 1872]"
"5859","(5859) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Mis'ar, meaning Ibn Habib al-Jarmi, narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Salamah narrated that his father and a group of his people came as a delegation to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when the people had embraced Islam. They learned the Qur'an, and when they had fulfilled their need, they said: \"Who will lead us in prayer?\" He said: \"The one among you who has memorized—or gathered—the most of the Qur'an.\" He said: \"They returned to their people and asked, but they found no one who had gathered or memorized more of the Qur'an than I had. At that time I was a boy, and I had a cloak upon me. They put me forward, and I led them in prayer. I have not attended a gathering of Jarm except that I was their imam to this very day.\" Mis'ar ibn Habib said: \"He used to lead them in their funeral prayers and in their mosques until he passed away.\" And it was narrated to us in the chapter of Imamah from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani from 'Amr, and he said in the hadith: \"I was a boy of seven or six years,\" and in another narration: \"seven or eight.\" [Sahih. Previously referenced 5137]"
"5860","(5860) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Bishran al-'Adl informed us in Baghdad: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us: Abu Muhammad al-Hilali narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, narrating that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"On the day of Jumu'ah, there are angels at every door of the mosque, recording the people in order—the first and the first. The one who hastens to the prayer is like one who offers a camel in sacrifice; then the one who follows him is like one who offers a cow; then the one who follows him is like one who offers a ram, until he mentioned the hen and the egg. When the imam sits, they fold up the scrolls and gather for the khutbah.\" [Sahih. Bukhari 887]"
"5861","(5861) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn and Muhammad ibn Hajjaj al-Warraq narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, mentioning something similar, except that he said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'They record the people according to their stations—the first and the first. When the imam sits, they fold up the scrolls and listen to the khutbah.'\" Then he mentioned the one who hastens with its meaning. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"5862","(5862) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn 'Abdillah narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu 'Abdillah al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"On the day of Jumu'ah, the angels stand at the door of the mosque and record the first and the first. The likeness of the one who hastens is like the one who offers a badanah (she-camel) in sacrifice; then like the one who offers a cow; then like the one who offers a ram; then like the one who offers a hen; then like the one who offers an egg. When the imam comes out, they fold up their scrolls and listen to the remembrance (dhikr).\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Yunus ibn Yazid from al-Zuhri. [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"5863","(5863) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and others informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Malik informed us. (H) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, from Malik, from Sumayy, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever performs ghusl on the day of Jumu'ah as one does after janabah, then sets out, it is as if he has offered a badanah (she-camel) in sacrifice. Whoever sets out in the second hour, it is as if he has offered a cow. Whoever sets out in the third hour, it is as if he has offered a horned ram. Whoever sets out in the fourth hour, it is as if he has offered a hen. Whoever sets out in the fifth hour, it is as if he has offered an egg. When the imam comes out, the angels are present, listening to the remembrance (dhikr).\" The wording of both their hadiths is the same. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from Malik. [Sahih. Bukhari 841]"
"5864","(5864) Abu 'Abdillah Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us: Hammam informed us: Matar informed us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'aib, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Angels sit at the doors of the mosque on the day of Jumu'ah, recording the coming of the people until the imam comes out. When the imam comes out, the scrolls are folded up and the pens are raised.\" He said: \"Then the angels say to one another: 'What detained so-and-so? What detained so-and-so?'\" He said: \"The angels say: 'O Allah, if he is ill, then heal him; if he is lost, then guide him; if he is in need, then enrich him.'\" [Weak. Ibn Khuzaymah 1771]"
"5865","(5865) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Harithi narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Ju'fi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to us, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, from Aws ibn Aws, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned the day of Jumu'ah and said: \"Whoever washed (his limbs) and performed ghusl, went early in the morning, and came early, drew near, listened attentively, and heard (with care), his sins between that Jumu'ah and the next, plus an additional three days, will be forgiven. And whoever touches the pebbles has engaged in idle talk (laghwa).\" And likewise Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari and Hassan ibn 'Atiyyah narrated it from Abu al-Ash'ath, and Hassan ibn 'Atiyyah mentioned that Aws heard it from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Sahih. Ibn Khuzaymah 1767]"
"5866","(5866) Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Isma'il al-Tabrani informed us there: 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Tusi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'igh narrated to us: Rawh narrated to us: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from 'Uthman al-Shami, who heard Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani narrating from Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever washed (his limbs) and performed ghusl on the day of Jumu'ah, went early in the morning, and came early, drew near, drew close, listened, and was attentive, for every step he takes he will have the reward of a year's standing in prayer and its fasting.\" Thus did a group narrate it from Thawr ibn Yazid, and the error in its isnad and matn is from this 'Uthman al-Shami. The correct narration is that of the group from Abu al-Ash'ath, from Aws, from the Prophet (ﷺ). And Allah knows best. And it was narrated to us from Makhul that he said regarding the words \"washed and performed ghusl\": it means he washed his head and his body. And Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz said the same, and this is the correct understanding, for they used to put kohl or the like in their heads, so they would first wash their heads and then perform ghusl. And Allah knows best. [Sahih in other respects. Ahmad 2/209]"
"5867","(5867) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Shayban narrated to us — Sufyan narrated from al-Zuhri — from Salim — from his father, who said: I never heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) recite it except as \"Famḍū ilā dhikri Allah\" (Proceed to the remembrance of Allah). [Sahih. Malik 239]"
"5868","(5868) And Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — Sufayn ibn 'Uyaynah mentioned it with a similar narration.\n\nAl-Shafi'i said: It is understood that al-sa'y (striving) in this context refers to action (al-'amal), not physical walking upon one's feet. Allah, the Most High, says: *\"Verily, your efforts are diverse\"* (al-Layl: 4). And He says: *\"And whoever desires the Hereafter and strives for it with due striving, while being a believer\"* (al-Isra': 19). And He says: *\"And your striving will be appreciated\"* (al-Insan: 22). And He says: *\"And there is nothing for man except what he strives for\"* (al-Najm: 39). And He says: *\"And when he turns away, he strives to spread corruption in the land\"* (al-Baqarah: 205).\n\nThe Shaykh said: And there has been narrated from Abu Dharr that which corroborates this. [Sahih. See the preceding narration]"
"5869","(5869) Abu 'Abd Allah Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu al-'Abbas al-A'ṣam narrated to us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us — Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to me — al-Awza'i narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir — from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ṣamit, who said: I went out to the mosque on the day of Jumu'ah and met Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him). While we were walking, I heard the call (al-nida'), so I hastened my walking, due to the words of Allah, the Almighty and Majestic: *\"When the call to prayer is made on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah\"* (al-Jumu'ah: 9). He (Abu Dharr) pulled me with a pull that nearly caused me to lose him, and he said: \"Are we not in a state of striving?\"\n\nThe Shaykh said: And in the Sunnah there is that which confirms all of this. [Da'if]"
"5870","(5870) Abu Ḥazim 'Umar ibn Ahmad al-'Abdawi al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Faḍl Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Sayyar al-'Adl informed us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Khuza'i narrated to us — Abu al-Yaman al-Ḥakam ibn Nafi' informed me — Shu'ayb ibn Abi Ḥamzah narrated from al-Zuhri, who said: Abu Salamah narrated to me that Abu Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) saying: \"When the prayer is established (iqāmat al-ṣalāh), do not come to it running; come to you walking, and upon you is tranquility (al-sakīnah). Whatever you catch, pray; and whatever you miss, complete it.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from Abu al-Yaman. [Sahih. Bukhari 866]"
"5871","(5871) Abu Aḥmad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajani informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Malik narrated from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman — from his father and Ishaq Abi 'Abd Allah, both of whom informed him that they both heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"When the call to prayer is made (thuwwiba bi-l-ṣalāh), do not come to it while you are running. Come to you, and upon you is tranquility. Whatever you catch, pray; and whatever you miss, complete it. For verily, one of you is in prayer as long as he intends the prayer.\" [Sahih. Muslim 602]"
"5872","(5872) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Naḍr al-Faqih narrated to us — Abu al-Faḍl Ṣalih ibn Muhammad al-Razi narrated to us — Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ja'far informed me — al-'Ala' narrated from his father — from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said — and he mentioned it with the like of it, except that he said: \"For verily, when one of you intends the prayer, he is in prayer.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from Yahya ibn Ayyub, and he extracted it from the ḥadith of Malik, as was previously mentioned in the Book of Prayer. [Sahih. See the preceding narration]"
"5873","(5873) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra' informed us — Abu Nu'aym narrated to us — Shaybān narrated — (ḥ) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri: Abu al-Faḍl, on a Thursday, the eleventh remaining of Sha'ban, in the year two hundred and sixty-seven — Yazid ibn Harun informed us — Shaybān: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Nahwi narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir — from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah — from his father, who said: While we were praying with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), he heard the clamour of men. When he completed the prayer, he called them and said: \"What is the matter with you?\" They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, we hurried to the prayer.\" He said: \"Do not do that. When you come to the prayer, upon you is tranquility. Whatever you catch, pray; and whatever you miss, complete it.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it from Abu Nu'aym, and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadith of Shaybān. [Sahih. Bukhari 609]"
"5874","(5874) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Umar ibn al-Ḥafṣ al-Muqri', known as Ibn al-Ḥammami, in Baghdad, narrated to us — Abu Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salman al-Faqih narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Anṣari narrated to us — Ḥumayd narrated from Anas, who said: A man came and walked briskly until he reached the people, and he was out of breath. When he stood for the prayer, he said: \"Al-ḥamdu lillāhi ḥadran kathiran ṭayyiban mubārakan fīhi\" (All praise is due to Allah, an abundance of pure and blessed praise). When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) completed the prayer, he said: \"Who spoke, or who is the speaker? For indeed, he said good words and did not say anything bad.\" He said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I reached the row while I was out of breath, and my breath urged me on.\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"I saw twelve angels hastening to see which of them would take it up.\" Then he said: \"When one of you comes to the prayer, let him walk at his own pace, and let him pray what he catches, and let him make up what preceded him.\" [Sahih]"
"5875","(5875) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-'Alawi informed us orally — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sharqi narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Dhuhli narrated to us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak — from Ma'mar — from Hammam ibn Munabbih — from Abu Hurayrah — from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \"A good word (al-kalimah al-ṭayyibah) is charity, and your walking to the mosque is charity.\"\n\n'Abd al-Razzaq narrated it from Ma'mar, and in the ḥadith he said: \"And every step he takes toward the prayer is charity.\" And from that perspective, the two Shaykhs (al-Bukhari and Muslim) extracted it in the two Ṣaḥiḥ collections, and it is extracted at the end of the Book of Zakah, by the will of Allah. [Sahih. Aḥmad 2/312]"
"5876","(5876) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me — 'Imran ibn Musa narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Malik narrated to us — al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us — Yazid ibn Abi Maryam said: While I was heading to the Jumu'ah, 'Ubaydah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij caught up with me — he was riding while I was walking. He said: \"Count these steps of yours in the cause of Allah, for I heard Abu 'Abs ibn Jabr al-Anṣari saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Whosever two feet become dusty in the cause of Allah, Allah will forbid them to the Fire.'\" [Sahih. Bukhari 765]"
"5877","(5877) Abū ʿAmr informed us; Abū Bakr informed us; al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn Rushayd and Abū Hammām narrated to us, both saying: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us. He mentioned it with its meaning and said: \"I heard Abā ʿAbs, and he had been a Companion (ṣaḥbah).\"\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh from al-Walīd ibn Muslim."
"5878","(5878) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Jarjarāʾī informed us; Ibn al-Mubārak informed us, from al-Awzāʿī; Ḥassān ibn ʿAṭiyyah narrated to us; Abū al-Ashʿath al-Ṣanʿānī narrated to me; Aws ibn Aws al-Thaqafī narrated to me, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n\"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, then bathes (ightasala), then goes early (bakkara) and comes at the earliest hour (ibtakara), and walks and does not ride, and draws close to the Imam and listens and does not engage in idle talk — for every step he takes, he shall have the reward of a year's worth of fasting and standing in prayer.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See no. 5865]"
"5879","(5879) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; ʿAbdān, Ibn Abī ʢĀṣim, and Ḥasan ibn Hārūn narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us. He mentioned it in a similar manner, except that he said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said.\""
"5880","(5880) Abū ʿAlī — al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Shādhān — informed us in Baghdad; Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Isrāʾīl informed us, from Abī Isḥāq, from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — who said:\n\n\"Walk to the prayer, for those who were better than you — Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, the Muhājirūn, and the Anṣār, may Allah be pleased with them all — used to walk to it. Keep your steps close together, and increase the remembrance of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. It should not concern you that you do not accompany anyone except one who assists you in the remembrance of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5881","(5881) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd ibn Aḥmad al-Zawzanī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Rūḥ al-Madāʾinī narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn Qays informed us, from Saʿd ibn Isḥāq, from Abī Thumāmah al-Ḥannāṭ, who said:\n\n\"Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah met me while I was at the Balāṭ, heading toward the mosque, with my fingers interlocked. He said: 'Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you performs wuḍūʾ and does it well, then goes out intending to go to the mosque, let him not interlock his fingers.\"'\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi. Abū Dāwūd 562]"
"5882","(5882) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sahl al-Mujawwiz narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn al-Haytham al-Muʾadhdhin narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn Qays al-Farrāʾ narrated to us; Saʿd ibn Isḥāq informed us, from Abī Thumāmah al-Ḥannāṭ, who said:\n\n\"Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah met me while I was heading toward the mosque, interlocking my fingers. He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"When one of you performs wuḍūʾ and then comes to the mosque, let him not interlock his fingers, for he is in prayer.\"'\"\n\nAnd ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb and Abū ʿĀmir ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAmr also narrated it from Dāwūd ibn Qays.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi]"
"5883","(5883) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb informed us, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from a mawlā of Banī Sālim, from his father, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"When one of you performs wuḍūʾ and then goes out for the prayer, he is in prayer. So let none of you interlock his fingers after performing wuḍūʾ or after entering the prayer.\"\n\nAnd Shabābah said, from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from al-Maqburī, from a man from Banī Sulaym, that he informed him from his father, from Kaʿb, from the Prophet (ﷺ), and he said: \"And let not one of you oppose (cross) his fingers during the prayer.\" And it was also said: from a man from Banī Sālim.\n\nThis hadith is differed upon regarding Saʿīd. It was said from him thus; and it was said from him, from Kaʿb; and it was said from him, from a man, from Kaʿb; and it was said from him, from Abī Hurayrah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to Kaʿb; and it was said from Ibn ʿAjlān, from his father, from Abī Hurayrah. And the correct narration from Ibn ʿAjlān is from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, according to the three routes.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi. See what precedes it]"
"5884","(5884) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Abū Mūsā al-Harawī informed us; Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth informed us, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abī Thumāmah al-Burrī, who said:\n\n\"I went out intending to pray, and Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah accompanied me. He looked at me while I was interlocking my fingers. He said: 'Do not interlock your fingers, for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade us from interlocking our fingers during the prayer.' I said: 'I am not in prayer.' He said: 'Have you not performed wuḍūʾ and gone out intending to pray?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then you are in prayer.'\"\n\n(T) And ʿĪsā ibn Yūnūs also narrated it, from Saʿd ibn Isḥāq, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abī Thumāmah, thus returning the hadith to al-Maqburī from Abī Thumāmah.\n\nThe Sheikh said: In this is evidence that the prohibition pertains to what occurs during the prayer, and that Kaʿb included the one going out to the prayer in that, based on the evidence he mentioned.\n\nAnd it has also been narrated from another route from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah with the first wording.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi]"
"5885","(5885) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Qusayṭ narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr informed us, from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah, from al-Ḥakam, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him:\n\n\"O Kaʿb, when you perform wuḍūʾ and do it well, then go out to the mosque, do not interlock your fingers, for you are in prayer.\"\n\nThis is a ṣaḥīḥ chain, if al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Raqqī preserved it. I have not found a corroboration for him in what he narrated of that, and Allah knows best.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi]"
"5886","(5886) Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: I heard Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrating from Abī al-Zāhiriyyah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr, who said:\n\n\"I was sitting beside him on Friday. Then a man came, stepping over the necks of the people. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: 'Sit down, for you have caused harm and have come too late.'\"\n\nAbū al-Zāhiriyyah said: \"We used to converse with him until the Imam came out.\"\n\n[Strong. Abū Dāwūd 1118]"
"5887","(5887) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn Abī ʿUqayl and Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Miṣrī both said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us. Ibn Abī ʿUqayl said: Usāmah, meaning Ibn Zayd, informed me, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: \"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, applies some of the perfume of his wife if she has any, puts on his best garments, then does not step over the necks of the people, and does not engage in idle talk during the sermon, it shall be an expiation for the time between the two Fridays. But whoever engages in idle talk and steps over the necks of the people, it shall be [as though he has performed only] the ẓuhr [prayer].\" [Hasan. Abū Dāwūd 347]"
"5888","(5888) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī al-Qurashī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, uses the siwāk, puts on his finest garments, applies perfume from that of his household, then comes to the mosque and does not step over the necks of the people, and prays what is written for him, and when the imām comes out, remains silent — it shall be an expiation for what is between that and the next Friday.\" [Hasan. Abū Dāwūd 343]"
"5889","(5889) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Mazzī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, from one who narrated to him, from Abū Hurayrah, that he used to say: \"That one of you should pray at the back of the ḥarrah [stony tract] is better for him than that he sit until, when the imām stands to deliver the khutbah, he comes stepping over the necks of the people.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its chain. Mālik 244]"
"5890","(5890) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Sharīk narrated to us, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: \"When we came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we would sit wherever we reached.\""
"5891","(5891) Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us; Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us, from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, that Abū Murrah, the mawlā of ʿUqayl ibn Abī Ṭālib, informed him, from Abū Wāqid al-Laythī, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while he was sitting in the mosque with the people around him, three men came. Two of them came toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and one went away. The two stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. As for one of them, he saw a gap in the circle and sat in it. As for the other, he sat behind them. As for the third, he turned away and left. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had finished, he said: 'Shall I not inform you about the three men? As for one of them, he sought refuge with Allah, so Allah granted him refuge. As for the other, he felt shy, so Allah showed modesty toward him. As for the other, he turned away, so Allah turned away from him.'\" [al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd, from Mālik. Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 66]"
"5892","(5892) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed me; Ḥibbān ibn Mūsā informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Wadiʿah, from Salmān al-Fārisī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday and purifies himself as best he can, then applies oil from his oil, or touches some perfume from his household, then goes forth and does not separate between two people, then prays what is decreed for him, and when the imām comes out, remains silent — he shall be forgiven what is between that and the next Friday.\" [al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān, from Ibn al-Mubārak. And with this isnād, a group narrated it from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb; some of them did not mention Abū Saʿīd in their chain. It has also been narrated from Abū Dharr instead of Salmān, and other narrations have been mentioned. Those who established the chain are trustworthy and precise narrators, and Allah knows best. Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 843]"
"5893","(5893) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah informed us; al-Haytham ibn Sahl al-Tustarī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿĀmir al-Aḥwal narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man from sitting between two men except with their permission. [Hasan. Abū Dāwūd 4844]"
"5894","(5894) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me; Abū ʿAlī Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Khallād ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man to be made to stand from his seat so that another may sit in it. Rather, make room and expand. [al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Khallād ibn Yaḥyā. Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5915]"
"5895","(5895) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū ʿAmr informed me; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us. He said: I heard Nāfiʿ claim that Ibn ʿUmar said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Let not one of you, meaning his brother, be made to stand from his seat and then have another take his place.\" I said: Did he say it regarding Friday? He said: Regarding Friday and other than it. [Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ. Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2168]"
"5896","(5896) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Naḍr informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Kāmil informed me. And Abū ʿAmr informed me; Abū Yaʿlā informed us; Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us. Both said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When there are three, let not two of them converse privately to the exclusion of the third, and let not a man be made to stand from his seat and then have another sit in it.\" [Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kāmil and Abī al-Rabīʿ. And with this meaning, Mālik ibn Anas, al-Layth ibn Saʿd, and al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān narrated from Nāfiʿ: 'Let not' or 'let he not.' And similarly, Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from his father. Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 2/121]"
"5897","(5897) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Let no man make another man rise from his seat and then sit in it himself.\"\n\nSālim said: And when a man would rise for Ibn ʿUmar from his seat, he would not sit in it.\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā. Likewise, it was narrated by Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2177]"
"5898","(5898) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānʾ informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us; Salamah ibn Shabīb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Aʿyan narrated to us; Maʿqil narrated from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Let none of you make his brother rise on the day of Jumuʿah and then go to his place and sit in it; rather, let him say, 'Make room.'\"\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Salamah ibn Shabīb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2178]"
"5899","(5899) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Saʿd ibn Ḥamawayh al-Nasawī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us; Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAqīl ibn Ṭalḥah, who said: I heard Abā al-Khaṣīb narrate, saying: I was sitting when Ibn ʿUmar came, and a man rose from his seat, but he refused to sit in it and sat in another place. The man kept saying: \"What harm would it have done you to sit there?\" He said: \"I would not sit in your seat, nor in anyone else's seat, after I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) — a man came and another man rose for him, and he intended to sit in his place, but the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade him from that.\"\n\nThus did Abū al-Khaṣīb, Ziyād ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, narrate it, and he is correct in the narration of the action of Ibn ʿUmar. It was also narrated by Sālim ibn ʿAbdillāh likewise, except that he differed with Sālim and Nāfiʿ in the wording of the hadith that Ibn ʿUmar narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ), for they both narrated from him the hadith regarding making one rise (al-iqāmah) rather than merely rising (al-qiyām). It was also narrated from Abī Bakrah. [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 2/84]"
"5900","(5900) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbd Rabbih ibn Saʿīd, from Abī ʿAbdillāh, the mawlā of the family of Abī Burdah, from Saʿīd ibn Abī al-Ḥasan, who said: Abū Bakrah came regarding a testimony, and a man rose from his seat for him, but he refused to sit in it. He said: \"Indeed, the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade this,\" and he also said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) forbade that a man wipe his hand with the garment of one who has not clothed him.\"\n\nThus did a group narrate it from Shuʿbah. Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī also narrated it from him with doubt in its text. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 4827]"
"5901","(5901) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbd Rabbih ibn Saʿīd, who said: I heard Abā ʿAbdillāh narrate from Saʿīd ibn Abī al-Ḥasan: that Abū Bakrah entered upon them regarding a testimony, and a man rose from his seat for him. Abū Bakrah said: \"Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'When a man rises from his seat for you, do not sit in it' — or he said: 'Do not make a man rise from his seat and then sit in it — and do not wipe your hand with the garment of one over whom you have no authority.'\"\n\nIt is possible that the hadith from the Prophet (ﷺ) pertains to the prohibition of making one rise, as the ḥuffāẓ narrated from Ibn ʿUmar and Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh from the Prophet (ﷺ), and that Ibn ʿUmar and Abū Bakrah both used to refrain from sitting even when people rose for them voluntarily, without being made to rise. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī 871]"
"5902","(5902) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — and the wording is his — Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us. Both said: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you rises from a seat in which he was sitting and then returns to it, he has the most right to it.\"\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2179]"
"5903","(5903) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿAbbās al-Tarqafī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from one who narrated to him, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever rises from his seat on the day of Jumuʿah and then returns to it, he has the most right to it.\"\n\nThis narration is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected), except that it contains the mention of Jumuʿah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"5904","(5904) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, known as Ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī, in Kūfah, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī in Naysābūr, both said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated from al-Aʿmash, from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ, from Tamīm ibn Ṭarfah, from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us while we were in scattered circles, and he said: \"What is the matter with me? I see you in separate groups (ʿizīn).\"\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Saʿīd al-Ashajj, from Wakīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 430]"
"5905","(5905) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated from al-Layth, from Ibn ʿAjlān, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): that he forbade people from forming circles (taḥalluq) on the day of Jumuʿah before the prayer. [Jayyid. Ibn Mājah 1133]"
"5906","(5906) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Abān al-Aṭṭār narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Abī Murrah, from Abī Wāqid al-Laythī, who said: While the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was sitting with his Companions, three men came. As for one of them, he found a space in the circle and sat in it. As for another, he sat — I think he said — behind the circle. As for the third, he left. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Shall I not inform you about these three men? As for the one who sat in the circle, he took refuge, so Allah gave him refuge. As for the one who sat behind the circle, he showed modesty, so Allah showed modesty toward him. As for the one who left, he turned away, so Allah turned away from him.\"\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Abān al-Aṭṭār. And Ḥarb ibn Shaddād narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, saying: \"While we were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in a circle…\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2176]"
"5907","(5907) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Abān narrated to us; Qatādah narrated to me; from Abū Mijlaz; from Ḥudhayfah: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the one who sits in the middle of the circle.\n[Munkar. Abū Dāwūd 4826]"
"5908","(5908) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Hammām narrated to us; from Qatādah; from Abū Mijlaz: that a man came to Ḥudhayfah and said: \"Did you not know that so-and-so has died?\" He said: \"The One who caused him to die is capable of causing you to die.\" Then the man sat in the middle of the circle, so he said to him: \"Get up, for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the one who sits in the middle of the circle.\"\nThe Sheikh said: It is possible that he recognized hypocrisy in him, and that he did that deliberately, intending to show disregard and lack of concern for the people of the circle. [Munkar. See the preceding.]"
"5909","(5909) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us; from Qatādah; from Lāḥiq ibn Ḥumayd, who is Abū Mijlaz: that a man sat in the middle of the circle, so Ḥudhayfah said: \"Cursed upon the tongue of Muḥammad (ﷺ)\" — or he said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the one who sits in the middle of the circle.\" [Munkar. See the preceding.]"
"5910","(5910) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us; Khālid ibn Ḥayyān al-Raqqī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubriqān narrated to us; from Yaʿlā ibn Shaddād ibn Aws, who said: I witnessed Muʿāwiyah in Jerusalem, and he led us in the Jumuʿah prayer. I looked, and behold, most of those in the mosque were Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). I saw them sitting with their knees drawn up (iḥtibāʾ) while the imām was delivering the sermon.\nAbū Dāwūd said: Ibn ʿUmar used to sit with his knees drawn up while the imām was delivering the sermon, as did Anas ibn Mālik, Sharīḥ, Ṣaʿṣaʿah ibn Ṣūḥān, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī, Makḥūl, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿd, and Nuʿaym ibn Salamah. He said: \"There is no harm in it, and I have not heard of anyone disliking it except ʿUbadah ibn Nusayy.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1111]"
"5911","(5911) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Ayyūb ibn Suwayd narrated to us; from Yūnus; from Nāfiʿ: that Ibn ʿUmar used to sit with his knees drawn up (iḥtibāʾ) on the day of Jumuʿah while the imām was delivering the sermon. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5228]"
"5912","(5912) Abū ʿAbdullāh Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Muhtadī bi-Allāh al-ʿAbbāsī informed us, having it read to him in Makkah, then in Madīnah; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād narrated to us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated to us; from Abū Maḥrūm ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Maymūn; from Sahl ibn Muʿādh ibn Anas al-Juhanī; from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade sitting with the knees drawn up (al-ḥabwah) on the day of Jumuʿah while the imām was delivering the sermon. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1110]"
"5913","(5913) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāniʾ narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: \"Abū Maḥrūm narrated to me.\""
"5914","(5914) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī narrated to us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us; Abū Ghaziyyah Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn Miskīn, the judge of Madīnah, narrated to us; Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; from Nāfiʿ; from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sitting with his knees drawn up (muḥtabyan) in the courtyard of the Kaʿbah, gesturing with his hand thus — and Abū Ḥātim interlaced his fingers.\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Fuḍayl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5914]"
"5915","(5915) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar and Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl both said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥassān al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us; my two grandmothers — Ṣafiyyah and Duḥaybah, the daughters of ʿUlaybah (Mūsā said: the daughter of Ḥarmalah), who were the foster-mothers of Qaylah ibn Makhramah, and who were the grandmother of their father — narrated to me: that they informed them that they saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sitting in the position of al-qurfuṣāʾ. When I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in that humble and submissive posture (al-mukhtashiʿ — Mūsā said: al-mutakhashshiʿ) in his sitting, I trembled out of awe.\nAbū ʿUbayd said: Al-qurfuṣāʾ is that a man sits in the manner of one who sits with his knees drawn up (al-muḥtaby), his drawn-up posture being with his hands, and he places them on his shins, as one draws up with a garment.\nAbū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated it to us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; from Abū ʿUbayd, with that. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 4847]"
"5916","(5916) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Baṣrī narrated to us; Salamah ibn Shabīb narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghifārī narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Anṣārī narrated to us; from Rubayḥ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; from his father; from his grandfather Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sat in a gathering, he would sit with his knees drawn up (iḥtibāʾ) using his hands.\nThis was narrated uniquely by ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghifārī, and he is a shaykh whose ḥadīth is rejected (munkar al-ḥadīth), as stated by Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Abū Dāwūd 4846]"
"5917","(5917) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Qurashī narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us; Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd informed us from ʿUbaydah, Abū Khidāsh, from Abū Tamīmah al-Hujaymī, from Jābir, who said: \"I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was sitting with his knees drawn up (iḥtibāʾ) in a cloak, the fringe of which had fallen upon his feet.\"\n\nThis Jābir is al-Hujaymī, Abū Jurayy. [Ṣaḥīḥ via other chains. Abū Dāwūd 4075]"
"5918","(5918) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʢUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh, i.e. al-Qaʿnabī, narrated from Mālik; from Abū al-Zinād; from al-Aʿraj; from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited two types of garments, and two types of sales: al-mulāmasah (touch-sale) and al-munābadhah (toss-sale), and that a man should sit with his knees drawn up (iḥtibāʾ) in a single garment with nothing over his private parts, and that a man should wrap himself in a single garment exposing one of his sides.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī and Ibn Abī Uways. Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, all of them from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2038]"
"5919","(5919) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash; from Abū Ṣāliḥ; from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited two types of garments: that a man should sit with his knees drawn up (iḥtibāʾ) while his private parts are exposed toward the sky, wearing his garment with one of its sides exposed, and that he should throw his garment over his shoulder.\"\n\nIt was also narrated by Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī, and ʿĀʾishah bint al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5920","(5920) Al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Baḥr narrated to us; ʪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; ʪsā ibn Yūnus narrated from Ibn Jurayj; from Ibrāhīm ibn Mayṣarah; from ʿAmr ibn al-Sharīd; from his father, al-Sharīd ibn Suwayd, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by me while I was sitting like this, having placed my left hand behind my back and leaning on the flesh of my hand. He said: 'Do you sit in the posture of those upon whom Allah's wrath has fallen?'\" This is the wording of the hadith of ʿAlī ibn Baḥr.\n\nIn the narration of ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, he said: \"I was sitting in the mosque, placing my left hand behind my back, leaning on the flesh of my hand.\"\n\nAbū Dāwūd said: Al-Qāsim said: \"The flesh of the palm (alyat al-kaff) refers to the base of the thumb and what is beneath it.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 4848]"
"5921","(5921) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn al-Sarḥ and Makhlad ibn Khālid both said: Sufyān narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, who said: \"Someone who heard Abū Hurayrah narrate to me, saying: Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ said: 'When one of you is in the sun' — and Makhlad said: 'in the shade' — 'and the shade recedes from him, so that part of him is in the sun and part in the shade, let him stand up.'\"\n\nThe Sheikh said: In the narration of Abū al-Munīb al-ʿAtakī from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah from his father, it is narrated marfūʿ (as a Prophetic statement) prohibiting this. This may indicate that the intention is to prevent harm from the heat of the sun, as it was narrated from Qays from his father that he came while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering a sermon, and he stood in the sun, so he was commanded and was moved to the shade. [Ṣaḥīḥ via other chains. Abū Dāwūd 4821]"
"5922","(5922) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar informed us; Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr al-Salūlī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Muslim; from Mujāhid; from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sitting in the courtyard of the Kaʿbah, part of him in the shade and part in the sun, placing one of his hands upon the other.\" [Munkar]"
"5923","(5923) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ṣanʿānī narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us from Maʿmar; from Ibn al-Munkadir; from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"When one of you is in the shade and it recedes from him, let him stand up, for it is a sitting place of Satan.\" [Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 19799]"
"5924","(5924) With his chain of narration, he said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us from Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Abān, who said: \"I heard Ibn al-Munkadir narrating this hadith from Abū Hurayrah, who said: 'I was sitting in the shade and part of me was in the sun.' He said: 'I stood up when I heard it, and Ibn al-Munkadir said to me: \"Sit down, there is no harm upon you, for you were sitting like this.\"'\"\n\nThe narrator of this hadith is Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, and he interpreted the hadith as we have narrated from him. In this there is a reconciliation between the two reports, and a confirmation of what we have indicated. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"5925","(5925) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Ṣaydalānī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū Zurʿah al-Dimashqī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from Nāfiʿ; from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: 'When one of you feels drowsy while he is in the mosque on Friday, let him move from his place to another.'\"\n\nThis hadith is counted among the unique narrations (afrād) of Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yāsār, and it has been narrated through another chain from Nāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ via other chains. Abū Dāwūd 1119]"
"5926","(5926) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Qutaybah Salm ibn al-Faḍl al-Adamī informed us in Makkah; Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAbd al-Bāqī ibn Qāniʿ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Naṣr ibn Manṣūr al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Wakīʿī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad al-Muḥāribī narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī; from Nāfiʿ; from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'When one of you feels drowsy during the prayer in the mosque on Friday, let him move from his place to another.'\" This is the wording of the hadith of Abū Zakariyyā.\n\nThe hadith of Abū ʿAbd Allāh has the same meaning, and both of them mention \"the prayer.\" What is meant by \"the prayer\" is the place of prayer. The marfūʿ (Prophetic) attribution of this hadith is not established, and the well-known version from Ibn ʿUmar is from his own statement. [Ṣaḥīḥ via other chains]"
"5927","(5927) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and others narrated to us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who said: Ibn 'Umar used to say to a man when he became drowsy on the day of Jumu'ah while the imam was delivering the khutbah: \"Let him change his place.\" It has also been narrated through another chain from the Prophet ﷺ. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Shafi'i, 275]"
"5928","(5928) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas, al-Asamm, narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us. (H) And Abu 'Abd Allah Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-'Abbasi narrated to us in Makkah, and in Madinah Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salman al-Najjad narrated to us, he said: It was recited to Yahya while I was listening, 'Abd al-Wahhab, ibn 'Ata', narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated from al-Hasan from Samurah ibn Jundub that the Prophet ﷺ said: \"When one of you becomes drowsy on the day of Jumu'ah, let him change to the place of his companion, and let his companion change to his place.\" This Isma'il ibn Muslim is not strong. [Sahih through other chains. Narrated by al-Tabari in al-Kabir, 6956]"
"5929","(5929) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Dassah narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: I found in the book of my father, in his own handwriting, though I did not hear it from him. He said: Qatadah narrated from Yahya ibn Malik from Samurah ibn Jundub that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: \"Be present for the remembrance (of Allah) and draw close to the imam, for a man continues to keep himself distant until he will be kept back in Paradise, even if he enters it.\" Thus Abu Dawud narrated it from 'Ali, and it is sahih. [Hasan. Abu Dawud, 1108]"
"5930","(5930) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated it to us, Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Hamdan al-Sayrafi narrated to us in Marw, Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, my father narrated to me from Qatadah, and he mentioned it. I do not think he was anything but mistaken regarding the mention of Mu'adh's hearing from his father — either he or his shaykh was in error. As for Isma'il al-Qadi, he is far above that. And Allah knows best."
"5931","(5931) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Hasan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Shahriyar narrated to us. (H) And Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan narrated to us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ghalib and Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas al-Mu'addib narrated to us, they said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn 'Abd al-Malik narrated from Qatadah from al-Hasan from Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Attend the Jumu'ah and draw close to the imam, for a man lags behind from the Jumu'ah until he will be kept back from Paradise, even though he is among its people.\" In the narration of Ibn Shahriyar: \"He will be delayed from the Jumu'ah until he will be kept back from Paradise, even if he is among its people.\" [Hasan through other chains]"
"5932","(5932) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, 'Ali ibn 'Ayyash narrated from 'Utbah ibn Damrah, who said: I saw 'Abd Allah ibn Busr — that is, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — praying in the maqsurah. He used to change his dye with wars (a plant used for dyeing)."
"5933","(5933) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Mukram narrated to us, 'Umar ibn 'Umar narrated to us, 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated from his father from Tamim ibn Mahmud from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the pecking of the crow, the spreading out of the limbs like a predatory animal, and that a man should make a fixed place for himself in the mosque as the camel makes a fixed place for itself. Yazid ibn Abi Habib followed it up from Ja'far ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam. [Da'if. Abu Dawud, 862]"
"5934","(5934) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan narrated to us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, ibn Milhan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to me, al-Layth narrated from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Ja'far ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam from Tamim ibn Mahmud from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade in the prayer the spreading out of the limbs like a predatory animal, and that one should peck like the crow, and that a man should make a fixed place for himself as the camel makes a fixed place. [Da'if. See the preceding]"
"5935","(5935) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-'Anbari narrated to us, my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Isma'ili narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Hammad narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Abu al-Zubayr narrated from Jabir that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell ill, so we prayed behind him while he was sitting, and Abu Bakr was pronouncing the takbir so that the people could hear his takbir. He ﷺ turned towards us and saw us standing, so he gestured to us, and we sat down. We prayed with his prayer while sitting. When he said the salam, he said: \"You were about just now to do the deed of the Persians and the Romans, who stand over their kings while they are sitting. Do not do so. Follow your imams: if he prays standing, pray standing, and if he prays sitting, pray sitting.\" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Sahih. Previously mentioned, 5073]"
"5936","(5936) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih narrated to us, Abu al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: When the Prophet ﷺ fell ill with the illness in which he died, she mentioned the matter of his command that Abu Bakr should lead the prayer, and the prayer of Abu Bakr and his coming out. She said: When Abu Bakr saw him, he began to move back, but he ﷺ gestured to him to pray. So Abu Bakr stood, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat beside him, praying while Abu Bakr was making the people hear. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Musaddad. [Sahih. Previously mentioned, 5077]"
"5937","Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Hamid ibn Bilal informed us; Abd al-Rahman ibn Bashr narrated to us; Sufyan narrated — and Abu al-Husayn al-Alawi informed us; Hajjib ibn Ahmad ibn Sufyan informed us; Abd al-Rahim ibn Munib narrated to us; Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated from Amr, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs after Jumu'ah.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb and others, from Sufyan. [Sahih. Muslim 882]"
"5938","Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us; Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; Khalid ibn Abdillah informed us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"When one of you prays Jumu'ah, let him pray four (rak'ahs) after it.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Muslim 881]"
"5939","Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-Anbari informed us; my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated. Ishaq said: \"He informed us,\" and Hannad said: \"He narrated to us\" — Abdillah ibn Idris, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"When you pray after Jumu'ah, pray four (rak'ahs).\"\n\nIshaq said in his hadith: \"Ibn Idris informed us; I heard Suhayl,\" and he added in the hadith: \"If something urgent arises for you, then (pray) two rak'ahs in the mosque, and two rak'ahs after you return to your house.\"\n\nAhmad ibn Salamah said: The additional words in the hadith are from the statement of Suhayl.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih with this addition from Amr al-Naqid, from Abdillah ibn Idris. [Sahih. Ibn Hibban 2495]"
"5940","Abu Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"When you have prayed Jumu'ah, pray four (rak'ahs) after it.\"\n\nHe (Suhayl) said: My father said to me: \"O my son, when you have prayed two rak'ahs in the mosque and then come to the house, pray two rak'ahs.\" [Sahih. Abu Dawud 1131]"
"5941","Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu al-Fadl al-Abbas ibn Muhammad ibn Quhyar informed us; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra informed us; Ya'la ibn Ubayd informed us; Sufyan narrated, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Whoever prays after Jumu'ah, let him pray four (rak'ahs).\"\n\nMuslim extracted it from the hadith of Waki', from al-Thawri. [Sahih. See what precedes]"
"5942","Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Ali ibn Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn and Musa ibn Muhammad al-Dhuhli narrated; they said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us. He said: I recited before Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, regarding the voluntary prayers of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: \"He (peace and blessings be upon him) would not pray after Jumu'ah until he had departed, and then he would pray two rak'ahs in his house.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Abdillah ibn Yusuf, from Malik. [Sahih. See 5937]"
"5943","Abu Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Isma'il informed us; Ayyub narrated, from Nafi', who said: \"Ibn Umar used to lengthen the prayer before Jumu'ah and would pray two rak'ahs after it in his house. And he narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to do that.\" [Sahih. Abu Dawud 1128]"
"5944","Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Ahmad ibn Ubaydillah al-Narsi narrated to us; Hajjaj ibn Muhammad al-A'war narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated — and Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Ibrahim ibn Ishaq al-Anmati narrated to us; Harun ibn Abdillah narrated to us; Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated. He said: Ibn Jurayj said: Umar ibn Ata', who was Ibn Abi al-Khuwar, informed me that Nafi' ibn Jubayr had sent him to al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, the son of the sister of Namir, to ask him about something Mu'awiyah had seen him do in the prayer. He said: \"Yes, I prayed Jumu'ah with him in the maqsurah. When he gave the salam, I stood in my place and prayed. When he entered, he sent word to me and said: 'Do not repeat what you did. When you have prayed Jumu'ah, do not join it with another prayer until you have spoken or departed, for the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded that — that no prayer be joined with another until one departs or speaks.'\" In the narration of al-Narsi: \"Do not pray until you depart or speak.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Harun ibn Abdillah. [Sahih. Muslim 883]"
"5945","Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Muhammad al-Miqri' al-Mihrajani informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub informed us; Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyub narrated, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he saw a man praying two rak'ahs after Jumu'ah in his place. He pushed him and said: \"Do you pray Jumu'ah as four (rak'ahs)?\" He (the narrator) said: Ibn Umar used to pray two rak'ahs in his house and would say: \"The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to do that.\" [Sahih. See 5942, 5937]"
"5946","Abu Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Nasr al-Darabadhi informed us; Abu al-Muwajjih informed us; Yusuf ibn Isa informed us; al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated to us; Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ata', from Ibn Umar, who said: \"When he was in Makkah and prayed Jumu'ah, he would move forward and pray two rak'ahs, then move forward and pray four (rak'ahs). And when he was in Madinah, he would pray Jumu'ah, then return to his house and pray two rak'ahs, and he would not pray in the mosque. He was asked about it, and he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to do that.'\" [Sahih in other chains. Abu Dawud 1130]"
"5947","(5947) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʬāʾ, who said: I saw Ibn ʿUmar pray Jumuʿah, then he moved slightly from the place where he had prayed, not very far, then he performed two rakʿahs, then he walked a shorter distance than that, then he performed four rakʿahs. He (the narrator) said: I asked him: How many times did you see him do that? He said: Several times. When he finished, he would come to perform Ṭawāf. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1133]"
"5948","(5948) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us; Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr narrated to us; Ḥumayd narrated from Anas, who said: We would hasten to Jumuʿah and then take a nap after it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah from Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī from Ḥumayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 863]"
"5949","(5949) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Naḍr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from his father from Sahl ibn Saʿd, who said: We used to rejoice on the day of Jumuʿah. I said: Why? He said: We had an old woman who would be sent to Buḍāʿah; she would take from the roots of the silq (beet) and throw them into a pot and add grains of barley. When we had prayed, we would go to her, greet her, and she would serve it to us. We used to rejoice on the day of Jumuʿah because of that, and we would neither take a nap nor have lunch except after Jumuʿah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim also narrated it from al-Qaʿnabī in an abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 896]"
"5950","(5950) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Maḥbūb al-Ramlī narrated in Makkah; al-Qāsim ibn Mahdī narrated to us; Abū Muṣʿab al-Zuhrī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from his father from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, for you in every Jumuʿah there is a Ḥajj and an ʿUmrah. The Ḥajj is the hastening to Jumuʿah, and the ʿUmrah is waiting for ʿAṣr after Jumuʿah.\" Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ also narrated it from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mahdī; al-Qāsim is the sole narrator of it. It was also narrated from Abū Maʿshar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar in a marfūʿ form, and both chains are weak. [Munkar. Ibn ʿAdī narrated it in al-Kāmil 6/38]"
"5951","(5951) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated, reciting to Mālik from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāllāh be pleased with him saw a striped silk garment at the door of the mosque and said: O Messenger of Allah, if you were to buy this garment and wear it on Jumuʿah and for delegations when they come to you. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Only the one who has no share in the Hereafter wears this.\" Then some garments came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he gave ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāllāh be pleased with him one of them. He said: O Messenger of Allah, you have clothed me with it, yet you said what you said about the garment of ʿUṭārid. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"I did not give it to you to wear it.\" So ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāllāh be pleased with him gave it to his polytheist brother in Makkah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 846]"
"5952","(5952) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to me; ʿAmr narrated that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī narrated to him that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no blame on any one of you, if he finds—\" or he said: \"There is no blame on any one of you, if you find—\" that he should keep two garments for Jumuʿah besides his work clothes.\" ʿAmr said: And Ibn Abī Ḥabīb informed me from Mūsā ibn Saʿd from Ibn Ḥabbān from Ibn Salām that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say that from the minbar. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it. Abū Dāwūd 1072]"
"5953","(5953) Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us; Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān narrated to us; Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī. Ṭāwūs said: I said to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās: They have mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Bathe on Jumuʿah and wash your heads, even if you are not in a state of janābah, and apply perfume.\" Ibn ʿAbbās said: As for bathing, yes; as for perfume, I do not know. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīh ibn Mayṣarah from Ṭāwūs. This, along with the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah, indicates that the meaning of his words \"man ghaslaghhtalā\" is the one who washed his head and bathed his body. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 844]"
"5954","(5954) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Madanī narrated to us; Ḥarmī ibn ʿUmarah narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Bakr ibn al-Munkadir; ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Anṣārī narrated to me, who said: I bear witness regarding Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī that he bore witness regarding the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"Bathing on Jumuʿah is obligatory, and that one should use the miswāk, and that one should touch perfume if he finds it.\" ʿAmr ibn Sulaym said: And I bear witness that bathing is obligatory; as for the miswāk and perfume, Allah knows best, but this is what I heard. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn al-Munkadir from ʿAmr from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Saʿīd from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 840]"
"5955","(5955) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ḥusayn ibn Ḥasan ibn Muhājir narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Sawwād al-Sarḥī narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated that Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl and Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him from Abū Bakr ibn al-Munkadir from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī from his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Bathing on Jumuʿah is upon every pubescent person, and he should use the miswāk and touch of perfume as much as he is able.\" Except that Bukayr did not mention ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and said: \"Of perfume, even the perfume of a woman.\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Sawwād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"5956","(5956) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād; Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Munādī narrated to us; Shabābah ibn Sawwār narrated to us. And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar and Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us; they said: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī from his father from Ibn Wudīʿah al-Anṣārī from Salmān that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever bathes on Jumuʿah and purifies himself as much as he can of purification, and touches some oil of his house or its perfume, then goes to Jumuʿah and prays what is easy for him, and when the imām comes out listens and is silent, he will be forgiven what is between that and the next Jumuʿah.\" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. In the narration of al-Qaṭṭān: from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wudīʿah from Salmān al-Khayr from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: \"No man bathes on Jumuʿah, then touches some of his oil or the perfume of his family, then comes to the mosque without separating between two people, then is silent when the imām speaks, except that he will be forgiven what is between that and the next Jumuʿah.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, close to the wording of the ḥadīth of Shabābah, except that he mentioned: \"And he purifies himself as much as he can of purification.\" And Ṣāliḥ ibn Kayṣān narrated it from Saʿīd al-Maqburī from his father from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See 5892]"
"5957","(5957) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqriʾ informed us in Baghdad; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī informed us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Uwaysī informed us; Sulaymān, meaning Ibn Bilāl, informed us from Ṣāliḥ, meaning Ibn Kaysān, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, that his father informed him that Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When the day of Jumuʿah arrives, let a man perform ghusl, wash his head, then apply the best of his perfume, then wear the best of his garments, then go out for the Ṣalāh and not separate between two people, then listen attentively to the Imam — he will be forgiven what is between that Jumuʿah and the next Jumuʿah, plus an additional three days.\"\n\nAnd this has been narrated through another chain from Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see no. 5849]"
"5958","(5958) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us; my father informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed me from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl, from Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: We heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Whoever performs ghusl on the day of Jumuʿah, uses the siwāk, applies perfume if he has any, wears the best of his garments, then comes to the mosque, does not step over the necks of the people, then prays as much as Allah wills him to pray, then listens attentively when his Imam comes out until he completes the prayer — it will be an expiation for what is between that Jumuʿah and the one before it.\"\n\nAbū Hurayrah would say: \"And three additional days, for Allah has made one good deed equal to ten of its kind.\" [Ḥasan; see no. 5683]"
"5959","(5959) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us from Ibn Shihāb from Ibn al-Sabbāq that the Prophet (ﷺ) said on one of the Jumuʿahs: \"O assembly of Muslims, indeed this is a day that Allah has made an ʿĪd for the Muslims, so perform ghusl, and whoever has perfume, let him apply some of it, and upon you is the siwāk.\"\n\nThis is the correct version as a mursal narration, and it has been narrated as a mawṣūl narration, but its being connected is not sound. [Ḍaʿīf; ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5301]"
"5960","(5960) The judge Abū ʿUmar Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad informed us; Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭabarānī informed us; Abū ʿUlāthah Muḥammad ibn Abī Ghassān al-Farāʾiḍī informed us; Yazīd ibn Saʿīd al-Ṣabbāḥī al-Iskandarānī informed us; Mālik ibn Anas informed us from Saʿīd, meaning Ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on one of the Jumuʿahs: \"O assembly of Muslims, this is a day that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has made an ʿĪd for you, so perform ghusl, and upon you is the siwāk.\" [Munkar; see the preceding]"
"5961","(5961) ʿAbd Allāh ibn Lahīʿah narrated it; ʿUqayl informed me that Ibn Shihāb informed him from Anas ibn Mālik that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on one of the Jumuʿahs, and he mentioned it in the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Shihāb from Ibn al-Sabbāq. Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us; my father informed us; Ibn Lahīʿah informed us, and he mentioned it. And the correct version is what Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb as a mursal narration."
"5962","(5962) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Narsī informed us; Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī Sufyān informed us from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Among the acts of fiṭrah are: trimming the moustache, cutting the nails, and shaving the pubic area.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm, and some of them added from Ḥanẓalah in this ḥadīth: \"plucking the armpit hair.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī 5549]"
"5963","(5963) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, and Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us. And Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: \"Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you from Ibn Shihāb from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab from Abū Hurayrah from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: 'The fiṭrah consists of five things: circumcision, shaving the pubic area, trimming the moustache, cutting the nails, and plucking the armpit hair.'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir and Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see no. 686]"
"5964","(5964) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, informed us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: \"Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ informed you from Bakr ibn ʿAmr from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ashajj from Nāfiʿ that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to cut his nails and trim his moustache every Jumuʿah.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated from Abū Jaʿfar as a mursal narration, saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to recommend taking from one's moustache and nails on the day of Jumuʿah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"5965","(5965) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us; Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq informed us; Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yamān informed us from Sufyān from a man from Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah, who said: \"I had two paternal uncles who witnessed the Tree (i.e., the Pledge of al-Riḍwān), and they used to trim their moustaches and cut their nails every Jumuʿah.\"\n\nAs for the ḥadīth narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās as a marfūʿ narration: \"The believer on the day of Jumuʿah is like the muḥrim — he does not take from his nails or his hair until the Ṣalāh is completed.\"\n\nAnd from Ibn ʿUmar as a marfūʿ narration: \"The Muslim on the day of Jumuʿah is muḥrim; when he prays, he exits the state of iḥrām.\"\n\nBoth of these have been narrated through two weak chains by which no proof is established. And in the authentic narration from Ibn ʿUmar regarding his own practice is evidence of the weakness of that which contradicts it. And with Allah is the success. [Ḍaʿīf; narrated by Ibn al-Jaʿd 1081]"
"5966","(5966) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā, Abū Ṭāhir, and Ḥarmalah informed us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us; Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me from his father from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that when he performed istijmār, he performed it with an aloeswood stick that was not moistened, and with camphor which he would place along with the aloeswood. Then he would say: \"Thus the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform istijmār.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir and Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā. And Ibn Lahīʿah narrated it from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, restricting it to the day of Jumuʿah. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim 2254]"
"5967","(5967) It was narrated to us by Abū Muḥammad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, who informed us that Abū Isḥāq: Ibrāhīm ibn Aḥmad ibn Firās al-Mālikī in Makkah informed us, who said: Saʿīd ibn ʿAjab al-Anbārī narrated to us, who said: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dimashqī narrated to us, who said: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from Ibn Lahīʿah, from Bukayr, from Nāʿif, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"When he (the Prophet ﷺ) would perfume himself for Jumuʿah, he would use an unscented piece of wood and apply camphor over it. And he would say: 'This is the fragrance of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'\"\n\nAnd it was narrated to us previously from Ibn ʿUmar that he was invited to Saʿīd ibn Zayd while he was perfuming himself for Jumuʿah. [Weak]"
"5968","(5968) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who said: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ and Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Zāhid both said: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah informed us, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī informed us, who said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, who said: ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Tarqafī narrated to us, who said: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, who said: Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated to me, who said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar narrated to me from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: \"Whoever is offered perfume, let him not reject it, for it is light in burden and pleasant in fragrance.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from al-Muqriʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2253]"
"5969","(5969) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us by dictation, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī informed us, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us, who said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, who said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, who said: Ibn Khuthaym informed me from Saʿīb ibn Jubayr that Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Wear your white garments, and shroud your dead in them. And the best of your kohl is ithmid (antimony), for it clears the vision and promotes the growth of hair.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than him. Abū Dāwūd 3878]"
"5970","(5970) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who said: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh informed us, who said: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, who said: Sahl ibn Bakkār, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, and Hudbah all said: Hammām narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, who said: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī and Hilāl ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Raqqī both said: ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd narrated to us, who said: Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Qatādah, who said: \"I asked Anas: 'Which garment was most beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, or most pleasing to him?' He said: 'The ḥibarah (a striped garment).'\"\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim from Hammām.\n\nAnd Muslim narrated it from Haddāb, who is Hudbah ibn Khālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5475]"
"5971","(5971) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, who said: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, who said: Abū Dāwūd informed us, who said: Hishām narrated from Yaḥyā, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, that Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrated to him that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated to him, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me wearing two dyed garments, and he said: 'O ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, these are the garments of the disbelievers, so do not wear them.'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām al-Dastawāʾī and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2077]"
"5972","(5972) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, who said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, who said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, who said: Musaddad informed us, who said: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus informed us, who said: Hishām ibn al-Ghāz narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: \"We descended with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from a mountain pass, and he mentioned the ḥadīth regarding his prayer. Then he turned to me, and I was wearing a cloak dyed with saffron, and he said: 'What is this cloak on you?' I recognized what he disliked. So I went to my family while they were stoking an oven of theirs, and I threw it into it. Then I came to him the next morning, and he said: 'O ʿAbdullāh, what happened to the cloak?' I informed him, and he said: 'Why did you not give it to some of your family to wear, for there is no harm in that for women.'\" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 8902]"
"5973","(5973) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdullāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, who said: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed us, who said: My father informed me, who said: I heard al-Awzāʿī say: Mūsā ibn Yasār narrated to me from Abū Hurayrah that a woman passed by him whose fragrance was wafting, and he said: \"O bondwoman of al-Jabbār, do you intend the mosque?\" She said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"And for that you have perfumed yourself?\" She said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Go back and bathe, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'There is no woman who goes out to the mosque with her fragrance wafting, but that Allah will not accept her prayer until she returns to her house and bathes.'\" [Ḥasan by other than him. Abū Dāwūd 4174]"
"5974","(5974) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us, who said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us, who said: Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām informed us, who said: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us, who said: Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: \"I do not ride on purple (silk), nor do I wear saffron-dyed garments, nor do I wear a shirt trimmed with silk.\" And al-Ḥasan gestured towards the collar of his shirt. And he said: \"Beware! The perfume of men is fragrance without colour, and the perfume of women is colour without fragrance.\"\n\nSaʿīd said: \"We understood his statement regarding the perfume of women to refer to when she goes out; but as for when she is with her husband, she may perfume herself with whatever she wishes.\" [Ḥasan by other than him. Abū Dāwūd 4048]"
"5975","(5975) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, who said: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us, who said: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us, who said: al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us, who said: Thābit ibn ʿUmarah al-Ḥanafī informed us, who said: Ghunaym ibn Qays al-Kaʿbī informed us from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Any woman who perfumes herself and passes by a people so that they may detect her fragrance, she is an adulteress, and every eye is an adulteress.\" [Strong. al-Tirmidhī 2786]"
"5976","(5976) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī informed us, who said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, who said: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad informed us, who said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, who said: Wakīʿ narrated from Musāwir al-Warrāq, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ addressed the people while wearing a black turban.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1359]"
"5977","(5977) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī informed us, Abū Usāmah narrated from Musāwir al-Warrāq, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, from his father, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ upon the minbar, and upon him was a black turban, the end of which he had let hang down between his shoulders.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī and Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Abū Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5978","(5978) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Sharīk narrated from ʿAmmār al-Duhnī, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered on the day of the Conquest, and upon him was a black turban.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Ḥakīm, from Sharīk. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1358.]"
"5979","(5979) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Jamīl narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, a similar narration."
"5980","(5980) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us in Baṣrah, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated to us. He said: I heard Milḥān ibn Thawbān say:\n\nʿAmmār ibn Yāsir was in charge of us in Kūfah for a year, and he used to deliver the sermon to us every Friday, and upon him was a black turban. [Ḥasan.]"
"5981","(5981) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī informed us in Kūfah, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; they both said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Thābit ibn ʿUbayd, from Abū Jaʿfar al-Anṣārī, who said: I was present at the house on the day ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed. I passed through the mosque and suddenly a man was calling out from the women's enclosure, girded with his sword, and upon him was a black turban. It was ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: \"What has been done to the man?\" I said: \"He has been killed.\" He said: \"May you be ruined for the rest of time!\"\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 24951.]"
"5982","(5982) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us, Abū Luʾluʾah said: I saw upon Ibn ʿUmar a black turban."
"5983","(5983) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from Hilāl ibn ʿĀmir, from his father, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ at Minā, delivering the sermon upon a she-mule, and upon him was a red mantle, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) was in front of him, interpreting on his behalf.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1956.]"
"5984","(5984) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from Ḥajjār ibn Arṭāh, from Abū Jaʿfar, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to wear his red mantle on the two ʿĪds and on Friday.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Ṭabaqāt Ibn Saʿd 1/451.]"
"5985","(5985) Abū Saʿd al-Zāhid narrated to us, Abū al-Walīd Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad al-Qurashī informed us, Muḥammad ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated it with his chain, except that he said: The Prophet ﷺ had a mantle which he would wear on the two ʿĪds and on Friday.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding hadith.]"
"5986","(5986) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAlqamah narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated from ʿUbaydah ibn Sufyān al-Ḥaḍramī, from Abū al-Jaʿd al-Ḍamrī, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:\n\n\"Whoever abandons the Friday prayer three times out of negligence, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will set a seal upon his heart.\"\n\nAnd thus Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān and others narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAlqamah. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other routes. Previously mentioned at 5576.]"
"5987","(5987) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, and Abū al-ʿAbbās — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Shādhīyākhī, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated to us, from Asīd ibn Abī Asīd al-Barrād, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever abandons three consecutive Jumuʿahs without any necessity, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will set a seal upon his heart.\"\n\nSulaymān ibn Bilāl followed him [in narration], from Asīd. [Ṣaḥīh through other routes; see 5576]"
"5988","(5988) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ibn Milḥān narrated to us: Yaḥyā, i.e. Ibn Bukayr, narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd Allāh, the client (mawlā) of Ghufrah, that he heard Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Mālik narrating from Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said: \"A man may have his flock on the outskirts of the village, where he resides, and he attends the prayers. When it becomes difficult for him [to come to the mosque], he says: 'If only I were to move to a ravine that is more sheltered than this.' So he moves to it, until he does not come to the mosque except every Jumuʿah. Then when it becomes difficult for him there as well, and the area around it is grazed bare, he says: 'If only I were to move to a ravine that is more sheltered than this.' So he moves to it, until he does not attend Jumuʿah, nor does he even know what day Jumuʿah is — until Allah sets a seal upon his heart.\"\n\nAnd likewise, Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal narrated it from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd Allāh. [Ḥasan; al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Kabīr, 3229]"
"5989","(5989) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us, from Qatādah.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Qatādah narrated to us, from Qudāmah ibn Wabarah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever misses Jumuʿah without an excuse should give charity of a dīnār, and if he cannot find one, then half a dīnār.\"\n\n[Ḍaʿīf; Abū Dāwūd 1053]"
"5990","(5990) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd informed us: Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb informed us: Saʿīd ibn Bashīr informed us, that Qatādah narrated to them, from Qudāmah ibn Wabarah, from Samurah ibn Jundub al-Fazārī, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever misses Jumuʿah without an excuse should give charity of a dirham, or half a dirham, or a ṣāʿ, or a mudd.\" Saʿīd said: \"I asked Qatādah: 'Does he attribute it to the Prophet (ﷺ)?' He was uncertain about that.\" Saʿīd said: \"And some of our companions mentioned that Qatādah does attribute it to the Prophet (ﷺ).\" And Ayyūb ibn Miskīn, Abū al-ʿAlāʾ, narrated it from Qatādah, but he sent it as a mursal [hadith]. [Ḍaʿīf; see the preceding]"
"5991","(5991) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yazīd and Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, from Ayyūb Abī al-ʿAlāʾ, from Qatādah, from Qudāmah ibn Wabarah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever misses Jumuʿah without an excuse should give charity of a dirham, or half a dirham, or a ṣāʿ of wheat, or half a ṣāʿ.\"\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālūyah narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, saying: I heard my father, when asked about the hadith of Hammām from Qatādah and the disagreement of Abī al-ʿAlāʾ with him regarding it, say: \"Hammām, in our view, is more precise (aḥfaẓ) than Ayyūb Abī al-ʿAlāʾ.\"\n\nImām Aḥmad said: And Khālid ibn Qays narrated it from Qatādah, agreeing with Hammām in the text (matn) of the hadith but differing from him in its chain (isnād). [Ḍaʿīf; see the preceding]"
"5992","(5992) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed it: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Yaʿlā informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿArʿarah narrated to us: Nūḥ ibn Qays narrated to us, from his brother Khālid ibn Qays, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever deliberately abandons a Jumuʿah should give charity of a dīnār, and if he cannot find one, then half a dīnār.\" He [the narrator] said it thus, and I do not think he was anything but mistaken in its chain (isnād), given that all those who preceded him disagreed with him regarding it. As for the text (matn), it supports the authenticity of the narration of Hammām.\n\nMuḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī did not consider him [i.e., Khālid ibn Qays] strong. For Qudāmah ibn Wabarah's hearing (samāʿ) from Samurah has not been established. Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us, saying: I heard Ibn Ḥammād say: al-Bukhārī said: \"Qudāmah ibn Wabarah's narration from Samurah — his hearing has not been authenticated.\"\n\nAbū Aḥmad said: What al-Bukhārī mentioned regarding the hadith of Qudāmah ibn Wabarah is specifically the hadith of Qatādah, from Qudāmah, from Samurah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), concerning missing Jumuʿah. [Ḍaʿīf; see 5989]"
"5993","(5993) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us: Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Juʿfī narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir, from Abī al-Ashʿath al-Ṣanʿānī, from Aws ibn Aws, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The best of your days is the day of Jumuʿah. In it Ādam was created; in it he was taken in death; in it is the Blowing [of the Trumpet]; and in it is the Convulsion [of death]. So send abundant blessings (ṣalāh) upon me on that day, for your blessings are presented to me.\" They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, how can our blessings be presented to you when you will have decayed [in the grave]?\" — meaning, when you will have become worn away. He said: \"Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has forbidden the earth from consuming the bodies of the Prophets.\"\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh said on another occasion: \"Indeed, the best of your days is the day of Jumuʿah.\"\n\nAbū Dāwūd narrated it in the book of Sunan. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Abū Dāwūd 1047]"
"5994","(5994) Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mihrānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Sakhtiyānī narrated to us: Abū Khalīfah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Salām informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Send abundant blessings (ṣalāh) upon me on the day of Jumuʿah and the night of Jumuʿah. For whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah will send ten [mercies] upon him.\" [Ḥasan through other routes; al-Quṭayʿī narrated it in al-Shuʿab, 142]"
"5995","(5995) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Burd ibn Sinān, from Makḥūl al-Shāmī, from Abī Ummāmah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Send abundant blessings (ṣalāh) upon me every day of Jumuʿah, for the blessings of my Ummah are presented to me every day of Jumuʿah. Whoever among them sends the most blessings upon me will be the closest of them to me in rank.\"\n\nAnd that has been narrated through various routes from Anas with different wordings, all of which encourage sending blessings upon the Prophet (ﷺ) on the night of Jumuʿah and the day of Jumuʿah. Some of their chains contain weakness, and in what we have mentioned there is sufficiency. And with Allah lies success. [Ḣasan through other routes; narrated by Abū ʿAwānah in al-Shuʿab, 3032]"
"5996","(5996) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal narrated to us: al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us: Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: Abū Hāshim informed us: Abī Mijlaz narrated to us: Qays ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us, from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever recites Sūrat al-Kahf on the day of Jumuʿah, light will shine for him between the two Jumuʿahs.\"\n\nAnd Yazīd ibn Makhlad ibn Yazīd narrated it from Hushaym, and he said in its text: \"Light will shine for him between him and the Ancient House (al-Bayt al-ʿAtīq).\"\n\nAnd Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated it from Hushaym, but he stopped it at Abī Saʿīd [i.e., as a mawqūf narration], and said: \"Between him and the Ancient House.\"\n\nAnd with the same meaning, al-Thawrī narrated it from Abī Hāshim as a mawqūf narration. And Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr narrated it, from Shuʿbah, from Abī Hāshim, with his chain, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever recites Sūrat al-Kahf as it was revealed, it will be for him a light on the Day of Resurrection.\" [Munkar; al-Ḥākim 2/399]"
"5997","(5997) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad al-Ṣaffār al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us: Abū Yaḥyā Aḥmad ibn ʿIṣām ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us: Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us: my father narrated to me from Qatādah, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Maʿdān ibn Abī Ṭalḥah al-Yaʿmarī, from Abū al-Dardāʾ that the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever memorises ten verses from the beginning of Sūrah al-Kahf will be protected from the tribulation of the Dajjāl.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Mūsā from Muʿādh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 809]"
"5998","(5998) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh — that is, al-Qaʿnabī — narrated from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated, saying: I read to Mālik from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned the day of Jumuʿah and said: \"In it there is an hour which no Muslim servant encounters.\"\n\nAnd in the narration of al-Shāfiʿī: \"A Muslim person, standing in prayer, asking Allah for anything, except that He grants it to him.\" And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gestured with his hand, indicating its brevity.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 893]"
"5999","(5999) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Makhrah — that is, Ibn Bukayr — narrated to me from his father, from Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, who said: Ibn ʿUmar said to me: \"Did you hear your father narrating from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding the matter of the hour of Jumuʿah?\" He said: \"Yes, I heard him say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'It is between the time the Imam sits [on the pulpit] until the prayer is completed.'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā and a group from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 853]"
"6000","(6000) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated, saying: I heard Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj say: \"I mentioned to him the hadith of Makhrah, and he said: 'This is the most excellent and most authentic hadith regarding the clarification of the hour of Jumuʿah.'\"\n\nThe Shaykh said: \"And it has been narrated in another report that the command to seek it is at the last hour after ʿAṣr.\""
"6001","(6001) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed it to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to you from al-Julāḥ, the freedman of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, that Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to him from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said: \"On the day of Jumuʿah, no Muslim servant asks Allah for anything except that Allah grants it to him. So seek it in the last hour after ʿAṣr.\"\n\nAnd Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī narrated it from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām. [Strong. Abū Dāwūd 1048]"
"6002","(6002) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh in Baghdād narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī and Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Birtī both narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Usāmah Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-Muqriʾ in Makkah informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbāb ibn Waṣīf al-Ghazī in Ghazzah narrated to us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn al-Faraj al-Ghazī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bukayr narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad ibn Mahrūyah al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Hād, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"I went out to al-Ṭūr and met Kaʿb al-Aḥbār. I sat with him and he narrated to me from the Tawrāh, and I narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Among what I narrated to him was that I said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The best day upon which the sun rises is the day of Jumuʿah. In it Ādam was created, in it he was brought down [from Paradise], in it he was granted repentance, in it he died, and in it the Hour shall be established. There is no creature except that it is apprehensive on the day of Jumuʿah from the time of dawn until the sun rises, out of fear of the Hour, except the jinn and mankind. And in it there is an hour which no Muslim servant, while in prayer, asks Allah for any good thing except that Allah grants it to him.'\"\n\nKaʿb said: \"That is one day every year.\" I said: \"Rather, it is in every Jumuʿah.\" Kaʿb al-Aḥbār then recited the Tawrāh and said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) spoke the truth.\" Then he mentioned another hadith, then said: Abū Hurayrah said: \"Then I met ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and I told him about my sitting with Kaʿb al-Aḥbār and what I had narrated to him regarding the day of Jumuʿah. I said to him: Kaʿb said that is one day every year. ʿAbd Allāh said: 'Kaʿb has lied.' I said: 'Yes.' Then Kaʿb recited the Tawrāh and said: 'Rather, it is in every Jumuʿah.' ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām said: 'Kaʿb has spoken the truth.' ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām said: 'I already know which hour it is.' Abū Hurayrah said: 'So tell me about it and do not be stingy.' ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām said: 'It is the last hour of the day of Jumuʿah.' Abū Hurayrah said: 'How can it be the last hour of the day of Jumuʿah, when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"No Muslim servant encounters it while he is praying\"? And that is a time in which no prayer is performed.' ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām said: 'Did not the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Whoever sits in a place waiting for the prayer, he is in prayer until he prays\"?' Abū Hurayrah said: 'Yes.' He said: 'That is it.'\"\n\nThe wording is from the hadith of Ibn Bukayr.\n\nAnd likewise al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated it from Yazīd, from Muḥammad, from Abū Salamah. And Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated it from Abū Salamah, making his statement \"The best day upon which the sun rises\" a narration from Abū Hurayrah from Kaʿb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī 1430]"
"6003","(6003) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed it to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū Maʿmar narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from al-Ḥusayn, from Yaḥyā, who narrated to me: Abū Salamah narrated to me that he heard Abū Hurayrah say: \"The best day upon which the sun rises is the day of Jumuʿah. In it Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, created Ādam. In it he was admitted into Paradise. In it he was expelled from it. And in it the Hour shall be established.\"\n\nAnd al-Awzāʿī narrated it from Yaḥyā, adding: \"I said to him: 'Something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?' He said: 'Rather, something Kaʿb narrated to us.'\" And ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz al-Aʿraj narrated it from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 854]"
"6004","(6004) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Nuṣayr, Abū al-Qāsim al-Khawwāṣ, narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"The best day upon which the sun rises is the day of Jumuʿah. In it Ādam was created. In it he was admitted into Paradise. In it he was expelled from it. And the Hour shall not be established except on the day of Jumuʿah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and likewise he extracted it from the hadith of al-Zuhrī from al-Aʿraj.\n\nAnd ʿAbd Allāh ibn Farrūkh narrated it from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ). And Muḥammad ibn Isḥāh ibn Khuzaymah was of the view that this difference regarding his statement \"In it Ādam was created\" and what follows it [is reconcilable]. As for his statement \"The best day upon which the sun rises is the day of Jumuʿah,\" it is from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ), without doubt. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6005","(6005) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʿb narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Saʿīd narrated to me from his father, who said: \"We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the day of the Khandaq and we were occupied from [performing] certain prayers. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded Bilāl, and he called the iqāmah for each prayer separately. That was before the revelation of: {And if you fear, then [pray] on foot or mounted} [al-Baqarah: 239].\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Dārimī 1524]"
"6006","(6006) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Dabbī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Sulaym ibn ʿAbd al-Salūlī, who said: \"I was with Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ in Ṭabaristān, and with him was a group of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ). Saʿīd said to them: 'Which of you witnessed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?' Ḥudhayfah said: 'I did.' [He said:] 'Command your companions to stand in two groups: one group facing the enemy, and one group behind you. You say the takbīr and they all say the takbīr together. You bow and they all bow together. You raise [your head] and they all raise [their heads] together. Then you prostrate, and the group that is with you prostrates, while the other group stands facing the enemy. When you raise your head, those who are with you stand, and the others fall into prostration. Then you bow and they all bow together. Then you raise [your head] and they all raise [their heads] together. You prostrate, and the group that is with you prostrates, while the other group stands facing the enemy. When you raise your head from prostration, those who are facing the enemy prostrate. Then you give salām upon them, and command your companions: if any fighting breaks out, then fighting and speech are permissible for them.'\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it. Aḥmad 5/385]"
"6007","Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Muslim ibn Ibrahim informed us; 'Abd al-Samad ibn Habib informed me; from his father, who said: They raided Kabul alongside 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, and he led us in the prayer of fear (Salat al-Khawf).\n\n[Weak. Abu Dawud 1245]"
"6008","Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Isbahani informed us; Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Jamal informed us; Muhammad ibn Muqatil informed us; Hakam informed us; from Abu Ja'far al-Razi; from Qatadah; from Abu al-'Aliyah, who said: Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in the prayer of fear (Salat al-Khawf) at Isbahan.\n\nHittan al-Raqashi narrated from Abu Musa that he led the prayer of fear. And it is narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, that 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, led the Maghrib prayer as the prayer of fear on the night of al-Harir.\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah that he taught them the prayer of fear. And from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that when he was asked about the prayer of fear, he described it.\n\nThose who narrated it from the Prophet ﷺ did not intend by it to restrict it specifically to the Prophet ﷺ, nor to suggest that it was abandoned. Rather, each one of them narrated it while believing in its permissibility in the manner in which he narrated it. And with Allah lies success.\n\n[Sahih in its own right. Al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Awsat 7476]"
"6009","Abu Zakariya Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us, and among others they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us.\n\n(And) Abu Ja'far Kamil ibn Ahmad al-Mustamli informed us; Bishr ibn Ahmad ibn Bishr al-Mihrajan Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Bayhaqi informed us; Yahya ibn Yahya informed us. He said: I read to Malik ibn Anas, from Yazid ibn Ruman, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from the one who prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Dhat al-Riqa' the prayer of fear: that one group stood in row with him and one group faced the enemy. He led the group that was with him in one rak'ah, then he remained standing, and they completed their prayer on their own. Then they went and stood facing the enemy. The other group came, and he led them in the remaining rak'ah, then he remained seated and they completed their prayer on their own, then he pronounced the salam with them.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Qutaybah, from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Bukhari 3900]"
"6010","Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn al-Hammami al-Muqri' in Baghdad informed us; Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salman informed us; Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sulami informed us; 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Uwaysi informed me; 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar informed me, from his brother 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from his father, who said: The Prophet ﷺ led the prayer of fear. He placed one group in a row with him and one group facing the enemy. The Prophet ﷺ led those who were with him in one rak'ah. Then he stood, and they stood, and they completed their prayer on their own. Then they went to the place of their companions, and the others came. The Prophet ﷺ led them in the remaining rak'ah. Then they completed their prayer on their own. 'Ubayd Allah said: al-Qasim said: \"I have not heard anything regarding the prayer of fear more beloved to me than this.\"\n\n[Sahih. See the preceding]"
"6011","Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh informed us; my father informed me; Shu'bah informed us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah: that the Prophet ﷺ led his companions in the prayer of fear. He placed them behind him in two rows. He led the row nearest to him in one rak'ah. Then he stood and remained standing until those behind them prayed one rak'ah. Then they moved forward and those who had been leading them fell back. The Prophet ﷺ led them in one rak'ah. Then he sat until those who had remained behind prayed one rak'ah. Then he pronounced the salam with them.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh. [Sahih. Abu Dawud 1239]"
"6012","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih informed us; Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi informed us; Musaddad informed us; Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us.\n\n(And) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-'Anbari informed us; my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi informed us; Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muhammad ibn Bashshar al-'Abdi informed us; Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari informed us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, who said regarding the prayer of fear: The Imam stands facing the qiblah, and a group of them stands with him, and a group faces the enemy with their faces toward the enemy. He leads them in one rak'ah, and they bow and prostrate on their own, performing two prostrations in their place. Then they go to the position of the others, and those others come. He leads them in one rak'ah and prostrates with them twice. Thus, for him (the Imam) it is two rak'ahs and for them one. Then they bow one rak'ah and prostrate two prostrations.\n\nThis is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Bashshar. And in the hadith of Musaddad: he leads those who are with him in one rak'ah, then they stand and bow one rak'ah on their own, and the rest carries the same meaning.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Musaddad. [Sahih. Bukhari 3902]"
"6013","Abu Salih informed us; my grandfather informed us; Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us. He said: I heard Ibn Bashshar say: I asked Yahya ibn Sa'id about this hadith, and he narrated to me from Shu'bah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, from the Prophet ﷺ, a hadith similar to that of Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari. And Yahya said: \"Write it alongside it, for I do not memorize the hadith, but it is like the hadith of Yahya.\"\n\n[Also 6013]"
"6014","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed him; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us; Musaddad informed us; Yahya informed us, from Shu'bah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, from the Prophet ﷺ, regarding the prayer of fear. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Musaddad, thus.\n\n[Also 6014]"
"6015","Abu Ahmad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-'Adl informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka informed us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi informed us; Yahya ibn Bukayr informed us; Malik informed us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Salih ibn Khawwat al-Ansari, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah al-Ansari, who narrated to him: that the prayer of fear is that the Imam stands, and a group of his companions stands with him, and a group faces the enemy. The Imam leads them in one rak'ah and prostrates with those who are with him. Then he stands; when he is fully upright, he remains standing, and they complete the remaining rak'ah on their own. Then they pronounce the salam and depart, while the Imam remains standing, and they face the enemy. Then the others who have not yet prayed come forward and pronounce the takbir behind the Imam. He leads them in one rak'ah and prostrates. Then he pronounces the salam, and they stand and complete the remaining rak'ah on their own, then they pronounce the salam.\n\nThus did Malik ibn Anas narrate it from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari. [Sahih. See 6009 above]"
"6016","Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri differed from him. He narrated it from Yahya ibn Sa'id with its chain and its meaning, except that he said at the end: \"Then they went to the positions of those others, and those others came and stood behind the Imam. He led them in one rak'ah. Then they stood and made up that rak'ah. Then the Imam pronounced the salam.\" Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us of this. They said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Asid ibn 'Asim informed us; al-Husayn ibn Hafs informed us, from Sufyan, and he mentioned it.\n\nAnd thus did Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrate it from Shu'bah and Malik, who said at the end: \"Then he pronounces the salam.\" And this is more deserving of being considered correct, due to its agreement with the narration of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim from his father, and with all that has preceded in this chapter. [Sahih]"
"6017","(6017) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak — informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far — narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib — narrated to us; Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi — narrated to us; Warqa' — from Mansur — from Mujahid — from Abu al-Ayyash al-Zurqi, who said:\n\nWe were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at 'Usfan when the time for the Zuhr prayer arrived, and over the polytheists was Khalid ibn al-Walid. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led his Companions in the Zuhr prayer. The polytheists said: \"They have a prayer after this one that is dearer to them than their children, their wealth, and their very lives\" — meaning the 'Asr prayer. Jibril (peace be upon him) descended upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) between Zuhr and 'Asr and informed him, and this verse was revealed: {And when you are among them and you stand to lead them in prayer, let a group of them stand with you and let them take their weapons} [al-Nisa': 102] — the verse to its end.\n\nWhen the time for prayer came, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arranged two rows, and they were armed. He said the takbir, and the enemy was before the Prophet (ﷺ), and they all said the takbir together, and they all bowed together. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prostrated, along with the row that was next to him, while the other row stood guard over them. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) finished and rose for the second rak'ah, the others prostrated. Then these advanced to the positions of those, and those retreated to the positions of these. He led them in another rak'ah; they all bowed together. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prostrated, along with the row next to him, while the others stood guard over them. When they finished, these prostrated. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the salam.\n\nAbu al-Ayyash said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed this prayer twice — once at 'Usfan and once in the territory of Banu Sulaym.\n\n[Sahih in its chain. Abu Dawud 1236]"
"6018","(6018) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan — narrated to us; Abu Yahya al-Himmani — narrated to us; from Abu Sa'd — from Makhul — from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', who said:\n\nThere were some of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) who would tie their toothsticks (masawik) to the scabbards of their swords. When the time for prayer came, they would use the toothstick and then pray. When the time for prayer came with the Prophet (ﷺ), one of them would take his sword or his bow and pray with the Prophet (ﷺ).\n\nAbu Sa'd al-Baqqal is not strong (in hadith). [Da'if]"
"6019","(6019) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Fami — both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani — narrated to us; Hajjaj — narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj said: Ya'la informed me — from Sa'id ibn Jubayr — from Ibn 'Abbas, regarding His words {If you are afflicted by rain or you are ill, you may lay down your weapons} [al-Nisa': 102]: He said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) was wounded.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn Muqatil from Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Bukhari 4323]"
"6020","(6020) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed me; Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far al-Muqri — narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad — narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim — informed us; 'Uqbah ibn Khalid al-Sakuni — narrated to us; Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim — from his father — from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about praying while carrying a bow. He said: \"Pray carrying the bow, and discard the string (horn).\"\n\nMusa ibn Muhammad is not strong (in hadith). [Munkar. al-Hakim 486]"
"6021","(6021) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Adib — informed us; Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Isma'ili — narrated to us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id — narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Sa'id — narrated to us; Hajjaj ibn Muhammad — from Ibn Jurayj — from Ibn Kathir — from Mujahid, who said: \"When they are engaged in close combat, it is only the takbir and gesturing with the head.\"\n\nIbn Jurayj said: Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated to me — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar — from the Prophet (ﷺ), similar to the statement of Mujahid: \"When they are engaged in close combat, it is only the takbir and gesturing with the head.\" And he added from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"If they are numerous, then let them pray mounted or standing on their feet\" — meaning the prayer of fear (Salat al-Khawf).\n\n[Sahih. Bukhari 901]"
"6022","(6022) And Abu 'Amr al-Adib — informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili — informed me; al-Haytham ibn Khalaf al-Durawi — narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Yahya al-Umawi — narrated to us; my father — narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj — from Musa ibn 'Uqbah — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, similar to the statement of Mujahid: \"When they are engaged in close combat, it is only the remembrance (dhikr) and gesturing with the head.\" And Ibn 'Umar added from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"And if they are more than that, then let them pray standing and mounted.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Sa'id ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id. [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"6023","(6023) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman — informed us; al-Shafi'i — informed us; Malik ibn Anas — from Nafi', who said:\n\nWhen 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar was asked about the prayer of fear, he would say: \"The imam advances with one group\" — and then he narrated the hadith. And Ibn 'Umar said in the hadith: \"If the fear is more severe than that, then let them pray on foot or mounted, facing the qibla or not facing it.\"\n\nMalik said: Nafi' said: \"I do not think 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar mentioned that except from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\"\n\n[Sahih. See the preceding]"
"6024","(6024) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah — informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Sa'id al-Hafiz — narrated to us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi — narrated to us; al-Nufayli — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Salamah — from Muhammad ibn Ishaq — narrated to me; Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr — from 'Ubaydullah, i.e., 'Abdullah ibn Unays — from his father 'Abdullah ibn Unays, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called me and said: \"It has reached me that Ibn Nubayh al-Hudhali is gathering the people to wage war against me, and he is at Nakhlah or 'Uranah. Go to him and kill him.\"\n\nI said: \"O Messenger of Allah, describe him to me so that I may recognize him.\" He said: \"The sign between you and him is that when you see him, you will feel a shudder (qusha'rirah).\"\n\nI set out with my sword wrapped in my garment until I came upon him among womenfolk who were seeking a place to camp, until the time of 'Asr arrived. When I saw him, I found in him what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had described to me of the shudder. I moved toward him, and I feared that there might be a struggle between us that would distract me from the prayer. So I prayed while walking toward him, gesturing with my head. When I reached him, he said: \"Who is the man?\" I said: \"A man from the Arabs who heard about you and your gathering for this man, and so I came for that.\" He said: \"Yes, we are engaged in that.\" I walked with him for a while until I found an opportunity, and I struck him with the sword and killed him. Then I departed, leaving his womenfolk bent over him. When I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he said: \"May his face prosper!\" I said: \"I have killed him, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"You have spoken the truth.\"\n\nThen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood with me and took me into his house and gave me a staff, saying: \"Keep this with you, O 'Abdullah ibn Unays.\" I went out with it among the people, and they said: \"What is this staff with you, O 'Abdullah ibn Unays?\" I said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave it to me and commanded me to keep it with me.\" They said: \"Will you not return to him and ask him about it?\" So I returned to him and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, why did you give me this staff?\" He said: \"It is a sign between you and me on the Day of Resurrection. Indeed, the fewest of people on that Day will be those who lean upon staffs.\"\n\n'Abdullah attached it to his sword, and it remained with him until, when he died, he was commanded regarding it, and it was placed with him in his shroud, and they were both buried together.\n\nSimilarly, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id and al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated it from Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar. [Hasan in its chain. Abu Ya'la 905]"
"6025","(6025) Abu Ja'far Kamil ibn Ahmad al-Mustamli — informed us; Abu Sahl Bishr ibn Ahmad al-Mihrajan — narrated to us; Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Bayhaqi — narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya — informed us; Jarir —\n\n(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl al-Dabbas in Makkah — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Zayd al-Sa'igh — narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Mansur — narrated to us; Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid — from Mansur — from Mujahid — from Abu al-Ayyash al-Zurqi, who said:\n\nWe were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at 'Usfan, and over the polytheists was Khalid ibn al-Walid. We prayed Zuhr, and the polytheists said: \"We missed an opportunity — we missed a moment of heedlessness — had we attacked them while they were in prayer!\" The verse of shortening (ayat al-qsr) was revealed between Zuhr and 'Asr. When the time for 'Asr arrived, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood facing the qibla, and the polytheists were before him. A row formed behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and after that row, another row formed. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) bowed, and they all bowed together. Then he prostrated, and the row who were next to him prostrated, while the other row stood guard over them. When these two had completed their two prostrations and stood up, the other row who had been behind them prostrated. Then the row that was next to him retreated to the position of the others, and the last row advanced to the position of the first row. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) bowed, and they all bowed together. Then he prostrated, along with the row next to him, while the other row stood guard over them. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and the row next to him sat, the others prostrated. Then they all sat together, and he pronounced the salam upon them all. He performed this prayer at 'Usfan, and he performed it on the day of Banu Sulaym.\n\nThis is the wording of the hadith of Sa'id ibn Mansur, and the hadith of Yahya ibn Yahya carries the same meaning. In it there is the additional wording: \"The people took up their weapons and formed two rows behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), facing the qibla, and the polytheists were facing them. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said the takbir, and they all said the takbir together. Then he bowed, and they all bowed together. Then he raised his head, and they all raised their heads together. Then he prostrated, and the row next to him prostrated.\" And the remainder is to the same effect. This is a sound isnad.\n\nQutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated it from Jarir, and he mentioned in it that Mujahid heard from Abu al-Ayyash Zayd ibn al-Samit al-Zurqi. And Jabir ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari narrated it from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Sahih. See no. 6017 above]"
"6026","(6026) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-'Anbari — informed us; my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi — narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah — narrated to us; Hannad ibn al-Sarri al-Tamimi — informed us; 'Abdah — from 'Abd al-Malik — from 'Ata' — from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, who said:\n\nI witnessed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). We formed two rows behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the enemy was between us and the qibla. The Prophet (ﷺ) said the takbir, and we all said the takbir together. Then he bowed, and we all bowed together. Then he raised his head, and we all raised his head together. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) went down in prostration, along with the row next to him, and the rear row stood facing the enemy. When the Prophet (ﷺ) and the row next to him completed the prostration and stood up, the rear row went down in prostration. When they completed their prostration and stood up, the rear row advanced and the front row retreated. Then he bowed, and we all bowed together. Then he raised his head, and we all raised his head together. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) went down in prostration, along with the row next to him — the front row that had been the rear row in the first rak'ah. When he completed the prostration and the row next to him, the rear row went down in prostration, and they prostrated. Jabir said: \"Just as your guards do this with their commanders.\"\n\n[Muslim 840]"
"6027","(6027) And Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us, my grandfather informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salama informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-'Abdī informed us, Yaḥyā—meaning Ibn Sa'īd al-Qaṭṭān—informed us, 'Abd al-Malik informed us, 'Aṭā' informed us from Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh: that he performed Ṣalāt al-Khawf (the Prayer of Fear) with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith with its meaning and added at its end: \"Then he (the Prophet ﷺ) pronounced the salām, and we all pronounced the salām together.\" Jābir said: \"Just as your royal guards do this with their commanders.\"\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Numayr, from his father, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
"6028","(6028) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū 'Abd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us, Zuhayr informed us, Abū al-Zubayr informed us from Jābir, who said: \"We went on a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against a group from Juhayna. They fought fiercely. When we had performed the Ẓuhr prayer, the polytheists said: 'If we were to make a single assault upon them, we would cut them off.' Jibrīl informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of that, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned it to us.\" He (Jābir) said: \"And they said: 'There will come to them a prayer that is more beloved to them than their children.'\" He said: \"When the prayer time came, we formed two rows, and the polytheists were between us and the qibla.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced the takbīr, and we pronounced the takbīr. He bowed, and we bowed. Then he prostrated, and the first row prostrated with him. When they stood up, the second row prostrated. Then the first row moved back and the second row moved forward, taking the place of the first. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced the takbīr, and we pronounced the takbīr. He bowed, and we bowed. Then he prostrated, and the first row prostrated with him, while the second row remained standing. When the second row had prostrated, they all sat together, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced the salām upon them.\" Abū al-Zubayr said: \"And Jābir specifically said: 'Just as your commanders pray.'\"\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, and al-Bukhārī used the narration of Hishām al-Dastawā'ī from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir as corroborating evidence for that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the hadith of Subayḥ.]"
"6029","(6029) Abū 'Amr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed us, al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā informed us, Abū Hammām informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb informed me, al-Zubaydī—meaning Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd—informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from 'Ubayd Allāh ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Utba, that 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abbās said: \"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ stood up, and the people stood with him. He pronounced the takbīr, and they pronounced the takbīr. Then he bowed, and a group of them bowed with him. Then he prostrated, and they prostrated. Then he stood up for the second rak'a, and those who had prostrated with him stepped back and guarded their brothers. The other group came and bowed with the Prophet ﷺ and prostrated, while all the people were in prayer, pronouncing the takbīr, but they were guarding one another.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 902.]"
"6030","(6030) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, 'Alī ibn 'Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Ibn Ṣā'id informed us, Muḥammad ibn 'Awf informed us, Ḥaywā ibn Sharīḥ informed us, and Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb informed us—with its chain of narration, similar to it.\n\nIt was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥaywā ibn Sharīḥ, and it is possible that what is intended by this is what we have narrated from others in this chapter, and it is also possible that it refers to something else. And it was narrated explicitly by al-Nu'mān ibn Rāshid from al-Zuhrī."
"6031","(6031) And Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, 'Alī ibn 'Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Ibn Ṣā'id informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Akhī Ḥazm al-Qaṭī'ī and al-Jarrāḥ ibn Makhlad informed us. (Ḥ) And al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismā'īl informed us, Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Bāhilī informed us, they said: Bishr ibn 'Umar informed us, Wuhayb ibn Khālid informed us, from al-Nu'mān ibn Rāshid, from al-Zuhrī, from 'Ubayd Allāh ibn 'Abd Allāh, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was commanded to perform Ṣalāt al-Khawf. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up, and we stood behind him in two rows. He pronounced the takbīr, and we bowed—both rows, all of them together. Then he raised his head, then he fell prostrating, and the row that was next to him prostrated, while the others remained standing, guarding their brothers. When he finished his prostration and stood up, the rear row fell prostrating, and they prostrated two prostrations. Then they stood up, and the front row that was next to him moved back, and the rear row moved forward. He bowed, and they bowed all together. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, and the row that was next to him prostrated, while the others remained standing, guarding their brothers. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat, the rear row fell prostrating, and they prostrated. Then the Prophet ﷺ pronounced the salām.\" And similarly it was narrated by Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn from 'Ikrima from Ibn 'Abbās.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in its own right. al-Dāraquṭnī 2/58.]"
"6032","(6032) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl informed us, Abū al-Azhar informed us, Ya'qūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Sa'd informed us, from his father, from Ibn Isḥāq, Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, the freedman of 'Amr ibn 'Uthmān, informed me, from 'Ikrima, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: \"Ṣalāt al-Khawf was nothing other than the prayer of your guards today behind your imāms—except that it was, as I think, performed in succession. A group stood, and they were all with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and a group prostrated with him. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up, and those who had been standing prostrated by themselves. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up, and they stood with him all together. Then he bowed, and they bowed with him all together. Then he prostrated, and those who had been standing the first time prostrated with him, while the others—those who had prostrated with him the first time—stood up. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat, and those who had prostrated with him at the end of their prayer, those who had been standing by themselves prostrated, then they sat, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gathered them all with the salām.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in its own right. Aḥmad 1/265.]"
"6033","(6033) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, 'Affān informed us, Abān al-'Aṭṭār informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr informed us, from Abī Salama ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān, from Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh, who said: \"We went forth with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until we reached Dhāt al-Riqā'. Whenever we came to a large, shady tree, we would leave it for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" He said: \"A man from the polytheists came, and the sword of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was hanging on a tree. He took the sword of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ and drew it, and said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'Do you fear me?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Then who will protect you from me?' He said: 'Allah will protect me from you.'\" He said: \"The companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threatened him.\" He said: \"So he sheathed the sword and hung it up.\" He said: \"Then the call to prayer was made.\" He said: \"He (the Prophet ﷺ) prayed with one group two rak'as, then they stepped back, and he prayed with the other group two rak'as.\" He said: \"So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had four rak'as, and the people had two rak'as.\"\n\nIt was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shayba, from 'Affān, and he also narrated it from the hadith of Mu'āwiya ibn Salām, from Yaḥyā. And similarly it was narrated by Sulaymān ibn Qays al-Yashkurī from Jābir, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fought the war of Khashaṣa, and a man from them came,\" then he mentioned the hadith with its meaning and completed it. And it was narrated from al-Ḥasan from Jābir.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 843.]"
"6034","(6034) And Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Ja'far al-Ḥaffār in Baghdad informed us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn 'Ayyāsh al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Abū al-Ash'ath informed us, 'Abd al-A'lā informed us, from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, who said: \"Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with his companions, and a group of them prayed with him while another group faced the enemy. He prayed with them two rak'as, then they stood up, and the others came, and he prayed with them two rak'as and pronounced the salām.\" And it was said regarding that, from Yūnus, in the area of Baṭn Nakhl.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
"6035","(6035) And Abū 'Abd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us, Abū Ja'far Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Baghdādī informed us, Ismā'īl ibn Isḥāq informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us, Ḥammād ibn Salama informed us, from Qatāda, from al-Ḥasan, from Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with a group of his companions two rak'as, then pronounced the salām, then prayed with the others two rak'as, then pronounced the salām. This is how they both narrated it from al-Ḥasan from Jābir. But al-Ash'ath differed from them, narrating it from al-Ḥasan from Abī Bakra, and Abū Ḥurra al-Raqāshī agreed with him on that.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"6036","(6036) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqri', and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-Ṣaydalānī informed us, they said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq al-Baṣrī informed us, Sa'īd ibn 'Āmir informed us, from al-Ash'ath, from al-Ḥasan, from Abī Bakra, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with some of them two rak'as, then pronounced the salām, so they stepped back. Then the others came, and he prayed with them two rak'as, then pronounced the salām. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had four rak'as, and the Muslims had two rak'as each in Ṣalāt al-Khawf.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1248.]"
"6037","(6037) And similarly, Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated it from al-Ash'ath, and he said regarding Dhuhr, and he added: \"And according to this, al-Hasan used to issue fatwa.\" And similarly, regarding Maghrib, the imam would have six rak'ahs and the people three and three. Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us of that; Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh informed us; my father informed us; al-Ash'ath informed us, and he mentioned the hadith with the same meaning but with different wording, and he mentioned this addition. And his statement, \"And similarly regarding Maghrib,\" I found it in my book connected to the hadith, and it appears to be from the words of al-Ash'ath. And in some copies, Abu Dawud said: \"And similarly regarding Maghrib.\" And some people narrated it from al-Ash'ath regarding Maghrib as marfu', but I do not think he was anything but mistaken in that. [Sahih li ghayrihi. Abu Dawud 1248]"
"6038","(6038) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Hafiz informed me; 'Abdan al-Ahwazi informed us; Muhammad ibn Ma'mar ibn Rib'i al-Qaysi informed us; 'Amr ibn Khalifa al-Bakrawi informed us; al-Ash'ath ibn 'Abd al-Malik al-Humrani narrated from al-Hasan from Abu Bakrah that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed with the people the prayer of fear — the Maghrib prayer — three rak'ahs, then he departed, and the others came, and he prayed with them three rak'ahs. [Munkar]"
"6039","(6039) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us; Abu Muhammad Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Muzani informed us; 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa informed us; Abu al-Yaman informed us; Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamza informed us from al-Zuhri; Salim ibn 'Abd Allah informed me from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar who said: \"I went on an expedition with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) towards Najd. We encountered the enemy and drew up ranks against them. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood and prayed for us. A group of us stood with him, and a group faced the enemy. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed one rak'ah and two prostrations with those who were with him, then they departed and took the place of the group that had not prayed. Then the group that had not prayed came, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed one rak'ah and two prostrations with them. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the salam, and every man among the Muslims stood and performed one rak'ah and two prostrations by himself.\"\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu al-Yaman. [Sahih. Bukhari 900]"
"6040","(6040) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawi informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Musa al-'Allaf informed us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar informed us.\n(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, and the wording is his; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us; Musaddad informed us; Yazid ibn Zurai' informed us; Ma'mar informed us from al-Zuhri from Salim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar from his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed the prayer of fear with one of the two groups one rak'ah, while the other group faced the enemy. Then they departed and stood in the place of those others, and those others came, and he prayed with them another rak'ah. Then he pronounced the salam upon them. Then these stood and completed their rak'ah, and those stood and completed their rak'ah.\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from 'Abd ibn Humayd from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih; see the preceding]"
"6041","(6041) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abd informed us; Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Tabari informed us; Hafs ibn 'Umar informed us; Qabisah informed us; Sufyan informed us from Musa ibn 'Uqba from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) prayed the prayer of fear. A group stood with him, and a group stood between them and the enemy. He prayed with them one rak'ah. Then these went to the positions of those, and those came to the positions of these. He prayed with them one rak'ah. Then he pronounced the salam upon them. Then the two groups each completed one rak'ah.\" [Sahih; see the preceding]"
"6042","(6042) And Abu al-Hasan informed us; Sulayman informed us; 'Ubayd ibn Ghanam informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us; Yahya ibn Adam informed us from Sufyan from Musa ibn 'Uqba from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet (ﷺ), similar to it. Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih."
"6043","(6043) And from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, and he added in it, and Ibn 'Umar said: \"When there is fear greater than that, one prays riding or standing, gesturing with one's head.\"\nAbu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us of it; 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Ka'bi informed us; Isma'il ibn Qutaybah informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us, and he mentioned it with its meaning and its addition. [Sahih. Ibn Abi Shaybah 8284]"
"6044","(6044) Abu Muhammad Janah ibn Nadhir ibn Janah al-Qadi in Kufa informed us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duhaym al-Shaybani informed us; Ahmad ibn Hazim informed us; Abu Nu'aym informed us; 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb informed us; Khusaif informed us from Abu 'Ubaydah from 'Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Mas'ud, that he said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed the prayer of fear with us. He arranged us in two rows: a row behind him and a row facing the enemy. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the takbir with the two rows behind him. He prayed with those behind him one rak'ah and two prostrations. Then they departed to the positions of their brothers, and the others came, threading through them. He prayed with them one rak'ah and two prostrations. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the salam, and those who were behind him prayed one rak'ah and two prostrations for themselves. Then they departed to their positions, and the others came and prayed one rak'ah and two prostrations for themselves.\" Khusaif said: \"And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was between the enemy and the qiblah.\"\nAnd al-Thawri narrated it from Khusaif, and he said in the hadith: \"A row behind him and a row parallel to the enemy, and all were in prayer.\"\nAnd Sharik narrated it from Khusaif, and he said in the hadith: \"The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the takbir, and both rows pronounced the takbir together.\"\nThis hadith is mursal. Abu 'Ubaydah did not meet his father, and Khusaif al-Jazari is not strong. [Da'if. Abu Dawud 1244]"
"6045","(6045) Abu Dawd al-Sijistani said: \"'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah prayed thus, except that the group with whom he prayed one rak'ah and then pronounced the salam went to the positions of their companions, and these came and prayed one rak'ah for themselves. Then they returned to the positions of those, and they prayed one rak'ah for themselves.\" Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; 'Abd al-Samad ibn Habib informed me; my father informed me that they went on an expedition with 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah to Kabul, and he prayed the prayer of fear with us. Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us of it; Abu Bakr ibn Dasa informed us; Abu Dawud informed us, and he mentioned it. [Da'if; see 6007]"
"6046","(6046) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Asid ibn 'Asim informed us; al-Husayn ibn Hafs informed us from Sufyan; al-Ash'ath, meaning Ibn Sulaym, informed me; al-Aswad ibn Hilal informed us; Tha'labah ibn Zahdam al-Hanazali informed us, saying: \"We were with Hudhayfah in Tabaristan, and Sa'id ibn al-'As said: 'Which of you witnessed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?' Hudhayfah said: 'I did.' A row stood behind him and a row parallel to the enemy. He prayed with them one rak'ah. Then these went to their positions, and those came, and he prayed with them one rak'ah, then he pronounced the salam upon them.\"\nAnd Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated it from Sufyan, except that he said: \"Hudhayfah stood, and the people arranged themselves behind him in two rows: a row behind him and a row parallel to the enemy. He prayed with those behind him one rak'ah. Then these departed to the place of these, and those came, and he prayed with them one rak'ah, and they did not make it up.\" [Sahih. Abu Dawud 1246]"
"6047","(6047) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; he said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; he said: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; he said: Sufyān narrated it. Thus Thaʿlabah ibn Zahdam narrated it from this chain from him. And we have narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Isḥāq from Sulaym ibn ʿAbd al-Salūlī, who said: I was with Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ in Ṭabaristān, so Saʿīd said to them: \"Which of you was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the prayer of fear (Ṣalāt al-Khawf)?\" Ḥudhayfah said: \"I was.\" He then mentioned a prayer similar to the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ at ʿUsfān. The statement of the narrator in the narration of Thaʿlabah — \"a row parallel to the enemy\" — refers to the state of prostration. And his statement, \"then these went to the place of those, and those came\" — means that the rear row moved forward and the front row moved back after completing the first rakʿah. In this way, both rakʿahs were performed with the imam, and they did not need to make up anything thereafter. This is clear in the narration of Sulaym ibn ʿAbd from Ḥudhayfah, and that account and this one are the same. Therefore, it is necessary to reconcile one narration with the other, given the agreement of the rest of the narrations. And with Allah is the granting of success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See what precedes it.]"
"6048","(6048) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; he said: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, who said: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Jahm narrated to us; he said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the prayer of fear at Dhī Qarad. A row stood behind him and a row stood parallel to the enemy. He prayed one rakʿah with them. Then these went to the positions of those, and those came and prayed one rakʿah with the Prophet ﷺ. Then he said the salām upon them. Sufyān said: Thus the Prophet ﷺ had two rakʿahs and each group had one rakʿah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī 1533.]"
"6049","(6049) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; he said: al-Shāfiʿī said: A ḥadīth has been narrated which the scholars of ḥadīth do not consider established: that the Prophet ﷺ prayed at Dhī Qarad one rakʿah with one group, then they said the salām, and one rakʿah with another group, then they said the salām, so the imam had two rakʿahs and each group had one rakʿah. Al-Shāfiʿī said: We abandoned it because all the aḥādīth on the prayer of fear are agreed that the followers have the same number of rakʿahs as the imam, and likewise the original obligation of prayer upon the people is one and the same in number. And it is not established for us because of a weakness in part of its chain of narration.\n\nThe Sheikh said: This is a ḥadīth that al-Bukhārī and Muslim did not include in their two books, and Abū Bakr ibn Abī al-Jahm is alone in narrating it in this manner from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh. It is possible that it is like his prayer at ʿUsfān, for his statement, \"then these went to the positions of those, and those came,\" may refer to the rear row moving forward and the front row moving back.\n\nAl-Zuhrī narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Ibn ʿAbbās what indicates that, with some difference regarding the time of guard duty of one of the two rows. And ʿIkrimah narrated it from Ibn ʿAbbās. The mention of these narrations has preceded, and in that is evidence for the correctness of this interpretation. And something similar is also to be understood. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"6050","(6050) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; he said: Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us; he said: Sufyān narrated to us, from al-Rukayn, from al-Qāsim ibn Ḥassān, who said: I came to Fulān ibn Wadiʿah and asked him about the prayer of fear. He said: \"Go to Zayd ibn Thābit and ask him.\" So I went to Zayd and asked him. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the prayer of fear. A row stood behind him and a row stood parallel to the enemy. He prayed one rakʿah with them. Then these went to the positions of those, and those came to the positions of these. He prayed one rakʿah with them, then said the salām. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Aḥmad 5/183.]"
"6051","(6051) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us; he said: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us; he said: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; he said: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; he said: al-Masʿūdī informed us, from Yazīd al-Faqīr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the prayer of fear. He ordered one group to stand facing the enemy, and he stood and prayed one rakʿah with one group. When they prostrated, those who had prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went and stood in the place of those, and those came and stood behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He prayed one rakʿah with them. When they had prostrated, he sat and said the salām with them. Thus the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two rakʿahs and those behind him had one rakʿah. When he said the salām with those behind him, the others also said the salām.\n\nThe Sheikh said: This is open to the same interpretation as the ḥadīth of Ḥudhayfah, Ibn ʿAbbās, and Zayd. And in his statement, \"the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two rakʿahs and those behind him had one rakʿah,\" it is possible that this is on the authority of some of the narrators before Jābir. We have narrated from ʿAṭāʾ and Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir what indicates that. And some of them said in the ḥadīth of Yazīd al-Faqīr that they made up another rakʿah. Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī said that.\n\nThe Sheikh said: al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah narrated it from Yazīd al-Faqīr from Jābir, and he said: \"We formed two rows,\" and he mentioned it with wording open to the interpretation we have mentioned. However, al-Masʿūdī narrated it once with an additional statement on the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh that prevents this interpretation. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. al-Nasāʾī 1545.]"
"6052","(6052) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; he said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; he said: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; he said: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; he said: al-Masʿūdī narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn Ṣuhayb al-Faqīr, who said: I asked Jābir about the two rakʿahs in travel — are they shortened? Jābir said: \"The two rakʿahs in travel are not shortened. Rather, shortening is one rakʿah at the time of combat.\" Then he began to narrate: He was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the time of combat, and the prayer time came. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood, and a group formed a row behind him, and a group stood facing the enemy. He prayed one rakʿah with them and performed two prostrations with them. Then those who had prayed behind him went and stood in the place of those, and those came and prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He prayed one rakʿah with them and performed two prostrations with them. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat and said the salām, and those behind him said the salām, and those also said the salām. Thus the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two rakʿahs and the people had one rakʿah each. Then Yazīd recited: {And when you are among them and you stand to lead them in prayer} [al-Nisāʾ: 102].\n\nThe Sheikh said: What has been narrated from Jābir — if it does not allow for the interpretation we have mentioned — may be a report about his prayer in the battle which he and others described, and that they made up their remaining rakʿah. It would then be a matter of something affirmed by some narrators but not others. The statement of the one who affirms is to be taken, and the original principle is the obligation of the full number, until it is established that reduction from it is permissible by something that does not allow for interpretation. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. See what precedes it.]"
"6053","(6053) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; he said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; he said: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Misʿar, from Simāk al-Ḥanafī, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet ﷺ: that he prayed one rakʿah with these and one rakʿah with these in the prayer of fear. Thus Simāk reported it in an abbreviated form.\n\nAnd we have narrated it from Sālim and Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that each of the two groups made up their rakʿah. The ruling in such a case is given to the one who affirms. And with Allah is the granting of success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded at 6039.]"
"6054","(6054) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; he said: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Aḥmasī narrated to us; he said: al-Muḥāribī narrated to us, from Ayyūb ibn ʾĀʾiḍh al-Ṭāʾī, from Bukayr ibn al-Akhnas, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: Allah obligated prayer upon the tongue of your Prophet ﷺ as four rakʿahs in residence, two rakʿahs in travel, and one rakʿah in fear. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded at 5383.]"
"6055","(6055) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; he said: ʿUthmān and Abū Bakr, the two sons of Abī Shaybah, both said: al-Qāsim ibn Mālik al-Muzanī narrated to us; he said: Ayyūb ibn ʾĀʾiḍh al-Ṭāʾī narrated to us, and he mentioned something similar to it.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.\n\nIt is possible that what is meant by it is one rakʿah with the imam and the individual performs another rakʿah alone, according to the view of one who holds that the obligation of prayer in congregation is upon individuals. And in the manner of the prayer of fear in the established aḥādīth, despite their varying forms, and the agreement in their number, there is evidence for the correctness of this interpretation. And Allah knows best.\n\nAḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, may Allah have mercy on him, and a group of the scholars of ḥadīth held the view that every ḥadīth that has been narrated regarding the chapters of the prayer of fear, acting upon it is permissible. And with Allah is the granting of success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded at 5383.]"
"6056","(6056) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Anas al-Qurashī narrated to us; he said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; he said: Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ narrated to us; he said: Abū al-Aswad informed us that he heard ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam: that he asked Abū Hurayrah, \"Did you pray the prayer of fear with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" Abū Hurayrah said: \"Yes.\" Marwān said: \"When?\" Abū Hurayrah said: \"In the year of the battle of Najd. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for the ʿAṣr prayer. A group stood with him and another group stood facing the enemy, their backs toward the qiblah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the takbīr, and all of them said the takbīr — those with him and those facing the enemy. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed one rakʿah, and the group with him performed the rakʿah. Then he prostrated, and the group behind him prostrated, while the others stood facing the enemy. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood, and the group with him stood. They went to face the enemy, and the group that had been facing the enemy came. They performed rukūʿ and sujūd while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood as he was. Then they stood, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed another rakʿah, and they performed it with him. He prostrated, and they prostrated with him. Then the group that had been facing the enemy came. They performed rukūʿ and sujūd while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat, as did those with him. Then came the salām. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the salām, and all of them said the salām. Thus the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two rakʿahs, and each man of the two groups had one rakʿah, one rakʿah.\" Thus he said.\n\nAnd the correct view is that each of the two groups had two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs.\n\nAbū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us of that; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; he said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; he said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd narrated to us; he said: Ḥaywah and Ibn Lahīʿah both said: Abū al-Aswad narrated to us, and he mentioned it with its meaning.\n\nThis is clear in the explanation of the ḥadīth. Perhaps he meant one rakʿah, one rakʿah with the imam. And likewise Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated it from ʿUrwah from Abī Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1240.]"
"6057","(6057) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me from ʿUrwah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led the people in the Prayer of Fear (Ṣalāt al-Khawf). He divided the people into two groups: one group stood behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and one group faced the enemy. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led those who were behind him in one rakʿah, and he performed two prostrations with them. Then he stood, and they stood with him. When he was fully upright in standing, those who had been behind him retreated walking backwards until they stood behind those who were facing the enemy. Then the other group came and stood behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and they performed one rakʿah by themselves while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) remained standing. Then they stood, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led them in another rakʿah. Thus they had two rakʿahs and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had two rakʿahs. Then those who had been facing the enemy came and performed one rakʿah and two prostrations by themselves. Then they sat behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he pronounced the salām with all of them together.\n\n(Tirmidhī) Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated it thus from Ibn Isḥāq. And Salamah ibn al-Faḍl narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, from ʿUrwah, from Abū Hurayrah. And Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated it from Ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad ibn Nawfal, from ʿUrwah, from Abū Hurayrah; and from Ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah — with a difference in the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), which is not found in the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah.\n\nAs for his narration from Ibn Isḥāq from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Abū al-Azhar mentioned it from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd, from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains]"
"6058","(6058) As for his narration from Ibn Isḥāq from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Muqriʾ in Baghdād narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Dūrī narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us, Abū al-Azhar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us, my father narrated to us from Ibn Isḥāq, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led the people in the Prayer of Fear at Dhāt al-Riqāʿ. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) divided the people into two groups: one group lined up behind him, and one group stood facing the enemy. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said \"Allāhu Akbar,\" and the group who lined up behind him said \"Allāhu Akbar.\" Then he bowed, and they bowed. Then he prostrated, and they prostrated. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) raised his head, and they raised their heads with him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) remained sitting, and they prostrated by themselves the second prostration. Then they stood and retreated on their heels, walking backwards until they stood behind them. Then the other group came and lined up behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and they said \"Allāhu Akbar.\" Then they bowed by themselves. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prostrated his second prostration, and they prostrated with him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stood for his second rakʿah, and they prostrated by themselves the second prostration. Then both groups stood together and lined up behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) bowed one rakʿah with them, and they all bowed together. Then he prostrated, and they all prostrated together. Then he raised his head, and they raised their heads with him. All of that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was very quick — he did not fall short in lightening the prayer as much as he was able. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) pronounced the salām, and they pronounced the salām. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stood, and the people had indeed shared with him in his entire prayer.\n\nThe two ḥadīths are the same in meaning, though one of them may add a word or something similar. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Aḥmad 6/275]"
"6059","(6059) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bukr informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, Sulaymān al-Aḥwāl informed me that Thābit, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, informed him:\n\nThat when there was what there was between ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr and ʿAnbasah ibn Abī Sufyān, they prepared for battle. So Khālid ibn al-ʿĀṣ rode to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr and admonished him. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr said: \"Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Whoever is killed in defence of his property, he is a martyr (shahīd).'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim, from Muḥammad ibn Bukr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 141]"
"6060","(6060) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, Abū Kurayb narrated to us, Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said:\n\nA man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what do you say if a man comes wanting to take my property?\" He said: \"Do not give him your property.\" He said: \"What if he fights me?\" He said: \"Fight him.\" He said: \"What if he kills me?\" He said: \"Then you are a martyr (shahīd).\" He said: \"What if I kill him?\" He said: \"Then he is in the Fire.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 140]"
"6061","(6061) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAwf, from Saʿīd ibn Zayd, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) say: \"Whoever is killed in defence of his property, he is a martyr (shahīd).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 4772]"
"6062","(6062) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated from his father, from Abī ʿUbaydah ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAwf, from Saʿīd ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Whoever is killed in defence of his property, he is a martyr (shahīd); and whoever is killed in defence of his family, he is a martyr (shahīd); and whoever is killed in defence of his blood, he is a martyr (shahīd).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī 4094]"
"6063","(6063) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī narrated to us, Abū al-Walīd narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to me, my father narrated, mentioning its chain and its meaning, except that he said: \"And whoever is afflicted in defence of his religion, he is a martyr (shahīd).\"\n\nHe did not mention \"blood,\" though some of the narrators from Abū Dāwūd mentioned both together. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 4772]"
"6064","(6064) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us, Ḥarb, meaning ibn Shaddād, narrated from Yaḥyā, meaning ibn Abī Kathīr, who said: ʿImrān ibn Ḥiṭṭān narrated to me:\n\nThat he asked Ibn ʿAbbās about wearing silk. He said: \"Ask ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) about it.\" So I asked ʿĀʾishah, and she said: \"Ask Ibn ʿUmar.\" So I asked Ibn ʿUmar, and he said: Abū Ḥafṣ narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Whoever wears silk in this world shall have no share in the Hereafter.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5497]"
"6065","(6065) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to me from the original of his book; and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bukr narrated from al-Awzāʿī, who said: Shaddād, Abū ʿAmmār, narrated to us.\n\nAbū Umāmah narrated to us that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: \"No one wears silk in this world except the one who shall have no share in the Hereafter.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm al-Rāzī, from Shuʿayb ibn Isḥāq, from al-Awzāʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6066","(6066) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Qāsim, who is ibn Zakariyyā al-Muqriʾ, narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Jarawī and al-Jurjānī narrated to us, both saying: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us, my father narrated to me, and he said: I heard Ibn Abī Najīḥ narrating from Mujāhid, from Ibn Abī Laylā, who said:\n\nḤudhayfah asked for water, so a landowner (dihqān) came to him with a vessel of silver. He took it and threw it at him, and said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade us to drink from vessels of gold and silver, and to eat from them, and to wear silk and brocade, and to sit upon it.\" And he said: \"It is for them in this world and for you in the Hereafter.\"\n\nHe said: And al-Qāsim informed us, saying: al-Faḍl ibn Sahl narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Jarīr narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, similarly.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, from Wahb ibn Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5110]"
"6067","(6067) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Sahl Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Ziyad al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; Muhammad ibn al-Jahm al-Simari narrated to us; Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated to us; Abu Ishaq Sulayman al-Shaybani narrated to us from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha' from Mu'awiyah ibn Suwayd from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us with seven things and forbade us from seven things.\n\nHe commanded us to visit the sick, to follow funeral processions, to spread the greeting of peace (salam), to accept the invitation of the host, to say \"Yarhamuk Allah\" to one who sneezes, to help the oppressed, and to fulfil oaths.\n\nAnd he forbade us from drinking from silver vessels, for whoever drinks from them in this world shall not drink from them in the Hereafter; and from wearing gold rings; from riding upon silk-covered saddles; and from wearing garments of qassi (a type of silk), silk, brocade (dibaj), and istabraq (thick silk brocade).\n\n[Sahih. Bukhari 1182]"
"6068","(6068) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Walid informed us; 'Ali ibn Zatiya narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Jarir narrated to us; al-Shaybani narrated it with the same meaning. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah from Jarir, and Muslim also narrated it."
"6069","(6069) From 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah. And 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated it, saying: Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani narrated it, and he said in the hadith: \"and sitting upon al-mayathir (silk cushions/saddles).\"\n\nAbu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; al-Qasim informed us; Abu Kurayb, Ibn Abi Madh'ur, and Yusuf narrated to us, saying: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, and he mentioned it.\n\nMuslim narrated it from Abu Kurayb. [Sahih — see previous]"
"6070","(6070) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us by dictation; 'Ali ibn Hamshadh al-'Adl narrated to us; Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us; al-Humaydi narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who heard Safwan ibn 'Abdillah ibn Safwan say:\n\nSa'd sought permission to enter upon Ibn 'Amar, and beneath him were silk cushions. He ordered them to be removed. Then Sa'd entered upon him, and upon him was a garment of khazz (a fabric mixed with silk). Sa'd said to him: \"You sought permission to enter upon me while beneath me were silk cushions, so I ordered them to be removed.\" Ibn 'Amar said to him: \"You are a good man, O Ibn 'Amar — unless you are among those about whom Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: *'You have exhausted your good things in your worldly life'* [al-Ahqaf: 20].\" Then he said: \"By Allah, lying upon burning embers of ghada wood is more beloved to me than lying upon them.\"\n\n[Sahih. Hakim 2/494]"
"6071","(6071) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah informed us; Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us something similar to it, and added in it that he said: \"O Abu Ishaq, that which is upon you — half of it is silk and half of it is khazz.\" He said: \"That which touches my skin is only the khazz.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) that a mount with a brocade saddle was brought to him, but he refused to ride it.\n\n[Sahih. Ibn Abi Shaybah 24639]"
"6072","(6072) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us; Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us; Hammam narrated to us from Qatadah from Anas, that:\n\nAl-Zubayr and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf (may Allah be pleased with them both) complained to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) of lice during one of their military expeditions, so he permitted them to wear shirts of silk. Anas said: \"I saw each of them wearing a shirt of silk.\"\n\nBoth al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their Sahihs from the hadith of Hammam ibn Yahya and others from Qatadah. [Sahih. Bukhari 2762]"
"6073","(6073) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi and Ziyad ibn al-Khalil narrated to us, saying: Musaddad narrated to us; al-Wahid narrated to us; al-Hajjaj narrated to me; Abu 'Umara, the brother-in-law of 'Ata', narrated to me, saying:\n\nI saw with Asma' bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) a jubbah (long outer garment) fastened with brocade buttons. She said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to wear this during battle.\"\n\n[Da'if. Ibn Majah 2819]"
"6074","(6074) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us from Qatadah from Anas ibn Malik, who said:\n\nPermission was granted to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf and al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam (may Allah be pleased with them both) to wear silk on account of an itching (hikkah) they suffered from.\n\n[Sahih. See 6072 above]"
"6075","(6075) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; 'Imran ibn Musa informed us; Musa ibn al-Sindi narrated to us; Waki' narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us from Qatadah from Anas, who said:\n\nThe Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) granted permission to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf and al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam to wear silk on account of an itching (hikkah) they suffered from.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad from Waki', thus. [Sahih. See 6072 above]"
"6076","(6076) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Amr al-Hayri informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Waki' narrated to us from Shu'bah from Qatadah from Anas, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) granted permission — or permission was granted — to al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf to wear silk on account of an itching (hikkah) they suffered from.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, thus.\n\nAnd Ahmad ibn Hanbal said from Waki': \"Permission was granted.\" And Ghundar said from Shu'bah: \"Permission was granted\" or \"The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) granted permission.\"\n\n[Sahih. See above]"
"6077","(6077) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd was asked about wearing silk, and he informed us from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Prophet ﷺ granted permission to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf to wear a shirt made of silk during a journey, on account of an irritation (ḥikkah) he experienced on his skin, and likewise to al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding]"
"6078","(6078) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me, ʿImrān ibn Mūsā narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Abū Usāmah narrated to us, Saʿīd narrated to us, Qatādah informed us that Anas ibn Mālik informed them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted permission to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf and al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām to wear silk shirts during a journey, on account of an irritation (ḥikkah) that afflicted them or some ailment that afflicted them.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kurayb, from Abī Usāmah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah. They both also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hammām ibn Yaḥyā, from Qatādah, in which it states: \"He granted them permission to wear a silk shirt during a military expedition (ghazāh) of theirs.\"\n\nIt is thus likely that the permission to wear it was for the purpose of war, even though its apparent indication is that it was for the irritation. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding]"
"6079","(6079) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Birtī narrated to us, Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf had a shirt of silk which he wore beneath his garments. ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: \"What is this?\" He replied: \"I wore it in the presence of one who is better than you.\"\n\nThe apparent meaning of this indicates its permissibility outside of war. However, the ḥadīth is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains]"
"6080","(6080) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal narrated to us, from Abī ʿUthmān, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to us while we were in Adharbījān: \"O ʿUtbah ibn Farqad, it is not from your toil, nor the toil of your father, nor the toil of your mother\" — he said it three times — \"so feed the Muslims in their dwellings from that with which you would suffice in your own camp. Beware of luxury, and the adornment of the people of shirk, and wearing silk, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the wearing of silk except this much,\" and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his two fingers.\n\nZuhayr said: ʿĀṣim said: \"This is in the letter.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, in abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī 5490]"
"6081","(6081) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us in Baṣrah, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, Qatādah narrated to us, saying: I heard Abā ʿUthmān al-Nahdī say: The letter of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us while we were with ʿUtbah ibn Farqad in Adharbījān, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade silk except this much, and he indicated with his two fingers adjacent to the thumb. He said: \"We understood that he meant the markings (aʿlām).\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ; same meaning as the preceding]"
"6082","(6082) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us, his father narrated to us, from Qatādah, from Abī ʿUthmān, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet of Allah ﷺ forbade the wearing of silk except the extent of two fingers.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Ghassān al-Mismaʿī and Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, from Muʿādh ibn Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ; same meaning as the preceding]"
"6083","(6083) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us, his father narrated to us, from Qatādah, from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, delivered a sermon in al-Jābiyah and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the wearing of silk except the extent of two fingers, or three, or four.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd Allāh al-Qawārīrī and a group, from Muʿādh ibn Hishām.\n\nIbn Abī ʿArūbah likewise narrated it from Qatādah. And Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth also narrated it from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from Abī ʿUthmān, that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to forbid silk and brocade except what was this much, and then he indicated with his finger, then the second, then the third, then the fourth. He said: \"And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid us from it.\"\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me of it, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us, and he mentioned it.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Numayr, from Ḥafṣ. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim 2069]"
"6084","(6084) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ, regarding the marking (ʿalam) in a garment. He intended to open a ḥadīth, then said: \"This man from among the people — his name is ʿAbd Allāh, the freedman of Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr — informed me.\" ʿAṭāʾ said: \"Narrate.\" So he narrated in the presence of ʿAṭāʾ, saying: \"Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr sent me to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, saying: 'It has reached me that you declare three things to be forbidden: fasting the entire month of Rajab, the mītharah of urjūwān (purple silk), and the marking (ʿalam) in a garment.'\"\n\nHe said: \"As for what you mentioned regarding fasting the entire month of Rajab, what about the one who fasts perpetually? As for the marking in a garment, ʿUmar narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever wears silk in this world shall not wear it in the Hereafter.' So I fear that the marking in a garment may be considered among the wearing of silk. As for the mītharah of urjūwān, this is the mītharah of Ibn ʿUmar — urjūwān, as you see it.\" I said: \"Yes.\" He went to Asmāʾ and informed her of that. ʿAbd Allāh said: \"She brought out to me a jubbah of ṭaylasān, the collar of which was lined with Khusrawānī brocade, and its two sleeves were hemmed with it.\" She said: \"This is the jubbah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; he used to wear it. When he passed away, it was with ʿĀʾishah. When she passed away, I took possession of it. We wash it for the sick among us when they are ailing, and we seek healing through it.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim 2069]"
"6085","(6085) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us, al-Mughīrah ibn Ziyād narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh, Abū ʿUmar, the freedman of Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, narrated to us, saying: \"I saw Ibn ʿUmar in the market; he purchased a Syrian garment and noticed in it a red thread, so he returned it. I came to Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr and mentioned that to her. She said: 'O girl, bring me the jubbah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' She then brought out a jubbah of ṭaylasān, the opening of the collar, the two sleeves, and the two edges of which were hemmed with brocade.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ; Abū Dāwūd 4054]"
"6086","(6086) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī al-Dhuhlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Abū Khaythamah informed us, from Khuṣayf, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ only disliked the garment made entirely of silk. As for the marking (ʿalam) of silk or the warp (sadā) of a garment, there is nothing wrong with it.\"\n\nThis is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā. In the narration of ʿAmr, it states \"forbade\" (nahā) instead of \"disliked\" (kariha), and he said: \"As for the marking of silk and the nīr (decorative stripe), there is nothing wrong with it.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains; Abū Dāwūd 4055]"
"6087","(6087) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayy al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū Muslim informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū 'Amr Ismā'īl ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī informed us, Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn 'Abd Allāh informed us, Abū 'Āṣim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Khuṣayf, from 'Ikrimah and Sa'īd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: \"Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) only prohibited silk that is entirely (solid) silk. As for that whose warp or weft is silk, there is no harm in wearing it.\"\n\nThus did Abū 'Āṣim narrate it from Ibn Jurayj, and it was reported from him in this hadith: \"As for the garment whose warp is silk and whose weft is not silk, it is not (considered) entirely silk, and we see no harm in it.\" [Ḍa'īf]"
"6088","(6088) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, they said: Abū al-'Abbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, 'Amr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, from Bukayr ibn 'Abd Allāh, from Busr ibn Sa'īd, that he saw upon Sa'd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) a Syrian jubbah whose standing (length) was of qazz (silk). Busr said: \"I also saw upon Zayd ibn Thābit a decorated cloak (khamā'iṣ mu'allamah).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Sharḥ Ma'ānī al-Āthār 4/256]"
"6089","(6089) As for the hadith which Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr informed us, they said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, 'Abd al-Wahhāb — i.e., ibn 'Aṭā' — informed us, Sa'īd informed us, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from 'Imrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"I do not ride upon al-urjūān, nor do I wear al-qassī, nor al-mu'aṣfar, nor a shirt hemmed with silk.\"\n\nIt may be understood — and Allah knows best — that by al-urjūān he meant the cushions of al-urjūān, which are the mounts of the non-Arabs made of brocade or silk; and by the shirt hemmed with silk, he meant that the silk is abundant, exceeding the measure of the border (trimming) in which he granted permission; or he intended by it a recommendation against it (tanzīh). As for the jubbah which Asmā' ibn Abī Bakr produced, it is possible that she wore it in battle, for we have narrated that from her in another hadith. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. See 5974]"
"6090","(6090) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, they said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me, from Nāfi', from 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Umar, from his father 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to prohibit dyeing al-'aṣb (a type of cloth) with urine. And that a ḥullah (a set of garments) was woven for the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and a ḥullah would reach a thousand dirhams or more.\n\nThe Shaykh said: The ḥullah which they used to wear consisted of two garments — an izār (lower garment) and a ridā' (upper garment) — except that it was made of qazz (silk). [Ṣaḥīḥ. 'Abd al-Razzāq 1498]"
"6091","(6091) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, 'Uthmān ibn Muḥammad al-Anmāṭī informed us, 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Rāzī informed us. And Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Rāzī informed us, my father informed me, from my father 'Abd Allāh ibn Sa'd, from his father Sa'd, who said: \"I saw a man in Bukhārā upon a white mule, wearing a black turban of khazz (silk brocade), and he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) clothed me with it.'\" This is the wording of the hadith of 'Uthmān. [Ḍa'īf. Abū Dāwūd 4038]"
"6092","(6092) And Abū Bakr al-Fārisī informed us, Abū Isḥāq al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn Fāris informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismā'īl al-Bukhārī informed me, Makhlad informed us, 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Sa'd al-Dashtakī al-Rāzī informed us, and he mentioned it similarly. 'Abd al-Raḥmān said: \"We think he was Ibn Khāzim al-Sulamī.\" Abū 'Abd Allāh said: \"Ibn Khāzim — I do not think he met the Prophet (ﷺ), or this is another shaykh.\""
"6093","(6093) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, al-'Abbās al-Dūrī informed us, Rūḥ informed us, Shu'bah informed us, from al-Fuḍayl ibn Faḍālah, from Abū Rajā' al-'Uṭāridī, who said: \"'Imrān ibn Ḥuṣayn came out to us wearing a Ṣilā' (cloak) of khazz (silk brocade), and we said: 'O Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), do you wear this?' He said: 'Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, Allah loves that when He bestows a blessing upon a servant, the trace of My blessing be seen upon him.\"'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 4/448]"
"6094","(6094) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥamshādh al-'Adl informed us, Abū Ismā'īl Muḥammad ibn Ismā'īl al-Sulamī informed us, Ibn Abī Maryam informed me, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed me, Ḥumayd informed me, who said: \"Anas ibn Mālik used to wear the finest of khazz (silk brocade).\" Ḥumayd said: \"I said to Nāfi', the freedman of Ibn 'Umar: 'Did Ibn 'Umar clothe his family in khazz?' He said: 'He clothed Ṣafiyyah with a Ṣilā' (cloak) for five hundred (dirhams).'\" [Ḥasan. Ṭabaqāt Ibn Sa'd 3/330]"
"6095","(6095) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, they said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: I heard 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Umar narrating from Isḥāq ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah and Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl, that they both saw upon Anas ibn Mālik a wrap (kisā') of khazz (silk brocade). 'Abd Allāh said: And Wahb ibn Kaysān narrated to me, he said: \"I saw men among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) wearing garments (aksiyah) of khazz, among them Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh and Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it]"
"6096","(6096) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Ja'far informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Ibn Bukayr informed us, Mālik informed us, from Hishām ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she clothed 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr with a Ṣilā' (cloak) of khazz (silk brocade) which 'Ā'ishah herself used to wear. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 1624]"
"6097","(6097) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-'Abbas al-Dawri narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Hassaan al-Samti narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Mujalid narrated to us — 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr said: I saw upon Abu Musa al-Ash'ari a woolen cloak (burnus) of silk (khazz). [Hasan]"
"6098","(6098) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan informed us — Abu 'Amr ibn al-Sammak narrated to us — Hanbal ibn Ishaq narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us — 'Imran al-Qattan narrated to us — 'Ammar ibn Abi 'Ammar said: I saw upon my father, Qatadah, a silk (khazz) wrap (mitarraf).\n\nAnd it has been reported to us regarding the dispensation in that matter as well from Abu Hurayrah and 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfah and from Ibn 'Umar that he used to dislike wearing it, yet he would see it upon his son and would not rebuke him for that. [Hasan. Ibn Abi Shaybah 2463]"
"6099","(6099) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Qasim Sulayman ibn Muhammad al-Madini informed me — Ibn Salamah narrated to us — Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us — al-Mughalis ibn Ziyad — Abu al-Walid al-'Amiri narrated to us — 'Amir ibn 'Ubaydah al-Bahili, the Qadi of Basrah, said: I set out with a group from the Banu Bahil until we came to Anas ibn Malik. He mentioned the hadith and said within it: We said, \"Then inform us about silk (al-khazz).\" He said: \"He brought out to us a silk (khazz) tunic (jubbah) with two shirts and said: 'Here it is — I wear it, though I wish I had not worn it. There is no one among the Companions of the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) except that he wore it, save 'Umar and Ibn 'Umar, for they did not wear it.'\" And he mentioned the hadith. [Hasan]"
"6100","(6100) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me — al-Hasan, meaning Ibn Sufyan, narrated to us — Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us — Sadaqah, meaning Ibn Khalid, narrated to us — Ibn Jabir narrated to us — 'Ata' ibn Qays narrated to us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm narrated to me — Abu 'Amir, or Abu Malik, al-Ash'ari — and by Allah, upon another oath, he did not lie to me — that he heard the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) saying:\n\n(Ḥ) And al-Hasan also informed me — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — Bishr, meaning Ibn Bakr, narrated to us — Ibn Jabir narrated to us — 'Ata' ibn Qays said: Rabi'ah al-Jurashi stood among the people and mentioned a lengthy hadith. He said: And lo, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm al-Ash'ari was present. I said: \"An oath by which I have bound myself.\" He said: \"My father — Abu 'Amir or Abu Malik — and by Allah, upon another oath, narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) saying: There shall surely be among my Ummah people who will deem lawful...\"\n\nIn the hadith of Hisham: \"Khamr (wine) and silk (harir).\"\n\nAnd in the hadith of Duḥaym: \"Silk (khazz), silk (harir), wine (khamr), musical instruments (ma'azif), and a people shall alight beside a mountain (‘alam), their flocks grazing freely, and a person in need shall come to them, and they shall say, 'Return to us tomorrow.' Then He shall leave them overnight, and the mountain shall be placed upon them, and He shall transform others among them into apes and swine until the Day of Resurrection.\"\n\nDuḥaym said: \"And He shall transform others among them...\" then he mentioned it.\n\nAl-Bukhari extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ and said: And Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us — Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated — and he mentioned it, and in his narration he included silk (al-khazz).\n\nAnd it has been reported to us from Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan from the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) that he said: \"Do not ride upon silk (khazz) or namirah (tiger-skin saddles).\" And it is as though he (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) disliked the adornment of the non-Arabs in their mounts and preferred moderation in clothing and conveyance. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhari 5268]"
"6101","(6101) Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us — Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us — from Abi Marhum, 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Maymun — from Sahl ibn Mu'adh al-Juhani — from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) said: \"Whoever leaves off clothing while having the ability to wear it, out of humility before Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, shall call him on the Day of Resurrection before all of creation and grant him the choice of the robes of faith (ḥulal al-iman), and he shall wear whichever he wishes.\" [Ḍa'if. Al-Tirmidhi 2481]"
"6102","(6102) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said — Abu al-'Abbas, that is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us — Bahr ibn Nasser narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to me — 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us — from Sa'id — from Harun ibn Kinanah, that the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) forbade two forms of notoriety (shuhrah): that a man wear beautiful garments so that people look at him in them, or that he wear lowly, shabby garments so that people look at him in them. 'Amr said: It has reached me that the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) said: \"The matter is between two matters, and the best of matters are the moderate ones.\" [Mawḍu'. The author extracted it in al-Shu'ab 6229]"
"6103","(6103) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us — Kamil ibn Talhah narrated to us — Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us — Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us — from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: A brocade (dibaj) tunic with gold buttons was gifted to the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), and he divided it among his Companions. Makhramah said: \"My son, let us go to the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), perhaps we may receive some of it.\" He said to me: \"Go inside and call the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace).\" So the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) came out to us wearing a qabā' from it, and he said: \"Here, O Makhramah — this is what I have kept for you.\" Al-Bukhari extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ and said: And Layth ibn Sa'd narrated. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhari 2459]"
"6104","(6104) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ṣaffar narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us — Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us — from Ayyub — from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, that the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) had divided brocade (dibaj) tunics with gold buttons, and this reached Makhramah ibn Nawfal — the father of al-Miswar. So he sent his son al-Miswar to the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace). He came and stood at the door and said: \"Call him for me.\" The Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) heard him, so he came out with tunics from among them, met him, and said: \"I have kept this for you, O Abu al-Miswar. I have kept this for you, O Abu al-Miswar.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab from Hammad, in this manner, as a mursal narration. And Ibn 'Ulayyah narrated it from Ayyub, and they both extracted it from the hadith of Hatim ibn Wardan from Ayyub, as a mawsul narration, except that in it there is no mention of brocade (dibaj) or the buttons. And likewise Qutaybah extracted it from al-Layth — in it there is no mention of brocade (dibaj) or the buttons. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6105","(6105) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Aṣbahani informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us — Yazid ibn Harun informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us — from Wathid ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, who said: I entered upon Anas ibn Malik, and he said: \"The Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) sent an army against Ukaydir of Dumat, and he sent to him a brocade (dibaj) tunic woven with gold. The Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) wore it, and the people began to touch it and look at it. The Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) said: 'Are you astonished at this tunic?' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we have never seen any garment more beautiful than it.' He said: 'By Allah, the handkerchiefs of Sa'd in Paradise are better than what you see.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhari 3076]"
"6106","(6106) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki both said — Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan narrated to us — Yahya ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayriqan narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' informed us — Sa'id narrated to us — from Qatadah — from Anas ibn Malik, that Ukaydir of Dumat gifted a tunic to the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace). Sa'id said: \"I think he said: 'Of sundus (fine silk).'\" He said: \"And that was before the prohibition of silk (harir).\" He said: \"So he wore it, and the people marveled at it, and he said: 'By the One in whose Hand is my soul, the handkerchiefs of Sa'd in Paradise are better than it.'\" They both extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through another chain from Qatadah, without the wording that Sa'id came with — \"that before the prohibition of silk (harir)\" — and this is more authentic than the narration of the one who narrated it and used to prohibit silk (harir).\n\nAnd al-Bukhari said: And Sa'id narrated from Qatadah from Anas that Ukaydir of Dumat gifted to the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) among the gifts of the polytheists, except that he did not mention the matn (text). [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6107","(6107) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Isma'il ibn Ahmad al-Jurjani informed us: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us: Harmalah ibn Yahya informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Ibrahim ibn 'Abdillah ibn Hunayn narrated to him that his father narrated to him that he heard 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: \"The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade me from reciting Qur'an while in ruku', and from wearing gold and dyed garments (al-mu'afar).\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Harmalah ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Muslim 2078]"
"6108","(6108) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: 'Amr ibn 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Himsi narrated to us: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Bahir, from Khalid, who said: \"Al-Miqdad ibn Ma'di Karib came to Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan and mentioned his story. Then al-Miqdad said: 'O Mu'awiyah, if I have spoken the truth, then confirm me, and if I have lied, then contradict me.' He said: 'I shall do so.' He said: 'I adjure you by Allah, did you hear the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbid the wearing of gold?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade the wearing of silk?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade wearing the skins of predatory animals and riding upon them?' He said: 'Yes.'\" [Sahih in other than this chain. al-Nasa'i 4255]"
"6109","(6109) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi informed us: Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn 'Abdillah al-Isbahani informed us: Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Faris narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari narrated to us: 'Ubaydillah ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from al-Harith ibn Mina', who said: \"'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, kept calling upon me, so I would bring him cloaks (qaba') from the cloaks of polytheism. He said: 'Remove the gold from them.'\" [Da'if. al-Bukhari narrated it in his Tarikh 2/282]"
"6110","(6110) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: 'Imran ibn Musa and Ahmad ibn al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab both said: Shayban narrated to us: Jarir, i.e. ibn Hazim, narrated to us: Nafi' narrated to us, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: \"'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saw 'Utayrid al-Tamimi selling in the market a silk brocade garment (hullah siyara'). He was a man who frequented kings and received gifts from them. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I saw 'Utayrid selling in the market a silk brocade garment. If you were to purchase it and wear it when the Arab delegations come to you'—and I think he said: 'and wear it on Fridays'—the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'Only he who has no share in the Hereafter wears silk in this world.' After that, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was brought some silk brocade garments, so he sent a garment to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and sent a garment to Usamah, may Allah be pleased with him, and gave 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, a garment, saying to him: 'Tear it into head-coverings for your women.' 'Umar came carrying his garment and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you sent this to me, yet you said yesterday about 'Utayrid's garment what you said.' He said: 'I did not send it to you to wear it; rather, I sent it to you so that you may benefit from it.' As for Usamah, he went about in his garment, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, looked at him with a look from which he knew that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, disapproved of what he had done. He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, why are you looking at me when you sent it to me?' He, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'I did not send it to you to wear it; rather, I sent it to you so that you may tear it into head-coverings for your women.'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Shayban ibn Farrukh. [Sahih. Muslim 2068]"
"6111","(6111) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Abu al-Fayyad Bakkar ibn 'Abdillah al-Zammani narrated to us: 'Abdillah, i.e. the brother of Juwayriyah, narrated to us: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi', that 'Abdillah ibn 'Umar informed him that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a silk brocade garment and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, if you were to purchase this garment and wear it for delegations and on Fridays,\" he said: \"Only he who has no share in the Hereafter wears this.\" Through this chain of narration: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent to 'Umar after that a silk brocade garment which he had given him. He said: \"O Messenger of Allah, you have clothed me with it, yet I heard you say about it what you said.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"I only sent it to you so that you may sell it, or clothe some of your women with it.\"\n\nal-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musa ibn Isma'il, from Juwayriyah ibn Asma'. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 846]"
"6112","(6112) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Musa, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Silk and gold have been made permissible for the females of my Ummah and forbidden for its males.\" [Sahih. al-Nasa'i 5148]"
"6113","(6113) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: 'Abdillah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to me: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam informed us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me: al-Hasan ibn Thawban and 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Abi Ruqayyah, who said: \"I heard Maslamah ibn Makhlad saying to 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir: 'Stand and inform the people of what you heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.' So 'Uqbah stood and said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: \"Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in Jahannam.\" And I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: \"Silk and gold are forbidden for the males of my Ummah and permissible for their females.\"'\" [Sahih in other than this chain. Ahmad 4/156]"
"6114","(6114) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi 'Awn narrated to me, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from his father, from his grandfather 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, who said: \"Umayyah said to me as I was walking with him: 'O 'Abd al-Ilah, which man among you has the mark of an ostrich feather on his chest?' I said: 'That is Hamzah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib.' He said: 'That one did to us what the serpents do.'\" [Sahih in other than this chain. al-Hakim 2/128]"
"6115","(6115) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Kamil narrated to us: Abu Qilabah al-Raqashi narrated to us: 'Amr ibn 'Asim al-Kalabi narrated to me: 'Ubaydillah ibn al-Wazi' ibn Thawr narrated to me: Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated to me, from his father, from al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, regarding the story of Abu Dujanah—i.e., Simakh ibn Kharashah—on the day of Uhud, when the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, gave him his sword. He said: \"Whenever he intended to fight, he would mark himself with a headband.\" [Da'if. al-Hakim 3/256]"
"6116","(6116) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Karim narrated to us: Ya'qub al-Dawraqi narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: Abu Hashim informed us, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn 'Ubad, who said: \"I heard Abu Dhar swearing an oath that this verse: 'These two opponents disputed concerning their Lord' [al-Hajj: 19] was revealed concerning those who came forth on the day of Badr: Hamzah, 'Ali, 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith, 'Utbah, Shaybah the sons of Rabi'ah, and al-Walid ibn 'Utbah.\"\n\nal-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Ya'qub al-Dawraqi, and Muslim narrated it from 'Amr ibn Zurarah, from Hushaym. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 3748]"
"6117","(6117) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Mihrān narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding the story of Badr, who said: \"ʿUtbah came forward, along with his brother Shaybah and his son al-Walīd, and said: 'Who will come out to duel?' A group of young men from the Anṣār came out, but ʿUtbah said: 'We do not want these; rather, we wish to duel with our paternal cousins, the Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Rise, O Ḥamzah! Rise, O ʿUbaydah! Rise, O ʿAlī!' So Ḥamzah went out against ʿUtbah, ʿUbaydah against Shaybah, and ʿAlī against al-Walīd. Ḥamzah killed ʿUtbah, ʿAlī killed al-Walīd, and ʿUbaydah killed Shaybah. However, Shaybah struck the leg of ʿUbaydah and severed it. Ḥamzah and ʿAlī rescued him, and he eventually passed away at al-Ṣafrāʾ.\" In the narration of Ibn Isḥāq from his companions regarding this story, it is reported that ʿUbaydah dueled ʿUtbah, Ḥamzah dueled Shaybah, and ʿAlī dueled al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 2665]"
"6118","(6118) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Ibn Idrīs narrated to us. He said: I heard ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb from Abū Burdah from ʿAlī, who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) forbade me from placing my ring on this finger or the one next to it\" — ʿĀṣim was uncertain which of the two he meant — \"and he forbade me from wearing silk garments (al-qassiyy) and from sitting upon silk cushions (al-mayāthir).\" He said: \"As for al-qassiyy, they are garments interwoven with silk, brought from Egypt and Syria. As for al-mayāthir, they are things that women used to prepare for their husbands on their saddles, like cushions of purple silk.\"\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2078]"
"6119","(6119) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Musaddad narrated to us: Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb narrated to me from Abū Burdah from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) forbade me from wearing al-qassiyy and from using al-mīthrah.\" Abū Burdah said: \"We asked ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): 'What is al-qassiyy?' He said: 'Garments that came to us from Syria or Egypt, interwoven with silk, containing patterns resembling citron.' And al-mīthrah is something that women used to make for their husbands, like cushions, which they would place on saddles.\"\nAl-Bukhārī alluded to it in a heading.\nAbū ʿUbayd said: \"Al-mayāthir were from the saddles of the non-Arabs, made of brocade or silk.\"\nAnother said: \"Al-mīthrah refers to the skins of predatory animals.\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 4225]"
"6120","(6120) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ḥumayd ibn Masʿadah narrated to us: Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Khālid narrated from Maymūn al-Qannād from Abū Qilābah from Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade riding upon tiger skins and wearing gold, except in a fragmented form.\"\nIt was also narrated by Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from Muʿāwiyah regarding riding upon tiger skins, and by Abū Shaykh al-Hunāʾī from Muʿāwiyah regarding both riding upon tiger skins and gold.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 4239]"
"6121","(6121) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam, Abū al-Aswad, Abū Zayd, and Yazīd ibn Khālid ibn Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mawhab said: al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah narrated to us: ʿAyyāsh ibn ʿAbbās narrated to us from Abū al-Ḥuṣayn, al-Haytham ibn Shufayy, who said: \"I and Abū ʿĀmir al-Maʿāfirī set out to pray in Īliyāʾ. Their judge was a man from the Azd called Abū Rayḥānah, who was among the Companions.\" Abū al-Ḥuṣayn said: \"My companion preceded me to the mosque, then I caught up with him and sat beside him. He asked me: 'Did you come across the narrations of Abū Rayḥānah?' I said: 'No.' He said: 'I heard him say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade ten things: filing the teeth (al-washr), tattooing (al-washm), plucking hair (al-natf), a man lying with another man without a garment between them, a woman lying with another woman without a garment between them, a man making the lower part of his garments of silk like the non-Arabs, and placing silk upon his shoulders like the non-Arabs, plundering (al-nuhbay), riding upon tiger skins, and wearing a ring except for one in authority (dhī sulṭān).'\"\n[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 4049]"
"6122","(6122) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hannād al-Sarī narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated from Isḥāq ibn Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr al-Qurashī from his father from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"I saw a group from the people of Yemen whose saddles were of leather. He said: 'Whoever wishes to look at a group most resembling the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), let him look at these.'\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 4144]"
"6123","(6123) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadhābādhī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Saʿdī informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl informed us.\n(Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmish al-Faqīh informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdūs ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Naysābūrī informed us: Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Rāzī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī narrated to me: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us.)\n(Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Hishām ibn Mallās al-Numayrī informed us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī narrated to us: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us.)\nHe said: Anas ibn Mālik said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) arrived in Madīnah while the people of Madīnah had two days during which they used to play in the Days of Ignorance. He said: 'I have come to you while you had two days during which you used to play in the Days of Ignorance. Allah has replaced them with two better days: the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Naḥr) and the Day of Breaking the Fast (Yawm al-Fiṭr).'\"\nThis is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Fazārī.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 34/1]"
"6124","(6124) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ḥafṣ narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us.\n(Abū ʿAbd Allāh said: And Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said: Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us from Shuʿbah from ʿAmr ibn Murrah from Zādhān, who said: \"A man asked ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) about ghusl (ritual bath). He said: 'Perform ghusl every day if you wish.' He said: 'No, I mean the obligatory ghusl.' He said: 'On Friday, the Day of ʿArafah, the Day of Sacrifice, and the Day of Breaking the Fast.'\")\n[Jayyid. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī 1765]"
"6125","(6125) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, along with others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.\n(Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"He used to perform ghusl on the Day of Breaking the Fast before going out (to the prayer place).\"\nIt was also narrated by Ibn ʿAjlān and others from Nāfiʿ, who said: \"On both ʿĪds: al-Aḍḥā and al-Fiṭr.\")\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 426]"
"6126","(6126) It has been narrated to us regarding this from Salamah ibn al-Akwʿ, and from Ibn al-Musayyab and ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr. Ḥajjāj ibn Tamīm, who is not strong, narrated from Maymūn ibn Mihrān from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform ghusl on the Day of Breaking the Fast and the Day of Sacrifice.\" Abū Saʿd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Mālinī narrated it to us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Jubārah narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Tamīm narrated to me: Maymūn ibn Mihrān narrated it, and he mentioned it.\nAbū Aḥmad said: \"His narration is not sound.\"\n[Very weak. Ibn Mājah 1315]"
"6127","(6127) Al-Shāfiʿī said: I heard those among the people of knowledge of the Qurʾān whom I am pleased with say: \"Complete the count of the fasting of the month of Ramadan, and magnify Allah upon its completion, as He has guided you.\" Its completion is at the setting of the sun on the last day of the days of the month of Ramadan.\n\nAbū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us of that; Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ reported from al-Shāfiʿī.\n\nThe Shaykh said: And it has reached me from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding His statement {and mentions the name of his Lord and prays} [al-Aʿlā: 15], he said: \"He mentions Allah as he sets out for the Eid.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Kitāb al-Umm 1/384]"
"6128","(6128) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; Ibn Abī ʿĀṣim narrated to us; Ibn Muṣaffá narrated to me; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-ʿAṭṭār, a trustworthy narrator, narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he used to make takbīr on the night of Eid al-Fiṭr until he went out to the muṣallā.\n\nThe mention of the night in it is unusual. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6129","(6129) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān, narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān; Nāfiʿ narrated to me: that Ibn ʿUmar used to go out to the Eid from the mosque, and he would raise his voice with takbīr until he reached the muṣallā, and he would continue with takbīr until the Imam arrived.\n\nIbn Idrīs narrated it from Ibn ʿAjlān and said: \"On the day of Eid al-Fiṭr and Eid al-Aḍḥā.\" And this is the correct version, being mawqūf (a statement of the Companion).\n\nIt has also been narrated through two weak chains as marfūʿ (attributed to the Prophet). As for the more reliable of the two: [Qawī. Ḥākim 1/438]"
"6130","(6130) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Wahb narrated to us; my uncle narrated to me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to go out on the two Eids with al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās, ʿAbd Allāh, al-ʿAbbās, ʿAlī, Jaʿfar, al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, Usāmah ibn Zayd, Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and Ayman ibn Umm Ayman (may Allah be pleased with them), raising his voice with tahlīl and takbīr, taking the path of the Jaddādīn until he reached the muṣallā. And when he had finished, he would return via the path of the Ḥaddādīn until he reached his house. [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Mājah 1431]"
"6131","(6131) As for the weaker of the two: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baghdādī narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Khunays al-Dimashqī narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; al-Zuhrī narrated; Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar informed him: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to make takbīr on the day of Eid al-Fiṭr from the time he left his house until he reached the muṣallā.\n\nMūsā ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAṭāʾ is denounced in ḥadīth and is weak, and al-Walīd ibn Muḥammad al-Muwarqī is weak; the narrations of the likes of these two are not used as proof. The preserved ḥadīth from Ibn ʿUmar is from his own statement.\n\nIt has also been narrated from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) and a group of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) (may Allah be pleased with them) similar to what we have narrated from Ibn ʿUmar regarding takbīr when going out to the muṣallā. [Very weak. Ḥākim 1/437]"
"6132","(6132) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, who said: \"They were more intense in takbīr on Eid al-Fiṭr than on Eid al-Aḍḥā.\"\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī narrated through his chain from a group of the Tābiʿūn that they used to make takbīr on the night of Eid al-Fiṭr in the mosque, raising their voices with it. And from a group of them: their raising of the voice with it when going out to the muṣallā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥākim 1/438]"
"6133","(6133) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; al-Aʿmash narrated from Tamīm ibn Salamah, who said: \"Ibn al-Zubayr came out on the day of sacrifice (Yawm al-Naḥr) and saw that the people were not making takbīr, so he said: 'What is the matter with them that they do not make takbīr? By Allah, they used to do that. I have seen us in the army whose two ends could not be seen, and a man would make takbīr, and the one next to him would make takbīr, until the whole army would reverberate with takbīr. And indeed, between you and them is what lies between the lowest earth and the highest heaven.'\" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6134","(6134) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us; Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to go out on the day of Eid al-Fiṭr and the day of Eid al-Aḍḥā to the muṣallā. The first thing he would begin with was the prayer. Then he would turn back and stand facing the people, while the people were sitting in their rows, and he would admonish them, enjoin upon them, and command them. If he wished to dispatch an expedition, he would dispatch it, and if he wished to command something, he would command it. Then he would depart.\"\n\nAbū Saʿīd said: \"The people continued upon that until I went out with Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam, who was the governor of Madīnah, on an Eid al-Aḍḥā or Eid al-Fiṭr. When we reached the muṣallā, there was a minbar made of bricks that Kathīr ibn al-Ṣalt had built. Marwān wanted to ascend it before praying. I grabbed his hand, but he pulled away from me and ascended it. The people gathered, and he delivered the sermon before the prayer. I said to him: 'By Allah, you have changed it.' He said: 'O Abū Saʿīd, what you know has passed away.' I said: 'By Allah, what I know is better than what I do not know.' He said: 'The people would not sit for us after the prayer, so we made it before the prayer.'\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 913]"
"6135","(6135) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī informed me.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū Muḥammad al-Muzanī informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān narrated to us; Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī; Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: \"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) found a garment of istabraq being sold in the market. He took it and brought it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, buy this and adorn yourself with it for the Eid and for delegations.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'This is the clothing of the one who has no share (in the Hereafter).'\"\n\nʿUmar lived for as long as Allah willed. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent him a jubbah of dibāj. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) brought it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you said: \"This is the clothing of the one who has no share.\" Yet you have sent me this jubbah.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Sell it, or fulfill your need with it.'\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 906]"
"6136","(6136) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from al-Ḥajjāj, from Abī Jaʿfar, from Jābir: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to wear his red cloak (burdah) on the two Eids and on Friday. [Ḍaʿīf. Previously referenced at 5985]"
"6137","(6137) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, among others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed me: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet ﷺ used to wear a striped Yemeni cloak (burd ḥibara) on every ʿĪd. [Very weak. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī, 320]"
"6138","(6138) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad said: The Prophet ﷺ used to wear a turban (ʿimāma) on every ʿĪd. [Very weak. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm, 1/388]"
"6139","(6139) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlāʾ ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Isfarāyīnī informed us therewith: Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Aḥmad informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī al-Dhuhlī informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Wakīʿ narrated from Mūsāwir al-Warrāq, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ addressed the people while wearing a black turban. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. Previously referenced, 5976]"
"6140","(6140) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, who said: I saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāblāh wearing a turban, having let its end hang behind him.\n\nIsmāʿīl said: And Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to me from Ibn Abī Ruzayn, who said: I witnessed ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib on the day of ʿĪd wearing a turban, having let its end hang behind him, and the people did likewise. Thus he said. It was also said, on the authority of Ismāʿīl, from Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf, from Abī Ruzayn, from ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿa, who said: I witnessed ʿAlī. [Weak. Reported by the author in al-Shuʿab, 6255]"
"6141","(6141) Abū Ḥāzim al-ʿAbdawī informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī informed us: Abū Hammām al-Sakūnī, meaning al-Walīd ibn Shuʿjāʿ, narrated to us: Ismāʿīl, meaning Ibn ʿAyyāsh, narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated from Abī Ruzayn, from ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿa, who said: I witnessed ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib on the day of ʿĪd, and I saw him wearing a turban, having let its end hang behind him, and the people did likewise. [Weak]"
"6142","(6142) Abū Ḥāzim informed us: Abū Aḥmad informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Mughallis in Baghdād informed us: Abū Hammām, meaning al-Sakūnī, narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Ruzayn, the seller of floor mats, narrated from al-Aṣbaḥ ibn Nubāta, who said: I saw ʿAlī on the day of ʿĪd coming out wearing a turban, walking on foot, and with him were approximately four thousand men walking on foot, all wearing turbans.\n\nIsḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm followed him in narrating this from ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus. This is a weak chain of narration. [Very weak]"
"6143","(6143) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn narrated to us: Ibn Abī Zāʾida narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, that Ibn ʿUmar used to wear his finest garments on the two ʿĪds. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6144","(6144) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī both said: Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Ḥassān ibn Ḥassān al-Baṣrī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would go out on the day of Fiṭr and the day of Aḍḥā walking, and the spear would be carried before him. Then it would be set up before him during the prayer, and he would take it as a screen (sutra). This was before houses were built around the place of prayer. He said: And he did the same at ʿArafa.\n\nHis statement \"walking\" (māshiyyan) is singular (gharīb); I have not recorded it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar except through this chain of narration, and it is not strong. As for the remainder of its wording, it is well-known. [Ḥasan through its chains]"
"6145","(6145) And it is narrated in the ḥadīth of Saʿd al-Qaraẓ that the Prophet ﷺ would go out walking and return walking.\n\nAbū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us: Abū Yaḥyā al-Ḥarrānī informed us: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd ibn ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd narrated to me: My father narrated to me from his fathers, and he mentioned it. [Ḥasan through its corroborating chain. Tirmidhī, 530]"
"6146","(6146) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Jaʿfar al-Muqriʾ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq informed us: Abū Ghassān and Muʿāwiya ibn ʿAmr both said: Zuhayr narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq narrated from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī, who said: It is from the Sunna that a man should walk to the place of prayer (al-muṣallā)."
"6147","(6147) Abū ʿAliyy al-Rūdhbārī informed us; he said: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab al-Muqriʾ in Wāsiṭ narrated to us; he said: Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; he said: Abū Nuʿaym and Abū Dāwūd al-Ḥafarī narrated to us, from Sharīk, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṣālib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: \"It is from the Sunnah that you come to the ʿĒd on foot.\" Abū Dāwūd added in his narration: \"Then you may ride when you return.\" [Ḥasan for something else; see before it.]"
"6148","(6148) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī narrated to us; he said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; he said: my father narrated to us; he said: Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us; he said: Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr narrated to us; he said: Yazīd ibn Khumayr al-Raḥabī said: \"ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr, the Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, went out with the people on the day of ʿĒd al-Fiṭr or ʿĒd al-Aḍḥā, and he disapproved of the Imām's delay, saying: 'Indeed, we used to be with the Prophet ﷺ, and we would have finished by this time, and that was at the time of the supererogatory prayers (al-Tasbīḥ).'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ; Abū Dāwūd 1135]"
"6149","(6149) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, along with others; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; he said: al-Shāfiʿī informed us; he said: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed us; he said: Abū al-Ḥuwayrith informed me: \"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm while he was in Najrān: 'Advance the ʿĒd al-Aḍḥā and delay the ʿĒd al-Fiṭr, and remind the people.'\" This is a mursal narration. I have searched for it in all the narrations of his letter to ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm but did not find it. And Allah knows best. [Very weak; al-Shāfiʿī 322]"
"6150","(6150) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; he said: al-Rabīʿ informed us; he said: al-Shāfiʿī informed us; he said: al-Thiqah (a trustworthy narrator) informed us that al-Ḥasan used to say: \"Indeed, the Prophet ﷺ would go out to the ʿĒd al-Aḍḥā and the ʿĒd al-Fiṭr when the sun rose and its rising was complete.\" This is also a mursal narration, and its witness is the practice of the Muslims regarding that, or close to it, with a slight delay from it. [Very weak; al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 1/386]"
"6151","(6151) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; he said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Fihrī, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah: \"That a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned the hadith, and he said in it: 'When you have prayed al-Fajr, then refrain from prayer until the sun rises, for it rises between the two horns of Satan. Then prayer is witnessed, attended, accepted, until the day is half gone.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ for something else; Ibn Khuzaymah 1275]"
"6152","(6152) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdad informed us, word for word; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Ḥamdān narrated to us, word for word, and he was the brother of Abī ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān al-Naysābūrī; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Sarī narrated to us; he said: Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Bakr, from Anas: \"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not go out on the day of ʿĒd al-Fiṭr until he had eaten some dates.\" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm from Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān. And likewise, Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī narrated it from Hushaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī 910]"
"6153","(6153) And ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn al-Wāsiṭī narrated it from Hushaym, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Anas, from Anas, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would break his fast on the day of ʿĒd al-Fiṭr on dates before he went out.\" Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; he said: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; he said: ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn informed us; he said: Hushaym informed us, and he mentioned it. And likewise, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Aḥmad ibn Manīʿ narrated it from Hushaym. And Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī confirmed what he narrated through the narration of Murjā ibn Rajāʾ from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Anas. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see before it]"
"6154","(6154) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us of it; he said: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; he said: al-Haytham ibn Khalaf al-Dūrī informed me; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Abī al-Naḍr narrated to us; he said: my father narrated to us; he said: Murjā ibn Rajāʾ al-Yashkurī said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated to me; he said: Anas ibn Mālik narrated to me, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not go out on the day of ʿĒd al-Fiṭr until he had eaten dates, and he would eat them in odd numbers.\" And likewise, ʿUtbah ibn Ḥumayd al-Dabbī narrated it from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Bakr. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī 910]"
"6155","(6155) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us; he said: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; he said: Abū Ghassān Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; he said: Zuhayr narrated to us; he said: ʿUtbah ibn Ḥumayd al-Dabbī narrated to us; he said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Anas narrated to us; he said: I heard Anas say: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not go out on the day of ʿĒd al-Fiṭr until he had eaten dates—three, or five, or seven, or less than that, or more than that—in odd numbers.\" And what confirms the correctness of what al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him, chose is the narration of Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān of the hadith from Hushaym through both chains of narration. [Ḥasan; Ibn Ḥibbān 2841]"
"6156","(6156) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us of it; he said: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; he said: Muʿādh ibn al-Mathannā narrated to us; he said: Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; he said: Hushaym narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Anas, from Anas, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not go out on the day of ʿĒd al-Fiṭr until he had eaten.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ for something else; Ibn Mājah 1754]"
"6157","And with this isnad, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hushaym, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Anas, from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar hadith."
"6158","(6158) Abu al-Rudhbari 'Ali informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar ibn Ahmad ibn Shawdhb narrated to us in Wasit: Shu'ayb ibn Ayyub narrated to us: Abu Ghassan al-Nahdi narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated to us: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Harith, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"It is from the Sunnah that a man should eat on the Day of Fitr before he goes out to the prayer ground.\" [Sahih according to others. Tirmidhi 530]"
"6159","(6159) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: Abu 'Asim informed us: Thawban ibn 'Utbah narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us: Yunus ibn Habib informed us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Thawban ibn 'Utbah al-Mahri narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Buraydah narrated to us, from his father, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would not go out on the Day of Fitr until he had eaten, and he would not eat on the Day of Sacrifice until he had slaughtered.\"\n\nThe wording is that of Abu Dawud's hadith. In the narration of Abu 'Asim, it reads: \"until he returned.\" [Jayyid. Tirmidhi 1600]"
"6160","(6160) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us: Abu Muslim informed us: Muslim, i.e., ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us: Thawban ibn 'Utbah al-Mahri narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah.\n\nHe mentioned it with its chain and its meaning, except that he said: \"until he returns and eats from his udhiyah (sacrifice).\" [Jayyid. See the one before it.]"
"6161","(6161) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us: Sa'id ibn 'Uthman al-Ahwazi narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Bahr al-Qattan narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us: Ibn Mahdi narrated to us: 'Uqbah ibn al-Assam narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when it was the Day of Fitr, would not go out until he had eaten something, and when it was the Day of Adha, he would not eat anything until he returned. And when he returned, he would eat from the liver of his udhiyah.\" [Sahih according to others.]"
"6162","(6162) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sadiq ibn Abi al-Fawaris both informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he would go out to the prayer ground on the Day of Adha without eating anything. [Sahih. 'Abd al-Razzaq 5743]"
"6163","(6163) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us: al-Rabi' informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: \"The Muslims used to eat on the Day of Fitr before the prayer, and they did not do that on the Day of Sacrifice.\" [Very weak. Al-Shafi'i narrated it in al-Umm 1/387]"
"6164","(6164) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Ahmad ibn Sahl al-Faqih in Bukhari informed us: Salih ibn Muhammad al-Hafiz, Jazarah, narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us: 'Imran ibn Musa narrated to us: 'Uthman narrated to us: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid and Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) addressed us on the Day of Adha after the prayer and said: 'Whoever prayed our prayer and offered our sacrifice has indeed achieved the sacrifice. And whoever sacrificed before the prayer, then his sheep is merely a sheep of meat, and there is no sacrifice for him.'\"\n\nAbu Burdah ibn Niyar, the maternal uncle of al-Bara', said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I sacrificed my sheep before the prayer, for I knew that today is a day of eating and drinking, and I liked that my sheep be the first thing slaughtered in my house, so I slaughtered my sheep and had my midday meal before coming to the prayer.\" He (ﷺ) said: \"Your sheep is a sheep of meat.\" He said: \"I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a young she-goat (jadh'ah) that is dearer to me than two sheep. Will it suffice for me?\" He said: \"Yes, but it will not suffice for anyone after you.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, and likewise Muslim, except that they did not mention Abu al-Ahwas from 'Uthman. Al-Bukhari narrated it from Musaddad.\n\nAnd Muslim narrated it from Hannad and Qutaybah, all of them from Abu al-Ahwas. [Sahih. Bukhari 5236]"
"6165","(6165) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'quq narrated to us by dictation: Ahmad ibn Sahl ibn Bahr and Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib both narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: 'Ata' informed me, from Ibn 'Abbas and from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, who both said: \"There was no adhan on the Day of Fitr, nor on the Day of Adha.\" Then I asked him about that after some time, and he informed me, saying: \"Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari informed me that there is no adhan for the prayer on the Day of Fitr when the Imam goes out, nor after he goes out, no iqamah, no call, and nothing — no call that day and no iqamah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi', and al-Bukhari narrated it in abridged form from the hadith of Hisham ibn Yusuf from Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Bukhari 917]"
"6166","(6166) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sahl and Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib both narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: 'Ata' informed me that Ibn 'Abbas sent him to Ibn al-Zubayr when he was first given the pledge of allegiance: that there was no adhan for the prayer on the Day of Fitr, so do not give the adhan for it. So Ibn al-Zubayr did not give the adhan for it. And he sent to him along with that: \"The sermon is only after the prayer, and that used to be done.\" So Ibn al-Zubayr prayed before the sermon.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi', and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of Hisham from Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Bukhari 916]"
"6167","(6167) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated —\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Ismā'īl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Simāk from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: \"I prayed the 'Īd prayer with the Prophet ﷺ more than once or twice, without an adhān or an iqāmah.\"\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1148]"
"6168","(6168) Abū 'Amr Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed us, al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Jarjarā'ī informed us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from al-Awzā'ī from Nāfi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: \"The Prophet ﷺ would proceed to the muṣallā on the day of 'Īd with a short spear ('anazah) carried before him. When he reached the muṣallā, the 'anazah would be planted before him and he would pray toward it.\"\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 930]"
"6169","(6169) Abū 'Amr informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed us, Ibn Abī Ḥassān narrated to us, Duḥaym narrated to us, Shu'ayb ibn Isḥāq narrated from al-Awzā'ī from Nāfi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: \"The Prophet ﷺ, whenever he went out to the muṣallā on the day of al-Aḍḥā or al-Fiṭr, a short spear ('anazah) would be brought before him until it was planted at the muṣallā, and he would pray toward it. That was because the muṣallā was an open space with no built structure to serve as a screen. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would command that the 'anazah be planted before him and he would pray toward it.\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
"6170","(6170) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn 'Abd Allāh informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us, my father narrated to us, 'Ubayd Allāh narrated from Nāfi' from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he went out on the day of 'Īd, would command that a spear (ḥarbah) be placed before him, and he would pray toward it with the people behind him. He would also do this while travelling. It is for this reason that the commanders adopted the practice of carrying it.\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Numayr, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Isḥāq from 'Abd Allāh ibn Numayr.\nAnd it has been narrated to us from Makhūl that he said: \"The spear was carried with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ only because he would pray toward it.\"\nAnd it has been narrated to us from al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Muzāḥim from the Prophet ﷺ, in a mursal narration, that he forbade weapons being carried out on the day of 'Īd.\nAnd we have narrated in the Book of Ḥajj from Ibn 'Umar what indicates the same. And with Allah lies success."
"6171","(6171) Abū al-Ḥusayn 'Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Bushrān al-'Adl in Baghdād informed us, Abū Ja'far Muḥammad ibn 'Amr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us, 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Ya'lā al-Thaqafī narrated to me, 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated from his father from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would make takbīr on the two 'Īds — the day of Fiṭr and the day of Aḍḥā — seven takbīrs in the first rak'ah and five takbīrs in the second, besides the takbīrah of the ṣalāh (i.e., the takbīrat al-iḥrām).\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 1151]"
"6172","(6172) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir narrated. He said: I heard 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Ṭā'ifī narrating from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father from 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Amr ibn al-'Āṣ, who said: The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: \"The takbīr on (the day of) Fiṭr is seven (takbīrs) in the first rak'ah and five in the second, and the recitation comes after them both.\"\nAnd similarly, Ibn al-Mubārak, Wakī', Abū 'Āṣim, 'Uthmān ibn 'Umar, and Abū Nu'aym narrated it from 'Abd Allāh.\nAnd in all of this there is evidence of the error of the narration of Sulaymān ibn Ḥayyān from 'Abd Allāh al-Ṭā'ifī in this hadith, which states seven in the first and four in the second.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. See the preceding.]"
"6173","(6173) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn Ziyād narrated to us, Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us, Kathīr ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Amr ibn 'Awf narrated from his father from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would make takbīr on the two 'Īds — seven takbīrs in the first rak'ah and five takbīrs in the second — before the recitation.\nAnd similarly, 'Abd Allāh ibn Nāfi' narrated it from Kathīr.\nAbū 'Īsā al-Tirmidhī said: \"I asked Muḥammad, meaning al-Bukhārī, about this hadith, and he said: 'There is nothing in this chapter more authentic than this, and I act upon it.' He also said: 'And the hadith of 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Ṭā'ifī from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father from his grandfather in this chapter is also ṣaḥīḥ.'\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Tirmidhī 536]"
"6174","(6174) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Shāfi'ī informed us —\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us — both saying: 'Ubayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Khālid narrated to us, Ibn Lahī'ah narrated from 'Uqayl from Ibn Shihāb from 'Urwah from 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would make takbīr on the two 'Īds — seven takbīrs in the first rak'ah and five takbīrs in the second — before the recitation.\"\nAnd Qutaybah also narrated it from Ibn Lahī'ah from 'Uqayl. And Ibn Wahb narrated it from Ibn Lahī'ah from Khālid ibn Yazīd from Ibn Shihāb.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1149]"
"6175","(6175) Abū Muḥammad 'Abd Allāh ibn Yūsuf and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, among others, narrated to us. They said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us. He said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: \"Ibn Lahī'ah informed you…\" and he mentioned it with his chain: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made seven takbīrs on (the day of) Fiṭr and Aḍḥā, and five, besides the takbīrah of rukū'. Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā said: \"This is the preserved (maḥfūẓ) narration, because Ibn Wahb's hearing from Ibn Lahī'ah is early (i.e., before any confusion).\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. See the preceding.]"
"6176","(6176) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq narrated it from Ibn Lahī'ah from Khālid ibn Yazīd, who said: \"It reached us from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī.\" Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, Abū Zakariyyā narrated to us, Ibn Lahī'ah narrated, and he mentioned it with the same meaning."
"6177","(6177) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Ḥaywah ibn Shurayḥ narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated from al-Zubaydī, from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar ibn Saʿd ibn Qaraẓ, that his father and his paternal uncles informed him from their father, Saʿd ibn Qaraẓ: The Sunnah in the prayer of ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā and ʿĪd al-Fiṭr is that the Imam should make seven takbīrs in the first rakʿah before the recitation, and five takbīrs in the second rakʿah before the recitation. [Ṣaḥīgh for others. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/47]"
"6178","(6178) And Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Zuhrī narrated to us in Makkah; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to me; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd and ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar ibn Saʿd narrated to me from their fathers, from their grandfathers: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed takbīr on the two ʿĪds — seven in the first rakʿah and five in the second — and he would make the takbīrs before the recitation, and he would go walking and return walking. [Ṣaḥīgh for others. See the preceding.]"
"6179","(6179) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed me; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated to me. He said: Nāfiʿ said that Abū Hurayrah —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us among others, and they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us from Nāfiʿ, the mawlā of Ibn ʿUmar, who said: I witnessed ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā and ʿĪd al-Fiṭr with Abū Hurayrah, and he made seven takbīrs in the first rakʿah before the recitation, and five takbīrs in the second rakʿah before the recitation.\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of Mālik, and the ḥadīth of Shuʿayb carries the same meaning. In his narration he added: \"And that is the Sunnah.\" And in the beginning of his narration he added the appointment of Marwān as his deputy over al-Madīnah. [Ṣaḥīgh. Mālik 434]"
"6180","(6180) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muslim narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us; Zāʾidah narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: Ibn ʿAbbās used to make thirteen takbīrs on the two ʿĪds — seven in the first rakʿah and five in the second. This is a ṣaḥīḥ isnād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5701]"
"6181","(6181) And it has been narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān: thirteen — seven in the first rakʿah and six in the second — as though he counted the takbīr of standing (takbīrat al-qiyām).\n\nAbū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us and said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Ḥumayd, i.e., al-Ṭawīl, narrated from ʿAmmār, the mawlā of Banī Hāshim, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that he made takbīr on the ʿĪd — seven in the first rakʿah, then he recited, and five in the second rakʿah. [Ṣaḥīgh for others. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5704]"
"6182","(6182) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Thābit ibn Qays narrated to us and said: I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and he made seven takbīrs in the first rakʿah before the recitation, and five in the second rakʿah before the recitation. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6183","(6183) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ and Ibn Abī Ziyād — the meaning being close — both said: Zayd ibn Ḥubāb narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān from his father, from Makhūl, who said: Abū ʿĀʾishah, a companion of Abū Hurayrah, informed me: that Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ asked Abū Mūsā and Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān: \"How would the Messenger of Allah ﷺ make takbīr on ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā and ʿĪd al-Fiṭr?\" Abū Mūsā said: \"He would make four takbīrs, like his takbīr over the funerals.\" Ḥudhayfah said: \"He has spoken the truth.\" Abū Mūsā said: \"That is how I used to make takbīr in Baṣrah when I was among them.\"\n\nAbū ʿĀʾishah said — and I was present with Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ: \"The narrator of this ḥadīth has been contradicted in two respects: one of them regarding its being raised (marfūʿ), and the other regarding the response of Abū Mūsā.\n\nThe well-known version of this narration is that they referred the matter to Ibn Masʿūd, and Ibn Masʿūd gave them a ruling to that effect, without attributing it to the Prophet ﷺ.\n\nLikewise, Abū Isḥāq al-Sabīʿī narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mūsā — or Ibn Abī Mūsā — that Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ sent to Ibn Masʿūd, Ḥudhayfah, and Abū Mūsā and asked them about takbīr on the ʿĪd, so they referred the matter to Ibn Masʿūd, who said: 'You make four takbīrs before the recitation, then you recite. When you have finished, you make takbīr and go into rukūʿ. Then you stand in the second rakʿah and recite. When you have finished, you make four takbīrs.' ʿAbd al-Raḥmān is Ibn Thābit ibn Thawbān; Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn declared him ḍaʿīf, and he said: \"He was a righteous man.\"\n\nAl-Nuʿmān ibn al-Mundhir narrated from Makhūl, from the messenger of Abū Mūsā and Ḥudhayfah, from both of them, from the Prophet ﷺ, and he did not name the messenger, and he said: \"Other than the takbīr of opening (iftitāḥ) and the takbīr of rukūʿ.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1153]"
"6184","(6184) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Miṣʿar narrated from Maʿbad ibn Khālid from Kurays, who said: Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ arrived before ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā and sent to ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, to Abū Mūsā, and to Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, asking them about the takbīr. They cast the matter to ʿAbdullāh, and ʿAbdullāh said: \"You stand and make four takbīrs, then you recite, then you go into rukūʿ on the fifth. Then you stand and recite, then you make four takbīrs and go into rukūʿ on the fourth.\" [Ḥasan]"
"6185","(6185) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlāʾ ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Isfarāʾinī informed us with it; Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Bashr narrated to us; Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad al-Kātib narrated to us; Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Hishām al-Dastuwāʾī from Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: \"The takbīr on the two ʿĪds is five in the first rakʿah and four in the second.\"\n\nThis is a view from ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him. The musnad ḥadīth, along with the practice of the Muslims, is more deserving of being followed. And in Allah is the success. [Ḥasan for others. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5697]"
"6186","(6186) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Hishām narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, that Ibn Masʿūd, Abū Mūsā, and Ḥudhayfah — al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah came out to them before the ʿĪd and said to them: \"This ʿĪd has drawn near, so how is the takbīr in it?\" ʿAbdullāh said: \"You begin and make a takbīr with which you open the prayer, and you praise your Lord and send ṣalawāt upon the Prophet ﷺ. Then you supplicate and make takbīr and do likewise. Then you make takbīr and do likewise. Then you make takbīr and do likewise. Then you make takbīr and do likewise. Then you recite and go into rukūʿ. Then you stand and recite, and you praise your Lord and send ṣalawāt upon the Prophet ﷺ. Then you supplicate. Then you make takbīr and do likewise. Then you make takbīr and do likewise. Then you make takbīr and do likewise. Then you make takbīr and do likewise.\" This is from the statement of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, and it is mawqūf upon him. We follow him in pausing between every two takbīrs for dhikr, since no contradiction of his view has been narrated from anyone else. However, we differ from him regarding the number of takbīrs and their being placed before the recitation in both rakʿahs, based on the ḥadīth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then the practice of the people of the two Ḥarams, and the practice of the Muslims up to this day of ours. And in Allah is the success."
"6187","(6187) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Maḥmūd al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿImrān al-Akhbārī informed us in Baghdād; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Faḍl ibn al-Aswad narrated to us in al-Baṣrah; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbbās al-Nārmūsī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim narrated from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from al-Shaʿbī, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: \"The established practice (sunnah) is that one pronounces takbīr for the prayer on the two ʿĪds — seven takbīrs and five takbīrs — and that Allah be mentioned between every two takbīrs.\" [Weak]"
"6188","(6188) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh and Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-ʿAnazī both said: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Rabbih al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated from al-Zubaydī, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"When the Prophet ﷺ stood for prayer, he would raise his hands until they were level with his shoulders, then he would pronounce the takbīr while they remained in that position, and then he would bow. And when he intended to raise his back upright, he would raise them until they were level with his shoulders, then he would say, 'Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah.' Then he would prostrate and would not raise his hands during prostration. He would raise his hands with every takbīr he pronounced before bowing, until his prayer was completed.\" [Weak. Ibn al-Munkadir narrated it in al-Talkhīṣ 2/86]"
"6189","(6189) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Abū Zakariyyā narrated to us; Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Bakr ibn Suwādah: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭām (may Allah be pleased with him) used to raise his hands with every takbīr in the funeral prayer and the two ʿĪd prayers. [This chain is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). Weak]"
"6190","(6190) Al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated it from Ibn Lahīʿah, from Bakr ibn Suwādah, from Abū Zurʿah al-Lakhmī: that ʿUmar mentioned it regarding the prayer of the two ʿĪds. And it has been narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, from ʬāʾ, that he said: \"He would raise his hands with every takbīr, then pause briefly, then praise Allah and send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, then pronounce another takbīr — meaning in the ʿĪd prayer.\" Abū Bakr ibn Ibrāhīm al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us; Sufyān al-Jawharī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿAdanī narrated from Sufyān, from Ibn Jurayj, to that effect. [Sound. Ibn Abī Shaybah 11382]"
"6191","(6191) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us among the last group; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated, he said: I read to Mālik from Ḍamrah ibn Saʿīd al-Māzinī, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭām (may Allah be pleased with him) asked Abū Wāqid al-Laythī: \"What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite during al-Aḍḥā and al-Fiṭr?\" He said: \"He used to recite in both of them: 'Qāf. Wa al-Qurʾān al-Majīd' and 'Iqtarabat al-Sāʿah wa inshaqqa al-Qamar.'\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. Al-Shāfiʿī said in the narration of Ḥarmalah: \"This is established, provided that ʿUbayd Allāh met Abū Wāqid al-Laythī.\" The Shaykh said: \"This is because ʿUbayd Allāh did not reach the time of ʿUmar and his question to him. It is for this reason that al-Bukhārī refrained from including this hadith in al-Ṣaḥīḥ.\" Muslim included it because Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated it from Ḍamrah, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Abū Wāqid, who said: \"ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) asked me,\" and thus the hadith became connected through that route. [Sound. Muslim 891]"
"6192","(6192) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Qanṭarī informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; Surayj narrated to us; Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated. (Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us; Fulayḥ narrated from Ḍamrah ibn Saʿīd, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, from Abū Wāqid al-Laythī, who said: \"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭām (may Allah be pleased with him) asked me what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite on the day of ʿĪd.\" I said: \"He used to recite 'Iqtarabat al-Sāʿah' [al-Qamar: 1] and 'Qāf. Wa al-Qurʾān al-Majīd.'\" [Sound. See the preceding narration.] The wording is that of the hadith of Abū Ṣāliḥ. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm."
"6193","(6193) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Abū ʿAwwānah narrated. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd, i.e., al-Sijistānī, narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Abū ʿAwwānah narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, from Ḥabīb ibn Sālim, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite on the two ʿĪds and the day of Jumuʿah: \"Sabbihi isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā\" and \"Hal atāka ḥadīth al-Ghāshiyah.\" And if the two occasions fell on a single day, he would recite both of them. The wording is that of the hadith of Qutaybah, and al-Ṭayālisī did not mention his words: \"And if the two occasions fell on a single day\" to the end. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Sound. Muslim 878]"
"6194","(6194) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us; ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; al-Masʿūdī narrated from Maʿbad ibn Khālid, from Zayd ibn ʿUqbah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite on the two ʿĪds: 'Sabbihi isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā' and 'Hal atāka ḥadīth al-Ghāshiyah.'\" The Shaykh said: \"This is not in contradiction with the hadith of Abū Wāqid regarding differing narrations; rather, this reports a recitation that occurred on one ʿĪd, and that reports a recitation that occurred on a different ʿĪd. There were multiple ʿĪds during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, so this narrator is truthful in what he reported reciting on an ʿĪd, and the other narrator is truthful in what he reported reciting on an ʿĪd.\" Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said this in the narration of Ḥarmalah. [Sound for other than him. Aḥmad 5/14]"
"6195","(6195) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Khaṭṭāb ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"He would make those near him hear his recitation on the two ʿĪds.\" [Weak]"
"6196","(6196) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us; Kathīr ibn Shihāb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd, i.e., ibn Sābiq, narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Abī Qays narrated from Muṭarrif, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"Reciting aloud in the prayer of the two ʿĪds is from the sunnah, and going out to the open prayer ground (al-jabbānah) on the two ʿĪds is from the sunnah.\" [Weak]"
"6197","(6197) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-Walīd and Abū ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī both said, Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdī ibn Thābit from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet ﷺ went out on the day of Fiṭr and prayed two rakʿahs, having not prayed before them nor after them. Then he came to the women, and Bilāl was with him, and he commanded them to give charity. So the woman would cast her earring and her necklace. The wording of both narrations is the same.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it from various routes from Shuʿbah. Some of them said in the hadith: \"on the day of Aḍḥā or Fiṭr.\""
"6198","(6198) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally and by recitation, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us, Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated, ʿAbd al-Malik narrated from ʬāṭāʾ from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh who said: I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of ʿĪd. He began with the prayer before the sermon, without an adhān or iqāmah. Then he stood, leaning upon Bilāl, and commanded the people to fear Allah and exhorted them to obey Him, and he admonished and reminded them. Then he went, leaning upon Bilāl, until he came to the women and commanded them to fear Allah and exhorted them to obey Him, and he admonished and reminded them, and he said: \"Give charity, for indeed most of you are the fuel of Hellfire.\"\n\nA woman from among them, from the lower class of women, with darkened cheeks, stood and said: \"Why is that, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Because you curse frequently and are ungrateful to your husbands.\"\n\nSo they began to remove their earrings, necklaces, and rings, and cast them into the garment of Bilāl, giving charity therewith.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from another route from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 885]"
"6199","(6199) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Abū ʿĀṣim informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim narrated from Ṭāwūs from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: I witnessed the ʿĪd with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān—may Allah be pleased with them all. Each of them would pray it before the sermon, then deliver the sermon afterwards. He said: Then the Prophet of Allah ﷺ descended, and it is as though I can see him now gesturing with his hand to seat the men. Then he came forward, passing through them, until he came to the women, and Bilāl was with him. He said: \"O Prophet, when the believing women come to you to pledge allegiance to you…\" [al-Mumtaḥanah: 12] —the verse. Then when he finished it, he said: \"Are you upon that?\" Only one woman answered him, no one else among them: \"Yes, O Prophet of Allah.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū ʿĀṣim in abridged form, and he and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Ibn Jurayj in its full length. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 884]"
"6200","(6200) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī who said: I heard ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ say: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: I bear witness concerning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he prayed before the sermon on the day of ʿĪd, then he delivered the sermon. He perceived that the women could not hear, so he came to them and reminded them, admonished them, and commanded them to give charity, and Bilāl was with him, spreading out his garment thus. So the woman would cast her earring and whatever else.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Ibn ʿUyaynah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the hadith of Shuʿbah from Ayyūb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 98]"
"6201","(6201) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated, Abū Bakr narrated, Abū ʿAbdah and Abū Usāmah narrated. He said: And al-Baghawī informed me, Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī narrated, Abū Usāmah narrated. They both said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar—may Allah be pleased with them both—used to pray the two ʿĪds before the sermon.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm.\n\nAnd Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 920]"
"6202","(6202) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdad informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated, Ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ from his father.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ from his father from Abū Saʿīd, and from Qays ibn Muslim from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb from Abū Saʿīd who said: Marwān brought out the minbar on the day of ʿĪd and began with the sermon before the prayer. A man stood and said: \"O Marwān, you have opposed the Sunnah! You brought out the minbar on the day of ʿĪd, when it was not brought out, and you began with the sermon before the prayer, when it was not begun with.\" Abū Saʿīd said: \"Who is this?\" They said: \"So-and-so ibn so-and-so.\" Abū Saʿīd said: \"As for this one, he has fulfilled what was upon him. I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: 'Whoever sees an evil and is able to change it with his hand, let him change it with his hand. If he is not able with his hand, then with his tongue. If he is not able with his tongue, then with his heart—and that is the weakest of faith.'\"\n\nThe wording is from the hadith of Abū Muʿāwiyah. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb from Abū Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 49]"
"6203","(6203) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated, Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated, Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated, Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated, Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym narrated, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated, Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated, Dāwūd ibn Qays al-Dabbāgh narrated from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go out on the day of Aḍḥā and the day of Fiṭr and begin with the prayer. When he had prayed his prayer and said the salām, he would stand and face the people while they were sitting in their place of prayer. If he had a need regarding an expedition, he would mention it to the people, or if he had any other need, he would command them regarding it. And he would say: \"Give charity.\" And most of those who gave charity were the women. Then he would depart. This continued thus until the time of Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam. I went out, holding the hand of Marwān, until we reached the place of prayer. There, Kathīr ibn al-Ṣalt had built a minbar of clay and bricks, and Marwān was pulling his hand as though he were dragging me toward the minbar while I was pulling him toward the place of prayer. When I saw that from him, I said: \"Is the beginning with the prayer?\" He said: \"No, O Abū Saʿīd, what you know has been abandoned.\" I said: \"No, by the One in whose Hand is my soul, you will not come with anything better than what I know\"—three times. Then he departed.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the hadith of Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿIyāḍ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 913]"
"6204","(6204) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated. He said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: \"Dāwūd ibn Qays informed you that ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to him that he heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go out on the day of the two ʿĪds and pray, beginning with the two rakʿahs. Then he would say the salām and stand, facing the people with his face, and speak to them and command them to give charity. If he wished to dispatch an expedition among the people, he would mention it; otherwise he would depart.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6205","(6205) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us orally, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, and Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ all said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated, Ibn Jurayj informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim narrated from Ṭāwūs from Ibn ʿAbbās who said: I witnessed the prayer of Fiṭr with the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān—may Allah be pleased with them. Each of them would pray it before the sermon, then deliver the sermon afterwards. He said: Then the Prophet of Allah ﷺ descended, and it is as though I can see him now gesturing with his hand to seat the men. Then he came forward, passing through them, until he came to the women, and Bilāl was with him. He said: \"O Prophet, when the believing women come to you to pledge allegiance to you…\" [al-Mumtaḥanah: 12] —the verse. Then when he finished it, he said: \"Are you upon that?\" Only one woman answered him, no one else among them: \"O Prophet of Allah, yes.\" We do not know at that time who she was. He said: \"Give charity.\" So Bilāl spread out his garment. Then he said: \"Come, may my father and mother be your ransom!\" So they began to cast their earrings and rings into the garment of Bilāl.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 936]"
"6206","(6206) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated, Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated, Ibn Jurayj informed me, ʬāṭāʾ informed me from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh who said: I heard him say: The Prophet ﷺ stood on the day of Fiṭr and prayed, beginning with the prayer before the sermon. Then he delivered the sermon to the people. When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ finished, he came down and went to the women and reminded them, while he was leaning upon the hand of Bilāl, and Bilāl was spreading out his garment, and the women were casting charity into it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previously, 6189]"
"6207","(6207) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — and he mentioned it with its similar isnād.\n\nAnd he added: I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"Was it the Zakāh of the day of Fiṭr?\" He said: \"No, rather it was charity that they were giving at that time. She would throw her ring, and they would throw and throw.\" I said to ʿAṭāʾ: \"Do you think it is an obligation upon the Imam now that he should come to the women when he has finished and admonish them?\" He said: \"Yes, by my life, that is indeed an obligation upon them. And what is the matter with them that they do not do that?\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and others with this addition.\n\nThe statement of Ibn ʿAbbās and Jābir in this hadith — \"When he had finished, he descended and came to the women\" — indicates that he was upon an elevated place and then descended.\n\nAnd ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān narrated it from ʿAṭāʾ from Jābir, and he said at its beginning: \"Then he stood, leaning upon Bilāl, and commanded the fear of Allah. Then he mentioned his going to the women, but he did not mention the wording of 'descending.'\" However, in the hadith of Abū Bakrah, the Prophet ﷺ addressed them at Minā on the Day of Naḥr upon his mount. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 918]"
"6208","(6208) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Ḥibbān informed us; Ibn al-Mubārak informed us; Ibn ʿAwn informed us, from Ibn Sīrīn, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakrah, from his father, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered the sermon upon his mount on the Day of Naḥr, and I was holding — either he said its nose-rope, or he said its reins.\" He said: \"What day is this?\" And he mentioned the rest of the hadith.\n\nThey both (al-Bukhārī and Muslim) extracted it in the two Ṣaḥīḥs from the hadith of Ibn ʿAwn and others from Ibn Sīrīn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 67]"
"6209","(6209) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād informed us; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī informed us; Wakīʿ informed us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from his brother, from Abū Kāhil — Ismāʿīl said: \"And I saw Abū Kāhil\" — who said: \"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivering the sermon on the day of ʿĪd upon a she-camel with a pierced nose, and an Ethiopian was holding its nose-rope.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from Abū Jamīlah that he saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, ʿAlī, and al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah — may Allah be pleased with them — deliver the sermon on the day of ʿĪd upon his mount. And from Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī that he delivered the sermon on the day of ʿĪd upon his mount. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 4/177]"
"6210","(6210) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr informed us; Shaybān informed us, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, who said: \"I saw al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah — may Allah be pleased with him — on the day of Aḍḥā or Fiṭr, he led the people in two rakʿahs, then delivered the sermon upon a camel, and no adhān was called and no iqāmah was made.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6211","(6211) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk and Ibn Milḥān both said: ʿAmr ibn Khālid al-Ḥarrānī informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Lahīʿah informed us, from Muḥammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhājir, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir, who said: \"When the Prophet ﷺ ascended the minbar, he would give salām.\" Ibn Lahīʿah alone narrated it. [Ḥasan for other than it. See 5741]"
"6212","(6212) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ al-Naysābūrī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Marwān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Bazzār in Damascus informed us; Hishām, meaning Ibn ʿAmmār, informed us; Ḥātim, meaning Ibn Ismāʿīl, informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān informed us, from Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to sit on the minbar on the day of Jumuʿah, Fiṭr, and Aḍḥā. When the muʾadhdhin fell silent on the day of Jumuʿah, he stood and delivered the sermon, then sat, then stood and delivered the sermon, then descended and prayed.\" He thus combined, if it is preserved, between Jumuʿah and the two ʿĪds regarding the sitting. Then he reverted the narration to the account of Jumuʿah. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 11518]"
"6213","(6213) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd informed me, from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maʿbad, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, who said: \"The Sunnah is that the Imam delivers two sermons on the two ʿĪds, separating them with a sitting.\" [Very weak]"
"6214","(6214) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd ibn ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd al-Muʾadhdhin informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad, ʿAmmār ibn Ḥafṣ, and ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ informed me, from their fathers, from their grandfathers, that: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to begin with the prayer before the sermon, and he used to love to make takbīr between the intervals of the sermon.\" [Weak. Ibn Mājah 1287]"
"6215","(6215) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Sufyān informed us, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from Masrūq, who said: \"ʿAbd Allāh used to make takbīr on the two ʿĪds nine and nine, beginning with takbīr and ending with it.\" [Ḥasan for other than it. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5686]"
"6216","(6216) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān al-Ahwāzī informed us; the Qāḍī Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūd ibn Kharrāzādh informed us; Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us; Muḥriz ibn Salamah informed us; al-Dāwardī informed us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to him from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd, that he said: \"The Sunnah is the takbīr of the Imam on the day of Fiṭr and the day of Aḍḥā when he sits upon the minbar before the sermon — nine takbīrs — and seven when he stands. Then he supplicates and makes takbīr thereafter as he wishes.\"\n\nAnd others narrated it from Ibrāhīm from ʿUbayd Allāh: nine consecutively when he stands in the first, and seven consecutively when he stands in the second sermon. [Weak]"
"6217","(6217) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed him that al-Rabīʿ informed him that al-Shāfiʿī informed him that Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, who said: The Sunnah regarding the takbīr on the day of al-Aḍḥā and al-Fiṭr at the minbar before the khutbah is that the imām should begin before the khutbah, while standing on the minbar, with nine consecutive takbīrs, without separating them with any speech, then he should deliver the khutbah, then sit briefly, then stand for the second khutbah and open it with seven consecutive takbīrs, without separating them with any speech, then deliver the khutbah.\n[Very weak. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm, vol. 1, p. 397]"
"6218","(6218) And with his chain of narration, he said: al-Shāfiʿī informed me: a trustworthy person from the people of Madīnah informed me that a document was confirmed for him from Abū Hurayrah containing the takbīr of the imām in the first khutbah on the day of al-Fiṭr and al-Aḍḥā: fifty-one or fifty-three takbīrs within the sections of the khutbah, interspersed between intervals of speech.\n[Weak. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm, vol. 1, p. 397]"
"6219","(6219) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us that Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr informed us that Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr informed us that Zāʾidah narrated from Abū Janāb al-Kalbī who narrated that Yazīd ibn al-Barāʾ ibn ʀāzib narrated from al-Barāʾ ibn ʀāzib, who said: We were sitting in the muṣallā on the day of al-Aḍḥā when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us and greeted the people, then said: \"Indeed, the first rite of your day is this prayer.\" He said: Then he stepped forward and prayed two rakʿahs, then gave salām, then faced the people with his face, and he was given a bow or a staff, so he leaned upon it and praised Allah and extolled Him.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Aḥmad 4/282]"
"6220","(6220) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī informed us by dictation that Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām informed us that Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān narrated from ʀāṭāʾ from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: He witnessed the prayer with the Prophet (ﷺ) on the day of ʿĪd. He began with the prayer before the khutbah, without adhān or iqāmah. Then he stood, leaning upon Bilāl, and addressed the people, praising Allah and extolling Him, and he admonished them and reminded them. Then he went, leaning upon Bilāl, to the women and admonished them and reminded them, and said: \"Give in charity, for indeed most of you are the fuel of Jahannam.\" A woman from among the lower-ranking women, with sallow cheeks, stood up and said: \"Why, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Indeed, you frequent complaints and you are ungrateful to your husbands.\" So they began giving charity from their rings, their necklaces, and their earrings, handing them to Bilāl to give in charity.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned, 6198]"
"6221","(6221) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed me that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr informed me that my father informed me that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān narrated it with a similar meaning. Except that he said: Then he stood, leaning upon Bilāl, and enjoined taqwā of Allah, exhorted to obedience to Him, admonished the people, and reminded them. Then he went until he came to the women, and at the end of it he said: So they began giving charity from their jewelry, casting into the garment of Bilār from their earrings and their rings.\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned, 6198]"
"6222","(6222) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us in Baghdād that Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAlī al-Khaṭṭābī informed us that Mūsā ibn Isḥāq informed us that Yaḥyā al-Ḥimyānī informed us that Qays and Yaḥyā ibn Salamah narrated from Salamah ibn Kuhayl from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: Speech is disliked in four places: the two ʿĪds, the prayer for rain (al-istisqāʾ), and the day of Jumuʿah. And this is a mawqūf narration. [Weak]"
"6223","(6223) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād that Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us that Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān informed us that Saʿīd ibn Ḥammād, Abū ʿUthmān, the brother of Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād, informed us that al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā al-Sīnānī informed us that Ibn Jurayj narrated.\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us that al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us that Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān Saʿdawayh informed us that al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā al-Sīnānī narrated from Ibn Jurayj from ʀāṭāʾ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Sāʾib, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) prayed the ʿĪd prayer with them, then delivered the khutbah and said: \"Whoever wishes to stay, let him stay, and whoever wishes to leave, let him leave.\" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Saʿdawayh. And in the narration of Ibn Ḥammād, he said: I was present with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the day of ʿĪd. When he finished his prayer, he said: \"Whoever wishes to listen to the khutbah, let him listen, and whoever wishes to depart, let him depart.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1155]"
"6224","(6224) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd al-Ṣayrafī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us. He said: I heard al-ʿAbbās, meaning al-Dūrī, say: I heard Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Maʿīn, say: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Sāʾib, who narrates that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed the ʿĪd prayer with them—this is an error. It is only ʀāṭāʾ who narrates it, and al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā al-Sīnānī makes the mistake in it by saying \"from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Sāʾib.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6225","(6225) The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The correctness of what Yaḥyā said was reported to me by Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ in al-Kūfah, who said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us that Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān from Ibn Jurayj from ʀāṭāʾ, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) prayed the ʿĪd prayer with the people, then said: \"Whoever wishes to go, let him go, and whoever wishes to sit, let him sit.\" [Weak]"
"6226","(6226) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us that Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl informed us that Shuʿbah informed me that ʿAdī ibn Thābit said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Jubayr.\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us in Ṭūs that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Shawdhab al-Muqriʾ in Wāsiṭ informed us that Aḥmad ibn Sinān informed us that Wahb ibn Jarīr informed us that Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdī from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: He went out on the day of al-Fiṭr and prayed two rakʿahs—he did not pray before them or after them. Then he came to the women, and Bilāl was with him, and he enjoined them to give charity. So a woman would cast her earring and cast her necklace.\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl and others, except that in the narration of Ḥajjāj he said: \"So a woman would cast her earring.\" And Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd referred his narration to that of others. And Muslim narrated it from various routes from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned, 6197]"
"6227","(6227) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan, meaning Ibn Abī ʿĪsā, narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Abān, who is ʿAbdullāh al-Bajalī, narrated to me; Abū Bakr ibn Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar ibn Saʿd said: I went out with Ibn ʿUmar on the day of Aḍḥā or the day of Fiṭr. He walked until he reached the muṣallā — I think he said — then he sat until the imām came. He prayed and departed. Then Ibn ʿUmar departed and did not pray before it or after it. I said: \"O Ibn ʿUmar, is there no prayer before it or after it?\" He said: \"Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do.\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd narrated it in al-Muntakhab, 838.]"
"6228","(6228) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn Abī Dārim al-Ḥāfiẓ both said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Jandal ibn Wāliq narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the muṣallā, he would pray two rakʿahs.\"\n[Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ḥākim narrated it, 1/737.]"
"6229","(6229) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Qurashī, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, is there any hour of the night and day in which you would command me not to pray?\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Yes. When you have prayed Fajr, refrain from prayer until the sun rises, for it rises between the two horns of Satan. Then prayer is witnessed and accepted until the forenoon. When the forenoon has passed, refrain from prayer until the sun declines, for at that time Hell is kindled, and the intensity of heat is from the breath of Hell. When the sun has set, prayer is witnessed, attended, and accepted until you pray ʿAṣr. When you have prayed ʿAṣr, refrain from prayer until the sun sets. Then prayer is witnessed, attended, and accepted until you pray Fajr.\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6230","(6230) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us; Sulaymān al-Taymī said: I saw Anas ibn Mālik, al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Ḥasan, Jābir ibn Zayd, and Saʿīd ibn Abī al-Ḥasan praying before the imām on the day of ʿĪd.\n\nHe also narrated to us: Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated from ʿAbdullāh al-Dānāj, who said: I saw Abū Burdah praying on the day of ʿĪd before the imām.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Yaʿlā, 4193.]"
"6231","(6231) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated from Ayyūb, who said: I saw Anas ibn Mālik come on the day of ʿĪd and pray before the imām came out.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6232","(6232) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Shādal ibn ʿAlī al-Hāshimī narrated to us; Abū Marwān al-ʿUthmānī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, meaning Ibn Muḥammad al-Dārawardī, narrated from Ibn Abī Dhiʿb, from ʿAbbās ibn Sahl, that he used to see the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Aḍḥā and Fiṭr praying in the mosque two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs, and they would not return to it.\n\nAnd through this chain from Ibn Abī Dhiʿb, from ʿĪsā ibn Sahl ibn Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj al-Anṣārī, that he used to see his grandfather Rāfiʿ and his sons sitting in the mosque until the sun rose, then they would pray two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs, and then proceed to the muṣallā. Ibn Abī Dhiʿb said: \"I asked him: 'Did they return to it (to pray again)?' He said: 'I do not know.'\"\n[Ḥasan]"
"6233","(6233) And through this chain from Ibn Abī Dhiʿb, from Shuʿbah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, that he said: \"I used to lead ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās to the muṣallā while he would pray supererogatory prayers in the mosque and would not return to it.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from one who heard Ibn ʿUmar, regarding a man who prayed on the day of ʿĪd before the imām came out, before the prayer, he said: \"Indeed, Allah does not return a good deed that His servant performs for Him.\"\n[Ḍaʿīf]"
"6234","(6234) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us; my father narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn narrated from Ibn Buraydah, who said: Buraydah used to pray on the day of Fiṭr and the day of Naḥr before the imām.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6235","(6235) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Balkhī in Baghdād narrated to us; ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Muslim al-Ṭūsī, narrated to us; Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī narrated to us; ʿAwn al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah said: \"I saw my father perform wuḍūʾ on the day of ʿĪd, then he prayed four rakʿahs among his family. Then he took my hand and we went out to the muṣallā. He drew close to the imām where he could hear. When the prayer was completed, he did not pray before it or after it, from the time he left his family until he returned. Then, when he returned, he prayed four rakʿahs among his family.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib that he used to pray on the day of ʿĪd before the imām prayed.\n\nAnd from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr that he used to pray on the day of Fiṭr before the prayer and after it in the mosque.\n\nAnd from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad that he used to pray four rakʿahs before proceeding to the muṣallā.\n\nAnd from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn that he used to pray eight rakʿahs after the ʿĪd.\n\nA group disliked prayer before it and after it. Some disliked it before it but did not dislike it after it. Some disliked it in the muṣallā but did not dislike it in the mosque or in his home. The day of ʿĪd is like any other day.\n\nPrayer is permissible when the sun has risen, wherever the muṣallā may be. And with Allah lies success.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6236","(6236) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Aḥmad al-Adīb narrated to us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ismāʿīlī informed me; Abū Yaʿlā and ʿImrān said: Abū Saʿīd ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawārīrī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Sufyān; Zubayd narrated to me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā narrated from a trustworthy person, from ʿUmar, who said: \"The prayer of Aḍḥā is two rakʿahs, and of Fiṭr two rakʿahs, and of Jumuʿah two rakʿahs, and of the traveller two rakʿahs — complete, not shortened — according to the tongue of the Prophet ﷺ.\"\n\nAnd Yazīd ibn Ziyād ibn Abī al-Jaʿd narrated it from Zubayd, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, from ʿUmar.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned, 5718.]"
"6237","(6237) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūm al-Faqīh and Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Isfarāyinī narrated to us in it, both saying: Abū Sahl — Aḥmad ibn Ḥumayd narrated to us; Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad al-Kātib narrated to us; Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Hushaym narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Anas ibn Mālik, the servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: When Anas missed the Eid prayer with the Imam, he would gather his family and lead them in prayer in the same manner as the Imam's Eid prayer.\n\nIt is also narrated from Anas ibn Mālik that when he was at his residence in al-Zāwiyah and did not attend the Eid in Basra, he would gather his freedmen and his children, then command his freedman ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī ʿUtbah to lead them in two rakʿahs, as the people of the city would perform, and he would pronounce the takbīrs as they did.\n\nAl-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī said regarding a traveller upon whom Eid al-Aḍḥā falls: he should refrain from eating until the sun rises, then pray two rakʿahs, and then offer the sacrifice if he wishes.\n\nIt is narrated from ʿIkrimah that he said: The people of al-Sawād would gather on the Eid and pray two rakʿahs, just as the Imam does.\n\nMuḥammad ibn Sīrīn said: They used to consider it desirable that when a man missed the prayer on the two Eids, he should go to the prayer ground and do as the Imam does. And ʿAṭāʾ said: If the Eid prayer is missed, one should pray two rakʿahs without any takbīr in them. [Weak]"
"6238","(6238) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdad; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us; Mālik ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Ibn ʿAwn narrated from Muḥammad, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: We were commanded to bring out on the two Eids the adolescent girls and those who secluded themselves behind curtains. As for the menstruating women, they would attend the congregation of the Muslims and their supplication, but would keep away from their place of prayer.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from Ibn Abī ʿAdī from Ibn ʿAwn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 318]"
"6239","(6239) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us; Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl al-Tustarī narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded us to bring out on the two Eids the adolescent girls and those who secluded themselves behind curtains, and he commanded the menstruating women to keep away from the prayer ground of the Muslims.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from al-Ḥajabī from Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6240","(6240) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us in Naysābūr, and Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm in Baghdad. Abū Zakariyyā said: Ḥamzah ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; and Abū ʿAlī said: Abū Aḥmad Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us. ʿAbbās — meaning Ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī — narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī narrated to us; Hishāb ibn Ḥassān narrated from Ḥafṣah, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), may my father and mother be sacrificed for him, commanded us to bring out on the Day of Fiṭr and the Day of Naḥr the adolescent girls, those who secluded themselves behind curtains, and the menstruating women. As for the menstruating women, they would keep away from the prayer ground but attend the goodness and the supplication of the Muslims. She said: It was said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what if one of them does not have a jilbāb?\" He said: \"Let her sister clothe her from her jilbāb.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Ḥassān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6241","(6241) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us. Isḥāq said: \"He narrated to us,\" and Ibn Bashshār said: \"He narrated to us.\" ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — meaning al-Thaqafī — narrated from Ayyūb, from Ḥafṣah, who said: We used to prevent our adolescent girls from going out on the two Eids. Then a woman arrived and stayed at the palace of Banī Khalaf, and she narrated from her sister, whose husband had gone on twelve military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). She said: \"My sister accompanied him on six expeditions.\" She said: \"We used to treat the wounded and care for the sick.\" She said: \"I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him): 'Is there any harm upon any of us if she does not have a jilbāb that she should not go out?' He said: 'Let her companion clothe her from her jilbāb, so that she may attend the goodness and the supplication of the believers.'\" When Umm ʿAṭiyyah arrived, I asked her: \"Did you hear this from the Prophet (peace be upon him)?\" She said: \"Yes, by my father\" — and she would never mention the Prophet (peace be upon him) without saying \"by my father.\" She said: \"I heard him say: 'Let the adolescent girls, those who secluded themselves behind curtains, and the menstruating women go out, so that they may attend the goodness and the supplication of the believers. And let the menstruating women keep away from the prayer ground.'\" Ḥafṣah said: I said: \"The menstruating women?\" She said: \"Does she not attend ʿArafah and attend such and such?\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Salām from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1569]"
"6242","(6242) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us by dictation; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah and Muḥammad ibn Ḥajjāj al-Warrāq both narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū Khaythamah narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded us to bring out on the two Eids the adolescent girls, those in seclusion, and the virgins. She said: The menstruating women would go out and stand behind the people, pronouncing the takbīr with the people.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from ʿĀṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Same meaning as previously]"
"6243","(6243) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Nuʿmān, from Ṭalḥah ibn Muṣarrif, from a woman of ʿAbd al-Qays, from the sister of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"Going out is incumbent upon every woman who has a girdle (i.e., every grown woman).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Abū Yaʿlā 7152]"
"6244","(6244) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis, who said: A man asked Ibn ʿAbbās: \"Did you attend the Eid with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?\" He said: \"Yes. Were it not for my close relationship with him, I would not have attended it due to my young age.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came to the landmark near the house of Kathīr ibn al-Ṣalt and prayed, then delivered a sermon, and no adhān or iqāmah was mentioned.\" He said: \"Then he commanded giving charity.\" He said: \"The women began pointing to their ears and their necks (removing their jewelry).\" He said: \"He commanded Bilāl, so he went to them, then returned to the Prophet (peace be upon him).\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Sufyān. He said: \"And Muḥammad ibn Kathīr said.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 825]"
"6245","(6245) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī narrated to us by dictation; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Dallawayh al-Daqqāq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-Sulamī narrated to us; Salamah ibn Qays narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from Ḥajjāj, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to bring out his wives and his daughters on the two Eids.\" [Weak. Ibn Mājah 1309]"
"6246","(6246) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn Abī al-Furāt narrated to us; Ibrāhīm — meaning al-Ṣāʾigh — narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she used to adorn her brother's sons with gold.\n\nIf the narrator preserved this regarding the sons, it indicates the permissibility of this before they reach puberty.\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) used to say: Boys should be dressed in the finest that can be afforded, whether male or female, and they should wear jewelry and dye — meaning on the day of Eid.\n\nThe Shaykh said: And Mālik ibn Anas (may Allah have mercy on him) used to dislike it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6247","(6247) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sarrāj informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Ghanām ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghayyāth narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn Ḥakīm al-Awdī informed us — Sharīk reported from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥassān, who said: Ibn ʿUmar saw upon me silver anklets and said: \"You have reached maturity\" — or he said: \"You have grown older\" — \"so cast them off from yourself.\" [Ḍaʿīf (Weak)]"
"6248","(6248) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us — Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — Fulayḥ narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥārith, from Jābir, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to the ʿEid, he would return by a route other than the one by which he had gone.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad, from Abī Tumaylah — Yaḥyā ibn Wāḍiḥ — from Fulayḥ, with the same meaning. Then he said: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad followed him, from Fulayḥ.\n\nThe Shaykh said: And it has been narrated from Abī Tumaylah, from Fulayḥ, from Saʿīd, from Abī Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic) — Bukhārī, no. 943]"
"6249","(6249) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — Abū Manṣūr Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-ʿAtakī informed us — Abū Yaḥyā Zakariyyā ibn Dāwūd al-Khaffāf narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us — Abī Tumaylah Yaḥyā ibn Wāḍiḥ narrated, mentioning it with his chain of narration, and said: \"When he came to the ʿEid, he returned by a route other than the one he took.\" And it has been narrated from Yūnus, from Fulayḥ, from Saʿīd, from Abī Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic) — see the preceding hadith]"
"6250","(6250) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl narrated to us — and Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Dāwūd al-Munādī narrated to us — both saying: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad al-Mu'addib narrated to us — Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥārith, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) went out to the two ʿEids, he returned by a route other than the one he took. And Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣalt similarly narrated it from Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān, and al-Bukhārī alluded to it in some of the manuscripts. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi (Authentic on account of other chains)]"
"6251","(6251) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn ʿIṣmah al-ʿAdl narrated to us — al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣalt narrated to us — Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥārith, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out on the day of ʿEid by a certain route, he returned by another route. Al-Bukhārī said: The ḥadīth of Jābir is more authentic (aṣaḥḥ). [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi (Authentic on account of other chains) — see the preceding hadith]"
"6252","(6252) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us — Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar informed you — and Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us — Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took one route on the day of ʿEid, then returned by another route.\n\nAnd in the narration of Ibn Wahb: He used to go out to the two ʿEids by one route and return by another route. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi (Authentic on account of other chains) — Abū Dāwūd, no. 1156]"
"6253","(6253) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us — Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Yaḥyā Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd al-Khuraymī al-Dimashqī narrated to us — Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd ibn ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd, the mu'adhin of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me from his fathers, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he went out to the two ʿEids, would pass by the house of Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and by the people of the tents (aṣḥāb al-fasāṭīṭ), then he would begin with the prayer before the sermon, then depart by the other route — the route of Banī Zurayq — and slaughter his udḥiyyah at the edge of al-Ruqāq with his own hand with a knife, then proceed to the house of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and the house of Abī Hurayrah, to al-Balāṭ. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi (Authentic on account of other chains) — Ibn Mājah, no. 1298]"
"6254","(6254) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Abū Ismāʿīl al-Tirmidhī narrated to us — Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd narrated to me — Unays ibn Abī Yaḥyā narrated to me — Isḥāq ibn Sālim, the mawlā of Banī Nawfal ibn ʿAdī, narrated to me — Bakr ibn Mubashshir, who said: I used to go early with the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to the muṣallā on the day of al-Fiṭr, and we would take the bottom of Buṭḥān until we reached the muṣallā, then pray with the Prophet (ﷺ), then return to our homes.\n\nAnd Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī similarly narrated it from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam, in other than al-Jāmiʿ. [Ḥasan (Good) — Abū Dāwūd, no. 1158]"
"6255","(6255) And Ḥamzah ibn Naṣīr narrated it from Ibn Abī Maryam, and said in the hadith: \"Then we would return from the bottom of Buṭḥān to our homes.\" And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ḥamzah ibn Naṣīr narrated it with his addition. [Ḥasan (Good) — see the preceding hadith]"
"6256","(6256) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, he is al-Aṣamm, informed us — al-Rabīʿ informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Ibrāhīm, meaning Ibn Muḥammad, narrated to me — Muʿādh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Taymī narrated from his father, from his grandfather, that he saw the Prophet (ﷺ) returning from the muṣallā on the day of ʿEid, and he passed by the date-sellers (al-tammārīn) from the lower part of the market, until when he reached the Mosque of al-Aʿraj, which is near the place of the pool (al-birkah) that is by the market, he stood and faced the open plain of Aslam, then supplicated, then departed. [Ḍaʿīf jiddan (Very Weak) — al-Shāfiʿī, no. 352]"
"6257","(6257) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us; al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us; 'Isa ibn 'Abd al-A'la ibn Abi Farwah narrated to me that he heard Abu Yahya 'Ubaidullah al-Taymi narrating from Abu Hurairah: that rain fell upon them on the day of 'Eid, so the Prophet (ﷺ) led them in the 'Eid prayer in the mosque.\nAbu Dawud narrated it in the Sunan from al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, and he narrated it from Hisham ibn 'Ammar from al-Walid from a man of the Farwiyyin. [Munkar. Abu Dawud 1160]"
"6258","(6258) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us; al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us; ibn Kasib narrated to us; Salamah ibn Raja' narrated to us; from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abd al-Rahman; from 'Uthman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Taymi who said: We were rained upon during the governorship of Aban ibn 'Uthman over al-Madinah, a severe rain on the night of al-Fitr. He gathered the people in the mosque and did not go out to the musalla where al-Fitr and al-Adha are prayed. Then he said to 'Abdullah ibn 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah: \"Stand and inform the people what you have informed me.\" So 'Abdullah ibn 'Amir said: The people were rained upon during the era of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and the people abstained from the musalla. So 'Umar gathered the people in the mosque and led them in prayer. Then he stood upon the minbar and said: \"O people, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to go out with the people to the musalla to lead them in prayer, because it was more convenient for them and more spacious for them, and the mosque could not accommodate them.\" He said: \"So when there is this rain, the mosque is more convenient.\" [Very weak]"
"6259","(6259) 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-Hasan ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Husayn ibn Mansur narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sulayman narrated to us; 'Asim ibn 'Ali narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us; from Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man who said: I heard Abu Qays narrating from Huzail: that 'Ali commanded a man to pray with the weak people in the mosque on the day of Fitr or the day of Adha, and he commanded him to pray four rak'ahs.\nAl-Thawri narrated it from Abu Qays. And it is possible that he meant two rak'ahs as tahiyyat al-masjid and then two rak'ahs of the 'Eid prayer, separate from them. [Hasan due to other supporting narrations. Ibn Abi Shaybah 5815]"
"6260","(6260) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Rabi' said: al-Shafi'i said, from Ibn 'Ulayyah, from al-Layth, from al-Hakam, from Hanish ibn al-Mu'tamir: that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"Pray on the day of 'Eid in the mosque four rak'ahs: two rak'ahs for the Sunnah and two rak'ahs for the going out.\"\nHe said: And al-Shafi'i said, reporting from Ibn Mahdi, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq: that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) commanded a man to pray with the weak people on the day of 'Eid in the mosque two rak'ahs. And similarly Bundar narrated it from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, except that he said: from Abu Ishaq, from some of his companions, that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him).\n[Weak. Narrated by al-Shafi'i in al-Umm, vol. 7, p. 295]"
"6261","(6261) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; Abu al-Husayn Ahmad ibn 'Uthman ibn Ja'far al-Muqri' narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Warraq narrated to us; Abu Ghassan and Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, and the wording is that of Abu Ghassan; Zuhair narrated to us; Abu Ishaq narrated to us; from al-Harith al-A'war; from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: \"It is from the Sunnah that a man walk to the musalla.\" He said: \"And going out on the two days of 'Eid is from the Sunnah, and no one goes out to the mosque except the weak or the sick.\" Mu'awiyah added: \"But go out to the musalla and do not prevent the women.\" [Weak. See 6146]"
"6262","(6262) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us; Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir narrated to us; from al-Sha'bi; from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) addressed us on the day of al-Nahr after the prayer and said: \"Whoever prayed our prayer and offered our sacrifice, he has indeed achieved the sacrifice. And whoever sacrificed before the prayer, that is merely a sheep of meat.\" So Abu Burdah ibn Niyar stood and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I sacrificed before I came out to the prayer, and I knew that today is a day of eating and drinking, so I hastened and fed myself, my family, and my neighbors.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"That is a sheep of meat.\" He said: \"I have a young she-goat (jadh'ah) that is better than two sheep of meat. Will it suffice for me?\" He said: \"Yes, but it will not suffice for anyone after you.\"\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Hannad and Qutaibah from Abu al-Ahwas. [Sahih. Bukhari 5236]"
"6263","(6263) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us; from Zubaid; from al-Sha'bi; from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out to us on the day of Adha to al-Baqi'. He stood and prayed two rak'ahs, then he turned to us with his face and said: \"The first of our rites on this day is that we begin with the prayer, then we return and offer the sacrifice. Whoever does that has conformed to our Sunnah, and whoever slaughtered before that, it is merely meat that he hastened for his family; it is not part of the sacrifice at all.\" So my uncle stood and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I have sacrificed, and I have a jadh'ah that is better than a musinnah.\" He said: \"Slaughter it, but no jadh'ah will suffice after you.\" Zubaid said: I heard some of our companions say that he said: \"a young she-goat (jadh'ah 'anaq).\"\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Nu'aim from Muhammad ibn Talhah, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Shu'bah from Zubaid. [Sahih. See the previous]"
"6264","(6264) Ibn Furk informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us; from al-Aswad who heard Jundab saying: I witnessed the Prophet (ﷺ) delivering a sermon on the day of Adha, and he said: \"Whoever among you has slaughtered before the prayer, let him replace his slaughtered animal with another, and whoever has not slaughtered, let him slaughter in the name of Allah.\"\nThey both (al-Bukhari and Muslim) narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Shu'bah. [Sahih. Bukhari 942]"
"6265","(6265) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us; Abu 'Asim informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us.\n(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, and the wording is from his hadith; Abu Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-'Adl narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who is Ibn Khuzaymah, narrated to us; 'Ali ibn Khashram informed us; 'Isa ibn Yunus informed us; from Ibn Jurayj; 'Ata' informed me; from Ibn 'Abbas: that the Prophet (ﷺ) had al-Fadl ride behind him from al-Muzdalifah. Ibn 'Abbas informed me that al-Fadl informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) continued saying the Talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah.\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Abu 'Asim, and Muslim narrated it from 'Ali ibn Khashram. [Sahih. Bukhari 1469]"
"6266","(6266) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Walid informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numair narrated to us; Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah narrated to us; from Khalid al-Hadha' who said: Abu Qilabah narrated to me; from Abu al-Malih; from Nabishah who said: Khalid said: I met Abu al-Malih and asked him, and he narrated it to me, and he mentioned from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"The days of al-Tashriq are days of eating, drinking, and remembrance of Allah.\"\nMuslim narrated it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numair. [Sahih. Muslim 1141]"
"6267","(6267) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, he said: Abū ʿUbayd said, and Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to me from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to make takbīr in his tent at Minā, and the people of the mosque would hear him and make takbīr, and the people of the market would hear them and make takbīr, until Minā would reverberate with takbīr.\n\nAnd it is narrated from Ibn ʿUmar that he used to make takbīr at Minā during those days, after the prayers, on his bed, in his tent, in his gathering, and while walking — throughout all of those days. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6268","(6268) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us from Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Marwazī, meaning Muḥammad ibn Naṣr, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us from Wakīʿ, from al-ʿUmarī, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he used to make takbīr from the Ẓuhr prayer on the Day of Naḥr (Yawm al-Naḥr) until the Fajr prayer on the last day of the Days of Tashrīq. [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5640]"
"6269","(6269) Abū ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Abū Bakr narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us from Abū ʿAbd Allāh, he said: Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us from Wakīʿ, from Sharīk, from Khuṣayf, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that he used to make takbīr from the Ẓuhr prayer on the Day of Naḥr until the ʿAṣr prayer on the last day of the Days of Tashrīq. [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5639]"
"6270","(6270) Abū al-Fatḥ al-Sharīf narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Firās narrated to us, Abū Jaʿfar al-Daybulī narrated to us, Abū ʿUbayd Allāh al-Makhzūmī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, he said: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās making takbīr on the day of Ṣadr (departure) and commanding those around him to make takbīr. I do not know whether he interpreted the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, {And remember Allah during the appointed days} [al-Baqarah: 203], or His words {And when you have completed your rites} [al-Baqarah: 200].\n\nThe Shaykh said: ʿAbd al-Ḥumayd ibn Abī Rabbāḥ narrated from a man from the people of Shām, from Zayd ibn Thābit: that he used to make takbīr from the Ẓuhr prayer on the Day of Naḥr until the last day of the Days of Tashrīq.\n\nAl-Wāqidī narrated with his chains from ʿUthmān, Ibn ʿUmar, Zayd ibn Thābit, and Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī something similar to what we narrated from Ibn ʿUmar. And we narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ that he said: The imāms used to make takbīr beginning with the Ẓuhr prayer on the Day of Naḥr and continuing until the last day of the Days of Tashrīq. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6271","(6271) By way of evidence, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us among others, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Mālik narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Mālik narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, he said: I read before Mālik from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Thaqafī: that he asked Anas ibn Mālik while they were both proceeding from Minā to ʿArafah: \"How did you used to do on this day with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?\" He said: \"Whoever among us made talbiyah, it was not objected to, and whoever among us made takbīr, it was not objected to.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī extracted it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf and others from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1576]"
"6272","(6272) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us by dictation, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī and al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram al-Bazzāz narrated to us, they said: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Shaybānī narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Salamah narrated to us from ʿUmar ibn Ḥusayn from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Salamah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from his father, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the morning of ʿArafah. Among us were those who made takbīr and those who made talbiyah. As for us, we made takbīr. He said: I said, \"By Allah, it is astonishing of you — why did you not ask him what you saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) doing?\"\n\nThe wording is from the ḥadīth of Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq from Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Shaybānī.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh and others from Yazīd. And it has been narrated regarding this from ʿUmar, ʿAlī, and Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them). [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1576]"
"6273","(6273) As for the ḥadīth of ʿUmar: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to me, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us from al-Ḥajjāj, he said: I heard ʿAṭāʾ narrating from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) used to make takbīr after the Fajr prayer on the day of ʿArafah until the Ẓuhr prayer on the last day of the Days of Tashrīq.\n\nThus narrated al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh from ʿAṭāʾ.\n\nYaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān used to reject it. Abū ʿUbayd al-Qāsim ibn Sallām said: I mentioned it to Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and he rejected it and said, \"This is an error from al-Ḥajjāj. The chain from ʿUmar is only that he used to make takbīr in his tent at Minā.\"\n\nThe Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It is well-known from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ that he used to make takbīr from the Ẓuhr prayer on the Day of Naḥr until the ʿAṣr prayer on the last day of the Days of Tashrīq. Had ʿAṭāʾ possessed this narration from ʿUmar that al-Ḥajjāj narrated from him, he would not have permitted himself to oppose ʿUmar. And Allah knows best.\n\nIt has also been narrated from Abū Isḥāq al-Sabīʿī that he reported it from ʿUmar and ʿAlī, but it is mursal. [Ḍaʿīf. al-Ḥākim 1/439]"
"6274","(6274) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Balkhī narrated to us in Baghdād, ʿAlī ibn Muslim al-Ṭūsī narrated to us, Abū Yūsuf, meaning al-Qāḍī, narrated to us, Muṭarrif ibn Ṭarīf narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq, he said: ʿUmar, ʿAlī, and Ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with them) agreed upon making takbīr after the morning prayer (ṣalāt al-ghadāh) on the day of ʿArafah. As for the companions of Ibn Masʿūd, they continued until the ʿAṣr prayer on the Day of Naḥr. As for ʿUmar and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both), they continued until the ʿAṣr prayer on the last day of the Days of Tashrīq.\n\nThe Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: As for the position of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd regarding this, al-Thawrī narrated it from Abū Isḥāq from al-Aswad from ʿAbd Allāh, connected (mawṣūl). And a group narrated it from Ibn Masʿūd.\n\nAs for the connected narration regarding it from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6275","(6275) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, Hannād narrated to us, Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī narrated to us from Zāʾidah, from ʿĀṣim, from Shaqīq, he said: ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to make takbīr after the Fajr prayer on the morning of ʿArafah, and he would not cease until the imām prayed on the last day of the Days of Tashrīq. Then he would make takbīr after ʿAṣr.\n\nAbū Janāb similarly narrated it from ʿUmayr ibn Saʿīd from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ḥasan. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5632]"
"6276","(6276) As for the narration regarding it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Ḥakam ibn Farrūkh narrated to us from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that he used to make takbīr from the morning of ʿArafah until the ʿAṣr prayer on the last day of the Days of Tashrīq.\n\nAnd Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zuhayr al-Qaysī narrated to us in Ṭūs, ʿAlī ibn Salamah, meaning al-Labqī, narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Abū Yaʿqūb al-Khurāsānī, meaning Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥanẓalī, narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān from al-Ḥakam, and he mentioned it similarly and added: \"He would make takbīr during ʿAṣr and cease during Maghrib.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5632]"
"6277","(6277) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us that Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd reported from Abū Bakkār al-Ḥakam ibn Farrūkh, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he used to pronounce the takbīr from the morning of the Day of ʿArafah until the last of the days of Tashrīq. Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ said: \"I met Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and said: 'Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to me from you, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd,' and I mentioned this hadith to him, and he narrated it to me just as Yaḥyā ibn Ādam had narrated it to me.\" Abū al-ʿAbbās said: \"I came to Isḥāq and said: 'Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to me from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam from you,' and he narrated it to me just as Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ had narrated it to me.\" Abū al-ʿAbbās said: \"I said to Isḥāq: 'How many hadiths did Yaḥyā ibn Ādam write from you?' Isḥāq said: 'Approximately two thousand hadiths.'\" And this has been narrated in a marfūʿ hadith with a chain by which one does not argue. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See before it.]"
"6278","(6278) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us that Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb al-Maqābirī narrated to us that Ḥassān ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muhṣir reported from ʿAmr ibn Shamir, from Jābir, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sābit, from Jābir, who said: \"The Prophet ﷺ used to pronounce the takbīr on the Day of ʿArafah from the morning prayer until the ʿAṣr prayer on the last of the days of Tashrīq.\" Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb said: \"And ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muhṣir narrated to me with this chain something similar.\" ʿAmr ibn Shamir and Jābir al-Juʿfī — one does not argue by them. And Nāʾil ibn Najīḥ narrated it from ʿAmr, from Jābir, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sābit and from Abū Jaʿfar, from Jābir. And in the narration of the trustworthy narrators there is sufficiency. [Munkar. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/49.]"
"6279","(6279) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Wuhaib ibn Khālid narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib reported from his father, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that he said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ resided in Madīnah for nine years without performing Hajj, then he announced to the people regarding Hajj.\" Then he mentioned the hadith concerning the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: \"Then he went out to al-Ṣafā and said: 'We begin with that which Allah began with.' And he recited: 'Indeed, al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah are among the symbols of Allah.' Then he ascended al-Ṣafā until the House became visible to him, and he pronounced the takbīr three times and said: 'Lā ilāha illā Allāh waḥdahu lā sharīka lahu, lahu al-mulku wa lahu al-ḥamdu yuḥyī wa yumītu biyadihi al-khayru wa huwa ʿalā kulli shayʾin qadīr.' Then he supplicated between that. Then he descended and walked until he reached the bottom of the masīl (valley), and he ran until he raised his feet in the masīl. Then he walked until he came to al-Marwah and ascended it until the House became visible to him, and he pronounced the takbīr three times and said: 'Lā ilāha illā Allāh waḥdahu lā sharīka lahu' — just as he had done, meaning, on al-Ṣafā. Then he descended.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us in the hadith of Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he mounted his camel setting out for a journey, would pronounce the takbīr three times. And from Ibn ʿUmar: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he returned from a military expedition or Hajj or ʿUmrah, would pronounce the takbīr three times upon every elevated place.\n\nThus, beginning with three takbīrs in succession is more consistent with the rest of the Sunnahs of the Prophet ﷺ than beginning with two of them, even though all of it is permissible. And with Allah is the success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1218.]"
"6280","(6280) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that Bundār narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd reported from al-Ḥakam, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: \"He would pronounce the takbīr from the morning of the Day of ʿArafah until the last of the days of Nafir, and he would not pronounce the takbīr in the Maghrib prayer: 'Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar wa lillāhi al-ḥamd, Allāhu Akbar wa ajall, Allāhu ʿalā mā hadānā.'\"\n\nThus he informed us from his book — three in succession. And al-Wāqidī narrated it from him and from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī said the same. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5646.]"
"6281","(6281) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us that Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us that Wahb, meaning Ibn Jarīr, narrated to us that Shuʿbah reported from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, regarding the takbīr on the days of Tashrīq: \"Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rābāḥ that he said: \"He would pronounce the takbīr three times.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 5646.]"
"6282","(6282) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us that Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmar reported from ʿĀṣim ibn Sulaymān, from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī, who said: \"Salmān, may Allah be pleased with him, used to teach us the takbīr, saying: 'Say the takbīr: Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar kabīran' — or he said 'takbīran' — 'Allāhumma anta aʿlā wa ajall an takūna laka ṣāḥibah aw yakūna laka walad aw yakūna laka sharīkun fī al-mulk aw yakūna laka waliyyun min al-dhull wa kabbirhu takbīran. Allāghfir lanā. Allāḥrumnā.' Then he said: 'By Allah, this will surely be written; these two will not be abandoned, and these two will be made a complement to those two.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 20581.]"
"6283","(6283) Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī narrated to us that Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad narrated to us that Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that Hushaym reported from Abū Bishr, who said: \"Abū ʿUmayr ibn Anas ibn Mālik informed me\" — and he was the eldest of his children — \"he said: 'My paternal uncles from the Anṣār, from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, informed me, saying: \"The new moon of Shawwāl was obscured from us, so we began the day fasting. Then a caravan arrived in the latter part of the day and testified before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that they had seen the new moon the previous evening. So he commanded them to break their fast that day and to go out for their ʿĪd the following morning.\"'\" This is a ṣaḥīḥ chain.\n\nAnd Shuʿbah narrated something to that effect from Abū Bishr Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ḥayshiyyah. And the paternal uncles of Abū ʿUmayr from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would only be trustworthy narrators.\n\nAnd al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: \"If this is established, we would rule by it.\" And he also said: \"If they do not go out for it the following day, they go out for it the day after that.\" And he said: \"He may pray it on the same day after the sun has passed its zenith.\" And this is what Abū Saʿīd narrated to us from Abū al-ʿAbbās, from al-Rabīʿ, from al-Shāfiʿī."
"6284","(6284) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us that Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Kawthar narrated to us that Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us that al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us that Maʿn ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Hilāl al-Tammār reported that testimony regarding the new moon of Fiṭr was given before ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz in the latter part of the day, so he commanded the people to break their fast and to go out for their ʿĪd the following morning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1157.]"
"6285","(6285) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that Ḥammād, in the hadith of Ayyūb, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Abū Hurayrah, mentioned the Prophet ﷺ therein, and he said: \"Your fast-breaking is on the day you break your fast, and your sacrifice is on the day you sacrifice. Every ʿArafah is a place of standing, and every Minā is a place of sacrifice, and every open road of Makkah is a place of sacrifice, and every Jamʿ (Muzdalifah) is a place of standing.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it. Abū Dāwūd 2324.]"
"6286","(6286) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us that Ibn Durustawayh narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us that Isrāʾīl — (ḥ) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us that Isrāʾīl reported from ʿUthmān ibn al-Mughīrah, from Iyās ibn Abī Ramlah al-Shāmī, who said: \"I witnessed Muʿāwiyah asking Zayd ibn Arqam: 'Did you witness with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two ʿĪds that coincided in one day?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'How did he handle it?' He said: 'He prayed the ʿĪd, then gave a concession regarding the Jumuʿah, saying: \"Whoever wishes to pray it, let him pray.\"'\" And in the narration of ʿUbayd Allāh: \"I heard Muʿāwiyah,\" and he said: \"in one day,\" and the rest is the same. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it. Abū Dāwūd 1070.]"
"6287","(6287) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdīy, the ḥāfiẓ, informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Samīnah narrated to us; Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: Two ʿĪds coincided during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"Indeed, your ʿĪd and Jumuʿah have coincided, and we shall perform Jumuʿah. So whoever wishes to combine [both prayers], let him do so.\" When he had prayed the ʿĪd prayer, he performed Jumuʿah. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 1073]"
"6288","(6288) And Abū Saʿd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī ʿUthmān al-Zāhid, by dictation; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Bundār ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Mūsā al-Ahwāzī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muṣaffá narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh, the ḥāfiẓ, informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, the ḥāfiẓ, informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muṣaffá narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from al-Mughīrah ibn Miqsam al-Ḍabbī, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: \"Two ʿĪds have coincided on this day of yours. So whoever wishes, it [the ʿĪd prayer] shall suffice him for Jumuʿah, and we shall perform Jumuʿah.\"\n\nIt was also narrated by ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Munīb al-Marwazī from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq; Abū Ḥamzah narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, connected [mawṣūl], and it is in the historical works. And Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz but sent it [as a mursal report]. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain]"
"6289","(6289) Abū ʿAbd Allāh, the ḥāfiẓ, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, from Dhakwān Abū Ṣāliḥ, who said: Two ʿĪds coincided during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—a day of Jumuʿah and a day of ʿĪd. He prayed, then stood and addressed the people, saying: \"You have attained remembrance and goodness, and we shall perform Jumuʿah. So whoever wishes to sit, let him sit, and whoever wishes to combine [both], let him do so.\"\n\nIt was also narrated from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, connected [mawṣūl], restricted to the people of al-ʿĀliyah, but its chain contains weakness.\n\nThat was also narrated from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz from the Prophet ﷺ, restricted to the people of al-ʿĀliyah, except that it is disconnected [munqaṭiʿ]. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain]"
"6290","(6290) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakkī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah narrated to me from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, who said: Two ʿĪds coincided during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"Whoever among the people of al-ʿĀliyah wishes to sit [out Jumuʿah], let him sit, without any sin.\"\n\nThat was also narrated through a sound chain from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, restricted to the people of al-ʿĀliyah, as a statement attributed to him [mawqūf]. [Munkar. al-Shāfiʿī 343]"
"6291","(6291) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik ibn Anas narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū ʿUbayd, the freedman of Ibn Azhar, who said: I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him. He came and prayed, then turned and addressed the people, saying: \"Indeed, two ʿĪds have coincided for you on this day. So whoever among the people of al-ʿĀliyah wishes to wait for Jumuʿah, let him wait for it, and whoever wishes to return, let him return. I have given him permission.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Shāfiʿī 344]"
"6292","(6292) And Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me; al-Ḥasan, meaning Ibn Sufyān, narrated to us; Ḥibbān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Yūnus narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: Abū ʿUbayd, the freedman of Ibn Azhar, narrated to me: He witnessed the ʿĪd on the day of al-Aḍḥá with ʿUmar. He prayed before the sermon, then delivered the sermon and said: \"O people, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has forbidden you from fasting on these two days of ʿĪd. As for one of them, it is the day of your breaking of the fast from your fasting, and as for the other, it is the day on which you eat from your sacrifices.\"\n\nAbū ʿUbayd said: Then I witnessed it with ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, and that was on the day of Jumuʿah. He prayed before the sermon, then delivered the sermon and said: \"O people, this is a day on which two ʿĪds have coincided for you. So whoever among the people of al-ʿAwālī wishes to wait for Jumuʿah, let him wait, and whoever wishes to return, let him return. I have given him permission.\"\n\nAbū ʿUbayd said: Then I witnessed it with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him. He prayed before the sermon, then addressed the people and said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has forbidden you from eating the meat of your sacrifices beyond three days.\"\n\nAnd from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū ʿUbayd, something similar to it.\n\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥibbān ibn Mūsā in its entirety. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5251]"
"6293","(6293) Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to me, who said: Thawr ibn Zayd said, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from Abū al-Dardāʾ, who said: Whoever stands [in prayer] on the two nights of ʿĪd for Allah, seeking reward, his heart shall not die when the hearts die.\n\nal-Shāfiʿī said: And it reached us that it used to be said: Supplication is answered on five nights: the night of Jumuʿah, the night of al-Aḍḥá, the night of al-Fiṭr, the first night of Rajab, and the night of the middle of Shaʿbān.\n\nHe said: And it reached us that Ibn ʿUmar used to spend in devotion the night of Jamʿ—and the night of Jamʿ is the night of ʿĪd, because sacrifice takes place on its morning. [Very weak. The author narrated it in al-Shuʿab 1/3711]"
"6294","(6294) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū ʿAlī Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj al-Muqrīʾ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāmī narrated to us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated from Thawr ibn Zayd, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, who said: I met Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ on the day of ʿĪd and I said: \"May Allah accept from us and from you.\" He said: \"Yes, may Allah accept from us and you.\" Wāthilah said: I met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of ʿĪd and I said: \"May Allah accept from us and from you.\" He said: \"Yes, may Allah accept from us and you.\" [Munkar. Ibn Ḥibbān in al-Ḥujr and Ḥayth 2/302]"
"6295","(6295) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdīy, the ḥāfiẓ, informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿĀṣim narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāmī narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated from Thawr ibn Zayd, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from Wāthilah, who said: I met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of ʿĪd and I said: \"May Allah accept from us and from you.\" He said: \"Yes, may Allah accept from us and you.\"\n\nAbū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, and he said: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdīy, the ḥāfiẓ, said: This is munkar; I do not know anyone who narrated it from Baqiyyah other than this Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm.\n\nThe shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: I have seen it through another chain from Baqiyyah, as a statement of a Companion [mawqūf], not raised [marfūʿ], and I do not consider it preserved. [Munkar. See the preceding]"
"6296","(6296) Abū ʿAbd Allāh, the ḥāfiẓ, informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Salām al-Bazzāz narrated from Adham, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, who said: We used to say to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz on the two ʿĪds: \"May Allah accept from us and from you, O Commander of the Faithful.\" He would respond to us and would not disapprove of that. And a hadith has been narrated, raised [marfūʿ], regarding the dislike of that, but it is not sound. [Weak. Ibn ʿAsākir narrated it in his Tārīkh 6/436]"
"6297","(6297) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Haytham ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn Zayd ibn Wāqid al-Dimashqī narrated from his father, from Makḥūl, from ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmiṭ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding the people's saying on the two Eids, \"Qabbal Allāhu minnā wa minkum\" (May Allah accept from us and from you). He said: \"That is the practice of the People of the Book.\" And he (peace and blessings be upon him) disapproved of it.\n\nʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn Zayd is munkar al-ḥadīth, as stated by al-Bukhārī."
"6298","(6298) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us among the last group. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, from Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, who said: The solar eclipse occurred on the day Ibrāhīm, the son of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), died. The people said: \"The solar eclipse has occurred due to the death of Ibrāhīm.\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. They do not eclipse due to the death of anyone nor due to his life. When you see that, then hasten to the remembrance of Allah and to prayer.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar from Sufyān, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Ismāʿīl."
"6299","(6299) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Jarīr and Wakīʿ both said: Ismāʿīl narrated to us, from Qays, from Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The people said: \"It has eclipsed due to the death of Ibrāhīm.\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon do not eclipse due to the death of anyone nor due to his life. Rather, they are two signs among the signs of Allah. When you see them, then pray.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. It was also narrated by ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, ʿĀʾishah, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, and Abū Bakrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) with a similar meaning."
"6300","(6300) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī and Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Khushaysh al-Muqrīʾ in Kūfah both said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Shaybān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both): When the solar eclipse occurred during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), an announcement was made that prayer would be performed in congregation. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) performed two bowings (rukūʿ) in one rakʿah, then stood and performed two bowings in one rakʿah, then sat. Then the sun was cleared.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shaybān."
"6301","(6301) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from al-Awzāʿī, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He sent a caller who announced: \"Prayer in congregation.\" The people gathered, and he (peace and blessings be upon him) led them in two rakʿahs with four bowings and four prostrations. Then he pronounced the tashahhud and gave the salām.\n\nBoth al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Mihrān from al-Walīd ibn Muslim."
"6302","(6302) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī and others informed us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Muqrīʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us in Baghdad; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Maymūn narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated by dictation, from what he read upon Mālik ibn Anas, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He prayed and the people prayed with him. He stood for a long standing, approximately the length of Sūrat al-Baqarah. Then he performed a long bowing. Then he raised his head and stood for a long standing, though shorter than the first standing. Then he performed a long bowing, though shorter than the first bowing. Then he prostrated. Then he stood for a long standing, though shorter than the first standing. Then he performed a long bowing, though shorter than the first bowing. Then he raised his head and stood for a long standing, though shorter than the first standing. Then he performed a long bowing, though shorter than the first bowing. Then he prostrated. Then he concluded the prayer, and the sun had cleared.\n\nHe (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. They do not eclipse due to the death of anyone nor due to his life. When you see that, then remember Allah.\"\n\nThey said: \"O Messenger of Allah, we saw you reaching for something in your place, then we saw you stepping back.\" He said: \"I saw Paradise — or I was shown Paradise — and I reached for a cluster of grapes from it. Had I taken it, you would have eaten from it as long as the world remained. And I was shown Hellfire, and I have never seen a more terrifying sight than it. And I saw that most of its inhabitants are women.\"\n\nThey said: \"Why, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Because of their ingratitude (kufr).\" It was asked: \"Do they show ingratitude to Allah?\" He said: \"They show ingratitude to their husbands and are ungrateful for kindness. If you were to treat one of them well for an entire lifetime, then she saw something (displeasing) from you, she would say: 'I have never seen any good from you at all.'\"\n\nThe wording is that of al-Qaʿnabī. In the narration of al-Shāfiʿī, it says: \"The solar eclipse occurred, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed and the people prayed with him.\" Similarly in the narration of Isḥāq from al-Qaʿnabī. Al-Shāfiʿī did not mention his words: \"a more terrifying sight than it.\" And the rest is the same.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from Isḥāq ibn ʿĪsā from Mālik."
"6303","(6303) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and others informed us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated. He said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you from Ibn Shihāb. He said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) went to the mosque, stood, and pronounced the takbīr. The people lined up behind him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) recited a lengthy recitation. Then he pronounced the takbīr and performed a long bowing. Then he raised his head and said: \"Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah, Rabbaka wa laka al-ḥamd\" (Allah hears those who praise Him, our Lord, to You is the praise). Then he stood and recited a lengthy recitation, though shorter than the first recitation. Then he pronounced the takbīr and performed a long bowing, though shorter than the first bowing. Then he said: \"Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah, Rabbaka wa laka al-ḥamd.\" Then he did the same in the second rakʿah. Thus he completed four bowings and four prostrations. The sun cleared before he concluded the prayer. Then he stood and addressed the people. He praised Allah as is His due, then said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. They do not eclipse due to the death of anyone nor due to his life. When you see them, then hasten to prayer.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Ṭāhir and others from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAnbasah from Yūnus ibn Yazīd."
"6304","(6304) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; ʿAnbasah narrated to us; Yūnus narrated from Ibn Shihāb. He said: Kathīr ibn ʿAbbās used to narrate that Ibn ʿAbbās used to narrate: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed during the solar eclipse in the same manner as the narration of ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): that he prayed two rakʿahs, performing two bowings in each rakʿah."
"6305","(6305) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us; al-Qāsim — he is ibn Zakariyyā — narrated to us; al-Ramādī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated with this (chain), and he added: I said to ʿUrwah: \"Indeed, your brother, when the solar eclipse occurred in Madīnah, did not exceed two rakʿahs, like the morning prayer.\" He said: \"Yes, indeed he missed the Sunnah.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ in its entirety along with these two additions."
"6306","(6306) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Mihrān narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Namir narrated: He heard Ibn Shihāb narrating that from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah.\n\nAl-Zuhrī said: I said to ʿUrwah: \"What did your brother ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr do? He prayed only two rakʿahs, like the morning prayer, when he prayed in Madīnah.\" He said: \"Yes, indeed he missed the Sunnah.\"\n\nIbn Shihāb said: Kathīr ibn ʿAbbās informed me from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed four bowings in two rakʿahs, with four prostrations.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Mihrān without the ḥadīth of Kathīr, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Mihrān with the ḥadīth of Kathīr, without the account of Ibn al-Zubayr."
"6307","(6307) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd informed me; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Yaḥyá ibn Yaḥyá informed us; Abū Muʿāwiya informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyá ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Hannād ibn al-Sarī al-Tamīmī informed us; Abū Muʿāwiya informed us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, who said:\n\n\"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to pray. He prolonged the standing (qiyām), then he bowed (rakʿah) and prolonged the bowing. Then he raised his head and prolonged the standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed and prolonged the bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he prostrated. Then he stood and prolonged the standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed and prolonged the bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he raised his head and prolonged the standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed and prolonged the bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he prostrated, and the sun cleared. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then he said: 'As to what follows: the sun and the moon do not eclipse because of the death or life of anyone. Rather, they are among the signs of Allah. When you see them, pray, give charity, remember Allah, and supplicate to Him.' Then he said: 'O Ummah of Muhammad, by Allah, none is more jealous than Allah that His male servant should commit adultery or His female servant should commit adultery. O Ummah of Muhammad, by Allah, if you knew what I know, you would weep much and laugh little.'\" She said: \"Then he raised his hands and said: 'Have I not conveyed?'\"\n\nHe said: And Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us; ʿAbdah narrated to us, from Hishām, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to pray,\" and he mentioned the hadith.\n\nThe wording is from the hadith of Abū Ṣāliḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad, from ʿAbdah, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyá ibn Yaḥyá. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1009]"
"6308","(6308) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muslimah narrated to us, from Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Muqriʾ in Baghdād informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us; Mālik informed us, from Yaḥyá ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ:\n\n\"A Jewish woman came to ask her, and she said: 'May Allah protect you from the punishment of the grave.' So ʿĀʾishah asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'Will people be punished in their graves?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I seek refuge in Allah from that.' Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rode one morning on a mount, and the sun was eclipsed. He returned at mid-morning. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed between the dwellings, then he stood to pray and the people stood behind him. He stood for a very long standing, then he bowed for a very long bowing, then he raised his head and stood for a very long standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed for a very long bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he raised his head and prostrated. Then he stood and stood for a very long standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed for a very long bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he raised his head and stood for a very long standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed for a very long bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he raised his head and prostrated, and he finished. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said what Allah willed him to say, then he commanded them to seek refuge from the punishment of the grave.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, who is ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muslimah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1002]"
"6309","(6309) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Bishr ibn Mūsá narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us. He said: I heard Yaḥyá ibn Saʿīd say: I heard ʿAmrah narrate from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said:\n\n\"A Jewish woman came and said: 'May Allah protect you from the punishment of the grave.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, will we be punished in our graves?' He said a word: 'I seek refuge in Allah from that.' She said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out one day in a caravan, and the sun was eclipsed. I went out with some women between the dwellings. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came quickly from his mount until he stood at his place of prayer. He pronounced the takbīr and stood for a very long standing, then he bowed for a very long bowing, then he raised his head and stood for a very long standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed for a very long bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he raised his head and prostrated a very long prostration. Then he raised his head, then prostrated a very long prostration, though it was less than the first prostration. Then he did the same in the second rakʿah. His prayer consisted of four rakʿahs in four prostrations. She said: I heard him after that seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave, and he said: 'You will be tested in your graves like the test of the Messiah, or like the test of the Dajjāl.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"6310","(6310) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me; Abū al-Walīd informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated with its chain and its meaning.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar, except that he did not quote the text (matn) and referred instead to the narration of Sulaymān ibn Bilāl from Yaḥyá ibn Saʿid. In the narration of Sulaymān there is no description of the prostration as being lengthy, whereas it is present in the narration of Ibn ʿUyaynah, as we have mentioned."
"6311","(6311) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥurfī in Baghdād informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá al-Birtī al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Shaybān narrated to us, from Yaḥyá ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said:\n\n\"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. It was announced: 'Prayer is to be gathered.' So he bowed twice in one rakʿah, then he stood and bowed twice in one rakʿah, then he sat until the sun cleared. ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: 'I never prostrated a prostration, nor bowed a bowing, longer than that.'\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from Abū al-Naḍr from Shaybān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See 6300]"
"6312","(6312) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād informed us. He said: It was recited to Yaḥyá ibn Jaʿfar while I was listening. He said: Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Yaʿlá ibn ʬāʾiʾ, from his father, and ʬAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from his father — all of them — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said:\n\n\"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He prolonged the standing until it was said: 'He will not bow.' So he bowed and prolonged the bowing until it was said: 'He will not raise his head.' So he raised his head and prolonged until it was said: 'He will not prostrate.' Then he prostrated and prolonged until it was said: 'He will not raise his head.' Then he raised his head and sat, and prolonged the sitting until it was said: 'He will not prostrate.' Then he prostrated and prolonged the prostration. Then he raised his head and did the same in the other rakʿah, until the sun cleared.\"\n\nThis narrator preserved from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr the lengthiness of the prostration, but he did not preserve the two bowings in one rakʿah. Abū Salamah preserved the two bowings in one rakʿah, and he preserved the lengthiness of the prostration from ʿĀʾishah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 1392]"
"6313","(6313) Muʾammal ibn Ismāʿīl narrated it from Sufyān and added in the hadith: \"Then he raised his head and prolonged the standing until it was said: 'He will not bow.' Then he bowed and prolonged the bowing until it was said: 'He will not raise his head.'\"\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ḥumayd ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Ramlī narrated to us; Muʾammal ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Sufyān narrated it with both chains along with this addition. Ibn Khuzaymah narrated it in his Mukhtaṣar al-Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Ibn Khuzaymah 1393]"
"6314","(6314) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Fāris narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said:\n\n\"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a very hot day. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed and prolonged the standing until people began to collapse. Then he bowed and prolonged. Then he raised his head and prolonged. Then he bowed and prolonged. Then he raised his head and prolonged. Then he prostrated two prostrations. Then he stood and did the same. Thus there were four rakʿahs and four prostrations. He would move forward and backward in his prayer. Then he turned to his Companions and said: 'Paradise and Hell were presented to me. Paradise was brought close to me, so that if I had reached out for a cluster of its fruit, I would have obtained it' — or he said: 'My hand fell short of it' — Hishām was uncertain. 'And Hell was presented to me, so I kept retreating in fear that it would overwhelm you. I saw a tall, dark-skinned Himyarī woman being punished on account of a cat of hers. She had tied it up and neither fed it nor given it to drink, nor did she let it eat from the vermin of the earth. And I saw in it Abū Thumāmah ʿAmr ibn Mālik dragging his intestines in the Fire.' They used to say: 'The sun and the moon do not eclipse except for the death of a great person.' But they are two signs among the signs of Allah that He shows you. So when they are eclipsed, pray until they clear.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from two routes from Hishām al-Dastawāʾī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 904]"
"6315","(6315) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd al-Bazzāz narrated to us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; Yaḥyá ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar:\n\n\"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ led the people in prayer — two rakʿahs, with two bowings in each rakʿah.\"\n\nThis hadith was also narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās al-Shāfiʿī from Yaḥyá ibn Sulaym. It is among the unique narrations of Yaḥyá ibn Sulaym from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. What has preceded is sufficient. [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Tārīkh Baghdād 10/119]"
"6316","(6316) Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Urmawī al-Faqīh narrated to us; the faqīh Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Nasawī narrated to us therein; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed me; al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; my father narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: al-Ḥārith ibn Fuḍayl al-Anṣārī, then al-Khaṭmī, narrated to us; Sufyān ibn Abī al-ʿAwjāʾ narrated to us, from Abū Shuraīḥ al-Khuzāʿī, who said:\n\n\"The sun was eclipsed during the caliphate of ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, in Madīnah, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd was there. ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, went out and led the people in that prayer — two rakʿahs with two prostrations in each rakʿah. Then ʿUthmān departed and entered his house. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd sat at the chamber of ʿĀʾishah, and we sat with him. He said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command prayer at the eclipse of the sun and the moon. When you see that has befallened them, hasten to prayer. For if it is the thing you fear, you will not be in a state of heedlessness, and if it is not, you will have attained or earned good.'\"\n\nAbū Khaythamah Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb narrated it similarly from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd. [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 1/459]"
"6317","(6317) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ informed us in Baghdād: Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us, he said: it was recited to Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Saʿīd narrated to us, from Qatādah, from ʿAzrah, from al-Ḥasan al-ʿUranī, that Ḥudhayfah prayed in al-Madāʾin the prayer of Ibn ʿAbbās during the solar eclipse. [Weak]"
"6318","(6318) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard ʿAṭāʾ say: I heard ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr say: one whom I consider truthful — meaning ʿĀʾishah — narrated to me: The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he stood for a long time, standing, then bowing, then standing, then bowing, then standing, then bowing — two rakʿahs with three bowings and four prostrations. Then he concluded, and the sun had cleared. Whenever he bowed, he said: \"Allāhu Akbar,\" then he would bow, and when he raised his head, he said: \"Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah.\" Then he stood and praised Allah and extolled Him, then he said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon do not eclipse because of the death of anyone or his life; rather, they are among the signs of Allah by which Allah instills fear. So when you see an eclipse, remember Allah until it clears.\"\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, and he said in the hadith: \"I think he meant ʿĀʾishah.\" And in the narration of ʿAbd al-Razzāq and a group from Ibn Jurayj: \"I thought he meant ʿĀʾishah.\" [Authentic. Muslim 901]"
"6319","(6319) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār — both — informed us from Muʿādh ibn Hishām, and this is the hadith of Isḥāq: Muʿādh ibn Hishām informed us: my father narrated to me, from Qatādah, from ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed six rakʿahs in four prostrations. I said to Muʿādh ibn Hishām: Is it from the Prophet (ﷺ)? He said: Yes, without doubt or uncertainty.\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ghassān al-Mismaʿī and Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from Muʿādh ibn Hishām.\nThe Shaykh said: Qatādah did not doubt that it was from ʿĀʾishah. However, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān contradicted both of them in its chain, narrating it from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and he stated that this occurred on the day Ibrāhīm, the son of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), died. [Authentic. Muslim 901]"
"6320","(6320) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-Ghaḍāʾirī — informed us in Baghdād: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Ṣūlī informed us by dictation in the year thirty-four and three hundred: Abū Dāwūd Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥanbal informed us: Yaḥyā narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Malik, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir, who said: The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and that was on the day Ibrāhīm, the son of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), died. The people said: The sun has eclipsed because of the death of Ibrāhīm. So the Prophet (ﷺ) stood and prayed with the people six rakʿahs in four prostrations. He said the takbīr, then recited and prolonged the recitation, then bowed approximately as long as he had stood, then raised his head and recited less than the first recitation, then bowed approximately as long as he had stood, then raised his head and recited a third recitation less than the second recitation, then bowed approximately as long as he had stood, then raised his head and went down into prostration and prostrated twice. Then he stood and bowed three rakʿahs before prostrating — there being no rakʿah except that the one before it was longer than it, except that his bowing was approximately equal to his standing. Then he moved back in his prayer, and the rows moved back with him. Then he moved forward and stood in his place, and the rows moved forward with him. He completed the prayer, and the sun had risen. He said: \"O people, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah; they do not eclipse because of the death of any human being. So when you see anything of that, pray until it clears.\" [Authentic. Muslim 904]"
"6321","(6321) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr informed us.\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Walīd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr informed us: my father informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik narrated it with its chain and its meaning, except that he said: \"And his bowing was approximately equal to his prostration.\" And he added regarding the retreat of the rows: \"Until he reached the women.\" And he added at the end of the hadith: \"There is nothing that you have been promised except that I have seen it in this prayer of mine, until the Fire was brought — and that was when you saw me retreat, fearing that its blaze might reach me. And I saw therein the owner of the hooked staff dragging his intestines in the Fire; he used to steal the belongings of the pilgrims with his hooked staff. If he was caught, he would say: 'It got caught on my staff.' If he was overlooked, he would take it away. And I saw therein the woman who had tied up a cat and neither fed it nor let it eat from the vermin of the earth until it died of hunger.' Then Paradise was brought — and that was when you saw me advance until I stood in my place. I stretched out my hand wanting to take some of its fruit so that you might look at it, then it occurred to me not to do so. There is nothing that you have been promised except that I have seen it in this prayer of mine.\"\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr.\nThe Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Whoever examines this story and the story narrated by Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir will know that it is one single story, and that the prayer he reported was performed only on the day Ibrāhīm, the son of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), died.\nThe narration of ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr and ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from ʿĀʾishah, the narration of ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār and Kathīr ibn ʿAbbās from Ibn ʿAbbās, the narration of Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, and the narration of Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh — all agree that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed it as two rakʿahs, each rakʿah containing two bowings.\nIn the narration of most of them is his statement (ﷺ) on that day: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah; they do not eclipse because of the death of anyone or his life\" — which indicates that he prayed it only on the day his son died, and he addressed the people and said this statement in response to their claim: \"It eclipsed only because of his death.\"\nThe agreement of these numerous narrators, along with the excellence of their memory, indicates that he did not exceed two bowings in each rakʿah, as al-Shāfiʿī and Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī (may Allah have mercy on them both) held. [Authentic. See the preceding]"
"6322","(6322) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us by dictation: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Ḥabīb, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed when the sun was eclipsed eight rakʿahs in four prostrations. Abū ʿAbd Allāh said: Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar added to me in it, from al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān, he said: and from ʿAlī the same.\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, and he mentioned ʿAlī in it. [Hasan for other than its chain. Muslim 908]"
"6323","(6323) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Abū al-Muthannā informed us: Musaddad informed us: Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Sufyān, who said: Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit narrated to me, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he prayed during an eclipse: he recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then prostrated — and in the second [rakʿah] the same.\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muthannā and others from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān.\nAs for Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī (may Allah have mercy on him), he turned away from these narrations in which there is a contradiction to the narration of the majority.\nWe have narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār and Kathīr ibn ʿAbbās, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he prayed it as two rakʿahs, each rakʿah containing two bowings.\nAnd Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, although he is among the trustworthy narrators, used to practice tadlīs (concealing the immediate source), and I have not found him stating his direct hearing of this hadith from Ṭāwūs. It is possible that he took it from someone other than a reliable narrator from Ṭāwūs.\nSulaymān al-Aḥwal narrated from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from his own action, that he prayed it as six rakʿahs in four prostrations, thus contradicting him in both the raising [of the head] and the number entirely. [Weak. See the preceding]"
"6324","(6324) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us. He said: one of those who used to debate him said: some of you narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed three bowings in each rakʿah. I said to him: It is from a disconnected source, and we do not establish a disconnected narration on its own, and in our view — and Allah knows best — it is an error. He said: Is the prayer of three bowings narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās? We said: Yes. Sufyān informed us, from Sulaymān al-Aḥwal, who said: I heard Ṭāwūs say: The sun was eclipsed, and Ibn ʿAbbās led us in prayer near the Ṣafāʾ — six rakʿahs in four prostrations. He said: So the narration of Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Ibn ʿAbbās is more established than the narration of Sulaymān al-Aḥwal from Ṭāwūs from Ibn ʿAbbās. I said: The evidence from Ibn ʿAbbās is in agreement with the hadith of Zayd ibn Aslam from him. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣafwān, who said: I saw Ibn ʿAbbās pray the eclipse prayer near Zamzam — two rakʿahs, each rakʿah containing two bowings.\nAnd Ibn ʿAbbās would not pray the eclipse prayer contrary to the prayer of the Prophet (ﷺ), if Allah wills. And when ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, Ṣafwān ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and al-Ḥasan narrate from Ibn ʿAbbās contrary to what Sulaymān al-Aḥwal narrated, the narration of three is more deserving of acceptance. And ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr and Zayd ibn Aslam are more prolific in hadith and more renowned for knowledge of hadith than Sulaymān.\nHe said: It has been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās that he prayed during an earthquake three bowings in each rakʿah.\nI said: If it is established from Ibn ʿAbbās, it is more likely that Ibn ʿAbbāh differentiated between the solar eclipse, the lunar eclipse, and the earthquake. And even if he equated between them, our aḥādīth are more numerous and more established than what has been narrated to you, so we take the more numerous and more established.\nThe Shaykh said: What al-Shāfiʿī meant by \"disconnected\" is the hadith of ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, where he attributed it to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) by assumption. And what he meant by \"error\" is the hadith of ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān, for Ibn Jurayj contradicted him, narrating it from ʿAṭāʾ from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr.\nAḥmad ibn Ḥanbal said: I give precedence to Ibn Jurayj over ʿAbd al-Malik in the hadith of ʿAṭāʾ. And in what Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī (may Allah have mercy on him) reported in his book of ʿIlal from Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī (may Allah have mercy on him), he said: The most authentic narrations in my view regarding the eclipse prayer are four rakʿahs in four prostrations.\nThe Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It has also been narrated through another weak chain from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Ṣilah ibn Zufar, from Ḥudhayfah. [Authentic. Kitāb al-ʿAmal 7/261]"
"6325","(6325) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā al-Māsarjisī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿImrān ibn Abī Laylā said: my father narrated to me, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Ṣilah ibn Zufar, from Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed with the people at the time of a solar eclipse. He stood and said the takbīr, then recited, then bowed as long as he had recited, then raised his head as long as he had bowed. He did this four bowings before prostrating, then he prostrated twice. Then he stood for the second [rakʿah] and did the same, and he did not recite between the bowings.\nMuḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā is not used as a proof. And five bowings in a rakʿah have been narrated through a chain that the authors of the Ṣaḥīḥ would not use as a proof, but Abū Dāwūd extracted it in the Sunan. [Weak. al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Duʿāʾ 2234]"
"6326","(6326) It is what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, and Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbbād informed us — and the wording is that of Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb — they said: Rūḥ ibn al-Muʾmin informed us: ʿUmar ibn Shaqīq informed us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Rāzī narrated to us, from Rabīʿ ibn Anas, from Abū al-ʿĀliyah, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed with them. He recited a chapter from the long sūrahs, then bowed five rakʿahs, then prostrated twice. Then he stood for the second [rakʿah] and recited a chapter from the long sūrahs, then bowed five rakʿahs, then prostrated twice. Then he sat facing the qiblah, supplicating until her eclipse cleared.\nIt is mentioned from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed the solar eclipse prayer with five rakʿahs and four prostrations. [Munkar. Aḥmad 5/134]"
"6327","(6327) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī reported it as a narration from Hushaym, from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan regarding that. And it is narrated from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, that he performed four rukūʿs in one rakʿah.\n[Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī narrated it in al-Umm.]"
"6328","(6328) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Munādī narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us, Sulaymān al-Shaybānī narrated to us, al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah narrated to us, from Ḥanash ibn Rabīʿah, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the time of ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: So he went out and led those who were with him in prayer. He recited Sūrat al-Ḥajj and Yā Sīn — I do not know which of the two he began with — and he recited aloud. Then he performed a rukūʿ approximately the length of his standing. Then he raised his head and stood for approximately the length of his standing. Then he performed a rukūʿ approximately the length of his standing. Then he raised his head and stood for approximately the length of his standing. Then he performed a rukūʿ approximately the length of his standing — four rukūʿs in total. Then he prostrated after the fourth. Then he stood and recited Sūrat al-Ḥajj and Yā Sīn. Then he stood and did as he had done in the first rakʿah — eight rukūʿs and four sujūds. Then he sat and made supplication. Then he departed, and his departure coincided with the sun having cleared.\nSulaymān al-Shaybānī did not raise it (to the Prophet), but al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥurr narrated it from al-Ḥakam and raised it.\n[Weak. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4936.]"
"6329","(6329) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab narrated to us in Wāsiṭ, Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam, Abū Nuʿaym, and Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Ṭanāfisī narrated to us, all saying: Zuhayr narrated to us, from al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥurr, from al-Ḥakam, from a man called Ḥanash, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The solar eclipse occurred, so ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, led the people in prayer. He recited Yā Sīn and something similar to it. Then he performed a rukūʿ approximately the length of his recitation of that sūrah. Then he raised his head and said: \"Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah.\" Then he stood for the length of the sūrah, making supplication and saying takbīr. Then he performed a rukūʿ the length of his recitation. Then he said: \"Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah.\" Then he stood again for the length of the sūrah. Then he performed a rukūʿ of similar length — until he had performed four rukūʿs. Then he said: \"Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah.\" Then he prostrated. Then he stood for the second rakʿah and did as he had done in the first rakʿah. Then he sat, making supplication and beseeching, until the sun cleared. Then he informed them that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had done the same.\n[Hasan in other than its chain. Ibn Khuzaymah 1388.]"
"6330","(6330) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Ḥanash ibn al-Muʿtamir — Abū al-Muʿtamir al-Kinānī — and some of them said: Ḥanash ibn Rabīʿah — heard ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him.\nSimāk ibn Ḥarb and al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah narrated from him, and they spoke regarding his ḥadīth. He was a Kūfan. I heard Ibn Ḥammād mention him from al-Bukhārī. Abū Aḥmad said: And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Nasāʾī said, as Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās informed me from him: Ḥanash ibn al-Muʿtamir is not strong.\nThe Sheikh said: Among our companions are those who held the view of authenticating the narrations regarding these various numbers, and that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, performed them on multiple occasions — once two rukūʿs in each rakʿah, once three rukūʿs in each rakʿah, and once four rukūʿs in each rakʿah — and each narrator conveyed what he had memorized. And that all of it is permissible, and that it is as though he, peace and blessings be upon him, would increase in rukūʿ when he did not see that the sun had yet cleared. Isḥāq ibn Rāhawayh held this view, and after him Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb al-Ṣabbaghī, and Abū Sulaymān al-Khaṭṭābī.\nAbū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir, the author of the Khilāfiyyāt, considered it good. And with Allah is the success.\nAnd that which al-Shāfiʿī preferred in terms of tarjīḥ (preponderance) is more correct, and Allah knows best.\n[Sound. ʿAdī, Ibn Kāmil 2/438.]"
"6331","(6331) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq informed us, Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir informed us, Shuʿbah informed us, from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he prayed two rakʿahs.\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Maḥmūd, from Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir.\nAnd this is a report about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, on the day his son Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, passed away, by indication.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1013.]"
"6332","(6332) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī informed us — it was read to him in Baghdad — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Naysābūrī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym informed us, ʿImrān ibn Mūsā informed us, ʿAbd al-Wārith informed us, Yūnus narrated to us, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when the solar eclipse occurred. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, went out dragging his cloak until he reached the mosque. The people gathered, and he led us in two rakʿahs. When the eclipse cleared, he said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. Allah frightens His servants through them. They do not eclipse for the death or the life of anyone. When you see that, pray until what is afflicting you is removed.\"\nHe said: And that was because a son of his had passed away, called Ibrāhīm, and some people spoke about that.\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Maʿmar, from ʿAbd al-Wārith, except that Abū Maʿmar did not mention his words: \"Allah frightens His servants through them.\" However, a group did mention it.\nAnd his statement in the ḥadīth: \"He led us in two rakʿahs\" — along with his informing that this occurred on the day Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, passed away — he means by it two rakʿahs with two rukūʿs in each rakʿah, as Ibn ʿAbbās, ʿĀʾishah, Jābir, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr affirmed it. And Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ and others narrated it from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, and they said in the ḥadīth: \"He prayed two rakʿahs as you pray.\"\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1014.]"
"6333","(6333) Abū ʿUmar Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qāḍī informed us, Abū Sahl al-Mihrajānī informed us, Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ narrated to us, from Yūnus, and he mentioned it with its meaning, and he said: \"As you pray,\" except that he did not mention the death of his son, peace be upon him. And the prayer of the eclipse was well known among them, so he pointed to it. And Allah knows best.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it.]"
"6334","(6334) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā ibn al-Sakan informed us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawārīrī informed us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal informed us, al-Jurayrī narrated to us, from Ḥayyān ibn ʿUmayr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah, who said: While I was practising archery with my arrows during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, the solar eclipse occurred. So I cast them aside and said: \"I shall certainly see what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, does today regarding the solar eclipse.\" I came to him and he was raising his hands, glorifying (tasbīḥ), praising (taḥmīl), declaring tahlīl, saying takbīr, and making supplication, until the sun cleared. Then he recited two sūrahs and performed two rukūʿs.\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawārīrī.\nAnd his statement: \"He recited two sūrahs and performed two rukūʿs\" — it is possible that he means by that in each rakʿah, for we have narrated it from a group who affirmed it. And the one who affirms is a witness, so he is more deserving of acceptance.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 913.]"
"6335","(6335) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miqrīʾ al-Mihrajānī informed us therein, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated to us, from Khālid, from Abū Qilābah, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. He went out in alarm, dragging his garment, until he reached the mosque. He continued praying until the sun cleared. When the sun cleared, he said: \"Indeed, there are people who claim that the sun and moon do not eclipse except for the death of a great person among the great ones, but it is not so. Indeed, the sun and moon do not eclipse for the death or the life of anyone. Rather, they are two signs among the signs of Allah. Indeed, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, when He manifests Himself to anything of His creation, it becomes humble before Him. So when you see that, pray as you would pray the most recent of the obligatory prayers you have performed.\"\nThis is a mursal narration. Abū Qilābah did not hear it from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr; rather, he narrated it from a man from al-Nuʿmān, and this last phrase is not in it.\n[Weak. Abū Dāwūd 1193.]"
"6336","(6336) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj informed us, ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us, from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from a man, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. He began praying two rakʿahs and saluting, then praying two rakʿahs and saluting, until the sun cleared. Then he said: \"Indeed, there were people from the Jāhiliyyah who used to say: 'When one of them eclipses, it only eclipses for the death of a great person among the great ones of the earth.' But it is not so. Rather, they are two creations among the creations of Allah. When Allah manifests Himself to anything of His creation, it becomes humble before Him. So when you see that, pray.\"\nAnd al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUmayr al-Baṣrī narrated it from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, and he said in it: \"He began praying two rakʿahs and asking about it until it cleared.\"\nAnd al-Ḥasan narrated it from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, free from these words that might suggest a contradiction, and free from the expression of tajallī (manifestation).\n[Weak. Aḥmad 4/367.]"
"6337","(6337) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us; my father narrated to me from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out in haste, dragging his cloak, until he reached the mosque, and the sun had been eclipsed. He prayed until it cleared, and then said: \"Indeed, the people of the Jāhiliyyah used to say that the sun and the moon do not become eclipsed except due to the death of a great man among the notables of the earth. But the sun and the moon do not become eclipsed due to the death of anyone. Rather, they are two of the creations of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and Allah brings about in His creation whatever He wills. So whichever of them becomes eclipsed, pray until it clears or until Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, brings about [another] matter.\"\n\nThis is more likely to be the preserved version. It has also been narrated from Abū Qilābah from Qabīṣah al-Hilālī. [Weak. Nasāʾī 1490]"
"6338","(6338) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Wuhayb narrated from Ayyūb.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥurfī in Baghdād informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated from Abū Qilābah from Qabīṣah al-Hilālī, who said: The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Prophet (ﷺ), and he prayed with them two rakʿahs, prolonging the standing therein. He said: Then it cleared, and the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"The signs are a means by which Allah frightens His servants therewith. So when you see that, pray as you would pray the most recent of the obligatory prayers you have performed.\"\n\nThe wording is that of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wārith, and the narration of Wuhayb does not contain \"takhwīfan\" (as a means of frightening). And he added at its beginning: \"He came out in alarm, dragging his garment, and I was with him that day in Madīnah.\" And this too was not heard by Abū Qilābah directly from Qabīṣah; rather, he narrated it from a man from Qabīṣah. [Weak. 1185]"
"6339","(6339) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Rayḥān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr narrated from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from Hilāl ibn ʀmir, that Qabīṣah al-Hilālī narrated to him: The sun was eclipsed—with the same meaning as the ḥadīth of Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl—he said: \"Until the stars appeared.\"\n\n(Q) And the wordings of these aḥādīth indicate that they refer back to the reports about his prayer on the day his son (peace be upon them both) passed away. And a group of his Ḥāfiẓ companions have established the number of rakūʿs in each rakʿah, and that is more deserving of acceptance than the narration of one who did not establish it. And with Allah is the success.\n\nAnd al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) mentioned the evidence of those who cite as proof the ḥadīth of Abū Bakrah: that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed during the eclipse two rakʿahs similar to your prayer, and the ḥadīth of Samurah ibn Jundub carries the same meaning—and that will come, Allah the Most High willing—and the ḥadīth of al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr. Then he gave preference to our aḥādīth, saying: the one who brings the addition is more deserving of having his statement accepted, because he has affirmed what the one who narrated a deficient ḥadīth did not affirm, and because our chain of narration in our ḥadīth is among the most established of people's chains.\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of that; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ narrated from al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him). [Weak. al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 9571]"
"6340","(6340) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū Muṣʿab narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated—all of them—from Mālik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The sun was eclipsed, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed, and the people were with him. He stood a long standing, approximately [the length of] Sūrat al-Baqarah. And in the narration of Abū Muṣʿab: \"He recited approximately [the length of] Sūrat al-Baqarah.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it through another route from Mālik.\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said: In this there is evidence that he did not hear what he recited, for had he heard it, he would not have estimated it by something else. [Previously referenced. 6302]"
"6341","(6341) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to me; Ibn Lahīʿah narrated to me; Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to me; ʿIkrimah narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed the eclipse prayer, and we did not hear his voice. [Ḥasan in other than this narration. Aḥmad 1/293]"
"6342","(6342) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Muqriʾ in Baghdād informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us, saying: It was recited to Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Birtī while I was listening; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Ibn ʿAbbād—that is, Thaʿlabah, a man from ʿAbd al-Qays—from Samurah ibn Jundub, that he said in his sermon, and he mentioned the ḥadīth about the Prophet's (ﷺ) prayer during the solar eclipse. He said: He came forward and prayed with the people, and we were with him. He stood the longest he ever stood in his place of prayer, and we did not hear his voice. Then he bowed with us the longest he ever bowed with us in any prayer, and we did not hear his voice. Then he did the same in the second [rakʿah]. [Ḥasan in other than this narration. Nasāʾī 1495]"
"6343","(6343) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Layth al-Rāzī narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Saʿd narrated to us; my paternal uncle narrated to us; my father narrated to us from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Salamah narrated to me from Sulaymān ibn Yasār—each of them having narrated to me—from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out and prayed with the people. I estimated his recitation and saw that he recited Sūrat al-Baqarah. Then he prostrated two prostrations. Then he stood and prolonged the recitation. I estimated his recitation and saw that he recited Sūrat Āl ʿImrān.\n\nAbū Dāwūd narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Saʿd in the book of Sunan, except that he said in the first rakʿah, after her words \"with Sūrat al-Baqarah\"—and he continued the ḥadīth—then he prostrated two prostrations.\n\nAnd in that there is evidence that he intended by this ḥadīth to describe the recitation rather than to describe the number of rakūʿs and standings. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1187]"
"6344","(6344) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-ʿAbbās al-Rāzī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Mihrān narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Namir narrated—he heard Ibn Shihāb informing from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited aloud in the eclipse prayer with his recitation. When he finished his recitation, he said the takbīr and bowed. And when he raised his head, he said: \"Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah, Rabbaka wa laka al-ḥamd.\" Then he would return to the recitation in the eclipse prayer. He prayed four rakūʿs in two rakʿahs, and four prostrations.\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Mihrān.\n\nAl-Bukhārī said: Sulaymān ibn Kathīr and Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn followed him, from al-Zuhrī, regarding the loud recitation. As for the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Kathīr—[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1016]"
"6345","(6345) He narrated it to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Kathīr narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He stood and said the takbīr, and the people said the takbīr. Then he recited and recited the Qurʾān aloud and prolonged it.\n\nAnd as for the ḥadīth of Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn—[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it]"
"6346","(6346) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ in Baghdād informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad—reciting to him—narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The sun was eclipsed—or she said: the sun became eclipsed—and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed and recited aloud in the recitation. And it has been narrated from al-Awzāʿī from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it]"
"6347","(6347) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazid narrated to us; my father narrated to us; al-Awza'i said; al-Zuhri narrated to me; he said; 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited a lengthy recitation aloud in the eclipse prayer. [Sahih. See its predecessor.]"
"6348","(6348) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad; Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Kamil ibn Khalaf al-Qadi informed us; Muhammad ibn Isma'il, meaning al-Sulami, narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Hafs, the maternal uncle of al-Nufayli, narrated to us; Musa ibn A'yan narrated from Ishaq ibn Rashid from al-Zuhri from 'Urwah from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed during the solar eclipse four rak'ahs and four prostrations. In the first rak'ah he recited Surah al-'Ankabut, and in the second, Surah Luqman or Surah al-Rum.\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us from Hanash from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he recited aloud in the solar eclipse prayer.\n\nAnd in what Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi related from Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari, that he said: The hadith of 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited aloud in the eclipse prayer is more authentic in my view than the hadith of Samurah that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited it silently therein.\n\nImam Ahmad (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The hadith of 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) regarding aloud recitation is uniquely narrated by al-Zuhri, and it has been narrated to us through another chain from 'A'ishah and then from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) what indicates silent recitation, and Allah knows best.\n[Munkar. Al-Daraqutni 2/64]"
"6349","(6349) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Tamim al-Asam informed us; al-Husayn ibn Fahm narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sa'd said; al-Waqidi narrated to me that Ibrahim, the son of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), died on Tuesday, the tenth of Rabi' al-Awwal, in the tenth year, and was buried in al-Baqi'. His death occurred in Bani Mazin at the house of Umm Barzah bint al-Mundhir from Bani al-Najjar, and he died at the age of eighteen months. [Sahih]"
"6350","(6350) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me by way of ijazah; Abu al-Walid narrated to us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Khalaf Waki' narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Mujammi' narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us; Usamah ibn Zayd narrated from al-Mundhir ibn 'Ubayd from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hassan ibn Thabit from his mother Sirin, who said: I was present at the death of Ibrahim, the son of the Prophet (ﷺ). The sun was eclipsed that day, and the people said: \"This is due to the death of Ibrahim.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The sun does not become eclipsed for the death of anyone, nor for his life.\" He died on Tuesday, the tenth of Rabi' al-Awwal, in the tenth year.\n\nAl-Zubayr ibn Bakkar also mentioned this. If it is preserved, then the death of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) occurred one year after him, in the eleventh year. And it has been narrated to us in authentic reports that the sun was eclipsed on the day Ibrahim, the son of the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ), passed away.\n[Sahih for other than him. Tabaqat Ibn Sa'd 1/143]"
"6351","(6351) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Thaqafi narrated to us; Abu al-Ash'ath narrated to us; Zuhyr ibn al-'Ala' narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah, who said: Al-Husayn ibn 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) was killed on Friday, the Day of 'Ashura', the tenth of Muharram, in the sixty-first year. He was fifty-four years, six months, and half a month old. [Da'if. Hakim 1943]"
"6352","(6352) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to me; Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Ibn Lahi'ah narrated from Abu Qabil, who said: When al-Husayn ibn 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) was killed, the sun was eclipsed such that the stars appeared at midday, until we thought it was the Day of Judgment."
"6353","(6353) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us; Isma'il narrated from Qays ibn Abi Hazim from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. Allah frightens His servants through them, and they do not become eclipsed for the death of anyone, nor for his life. So when you see anything of that, pray and supplicate until what is afflicting you is dispelled.\" [Sahih. Previously cited as 6298]"
"6354","(6354) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn Hajjaj al-Warraq and Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to me; they said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; Hushaym narrated from Isma'il, and he mentioned it with its like, except that he said: \"until it is uncovered.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid.\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us at the beginning of this book from the hadith of Jarir and Waki' from Isma'il, and in it: \"So when you see them, pray.\" And likewise 'Uqayl said it from al-Zuhri from 'Urwah from 'A'ishah. [Sahih. Meaning of its predecessor.]"
"6355","(6355) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me; Abu Ya'la narrated to me; Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to me; 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim; his father narrated to him from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon do not become eclipsed for the death of anyone, nor for his life. Rather, they are a sign among the signs of Allah. So when you see them, pray.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Asbagh, and Muslim narrated it from Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli from Ibn Wahb. [Sahih. Bukhari 3029]"
"6356","(6356) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; Bishr ibn Musa, Abu 'Ali al-Asadi narrated to us; Abu Zakariya, meaning al-Saylahini, narrated to us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Yunus ibn 'Ubayd from al-Hasan from Abu Bakrah, who said: The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He went out and prayed two rak'ahs, then he said: \"Indeed, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah, and they do not become eclipsed for the death of anyone, nor for his life. So when one of them is eclipsed, pray, supplicate, and remember Allah.\"\n\nThus narrated a group of imams from Bishr ibn Musa with this wording. Al-Bukhari used the narration of Hammad ibn Salamah from Yunus as a supporting evidence. [Sahih. Previously cited as 6332]"
"6357","(6357) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us.\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from Ashʿath, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah that the Prophet ﷺ prayed two rakʿahs similar to your prayer during the solar and lunar eclipse. [Ṣaḥīh. See 6331]"
"6358","(6358) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm informed us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm narrated to me, from al-Ḥasan, from Ibn ʿAbbās that the moon was eclipsed while Ibn ʿAbbās was in Baṣrah. Ibn ʿAbbās went out and prayed with us two rakʿahs, in each rakʿah two rūkūʿs. Then he mounted and addressed us, saying: \"I only prayed as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray.\" And he said: \"The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah; they are not eclipsed for the death of anyone nor for his life. When you see either of them eclipsed, let your recourse be to Allah.\" [Very weak. Published by al-Shāfiʿī, 346]"
"6359","(6359) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Muqriʾ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us.\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: \"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led the people in prayer. He stood and prolonged the standing, then he bowed and prolonged the bowing, then he stood and prolonged the standing, though it was less than the first standing. Then he bowed and prolonged the bowing, though it was less than the first bowing. Then he raised his head and prostrated. Then he did in the second rakʿah the same as that. Then he concluded the prayer, and the sun had cleared. He addressed the people and praised Allah and extolled Him, then he said: 'The sun and the moon are not eclipsed for the death of anyone nor for his life. When you see that, supplicate Allah, magnify Him, and give charity.' Then he said: 'O Ummah of Muhammad! By Allah, there is none more jealous than Allah that His male servant or His female servant should commit adultery. O Ummah of Muhammad! If you knew what I know, you would laugh little and weep much.'\"\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī. And Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīh. See 6307]"
"6360","(6360) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr and Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ both said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, who said: \"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I entered upon ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she was praying. I said: 'What is the matter with the people that they are praying?' She gestured with her head towards the sky. I said: 'A sign?' She said: 'Yes.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prolonged the standing greatly, until I was overcome by fainting. I took a water-skin of water beside me and began pouring water over my head. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded the prayer, and the sun had cleared. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people and praised Allah and extolled Him, then he said: 'To proceed: there is nothing of what you have been promised that I have not seen in this place of mine, even Paradise and Hellfire. And indeed it has been revealed to me that you will be tested in the graves, close to or like the trial of the Messiah Dajjāl' — Asmāʾ said: 'I do not know which of the two she said.' — 'One of you will be brought and it will be said to him: \"What do you know about this man?\" As for the believer, or the one certain of faith, he will say: \"He is Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; he came to us with clear proofs and guidance, so we responded and followed him\" — three times. Then it will be said to him: \"We knew that you believed in him. Sleep righteously.\" As for the hypocrite, or the one filled with doubt' — Asmāʾ said: 'I do not know which of the two she said' — 'he will say: \"I do not know\" — Asmāʾ said: 'I do not know which of the two she said' — 'he will say: \"I do not know; I heard the people saying something, so I said it too.\"'\"\nAbū al-Faḍl said: \"And this is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Ḥusayn.\"\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr. And al-Bukhārī narrated it through other routes from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīh. Bukhārī 6857]"
"6361","(6361) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us: Abū al-Naḍr informed us: Zuhayr narrated to us.\n(Ḥ) And Muḥammad informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us: Abū Nuʿaym informed us: Zuhayr narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays, who said: Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAbbād al-ʿAbdī, one of the people of Baṣrah, narrated to me that he witnessed a sermon one day of Samurah ibn Jundub, and he mentioned in his sermon: \"One day, while I and a young man from the Anṣār were shooting arrows at a target of ours during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the sun, when it was at the height of two spears or three in the eye of the observer on the horizon, turned black until it became as dark as a tannūmah. One of us said to his companion: 'Let us go to the mosque, for by Allah, this affair of the sun will surely bring the Messenger of Allah ﷺ some event concerning his Ummah.' We went to the mosque, and there he was, coming out. We met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ just as he came out to the people. He stepped forward and led us in the longest prayer he ever led us in, without his voice being heard. Then he bowed with us in the longest bowing he ever performed in a prayer, without his voice being heard. Then he prostrated with us in the longest prostration he ever performed in a prayer, without his voice being heard. Then he did the same in the second rakʿah. The clearing of the sun coincided with his sitting in the second rakʿah. Then he pronounced the salām, praised Allah, exalted Him, and testified that there is no god but Allah and that He is His servant and His Messenger. Then he said: 'O people, I am only a human being and the Messenger of Allah. I remind you of Allah, if you know that I have fallen short in conveying the messages of my Lord, that you inform me, so that I may convey the messages of my Lord as they ought to be conveyed. And if you know that I have conveyed the messages of my Lord, inform me.'\" He said: \"The people stood and said: 'We bear witness that you have conveyed the messages of your Lord, advised your Ummah, and fulfilled what was upon you.'\" He said: \"Then they fell silent.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"To proceed: there are men who claim that the eclipse of this sun, the eclipse of this moon, and the disappearance of these stars from their rising places are due to the death of great men among the people of the earth. But they have lied. Rather, they are signs among the signs of Allah by which He tests His servants, to see who among them repents. By Allah, I have seen, since I stood to pray, what you will encounter in your worldly life and your Hereafter. And by Allah, the Hour will not come until thirty liars emerge, the last of them being the one-eyed Dajjāl, with his left eye wiped out, as if it were the eye of Abū Yaḥyā, a shaykh from the Anṣār. When he emerges, he will claim that he is Allah. Whoever believes in him, testifies to his truth, and follows him — no righteous deed he previously performed will benefit him. And whoever disbelieves in him and calls him a liar — he will not be punished for any of his previous deeds. He will prevail over the entire earth except for the Ḥaram and Bayt al-Maqdis. He will confront the believers in Bayt al-Maqdis, and they will be shaken with a severe earthquake. Allah will defeat him and his armies, so much so that the trunk of a wall and the base of a tree will call out: \"O believer! This is a disbeliever hiding behind me. Come and kill him!\" That will not be until you see matters whose gravity intensifies in your hearts, and you ask among yourselves: \"Did your Prophet mention any of this to you?\" And until mountains are removed from their anchors. Then after that comes the Seizing.\" He gestured with his hand. He said: \"Then I witnessed another sermon,\" and he mentioned this ḥadīth without advancing or delaying anything. [Weak. See 6342]"
"6362","(6362) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb al-Ṭāʾī informed us: my grandfather ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb informed us: Abū Dāwūd, meaning al-Ḥafarī, narrated from Sufyān, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAbbād, from Samurah ibn Jundub that when the sun was eclipsed, the Prophet ﷺ delivered a sermon and said: \"To proceed…\" [Ṣaḥīh in other narrations]"
"6363","(6363) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī informed us: Abū Usāmah narrated from Burayd, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, who said: \"The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Prophet ﷺ. He stood up in fear, dreading that the Hour had come, until he reached the mosque. He stood and prayed with the longest standing, bowing, and prostration that I ever saw him perform in any prayer. Then he said: 'These are the signs that Allah sends; they do not occur for the death of anyone nor for his life. But Allah sends them to frighten His servants. When you see any of them, hasten to the remembrance of Allah, supplication to Him, and seeking His forgiveness.'\"\nal-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb from Abū Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīh. Bukhārī 1010]"
"6364","(6364) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us orally: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah and Ḥasan ibn Sufyān both said: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us: Hishām narrated from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: \"The sun was eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ,\" and she mentioned the ḥadīth about his prayer. She said: \"Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded the prayer, and the sun had cleared. He addressed the people and praised Allah and extolled Him, then he said: 'The sun and the moon are among the signs of Allah, and they are not eclipsed for the death of anyone nor for his life. When you see them, magnify Allah, supplicate Him, pray, and give charity. O Ummah of Muhammad! There is none more jealous than Allah that His male servant or His female servant should commit adultery. O Ummah of Muhammad! If you knew what I know, you would weep much and laugh little. Have I conveyed?'\"\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.\nAnd this ḥadīth was narrated from al-Layth ibn Saʿd from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, from the Prophet ﷺ, similarly, and in it he said: \"When you see that, supplicate Allah, pray, give charity, and manumit.\" [Ṣaḥīh. See 6307]"
"6365","(6365) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz informed us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us.\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ informed us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī informed us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated it. The wording of manumission (al-iʿtāq) in the narration of Hishām from his father from ʿĀʾishah is rare (gharīb). The well-known narration from Hishām is from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, that the Prophet ﷺ commanded that. [Ṣaḥīh. See what preceded]"
"6366","(6366) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Muqriʾ in Baghdād informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salmān informed us, saying: it was read to ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad while I was listening, and he said: Abū Ḥudhayfah informed us: Zāʾidah narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet ﷺ commanded manumission at the time of the eclipse.\nal-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ḥudhayfah Mūsā ibn Masʿūd and others. al-Bukhārī said: al-Dāwardī followed him (in narrating) from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīh. Bukhārī 2384]"
"6367","(6367) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad al-Sha'rani narrated to us: my grandfather narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Hamzah narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us — he mentioned it with a similar chain of narration, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the manumission of slaves when the solar eclipse occurred. [Sahih. See the preceding reference.]"
"6368","(6368) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: 'Atham ibn 'Ali narrated to us: Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated to us, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma' bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: We were commanded, at the time of the lunar eclipse, to manumit slaves. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr. [Sahih, as previously noted.]"
"6369","(6369) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us: Bahr ibn Nasser said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Yunus ibn Yazid informed you, from Ibn Shihab, who said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the mosque and mentioned the hadith. Muslim extracted it in his Sahih from the hadith of Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhari extracted it from the hadith of 'Anbasah, from Yunus. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, Abu Bakrah, Samurah ibn Jundub, and Asma' bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them, also narrated it regarding his prayer in the mosque. [Sahih. Previously referenced at 6303.]"
"6370","(6370) And Abu al-Qasim 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Iyadi al-Maliki in Baghdad informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ishaq al-Khurasani narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zibriqan informed us: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us: Habib ibn Hassan informed us, from Ibrahim and al-Sha'bi, from 'Alqamah, from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, who said: The solar eclipse occurred during the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they said, \"It has eclipsed only because of the death of Ibrahim.\" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the mosque and led the people in prayer. Then he said: \"O people, the sun and the moon do not eclipse because of the death or life of anyone. When you see that, hasten to prayer.\""
"6371","(6371) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, narrated to us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq informed us: Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us: Zaidah — (h) And Abu Bakr narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us: Abu al-Walid narrated to us: Zaidah, from Ziyad ibn 'Illaqah, who said: I heard al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah saying: The solar eclipse occurred during the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, on the day Ibrahim died, and the people said, \"It has eclipsed because of the death of Ibrahim.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah; they do not eclipse because of the death or life of anyone. When you see them, invoke Allah and pray until they become clear.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Walid, except that he said: \"until it clears.\" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari, 996.]"
"6372","(6372) And Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me: al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr both narrated to us: Mus'ab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us: Zaidah said: Ziyad ibn 'Illaqah said: I heard al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah saying — and he mentioned it with the same wording — and he said: \"Until it clears.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr. And we have been narrated, in the hadith of Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari and Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with both of them, from the Prophet ﷺ, in this hadith: \"Until what afflicts you is cleared.\" [Sahih. See the preceding reference.]"
"6373","(6373) And Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Muradi narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, who said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ — and he mentioned the hadith regarding the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ during the solar eclipse, as had passed in the hadith of Bahr ibn Nasser from Ibn Wahb — and he added at the end: He also said ﷺ: \"Pray until it is cleared from you.\" And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"I have seen, in this station of mine, everything you were promised, to the point that I saw myself wanting to take a cluster from Paradise when I saw you making me advance. And I saw Hellfire, some parts consuming others, when I saw you holding me back. And I saw therein Ibn Lu'ayy, who was the first to set free the animals for the idols.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Salamah. [Sahih. Muslim, 951.]"
"6374","(6374) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Ibn Milhan — (h) And Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim informed us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, who said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him: When the solar eclipse occurred, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood, pronounced the takbir, and recited a lengthy recitation. Then he performed a lengthy ruku', then raised his head and said, \"Sami' Allahu liman hamidah.\" He stood as he was and recited a lengthy recitation, shorter than the first recitation. Then he performed a lengthy ruku', shorter than the first ruku'. Then he performed a lengthy prostration. Then he did the same in the second rak'ah. Then he gave the salam, and the sun had cleared. He then addressed the people and said, regarding the solar and lunar eclipse: \"They are two signs among the signs of Allah; they do not eclipse because of the death or life of anyone. When you see them, hasten to prayer.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Bukayr. [Sahih. Previously referenced at 6303.]"
"6375","(6375) And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated to me: 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, from 'Amr — or Safwan ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Safwan — who said: I saw Ibn 'Abbas praying on the back of Zamzam for the solar eclipse, performing two rak'ahs, with two ruku's in each rak'ah. [Very weak. Al-Shafi'i extracted it, 350.]"
"6376","(6376) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-'Anbari informed us: my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Mansur ibn Safiyyah narrated to me, from his mother Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Asma' bint Abi Bakr, who said: The Prophet ﷺ was alarmed on the day the solar eclipse occurred. He grabbed his coat of mail until his cloak caught up with him. He stood with the people for a long standing — he would stand, then perform ruku'. Had someone come after he performed ruku', he would not have performed anything, for his heart would not have convinced him that he had performed ruku', due to the length of the standing. She said: I began to look at the woman older than me and the woman frailer than me, standing, and I said, \"I have a greater right than you to endure this long standing.\" Muslim extracted it in his Sahih from the hadith of Ibn Jurayj and others. [Sahih. Muslim, 906.]"
"6377","(6377) Abū Ḥāẓim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿArūbah al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī Maʿshar informed us; Ayyūb ibn Muḥammad al-Wazzān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ṣafiyyah bint Abī ʿUbayd, who said: The earth shook during the time of ʿUmar until the bedsteads clattered against one another, and Ibn ʿUmar was praying and was unaware of it. No one else was praying at that time, so he too became aware of it. Then ʿUmar addressed the people and said: \"You have committed [a sin]; you have been hasty.\" She said: \"And I do not know him to have said anything except: 'If it returns, I shall surely depart from among you.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 8335]"
"6378","(6378) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Ṣafwān narrated to us; Ḥarmiyyah ibn ʿUmarah narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Naḍr; my father narrated to me, saying: There was a darkness during the time of Anas ibn Mālik. He said: \"So I came to Anas ibn Mālik and said: 'O Abū Ḥamzah, did anything like this befall you during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?' He said: 'Allah forbid! If the wind grew fierce, we would hasten to the mosque out of fear of the Hour.'\" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1196]"
"6379","(6379) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad informed us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥakam ibn Abān narrated to me; my father narrated from ʿIkrimah.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Abī Ṣafwān al-Thaqafī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Salm ibn Jaʿfar narrated from al-Ḥakam ibn Abān from ʿIkrimah, who said: It was said to Ibn ʿAbbās: \"So-and-so, one of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ, has passed away,\" and he fell into prostration. It was said to him: \"Do you prostrate at this hour?\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'When you see a sign, prostrate.' And what sign is greater than the departure of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ?\" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Rūdhbārī.\n\nIn the narration of al-Qāḍī, he said: \"We heard a sound in Madīnah, and Ibn ʿAbbās said to me: 'O ʿIkrimah, go and see what this sound is.' So I went and found that Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, had passed away. I came to Ibn ʿAbbās and found him in prostration, and the sun had not yet risen. I said to him: 'Glory to Allah! You prostrate while the sun has not yet risen?' He said: 'O you, may you have no mother! Did not the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"When you see a sign, prostrate\"? And what sign is greater than the Mothers of the Believers departing from our midst while we are still alive?'\" [Jayyid. Tirmidhī 3891]"
"6380","(6380) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān; from Ḥabīb, meaning Ibn Ḥassān, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAlqamah, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd said: \"When you hear a thundering sound from the sky, turn to prayer.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6381","(6381) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said, by way of balāgh, from ʿAbbād, from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from Qazaʿah, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, that he prayed during an earthquake six rakʿahs in four sujūds: five rakʿahs with two sujūds in one rakʿah, and one rakʿah with two sujūds in one rakʿah.\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said: \"If this ḥadīth were established from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, we would rule according to it.\"\n\nThe Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: \"It is established from Ibn ʿAbbās as well.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 7/261]"
"6382","(6382) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Qatādah and ʿĀṣim, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he prayed during an earthquake in Baṣrah. He prolonged the qunūt, then bowed, then raised his head and prolonged the qunūt, then bowed, then raised his head and prolonged the qunūt, then bowed and prostrated. Then he stood for the second [rakʿah] and did the same. Thus his prayer consisted of six rakʿahs and four sujūds.\n\nQatādah said in his ḥadīth: \"Thus are the signs [prayers].\" Then Ibn ʿAbbās said: \"Thus is the prayer of the signs.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4929]"
"6383","(6383) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzzakī informed us, among others; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik ibn Anas informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn ʿĪsā ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us in Kūfah from the original of his hearing; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayd narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik, from Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Namir, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the livestock have perished and the people's routes have been cut off.\" And in the narration of al-Shāfiʿī: \"And the routes have been severed.\" \"So supplicate to Allah.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated. He said: \"Then we were rained upon from one Friday to the next Friday.\" A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the houses have collapsed, the routes have been cut off, and the livestock have perished.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and said: \"O Allah, upon the summits of the mountains, the hillocks, the valleys, and the places where trees grow.\" He said: \"Then the clouds parted from Madīnah as a garment parts [from the body].\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī and others, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Sharīk. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"
"6384","(6384) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm, from his paternal uncle, who said: The Prophet ﷺ went out to the muṣallā and sought rain. He faced the qiblah, turned his ridāʾ [cloak] inside out, and prayed two rakʿahs. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6385","(6385) And Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Jaʿfar al-Fāriyābī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Madanī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated that he heard ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm narrating from his paternal uncle, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd, the one to whom the adhān was shown [in a dream], that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the muṣallā, and he mentioned the ḥadīth with its like.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh.\n\nAl-Bukhārī said: Ibn ʿUyaynah used to say: \"He is the companion of the adhān.\" But he was mistaken, for this is ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd ibn ʿĀṣim al-Māzanī, Māzan of the Anṣār. He said in al-Tārīkh: \"He was killed on the day of al-Ḥarrah.\" And ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd ibn ʿAbd Rabbih al-Anṣārī, from Balḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, the Madanī, is the one to whom the adhān was shown. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"
"6386","(6386) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū Thābit al-Madanī narrated to us; Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from Hishām ibn Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kinānah, from the Banī Mālik ibn Ḥisl, from his father, that al-Walīd sent him to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the istisqāʾ [prayer for rain]. So I came to him and said: \"We have been deliberating in the mosque about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the istisqāʾ.\" He said: \"Nay, rather your brother's son, meaning al-Walīd—and he was the governor of Madīnah at that time—sent you.\" Then Ibn ʿAbbās said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out in a state of humility, modesty, and supplication. He sat upon the pulpit and did not deliver a sermon like your sermons. Rather, he continued in supplication, humility, and takbīr. Then he prayed two rakʿahs as he used to pray on the two ʿĪds.\" [Ḥasan. Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"6387","(6387) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ṣaffār in Baghdād informed us that Hārūn ibn Isḥāq al-Hamdānī narrated to us that Wakīʿ narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from Hishām ibn Isḥāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kinānah from his father, who said: A certain commander among the commanders sent me to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the prayer of Istisqāʾ (prayer for rain). Ibn ʿAbbās said to him: What prevented him from asking me directly? Then he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out in a state of humility, submissiveness, humbleness, supplication, and with a measured gait. He then performed two rakʿahs as he would perform the ʿĪd prayer, and he did not deliver a sermon like your sermons. [Ḥasan. Al-Ḥākim, Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"6388","(6388) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd informed us that Muʾammal ibn al-Faḍl al-Ḥarrānī informed us that al-Walīd informed us that Ibn Jābir informed us from Zayd ibn Arṭāh al-Fazārī from Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Ḥaḍramī, who reported that he heard Abū al-Dardāʾ say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Seek me among the weak, for indeed you are provided for and given victory on account of your weak ones.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd, al-Tirmidhī]"
"6389","(6389) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Qāsim ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ al-Hamadhānī in Hamadhān informed us that Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī informed us that ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth informed us that my father narrated from Misʿar from Ṭalḥah from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd from his father, who said: He thought that he was superior to those Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) who were below him in rank. The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) then said: \"Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, granted victory to this Ummah through its weak ones — by virtue of their supplications, their prayers, and their sincerity.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah from his father Ṭalḥah ibn Muṣarrif. [Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī]"
"6390","(6390) The Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us by dictation in the month of Ramaḍān, in the year 399 AH, that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ismāʿīl al-Shāshī al-Faqīh informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us that Surayj ibn Yūnus informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Khuthaym, meaning Ibn ʿIrāk ibn Mālik, informed us from his father, from his grandfather, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Be gentle, be gentle with Allah. Were it not for humble youth, grazing livestock, prostrating elders, and nursing children, the punishment would have been poured down upon you in a deluge.\" Ibrāhīm ibn Khuthaym is not strong (in narration), though it has a supporting chain that is also not weak. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā]"
"6391","(6391) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us that Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us that ʿAbdān and Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd both said: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār informed us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿd, meaning Ibn ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd al-Qaraẓ, informed us that Mālik ibn ʿUbaydah, meaning Ibn Musāfiʿ al-Dīlī, informed us from his father, who narrated from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Were it not for servants of Allah who bow in rukūʿ, infants who suckle, and livestock who graze, the punishment would have been poured down upon you in a deluge, and then you would have been crushed utterly.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr]"
"6392","(6392) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Bakr al-Marwazī informed us that al-Sahmī, meaning ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr, informed us that Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl informed us from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There are three supplications that are not rejected: the supplication of a parent, the supplication of one who is fasting, and the supplication of a traveler.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ḥāfiẓ transmitted it in al-Sunan 1/40]"
"6393","(6393) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad informed us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us that Abū Dāwūd informed us that Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah informed us from Saʿd al-Ṭāʾī, who informed me that Abū al-Mudillah heard Abū Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There are three whose supplications are not rejected: a just ruler, a fasting person until he breaks his fast, and the supplication of the oppressed. It is carried upon the clouds, and the gates of heaven are opened for it, and the Lord says: 'By My glory, I will surely help you, even if it is after some time.'\" [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Al-Tirmidhī]"
"6394","(6394) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-ʿAbdī and ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī both said: Abū Nuʿaym informed us that Fuḍayl ibn Marzūq informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us that Abū Kurayb informed us that Abū Usāmah informed us that Fuḍayl ibn Marzūq informed me that ʿAdī ibn Thābit informed me from Abū Ḥāzim from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, Allah is Pure and accepts only that which is pure. Indeed, Allah has commanded the believers with that which He commanded the Messengers. He said: 'O Messengers, eat of the good things and act righteously; indeed, I am Knowing of what you do' [al-Muʾminūn: 51]. And He said: 'O you who believe, eat of the good things which We have provided for you' [al-Baqarah: 172].' Then he mentioned a man who travels a long distance, dusty and disheveled, stretching his hands toward the heavens, saying: 'O Lord, O Lord!' — while his food is unlawful, his drink is unlawful, his clothing is unlawful, and he has been nourished by unlawful means. How then can his supplication be accepted?\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6395","(6395) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us by dictation that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Karāmah informed us that Khālid ibn Makhlad informed us from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, who informed me that Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Nimr informed me from ʿAṭāʾ from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has said: 'Whoever shows enmity to a wali (friend) of Mine, I declare war against him. And My servant does not draw near to Me with anything more beloved to Me than that which I have made obligatory upon him. And My servant continues to draw near to Me with voluntary acts of worship until I love him. And when I love him, I become his hearing with which he hears, his sight with which he sees, his hand with which he strikes, and his foot with which he walks. Were he to ask of Me, I would surely give him; and were he to seek refuge in Me, I would surely grant him refuge.'\"\n\nAnd he mentioned the remainder of the ḥadīth. I have already cited it in the Book of Names and Attributes along with its interpretation.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Karāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6396","(6396) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī informed us that ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb informed us that Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīm al-Ḍabbī informed us that Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ informed us from his father from Abū Hurayrah that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"The gates of heaven are opened every Monday and Thursday, and every servant who does not associate anything with Allah is forgiven, except a man between whom and his brother there exists mutual rancor. It is said: 'Postpone these two until they reconcile.'\"\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Dārim al-Ḥāfiẓ in al-Kūfah informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥaḍramī in al-Kūfah informed us that ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us that Jarīr narrated from Suhayl, and he mentioned it with the same meaning, except that he said: \"Look into the matter of these two until they reconcile,\" and he said it twice.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6397","(6397) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhabārī informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī informed us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Bashīr ibn Muhājir narrated from Ibn Buraydah from his father, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"No people ever break a covenant except that killing is made to prevail among them, and no indecent act ever becomes widespread among a people except that Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, sends death upon them, and no people ever withhold the zakāh except that Allah withholds rain from them.\" Thus narrated it Bashīr ibn al-Muhājir. [Munkar. Reported by al-Ḥākim]"
"6398","(6398) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb informed us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī informed us; Muʿādh ibn Asad al-Marwazī informed us; al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā al-Sīnānī narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"No people ever break a covenant except that Allah sets their enemy in authority over them, and no indecent act ever spreads among a people except that Allah seizes them with death, and no people ever cheat in weights and measures except that Allah seizes them with drought, and no people ever withhold the zakāh except that Allah withholds rain from them, and no people ever deviate in judgment except that enmity prevails among them\" — I think he said \"and killing.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by the author in al-Shuʿab]"
"6399","(6399) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us from the original of his hearing; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm from his paternal uncle, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out with the people to pray for rain. He prayed two rakʿahs, reciting aloud in them, turned his garment inside out, supplicated for rain, and faced the qiblah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī and Muslim]"
"6400","(6400) Others added in it from ʿAbd al-Razzāq: \"And he raised his hands, supplicated, and prayed for rain.\" Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated it with this addition. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"6401","(6401) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Sinān al-Qazzāz narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us; my father narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Nuʿmān — that is, ibn Rāshid — narrating from al-Zuhrī from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) went out one day to pray for rain. He prayed two rakʿahs without an adhān or an iqāmah. Then he addressed us, supplicated to Allah, turned his face toward the qiblah raising his hands, then turned his garment inside out, placing the right side over the left and the left side over the right. Al-Nuʿmān ibn Rāshid is alone in narrating this from al-Zuhrī. [Its chain is munkar. Reported by Ibn Mājah]"
"6402","(6402) Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī narrated to us; Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from —\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated —\n\nHishām ibn Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kinānah narrated to us. He said: My father informed me, and I heard him narrate, he said: Al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah — who was at that time the governor of al-Madīnah — sent me to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the prayer for rain. So I came to him and said: \"We have been discussing in the mosque about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the prayer for rain.\" He said: \"No, but your cousin sent you. Had he sent and asked, there would be nothing wrong with that.\" Then Ibn ʿAbbās said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out in a state of humility, in simple attire, and in a state of supplication, until he sat upon the pulpit. He did not deliver a sermon like your sermons, but he continued in supplication, in humble entreaty, and in extolling Allah's greatness, and he prayed two rakʿahs as he used to pray on the ʿĪd.\"\n\nThis is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā, and the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā has the same meaning, except that he said \"al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah.\" Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī said: The correct version is \"ibn ʿUtbah.\"\n\nThe Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This ḥadīth gives the impression that his supplication was before the prayer. And it was narrated by Sufyān al-Thawrī. [Ḥasan. Reported by Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"6403","(6403) As Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Hishām ibn Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kinānah from his father, who said: \"An emir among the emirs sent me to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the prayer for rain.\" He said: \"Who sent you?\" I said: \"So-and-so.\" He said: \"What prevented him from coming to me and asking?\" Then Ibn ʿAbbās said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out in a state of humility, in simple attire, and in a state of supplication. He did not deliver a sermon like your sermon, and he prayed two rakʿahs as he used to pray on the ʿĪd.\"\n\nSufyān said: \"I asked the shaykh: 'Was the sermon before the two rakʿahs or after them?' He said: 'I do not know.'\"\n\nThis indicates that Hishām did not have full retention of it. And it was narrated by Ismāʿīl ibn Rabīʿah ibn Hishām from his grandfather, applying it to the prayer of the two ʿĪds. [Ḥasan. Reported by Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"6404","(6404) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Sahl ibn ʿUthmān al-ʿAskarī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Rabīʿah from his grandfather Hishām ibn Isḥāq from his father from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out when he prayed for rain, in a state of humility and in simple attire, as he would do on the two ʿĪds.\n\nAnd ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Tinnīsī narrated it from Ismāʿīl ibn Rabīʿah with the same meaning. [Ḥasan. Reported by Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"6405","(6405) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿAlī al-Sudūsī narrated to us; Sahl ibn Bakkār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from his father from Ṭalḥah, who said: Marwān sent me to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the Sunnah of the prayer for rain. He said: \"The Sunnah of the prayer for rain is the Sunnah of the prayer of the two ʿĪds, except that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned his garment inside out, placing his right side over his left and his left side over his right. He prayed the two rakʿahs and pronounced seven takbīrs in the first, reciting {Sabbihi isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā} and reciting in the second {Hal ātaka ḥadīth al-Ghāshiyah}, and he pronounced five takbīrs in it.\" [Munkar. Reported by al-Ḥākim]"
"6406","(6406) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān — Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr, meaning ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq, narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb ibn ʿArabī narrated to us; Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from his father from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAwf, who said: \"I asked Ibn ʿAbbās about the Sunnah in the prayer for rain.\" He said: \"It is like the Sunnah of the two ʿĪds. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to pray for rain and prayed two rakʿahs without an adhān or an iqāmah. He pronounced thirteen takbīrs in them — seven in the first and five in the last — and recited aloud. Then he turned and delivered a sermon, faced the qiblah, turned his garment inside out, and then prayed for rain.\"\n\nMuḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz is not strong, but he is strengthened by the preceding corroborating narrations. [Munkar. Reported by al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"6407","(6407) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk informed us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Anṣārī went out to seek rain for the people, so he prayed two rakʿahs and then sought rain. That very day I met Zayd ibn Arqam, and there was only one man between me and him—or he said there was a man between me and him. I said: \"How many military expeditions did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) undertake?\" He said: \"Nineteen expeditions.\" I said: \"How many did you participate in with him?\" He said: \"Seventeen.\" I said: \"What was the first expedition he undertook?\" He said: \"Dhāt al-ʿUsayrah or Dhāt al-ʿUshayrah.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6408","(6408) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, he said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb and Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm al-Māzinī informed me that he heard his paternal uncle, who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), say: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out one day to seek rain. He turned his back toward the people, supplicated to Allah, faced the qiblah, and turned his cloak inside out, then he prayed two rakʿahs.\" Ibn Abī Dhiʾb said in the hadith: \"And he recited aloud in them.\" Ibn Wahb said: He meant al-jahr (loud recitation). Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, and he said in the hadith: \"He prayed two rakʿahs for us, reciting aloud in them.\" Similarly, it was narrated from Abū Nuʿaym from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb. Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir and Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb from Yūnus alone. Al-Thawrī, Yazīd ibn Hārūn, ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar, and Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated it from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb without his word \"then.\" Similarly, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated it from al-Zuhrī without the word \"then.\" Maʿmar narrated it from al-Zuhrī and described the prayer first, then described the turning of the cloak and the supplication. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6409","(6409) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mihrān narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī narrated to us: Khālid ibn Nizār narrated to us: al-Qāsim ibn Mabrazah narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from ʿĀʾishah, who said: \"The people complained to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about the lack of rain, so he ordered a minbar to be placed for him in the muṣallā, and he promised the people a day on which they would go out.\" ʿĀʾishah said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out when the edge of the sun first appeared. He sat upon the minbar, said the takbīr, and praised Allah, then he said: 'Indeed, you have complained of the drought of your lands and the delay of rain beyond its usual time for you, while Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has commanded you to supplicate to Him and has promised you that He will respond to you.' Then he said: 'All praise is due to Allah, the Lord of the worlds, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, the Master of the Day of Judgment. There is no god but Allah; He does what He wills. O Allah, You are Allah; there is no god but You, the Self-Sufficient, and we are the poor. Send down upon us the rain, and make what You send down for us strength and sustenance for a time.' Then he raised his hands and did not cease raising them until the whiteness of his armpits became visible. Then he turned his back toward the people and turned—or flipped—his cloak while still raising his hands. Then he turned toward the people, came down, and prayed two rakʿahs. Allah, the Most High, then produced clouds, and they thundered and lightning flashed, then it rained by the permission of Allah, the Most High. He had not yet reached his mosque when the torrents began to flow. When he saw the people's haste toward shelter, he smiled until his back teeth became visible, and he said: 'I bear witness that Allah has power over all things, and that I am His servant and His Messenger.'\" Abū Dāwūd narrated it in Kitāb al-Sunan from Hārūn. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"6410","(6410) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl, Abū Ghassān, narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated from Abū Isḥāq, who said: \"ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Anṣārī went out to seek rain, and he had seen the Prophet (ﷺ). Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib and Zayd ibn Arqam also went out among those who went out.\" Abū Isḥāq said: \"I was with him that day. He stood upon his feet—without a minbar—and sought rain and sought forgiveness. Then he prayed two rakʿahs with us, and we were behind him, reciting aloud in them. No adhān was called that day, and no iqāmah was given.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym from Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah. Al-Thawrī narrated it from Abū Isḥāq, and he said: \"Then he delivered a sermon, then he prayed.\" Shuʿbah narrated it from Abū Isḥāq, and he said: \"He prayed two rakʿahs, then he sought rain.\" The narration of al-Thawrī and Zuhayr is more likely. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6411","(6411) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me: My father informed me: Abū al-Yamān informed us: Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī. ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm informed me that his paternal uncle, who was among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), informed him that the Prophet (ﷺ) went out with the people to the muṣallā to seek rain for them. He stood and supplicated while standing, then faced the qiblah and turned his cloak inside out, and they were given rain. Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān narrated to us, and he mentioned something similar to it, except that he said: \"He supplicated to Allah while standing,\" and he said: \"And they were given rain.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6412","(6412) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ and Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, who said: Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm informed me that ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm informed him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd al-Anṣārī said: \"Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to the muṣallā to seek rain, and when he intended to supplicate, he faced the qiblah and turned his cloak inside out.\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī and Muslim.]"
"6413","(6413) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Shawdhab al-Muqriʾ in Wāsiṭ narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from Sufyān. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm from his paternal uncle, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to seek rain and turned his cloak inside out.\" In the narration of Ibn Mahdī: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) sought rain and turned his cloak inside out.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6414","(6414) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, he said: I read to Mālik from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr that he heard ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm say: \"I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd say: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to the muṣallā and sought rain, and he turned his cloak inside out when he faced the qiblah.'\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"6415","(6415) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf narrated to us, he said: I read in the book of ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, meaning al-Ḥimṣī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sālim from al-Zubaydī from Muḥammad ibn Muslim, with this hadith—meaning the hadith of al-Zuhrī from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm from his paternal uncle—regarding the going out of the Prophet (ﷺ) for the prayer of seeking rain. He said: \"He turned his cloak inside out, placing its right side upon his left shoulder and its left side upon his right shoulder, then he supplicated to Allah.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
"6416","(6416) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm narrated to us that he heard ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm narrate from his paternal uncle ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to the muṣallā to seek rain. He turned his cloak inside out, faced the qiblah, and prayed two rakʿahs.\" And with his isnād, he said: Sufyān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and al-Masʿūdī narrated from Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd with this hadith. Al-Masʿūdī said: \"I said to Abū Bakr: 'Did he place the right side upon the left and the left side upon the right, or did he place its top at the bottom?' He said: 'Rather, he placed the right side upon the left and the left side upon the right.'\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad from Sufyān from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, then his narration from al-Masʿūdī from Abū Bakr, his statement being abridged. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6417","(6417) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Shādhān al-Jawharī narrated to us, al-Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Thābit al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Abū al-Jamāhir narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from ʿUmārah ibn Ghaziyyah from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought rain (istisqāʾ), and upon him was a black cloak (khamīṣah). He intended to take its lower part and make it its upper part, but when it became heavy upon him, he turned it over onto his shoulder.\n\nThe wording of both is the same. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd]\n\n---"
"6418","(6418) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Jaʿfar ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Manṣūr narrated to us by dictation, Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf ibn ʿĪsā al-Ṭabbāʿ narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from Jābir, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought rain and turned over his cloak (ridāʾ) so that the drought might be reversed.\n\nThus he narrated it from Jābir. Others narrated it from Isḥāq ibn ʿĪsā but did not mention Jābir in it, and attributed it to the statement of Abū Jaʿfar.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Connected. Narrated by al-Ḥākim]\n\n---"
"6419","(6419) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Thalj narrated to us, my grandfather narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn al-Ṭabbāʿ narrated from Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth, and he mentioned it as a mursal hadith.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ḥākim]\n\n---"
"6420","(6420) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, who said:\n\nWakīʿ said regarding his statement — \"He placed the right side upon the left and the left side upon the right\" — meaning that the year of drought would be transformed into a year of abundance, just as this right side is turned over upon the left.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Naṣb al-Rāyah]\n\n---"
"6421","(6421) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, al-Walīd — who is Ibn Muslim — narrated to us, al-Ḥakam — who is Ibn Muṣʿab — narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated from his father, who narrated from his father ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Whoever adheres to seeking forgiveness (istighfār), Allah will grant him relief from every worry, an exit from every hardship, and will provide for him from where he does not expect.\"\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]\n\n---"
"6422","(6422) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Khalīfah narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faraj Abū al-Faḍl al-Riyāshī narrated to us, al-Aṣmaʿī narrated from his father, from Abū Wajzah al-Saʿdī, from his father, who said:\n\nʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to seek rain, and he did not go beyond seeking forgiveness (istighfār). So I said: \"Will he not speak for the purpose for which he went out?\" I did not know that seeking rain (istisqāʾ) was seeking forgiveness (istighfār). And we were rained upon.\n\n[Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Ibn Abī Ḥātim]\n\n---"
"6423","(6423) Abū al-Qāsim Mujālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mujālid al-Bajalī informed us in al-Kūfah, Abū al-Ḥusayn Muslim ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Muslim al-Tamīmī narrated to us, al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr al-Ashʿathī informed us, ʿAbthar narrated from Muṭarrif from al-Shaʿbī, who said:\n\nThe people were afflicted by drought during the time of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). So ʿUmar ascended the pulpit and sought rain, but he did not go beyond seeking forgiveness until he came down. They said to him: \"We did not hear you, O Commander of the Faithful, seek rain.\" He said: \"I indeed sought rain by the keys of the heavens by which rain is sent down.\" Then he recited this verse: *\"Seek forgiveness of your Lord; indeed, He is ever Forgiving. He will send rain upon you in continuous showers, and give you increase in strength upon strength, and do not turn yourselves into criminals.\"* (Hūd: 52) *\"And seek forgiveness of your Lord and then repent to Him.\"* (Hūd: 90)\n\nThus I found it in my book: \"by the keys of the heavens\" (bi-mafātīḥ al-samāʾ). Others narrated it from Muṭarrif and said: \"by the spouts of the heavens\" (bi-majādīḥ al-samāʾ).\n\n[Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]\n\n---"
"6424","(6424) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Sufyān and Hushaym narrated from Muṭarrif from al-Shaʿbī, who said:\n\nʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to seek rain, and he did not go beyond seeking forgiveness until he returned. It was said to him: \"We did not see you seek rain.\" He said: \"I indeed sought rain by the spouts of the heavens by which rain is sent down.\" Then he recited: *\"Seek forgiveness of your Lord; indeed, He is ever Forgiving. He will send rain upon you in continuous showers.\"* (Hūd: 52) *\"And O my people, seek forgiveness of your Lord and then repent to Him; He will send rain upon you in continuous showers.\"* (Hūd: 52)\n\n[Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Ibn Abī Shaybah]\n\n---"
"6425","(6425) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dārimī narrated to me, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Bisṭām ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, Abū Qutaybah narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār narrated from his father, that he heard Ibn ʿUmar recite the verse of poetry of Abū Ṭālib regarding the Prophet ﷺ:\n\n*\"A fair-faced one by whose countenance the clouds are entreated for rain, a shelter for the orphans and a protection for the widows.\"*\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī from Abū Qutaybah.\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī]\n\n---"
"6426","(6426) Al-Bukhārī said, and ʿUmar ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us, Sālim narrated from his father — meaning what Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us, Abū ʿAqīl narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, Sālim narrated from his father, who said:\n\nI would sometimes recall the poet's verse while I was looking at the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon the pulpit seeking rain. He would not come down until every waterspout was gushing forth, and I would recall the poet's verse:\n\n*\"A fair-faced one by whose countenance the clouds are entreated for rain, a shelter for the orphans and a protection for the widows.\"*\n\nHe said: \"And it is the saying of Abū Ṭālib.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī]"
"6427","(6427) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, orally: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī informed me: my father ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muthannā, from Thumāmah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Anas, from Anas, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) would, when they were afflicted with drought, seek rain through al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib (may Allah be pleased with him), saying: \"O Allah, we used to seek intercession through our Prophet (peace be upon him) and You would give us rain; and today we seek intercession through the uncle of our Prophet (peace be upon him), so give us rain.\" And they would be given rain.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī. And he said: it is from Anas ibn Mālik without doubt, and the mention of Anas had dropped from the book of our shaykh Abū Muḥammad (may Allah have mercy on him).\n\nAnd Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān and others narrated it from al-Anṣārī with a connected chain. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6428","(6428) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUtbah informed us: ʿAlī ibn Thābit informed us: Asbāṭ ibn Naṣr, from Manṣūr, from Abī al-Ḍuḥā, from Masrūq, from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saw the people turning away, he said: \"O Allah, seven years like the seven years of Yūsuf.\" So a year of drought seized them until they ate carrion, skins, and bones. Then Abū Sufyān and some of the people of Makkah came and said: \"O Muhammad, you claim that you were sent as a mercy, and your people have perished, so supplicate Allah for them.\" So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) supplicated, and they were given rain, which poured upon them continuously for seven days. Then the people complained of the excessive rain, and he said: \"O Allah, around us and not upon us.\" The cloud then parted from above his head, and the people around them were given rain. He said: \"The verse of al-Dukhān has passed — it is the hunger that befell them — and that is the word of the Almighty: 'Indeed, We will remove the punishment for a little; indeed, you will return.' [al-Dukhān: 15] And the verse of al-Rūm, and the Great Overwhelming (al-Batshah al-Kubrā) on the day of Badr, and the splitting of the moon.\"\n\nBoth al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through various routes from Manṣūr, and al-Bukhārī alluded to the narration of Asbāṭ with the additional material he brought in the hadith regarding the supplication of the Prophet (peace be upon him) and the answering of his supplication. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6429","(6429) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl informed me: Abū al-Ṭāhir informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Rabīʿah ibn Yazīd, from Abī Idrīs al-Khawlānī, from Abī Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), that he said: \"The supplication of a servant continues to be answered so long as he does not supplicate for sin or for the severing of kinship ties, and so long as he does not become impatient.\" It was said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what is impatience (al-isʿijāl)?\" He said: \"He says: 'I have supplicated, I have supplicated, and it was not answered for me,' and so he becomes weary at that point and abandons supplication.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Ṭāhir, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Abī ʿUbayd from Abī Hurayrah in an abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6430","(6430) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh informed us: Isḥāq al-Azraq informed us: Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah, from al-Shaʿbī, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: \"The example of the believers in their mutual affection, compassion, and mercy is like that of a single body: when one limb complains, the rest of the body responds with sleeplessness and fever.\"\n\nBoth al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Zakariyyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6431","(6431) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us: Mūsā ibn Tharwān al-Muʿallim informed me: Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Karīz al-Khuzāʿī said: Umm al-Dardāʾ informed me, saying: My master (i.e., her husband) informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: \"Whoever supplicates for his brother in his absence, the angel entrusted with him says: 'Āmīn, and for you the same.'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6432","(6432) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammil ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed us: ʿAbdān ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥalīm (i.e., al-Bayhaqī) informed us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Ṣanʿānī informed us, in the Mosque of al-Khayf. They all said: al-Muʿtamir (i.e., Ibn Sulaymān) informed us, from ʿUbaydullāh (i.e., Ibn ʿUmar), from Thābit, from Anas, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was delivering the sermon on Friday when the people stood up and shouted, saying: \"O Messenger of Allah, the rain has ceased, the trees have turned red, and the livestock have perished; supplicate Allah to give us rain.\" He said: \"O Allah, give us rain. O Allah, give us rain.\" He said: \"By Allah, we did not see any fragment of cloud in the sky.\" Then a cloud was raised, spread out, and it rained. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came down, prayed, and departed. It continued to rain until the following Friday. When the Prophet (peace be upon him) stood to deliver the sermon, they shouted, saying: \"O Prophet of Allah, the houses have collapsed and the paths have been cut off; supplicate Allah to withhold it from us.\" The Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him) smiled and then said: \"O Allah, around us and not upon us.\" The clouds cleared away from al-Madīnah, and it rained around it but did not rain a single drop upon al-Madīnah. I looked at al-Madīnah as though it were encircled like a crown.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī, and Muslim narrated it from him and from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6433","(6433) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me: Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Bishr informed us: al-Muʿāfā ibn ʿImrān, from al-Awzāʿī, from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Anas ibn Mālik, that a man complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) of the destruction of wealth and the hardship of dependents. He said: \"So he supplicated to Allah, and they were given rain.\" He did not mention that he turned his cloak around or faced the qiblah.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn Bishr. And along with what has preceded from the hadith of ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd, it serves as evidence that this is prescribed specifically in the sermon of the prayer for rain (khutbah al-istisqāʾ), not in the Friday sermon. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6434","(6434) Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us: Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Abū al-Yamān informed me: Shuʿayb, from al-Zuhrī, informed me: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was informed that Abū Lubābah was saying to the sky: \"Extend (your drought),\" seeking barrenness for the fruit of his date palms out of hypocrisy. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"O Allah, send it (rain) until Abū Lubābah blocks the hole of his enclosure with his cloak.\" So Allah sent the sky (with rain). When the floodwater reached the dates of Abū Lubābah while he was in the enclosure, Abū Lubābah was compelled to use his lower garment, and with it he blocked the hole of the enclosure.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf — al-Ṭabarānī narrated it]"
"6435","(6435) And Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād al-Ṭihrānī in al-Rayy informed us: my father informed me: al-Sindī (i.e., ibn ʿAbdawayh al-Dahhākī) informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Madanī (i.e., Abū Uways) informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥarmalah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir al-Anṣārī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) sought rain on Friday and said: \"O Allah, give us rain. O Allah, give us rain.\" Abū Lubābah stood up and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the dates are in the enclosures.\" He said: \"And there is no cloud in the sky that we can see.\" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"O Allah, give us rain until Abū Lubābah stands naked and blocks the hole of his enclosure with his lower garment.\" He said: \"The sky became heavy and it rained, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) led us in prayer.\" He said: \"Then the Anṣār circled around Abū Lubābah, saying to him: 'O Abū Lubābah, by Allah, the sky will never cease until you stand naked and block the hole of your enclosure with your lower garment, as the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said.'\" He said: \"So Abū Lubābah stood naked and blocked the hole of his enclosure with his lower garment.\" He said: \"And the sky cleared.\"\n\n[Ḍaʿīf — al-Ṭabarānī narrated it]"
"6436","(6436) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhṭawayh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr informed us: Ismāʿīl informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Jaʿfar al-Fāryābī informed us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad and Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar informed us, from Sharīk ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Nimr, from Anas ibn Mālik.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed us: Ismāʿīl (i.e., Ibn Jaʿfar) informed me: Sharīk, from Anas, that a man entered the mosque on Friday through a gate that was towards the Dār al-Qaḍāʾ, while the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was standing delivering the sermon. He stood facing the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and then said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the wealth has been destroyed and the paths have been cut off; supplicate Allah to give us relief.\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) raised his hands and then said: 'O Allah, give us relief. O Allah, give us relief. O Allah, give us relief,'\" three times. Anas said: \"By Allah, we did not see any cloud or fragment of cloud in the sky, and there was neither a house nor a dwelling between us and Ṣalʿ.\" He said: \"Then a cloud like a shield appeared from behind it. When it reached the middle of the sky, it spread out and then it rained.\" Anas said: \"By Allah, we did not see the sun for six days.\" He said: \"Then a man entered through that gate on the following Friday, while the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was standing delivering the sermon. He stood facing him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, the wealth has been destroyed and the paths have been cut off; supplicate Allah to withhold it from us.'\" He said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) raised his hands and then said: 'O Allah, around us and not upon us. O Allah, upon the hillocks, the ridges, the valleys, and the places where trees grow.'\" He said: \"It cleared, and we went out walking in the sun.\" Sharīk said: \"I asked Anas: 'Was it the first man?' He said: 'I do not know.'\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd from Ismāʿīl, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, Qutaybah, and ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr, all from Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6437","(6437) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan al-'Amiri narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us: Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated from Yazid al-Faqir from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, who said: [Some people] came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \"O Allah, grant us a beneficial, fertile, bountiful, immediate rain — not delayed — that is beneficial and not harmful.\" And [the clouds] covered them completely.\n\nThus he informed us with it in the book *al-Mustadrak*, and he informed us with it in *al-Fawa'id al-Kabir* from Abu al-'Abbas, and he said in the hadith: Hawazin came to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Say: 'O Allah, grant us rain.'\"\n\n[Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
"6438","(6438) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Ushnani informed us: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm narrated to us, and he mentioned it and said: \"Hawazin,\" and he did not say \"Say.\" Thus a group narrated it from Muhammad ibn 'Ubaid, and likewise it is in our copy of the book of Abu Dawud.\n\nAbu Sulayman al-Khattabi (may Allah have mercy on him) used to recite it: \"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raising his hands [in supplication].\" Then he explained it and said: His word \"raising his hands\" means the exertion and stretching forth of the hands in supplication.\n\n[Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
"6439","(6439) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me: Mujahid ibn Musa narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us, and he mentioned it with the first wording. 'Abdullah said: I narrated this hadith to my father, and my father said: Muhammad ibn 'Ubaid gave us his written record from Mis'ar, and we copied it, but this hadith was not in it. He said: This is nothing — as though he rejected it from Muhammad ibn 'Ubaid. My father said: Then Ya'la, the brother of Muhammad, narrated it to us, and he said: Mis'ar narrated from Yazid al-Faqir, mursal [without mentioning the Companion], and he did not say \"raising his hands\" — he contradicted him.\n\n[Sahih. Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"6440","(6440) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us: Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us: Abu 'Umar narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from 'Amr ibn Murrah from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd from Shurayh ibn al-Simt, who said: He said to Ka'b ibn Murrah — or Murrah ibn Ka'b — \"Narrate to us a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicate against Mudar, so I came to him and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, Allah has granted you and answered your supplication, but your people have perished. So supplicate to Allah for them.\" He said: \"O Allah, grant us a rain that is relieving, fertile, bountiful, abundant, immediate, not delayed, beneficial and not harmful.\" And it was not but a week — or thereabouts — until they were given rain.\n\n[Da'if. Reported by al-Hakim]"
"6441","(6441) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Bishran informed us in Baghdad: Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Ishaq ibn Nihab informed us: Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah ibn Muslim narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Minqari narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Ashhal narrated from Yahya ibn Sa'id from 'Amr ibn Shu'aib from his father from his grandfather: that when the Prophet ﷺ sought rain, he would say: \"O Allah, water Your servants and Your beasts, spread Your mercy, and revive Your dead land.\"\n\nAnd likewise 'Ali ibn Qadim narrated it from al-Thawri from Yahya ibn Sa'id. And Malik narrated it from Yahya ibn Sa'id from 'Amr that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say it, mursal.\n\n[Hasan. Abu Dawud]"
"6442","(6442) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Amr ibn Hafs informed us.\n\n(And) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Isbahani informed us: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us. Both said: Hisham ibn al-Qasim narrated to us: Ya'la narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Jarad narrated: that when the Prophet ﷺ sought rain, he would say: \"O Allah, grant us a rain that is relieving and fertile, through which You provide abundance to Your servants, through which You increase the udders, and through which You revive the crops.\" The wording is from the hadith of Abu 'Abdullah. And in the hadith of Ibn al-Harith, he said: Ya'la ibn al-Ashdaq narrated to us from 'Abdullah ibn Jarad, who said: The Prophet ﷺ used to… and he mentioned it, and he added: \"wholesome and fertile.\"\n\n[Very Da'if. 'Usdat al-Qari]"
"6443","(6443) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad informed me: Rabbih ibn Rabah narrated from al-Muttalib ibn Hanzab: that the Prophet ﷺ would say at the time of rain: \"O Allah, [grant us] a watering of mercy, and not a watering of punishment, nor of affliction, nor of destruction, nor of drowning. O Allah, upon the hilltops and the places where trees grow. O Allah, around us and not upon us.\"\n\nThis is mursal.\n\n[Very Da'if. Reported by al-Shafi'i]"
"6444","(6444) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us by dictation: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Shahid narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated from Anas ibn Malik, and Yunus ibn 'Ubaid narrated from Thabit from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The people of Madinah were afflicted with drought during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: While he ﷺ was delivering the sermon to us on Friday, a man stood up and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the horses have perished and the sheep have perished. Supplicate to Allah to give us rain.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stretched out his hands and supplicated. Anas said: And the sky was like glass. Then a wind blew, then it stirred up clouds, then they gathered, then the sky opened its floodgates. We went out wading through the water until we reached our homes. It continued to rain until the following Friday. Then that man — or another man — stood up and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the houses have collapsed. Supplicate to Allah to withhold it.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled and then said: \"O Allah, around us and not upon us.\" Anas said: I looked at the clouds, and they parted around Madinah as though they were a crown.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the *Sahih* from Musaddad.\n\n[Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6445","(6445) Abu Salih informed us: My grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us: Yahya and Ibn Abi 'Adi narrated from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah.\n\n(And) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id and Muhammad ibn Abi 'Adi both narrated from Ibn Abi 'Arubah from Qatadah from Anas, who said: The Prophet ﷺ did not raise his hands in any of his supplications except in the prayer for rain (istisqa'), for he would raise his hands until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the *Sahih* from Muhammad ibn Bashshar, and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna from both of them.\n\n[Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6446","(6446) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra' informed us: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from Thabit from Anas, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raising his hands in supplication until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen — meaning in the prayer for rain (istisqa').\n\nMuslim narrated it in the *Sahih* from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr.\n\n[Sahih. Muslim]"
"6447","(6447) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Abu Salamah and 'Uthman ibn 'Ali both said: Hammad narrated to us from Thabit from Anas ibn Malik that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed for rain and did thus — he raised his hands and turned their palms downward toward the earth, until I could see the whiteness of his armpits. 'Ali added: \"And he was upon the minbar.\"\n[Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
"6448","(6448) Abu 'Abdillah informed us; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Harun ibn 'Abdillah narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us from Thabit from Anas ibn Malik that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed for rain and pointed with the backs of his hands toward the sky.\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd ibn Humayd from al-Hasan ibn Musa. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6449","(6449) Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-Khaliq ibn 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Khaliq al-Mu'adhdhin informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khanb al-Baghdadi informed us in Bukhari; Abu Isma'il Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Tirmidhi narrated to us; Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me from Sulayman ibn Bilal, who said: Yahya ibn Sa'id said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: A Bedouin from the people of the desert came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the day of Jumu'ah and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the livestock have perished, the families have perished, and the people have perished.\" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raised his hands in supplication, and the people raised their hands along with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), supplicating. He said: \"We had not yet left the mosque when it rained upon us, and we continued to receive rain until the following Friday.\" Then the man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the traveler is bogged down in the mud, and the roads are blocked.\"\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih, saying: \"And Ayyub ibn Sulayman said…\" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6450","(6450) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us.\n(And) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; he said: I read to Malik from Salih ibn Kaysan from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Utbah from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the Subh prayer at Hudaybiyah following a night of cloud cover. When he turned (to face us after the prayer), he addressed the people and said: \"Do you know what your Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, has said?\" They said: \"Allah and His Messenger know best.\" He said: \"This morning, among My servants there are those who have believed in Me and those who have disbelieved. As for the one who says, 'We have been given rain by the bounty of Allah and His mercy,' that one has believed in Me and disbelieved in the stars. And as for the one who says, 'We have been given rain by such-and-such star,' that one has disbelieved in Me and believed in the stars.\"\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it from al-Qa'nabi and Ibn Abi Uways from Malik. Similarly, 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Majishun and Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Kathir narrated it from Salih ibn Kaysan. And al-Zuhri narrated it from 'Ubaydullah from Abu Hurayrah with the same meaning, as though he had heard it from both of them. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6451","(6451) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Husayn ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Sawwad narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed me; Yunus informed me from Ibn Shihab, who said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdillah narrated to us that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Have you not seen what your Lord has said? He says: 'I have not bestowed any blessing upon My servants but that a group of them have become disbelievers in it, saying: \"It is the stars, and because of the stars.\"'\"\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Amr ibn Sawwad and others. And Abu Yunus narrated it from Abu Hurayrah with the same meaning. And it was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Nasa'i]"
"6452","(6452) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Bakr al-Qattan narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf narrated to us; al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us.\n(And) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Mihran narrated to us; 'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-'Azim al-'Anbari narrated to us; al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us; 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to me; Abu Zuml narrated to me; Ibn 'Abbas narrated to me, saying: The people were given rain during the time of the Prophet (ﷺ), and the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"This morning, among the people there are those who are grateful and those who are ungrateful.\" The grateful ones said: \"This is a mercy that Allah has sent down.\" The ungrateful ones said: \"The prediction of such-and-such star has come true.\" Then this verse was revealed: \"So I swear by the setting places of the stars\" [al-Waqi'ah: 75] until His words: \"And you make your provision that you deny it\" [al-Waqi'ah: 82].\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-'Azim from al-Nadr ibn Muhammad. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6453","(6453) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, said — in the hadith of Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani — I understand the meaning of his (ﷺ) statement, and Allah knows best: Whoever says, \"We have been given rain by the bounty of Allah and His mercy,\" that is faith in Allah, for he knows that none gives rain and none bestows except Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. As for the one who says, \"We have been given rain by such-and-such star,\" in the manner that some of the people of shirk meant by attributing the rain to the claim that such-and-such star caused the rain — that is disbelief, as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, because a naw' (rising of a star) is a time, and time is a created thing that possesses no power for itself or for anyone else, and it neither causes rain nor does anything. As for the one who says, \"We have been given rain by the naw' of such-and-such,\" meaning \"We were given rain at the time of the naw' of such-and-such,\" that is like one saying, \"We were given rain in such-and-such month.\" This is not disbelief, and other such speech is more beloved to me than it. I would prefer that one say, \"We were given rain at such-and-such time.\"\nHe (al-Shafi'i) said: And it has reached me that some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when they woke up and the people had been given rain, would say: \"We have been given rain by the naw' of al-Fath,\" then they would recite: \"Whatever mercy Allah opens for people, none can withhold it\" [Fatir: 2].\nAl-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, said: And it is narrated from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said on a Friday while he was on the minbar: \"How much remains of the naw' of al-Thurayya?\" So al-'Abbas stood and said: \"Nothing remains of it except al-'Uwwa'.\" Then he supplicated and the people supplicated until he came down from the minbar, and rain fell — a rain by which the people were revived.\nAl-Shafi'i said: 'Umar's statement clarifies what I have described, for he only meant: \"How much time remains of al-Thurayya?\" based on their knowledge that Allah, the Exalted, has decreed rains at certain times, as they had experienced, just as they knew that He had decreed heat and cold at certain times, as they had experienced.\nHe said: And it has reached me that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, rebuked an elderly man from Banu Tamim who came leaning on a staff while the people had been given rain. The man said: \"How plentiful is what al-Mujaydih produced last night!\" 'Umar rejected his statement — \"How plentiful is what al-Mujaydih produced\" — because of his attribution of the rain to al-Mijdah (a star).\nImam Ahmad, may Allah have mercy on him, said: All of this is the speech of al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him. And what he narrated from some of the Companions regarding the naw' of al-Fath is narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. [Sahih. Al-Um of al-Shafi'i]"
"6454","(6454) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Malik narrated to us — that it reached him that Abu Hurayrah used to say this, and he mentioned it.\nAnd what he narrated first from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, is… [Da'if. Narrated by Malik]"
"6455","(6455) In what Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Bakr al-Qattan narrated to us; Abu al-Azhar narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; my father narrated to us from Ibn Ishaq; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi narrated to me from Salman al-Aghar, the mawla of Juhaynah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, bestows a blessing upon a people during the night, and they wake up in the morning, and most of them are ungrateful for it, saying: 'We have been given rain by the naw' of such-and-such and such-and-such.'\"\nMuhammad ibn Ibrahim said: So I narrated this hadith to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab from Salman from Abu Hurayrah, and Sa'id said: \"We have heard this from Abu Hurayrah, and someone whom I do not suspect informed me that he witnessed this prayer ground with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when he was praying for rain for the people during the year of al-Ramadah. He said: He supplicated for a long time, and the people supplicated for a long time. Then he said: 'O 'Abbas' — addressing 'Abd al-Muttalib — 'How much remains of the naw' of al-Thurayya?' Al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: 'O Commander of the Faithful, those who have knowledge of it claim that it crosses the horizon after its setting for seven days.' He said: 'By Allah, those seven days had not passed before the people were given relief.'\"\nThe Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The way to reconcile between the two narrations is what al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, mentioned. [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Narrated by Ahmad]"
"6456","(6456) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra' and Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Harashi narrated to us.\n(And) Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Imam informed us; Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Zurqwayh narrated to us; Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Ghalib al-Nasawi narrated to us; they said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Sulayman informed us from Thabit al-Bunani from Anas, who said: Anas said: Rain fell upon us while we were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) removed his garment until the rain touched him. We said: \"O Messenger of Allah, why did you do this?\" He said: \"Because it is a recent blessing from his Lord.\"\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and it was narrated concerning it from Ibn 'Abbas. [Sahih. Al-Silm]"
"6457","(6457) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us that al-Rabīʿ informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that one whom I do not suspect narrated from Yazīd ibn al-Hād that the Prophet ﷺ, when a torrent would flow, would say: \"Come out with us to that which Allah has made pure, so that we may purify ourselves from it and praise Allah for it.\" This is a broken (munqaṭiʿ) chain, and something similar has been narrated from ʿUmar. [Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī]"
"6458","(6458) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Ḥurfī informed us in Baghdad in al-Ḥarbiyyah that ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated from Hishām ibn Saʿd, who narrated to us that Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from ʿAmr ibn Saʿd, the companion of al-Jār and freedman of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by us while returning from the Ḥajj, accompanied by a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: \"Bathe in the sea, for indeed it is blessed.\" Then he called for towels, and they descended and bathed. [Weak. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
"6459","(6459) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us that ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk al-Bazzār narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us that Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb al-Zamʿī narrated from Abū Ḥāzim from Sahl ibn Saʿd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Two supplications are not rejected — or rarely rejected: the supplication at the time of the call (to prayer) and at the time of battle when they engage one another in combat.\"\n\nMūsā said: And Rizq ibn Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Madanī narrated to me from Abū Ḥāzim from Sahl ibn Saʿd from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \"And during the rain.\"\n\nSomething similar has been narrated from Abī Umāmah from the Prophet ﷺ, except that ʿUfayr ibn Maʿdān narrated it via a different route. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6460","(6460) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Saʿd al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us that al-Haytham ibn Khārijah Abū Aḥmad narrated to us that al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from ʿUfayr ibn Maʿdān, who narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn ʿĀmir narrated from Abī Umāmah, who heard him narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: \"The gates of the heavens are opened and supplication is answered in four situations: at the meeting of the ranks (in battle), at the descent of rain, at the establishment of the prayer, and at the sighting of the Kaʿbah.\" [Munkar. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī]"
"6461","(6461) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us that Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us that Ḥumayd narrated to us that he heard Anas ibn Mālik say: When a violent wind blew, that would be recognized in the face of the Prophet ﷺ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6462","(6462) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us that ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us that Abū al-Naḍr narrated to him from Sulaymān ibn Yasār from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \"I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughing fully so that I could see his uvula; rather, he would only smile.\" She said: \"And when he would see clouds or wind, it would be recognized in his face. So I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, when the people see the clouds they rejoice, hoping that there will be rain in them, but when I see you, upon seeing them, displeasure is recognized in your face.' He said: 'O ʿĀʾishah, what would make me safe from there being punishment in it? A people were punished by the wind, and a people saw the punishment.'\" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited: {And when they saw it as a cloud advancing towards their valleys, they said: \"This cloud will give us rain!\"} [al-Aḥqāf: 24] — the verse.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā and others, and Muslim narrated it from Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf and others, all of them from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6463","(6463) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us that Abū al-Ṭāhir informed us that Ibn Wahb said: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrate to us from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \"When the wind blew, the Prophet ﷺ would say: 'O Allah, I ask You for its good, the good of what is within it, and the good for which it was sent, and I seek refuge in You from its evil, the evil of what is within it, and the evil for which it was sent.'\" She said: \"When the sky became overcast, his colour would change, and he would go out and come in, advance and retreat. Then when it rained, his distress would be relieved.\" ʿĀʾishah recognized that from him and asked him about it. He said: \"Perhaps, O ʿĀʾishah, it is as the people of ʿĀd said: {And when they saw it as a cloud advancing towards their valleys, they said: 'This cloud will give us rain!'}\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Ṭāhir. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6464","(6464) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū Ṣāliḥ and Ibn Bukayr narrated from al-Layth from Yūnus from Ibn Shihāb from Thābit ibn Qays, one of Banī Zurayq, that Abū Hurayrah said. And Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us that Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us that ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah informed us that al-Awzāʿī narrated to me that Muḥammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī narrated to me that Thābit al-Zuraqī narrated that Abū Hurayrah said: \"A wind struck the people on the road to Makkah while ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) was performing Ḥajj, and it became severe upon him. So ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said to those around him: 'What is the wind?' But they did not respond to him with anything. Then that which ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) had asked about reached me, so I urged my mount until I caught up with him and said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, I was informed that you asked about the wind. Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"The wind is from the spirit (rūḥ) of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. It comes with mercy and it comes with punishment. So do not curse it, and ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for its good, and seek refuge in Allah from its evil.\"'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad, Ibn Ḥibbān]"
"6465","(6465) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Muʿādh ibn al-Mathannī narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated. And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us that al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ that he heard ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, say: \"When the day of wind and clouds came, that would be recognized in his face, and he would advance and retreat. And when it rained, he would be pleased and that would leave him.\" ʿĀʾishah said: \"So I asked him, and he said: 'I feared that it might be a punishment inflicted upon my Ummah.'\" And he would say when he saw the rain: \"Mercy.\" In the narration of Muʿādh: \"His distress would be relieved and that would leave him.\" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6466","(6466) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us that Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdūs ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr al-Simsār narrated to us that Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Rāzī narrated to us that Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us that Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad from ʿĀʾishah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he would see the rain, would say: \"O Allah, make it a beneficial and pleasant rain (ṣayyiban hanīʾan).\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muqātil from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak.\n\nAnd similarly ʿUqayl narrated it from Nāfiʿ. And al-Awzāʿī narrated it from Nāfiʿ, and he said in the hadith: \"O Allah, make it a beneficial and pleasant rain (ijʿalhu ṣayyiban hanīʾan).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Al-Bukhārī]"
"6467","(6467) Abu al-Hasan al-'Alawi informed us; Abu al-Fadl 'Ubdus ibn al-Husayn al-Simsar informed us; Abu Hatim al-Razi informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim al-Qurashi, Duhaym, informed us; al-Walid ibn Muslim informed us; al-Awza'i informed me; Nafi' narrated it with some additional wording. Al-Bukhari cited his narration as evidence, and al-Walid ibn Muslim mentioned that al-Awza'i heard from Nafi' through this chain from him. Yahya ibn Ma'in used to claim that al-Awza'i did not hear from Nafi', the freedman of Ibn 'Umar. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6468","(6468) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-'Abbas, meaning ibn al-Walid ibn Mazid, informed us; my father informed me; al-Awza'i informed me; a man informed me on the authority of Nafi' that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad informed him on the authority of 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him), and he mentioned this hadith. [Sahih in other than its chain. Al-Nasa'i]"
"6469","(6469) Abu Salih ibn Abu Tahir al-'Anbari informed us; my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur informed us; Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us; Muhammad ibn Bishr informed us; Mis'ar narrated on the authority of al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, on the authority of his father, on the authority of 'A'ishah, who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saw clouds or a darkening sky, he would become anxious. When it rained, he would say: 'O Allah, make it a beneficial rain.'\" [Sahih. Al-Shafi'i narrated it]"
"6470","(6470) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us; Ishaq ibn al-Hasan al-Harbi informed us; 'Affan informed us; 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad informed us; Hajjaj ibn Arta'ah informed me; Abu Matar narrated to me on the authority of Salim ibn 'Abd Allah, on the authority of his father, who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) heard thunder and lightning, he would say: 'O Allah, do not kill us with Your wrath, and do not destroy us with Your punishment, and grant us well-being before that.'\" [Da'if. Al-Tirmidhi narrated it]"
"6471","(6471) Abu al-Hasan ibn Abu al-Ma'ruf al-Faqih informed us; Abu Sahl, Bishr ibn Ahmad informed us; Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Bayhaqi informed us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us; Malik ibn Anas narrated on the authority of 'Amir ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, on the authority of 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, that when he heard thunder, he would cease conversation and say: \"Glory be to Him Whom the thunder glorifies with His praise, and the angels too, out of fear of Him.\" Then he would say: \"Indeed, this is a severe warning for the people of the earth.\" [Sahih. Malik narrated it]"
"6472","(6472) Abu Sa'id ibn Abu 'Amr informed us; Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us. He said: \"I said to Ibn Tawus: 'What would your father say when he heard thunder?' He said: 'He would say: \"Glory be to Him whose glorification You have declared.\"'\" Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: \"It is as though he was referring to the saying of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {And the thunder glorifies with His praise} [al-Ra'd: 13].\" [Sahih. Ibn Abi Shaybah narrated it]"
"6473","(6473) Abu Sa'id ibn Abu 'Amr informed us; Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; one whom I do not suspect informed me; Sulayman ibn 'Abd Allah narrated on the authority of 'Urwah, and it is in the musnad that Ibn Matar compiled, and we heard it from Abu Zakariyya and others, on the authority of Sulayman ibn 'Abd Allah, on the authority of 'Uwaymir, on the authority of 'Urwah. And in the expanded version that we heard from Abu Sa'id, Ibn 'Uwaymir. The correct narration is that of Abu Sa'id, for Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar narrated it on the authority of Sulayman ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Uwaymir, who said: \"I was with 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, and I pointed with my hand toward the clouds, so he said: 'Do not do that, for the Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade pointing toward it.'\" Muhammad ibn Muhammad informed us; Abu al-Husayn al-Fasawi informed us; Abu 'Ali al-Lu'lu'i informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Muhammad ibn Qudamah informed me; Jarir narrated on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned it. Abu Dawud said: \"And Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed me; Abu 'Asim narrated on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, on the authority of Ibn Abu Husayn, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade pointing toward the rain.\" This is the preserved narration, as a mursal hadith. [Da'if. Al-Shafi'i narrated it]"
"6474","(6474) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; al-Kudaymi informed us; Abu 'Asim al-Nabil informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, meaning Ibn Abu Husayn, informed us. He said: \"Abu 'Asim narrated it to me, and Ibn Jurayj informed me on the authority of 'Ata', on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas, who said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade pointing toward the rain.'\" And it has been narrated through another weak chain. [Very Da'if. Abu Dawud]"
"6475","(6475) Abu Sa'id ibn Abu 'Amr informed us; Abu al-'Abbas informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; the trustworthy one informed us that Mujahid used to say: \"Thunder is an angel, and lightning is the wings of the angel with which he drives the clouds.\" Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: \"What Mujahid said resembles the apparent meaning of the Qur'an.\" [Sahih. Ibn Majah narrated it]"
"6476","(6476) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad informed us; Muhammad ibn al-Salt informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Abu Zaidah informed us. He said: \"I heard 'Ikrimah, and a man asked him about His saying: {And the thunder glorifies with His praise} [al-Ra'd: 13]. He said: 'It is an angel who drives the clouds as a camel-driver drives the camels.'\" And it has been narrated on the authority of 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). [Sahih. Ibn Jarir narrated it]"
"6477","Abu Muhammad al-Hashimi narrated from his father, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"Thunder is an angel, and lightning is a whip of iron.\" [Al-Tabarani]"
"6478","It was also narrated by Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab from Hammad ibn Salamah, from al-Mughirah ibn Muslim, the freed slave of al-Hasan ibn Ali, from his father, that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"Thunder is the angel.\" Abu Abdullah and Abu Sa'id reported it, saying: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to us, Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Weak. Ibn Jarir]"
"6479","Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us, Abu Bakr al-Qattan narrated to us, Abu al-Azhar narrated to us, Rawh narrated to us, al-Thawri narrated from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Sa'id ibn Ashwa', from Rabi'ah ibn al-Abyad, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"Lightning is the whips of the angels.\" [Weak. Ibn Jarir]"
"6480","Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"Drought does not mean that you receive no rain; rather, drought is that you receive rain, and you receive rain, yet the earth produces nothing.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6481","Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Ali al-Hafiz narrated to us, Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Maktum narrated to us, Abu 'Attab Sahl ibn Hammad narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from 'Abdullah, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"No year is rainier than another, and no southerly wind blows except that a valley is flooded.\" It was thus narrated as a marfu' (elevated) hadith with this chain, but the correct version is mawquf (stopped). [Sahih]"
"6482","Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us, Abu 'Uthman 'Amr ibn 'Abdullah al-Basri narrated to us, Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us, Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, Sufyan narrated from al-Rukayn, from his father, who said: 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: \"No year has more rain than another, but Allah directs it as He wills.\" [Sahih. Ibn Ma'in considered it weak]"
"6483","Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us in Baghdad, Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik narrated to us, Yazid, that is ibn Harun, informed us, Sulayman, meaning al-Taymi, narrated from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: \"No year has less rain than another, but Allah, the Most High, distributes it wherever He wills.\" Then he recited this verse: \"And We have distributed it among them in order that they might remember, but most of mankind refuse to be grateful.\" [Al-Furqan: 50]"
"6484","Abu Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari narrated to us, Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahmuyah al-'Askari narrated to us in Basra, Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi narrated to us, Abu Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"I was granted victory by the east wind (al-Saba), and 'Ad was destroyed by the west wind (al-Dabur).\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Shu'bah. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6485","Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us verbally, Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri narrated to us in Makkah, al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'faranl narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated from Mas'ud ibn Malik, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"I was granted victory by the east wind (al-Saba), and 'Ad was destroyed by the west wind (al-Dabur).\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Kurayb and others from Abu Mu'awiyah. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6486","Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us, Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan narrated to us, Ishaq ibn al-Hasan al-Harbi narrated to us, 'Affan narrated to us, Abu 'Awanah narrated from al-A'mash, from al-Minhal ibn 'Amr, from Qays ibn Sakan, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, regarding the verse: \"And We send down from the rain clouds water pouring forth abundantly\" [An-Naba': 14], he said: \"Allah sends the wind, and it carries the water from the sky. It passes through the clouds until it flows as a full-flowing she-camel flows with milk. Then it is sent from the sky in channels like mountain passes, and the winds drive it, and it descends in scattered drops.\" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tabarani]"
"6487","(6487) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed us, Sulaymān narrated to us, from al-Minhāl ibn ʿAmr, from Qays ibn Sakan, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: \"Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sends the winds, and they carry the water from the sky. They pass through the clouds until they drip as a she-camel drips with milk, and then they bring rain.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭabarānī]"
"6488","(6488) Abū Saʿīd narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said: \"It has reached me that Qatādah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'No southerly wind blows except that it causes valleys to flow.'\" Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: \"He means that Allah created it to blow as glad tidings before His mercy, namely rain.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
"6489","(6489) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who heard Yazīd ibn Jaʿdabah narrating from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mikhraq, from Abū Dharr, who traced it back to the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: \"Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, created in Paradise a wind; after it, at a distance of seven years, there is a closed door. The spirit (ar-rūḥ) only reaches you through the gaps of that door. Were that door to be opened, it would overturn everything between the heaven and the earth. It is, in the sight of Allah, al-Azyab, and among you it is the southerly wind (al-janūb).\" [Munkar. Narrated by al-Ḥumaydī]"
"6490","(6490) Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Qurashī al-Harawī narrated to us on the road to Makkah, on the bank of the Euphrates. Abū al-Qāsim Manṣūr ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah narrated to us, al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Makhzūmī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd narrated to us, he said: \"I heard Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ — raising it (to the Prophet, if Allah wills) — that when the wind became fierce, he would say: 'O Allah, make it fruitful (laqḥan) and not barren (ʿaqīman).'\" [Jayyid. Al-Ḥākim]"
"6491","(6491) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mukrim narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Bakr narrated to us, Hishām narrated to us, from Muḥammad — who is Ibn Sīrīn — from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Do not revile Time (al-dahr), for indeed Allah is al-Dahr.\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Ḥassān and others. Al-Shāfiʿī said, in the narration of Ḥarmalah: \"Its interpretation — and Allah knows best — is that it was the custom of the Arabs to condemn and revile al-dahr when calamities befell them, such as death, old age, destruction, or other afflictions. They would say: 'It is al-dahr that destroys us,' meaning the night and day, which are the two that perish and are renewed. They would say: 'The calamities of al-dahr have struck them, and al-dahr has annihilated them,' thus attributing the night and day — which bring about these things — to al-dahr, and condemning al-dahr, saying: 'It is that which causes our destruction and does these things to us.' So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Do not revile al-dahr,' since it is that which causes your destruction and does these things to you. For when you revile the one who does these things, you are in fact reviling Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, for Allah is the Doer of all these things.\" The Sheikh said: \"The chains of this ḥadīth and what some of its narrators preserved in the way of additional wording are evidence for the soundness of this interpretation.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6492","(6492) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, among others, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me, he said: Abū Hurayrah said: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say.\" (Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk and Ibn Milḥān narrated to us, they said: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Yūnus, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me, he said: Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: 'The son of Ādam reviles al-dahr, and I am al-dahr; in My hand are the night and day.'\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from Ḥarmalah, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6493","(6493) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, al-Zuhrī narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: 'The son of Ādam harms Me; he reviles al-dahr, and I am al-dahr. In My hand is the affair; I alternate the night and day.'\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6494","(6494) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Zakariyyā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, Isḥāq narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, he said: \"The people of the Jāhiliyyah used to say: 'It is al-dahr that destroys us, it is that which causes us to die and gives us life.' So Allah refuted their statement.\" Al-Zuhrī said, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, says: 'The son of Ādam harms Me; he reviles al-dahr, and I am al-dahr. I alternate his night and his day, and if I will, I can seize them both.'\" Then Sufyān recited this verse: {And they say, \"There is not but our worldly life; we die and live, and nothing destroys us except al-dahr.\"} [Al-Jāthiyah: 24] Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, without the statement of Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān]"
"6495","(6495) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ḥajjāj and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, they said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Jarīr informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd and Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, from Jarīr, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Sufyān, who said: \"I heard Jābir saying: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: 'Indeed, between a man and shirk and kufr is the abandonment of ṣalāh.'\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6496","(6496) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, al-Mujawwiz — who is al-Ḥasan ibn Sahl — narrated to us, Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is nothing between a servant and kufr except the abandonment of ṣalāh.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6497","(6497) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Between a man and shirk and kufr lies the abandonment of ṣalāh.\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Ghassān, from Abū ʿĀṣim, with this wording. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6498","(6498) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Between a servant and kufr lies the abandonment of ṣalāh.\" And likewise Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Raqāshī narrated it from Ḥammād ibn Zayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than him. Tirmidhī]"
"6499","(6499) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī from the original of his book both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Mu'adhdhin informed us: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad, Abū Bakr ibn Khanb al-Baghdādī, narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated to us, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The covenant between us and them is ṣalāh; so whoever abandons it has committed kufr.\" And in the ḥadīth of ʿAlī, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, and the remainder is the same. And it has been narrated to us from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: \"There is no share in Islam for the one who abandons ṣalāh.\" And from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): \"Whoever does not perform ṣalāh is a kāfir.\" And from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd: \"Whoever does not perform ṣalāh has no religion.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than him. Tirmidhī narrated it]"
"6500","(6500) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us: Yazīd informed us: Muḥammad ibn Muṭarrif narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from ʿAbdullāh al-Ṣunābihī, who said: Abū Muḥammad claimed that witr is obligatory. So ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"Abū Muḥammad has lied. I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Five prayers which Allah has prescribed — whoever performs their wuḍūʾ well, and prays them at their proper times, and completes their rukūʿ and their khushūʿ, then Allah has a covenant with him that He will forgive him. And whoever does not do so, there is no covenant for him with Allah; if He wills, He will forgive him, and if He wills, He will punish him.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6501","(6501) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Musnadī narrated to us: Ḥarmī ibn ʿUmarah narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Wāqid ibn Muḥammad — meaning the son of Zayd ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar — who said: I heard my father narrate from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, and they establish ṣalāh, and they pay zakāh. When they have done that, they have protected from me their blood and their wealth, except by the right of Islam, and their reckoning is with Allah.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh al-Musnadī, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"6502","(6502) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Salamah ibn Shabīb and ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh both said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us: Maʿmar informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yazīd al-Laythī, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAdī ibn al-Khiyār, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAdī al-Anṣārī narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), while he was sitting among the people, a man came seeking his permission to speak privately with him. He gave him permission, and he spoke to him privately about killing a man from the hypocrites, seeking his permission regarding it. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raised his voice and said: \"Does he not testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah?\" He said: \"Yes, but it is not a testimony (of sincerity).\" He said: \"Does he not testify that I am Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Yes, but it is not a testimony (of sincerity).\" He said: \"Does he not pray?\" He said: \"Yes, but it is not a prayer (of substance).\" He said: \"Those are the ones whom I have been forbidden to kill.\" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Qaṭṭān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"6503","(6503) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath narrated to us: Musaddad and Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAtakī both said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Muʿallā ibn Ziyād and Hishām ibn Ḥassān, from al-Ḥasan, from Ḍabbah ibn Miḥṣan, from Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There will be over you imāms — you will recognize some of their actions and reject others.\" Sulaymān said: Hishām said: \"Whoever rejects with his heart has been absolved, and whoever dislikes has been saved, but whoever is pleased and follows...\" It was said: \"O Messenger of Allah, shall we not fight them?\" He said: \"No, so long as they pray.\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Rabīʿ, Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6504","(6504) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī — may Allah have mercy on him — narrated to us by dictation: Abū Ḥāmid Aḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ dictated it to us from his memory: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from al-Aʿmash, from Abī Wāʾil, from ʿAbdullāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Paradise is closer to one of you than the strap of his sandal, and the Fire is likewise.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Masʿūd, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6505","(6505) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī al-Faqīh informed us: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Prophet (ﷺ) drew lines and drew a line to one side, then said: \"Do you know what this is? This is the example of the son of Ādam and his hopes, and that line is his lifespan. While he is hoping, death comes to him.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6506","(6506) And al-Sayyid Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-Ḥasanī — may Allah have mercy on him — narrated to us by dictation: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Hāshim narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The son of Ādam grows old, yet two things remain in him: greed and hope.\" Al-Bukhārī said: And Shuʿbah narrated it from Qatādah, and he mentioned it. And Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6507","(6507) It was narrated to us by Abu al-Hasan al-Alawi, who informed us: 'Abdullah narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Hashim narrated to us: Waki' narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The heart of the old man remains youthful in its love for two things: the accumulation of wealth and long life.\" Both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah. [Sahih. al-Bukhari]"
"6508","(6508) It was narrated to us by Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Shirazi al-Faqih and Abu Ahmad 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Mihrajan, who both said: It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'quq al-Shaybani: Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us: Abu 'Asim narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"If the son of Adam had two valleys of wealth, he would seek a third like them. And nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam except dust. And Allah accepts the repentance of whoever repents.\" Ibn 'Abbas said: \"I do not know whether this is from the Qur'an or not.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu 'Asim, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Ibn Jurayj. And it has been narrated to us from Ubayy ibn Ka' radiyAllahu 'anhu that they used to consider it to be from the Qur'an until the verses were revealed: \"Competition in [worldly] increase diverts you, until you visit the graves\" (al-Takathur, 102:1–2) to the end of the surah. [Sahih. al-Bukhari]"
"6509","(6509) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, who both said: It was narrated to us by al-'Abbas ibn Ya'qub: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from 'Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"Which of you loves the wealth of his heir more than his own wealth?\" They said: \"There is none among us who does not love his own wealth more than the wealth of his heir.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Know that there is none among you except that the wealth of his heir is more beloved to him than his own wealth. Your wealth is what you have sent forth, and the wealth of your heir is what you have left behind.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and others from Abu Mu'awiyah, and al-Bukhari narrated it from another route from al-A'mash. [Sahih. al-Bukhari]"
"6510","(6510) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz, who informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Isa ibn Mina narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Kathir narrated from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The servant says, 'My wealth, my wealth!' Yet from his wealth, he has only three things: what he ate and consumed, or what he wore and wore out, or what he gave and sent forth. Everything else is departing and he leaves it for the people.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from another route from Muhammad ibn Ja'far. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6511","(6511) It was narrated to us by Abu Tahir al-Faqih, who informed us: Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hafs narrated to me: My father narrated to me: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated from al-Hajjaj, from Qatadah, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, the world is sweet and verdant, and indeed Allah is placing you as successors therein, observing how you act. So beware of the world and the trial of women.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Abu Maslamah from Abu Nadrah. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6512","(6512) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz: Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn Yazid al-Hafiz informed us: Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Sulayman narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Tufawi, Abu al-Mundhir — and he was trustworthy — narrated to me: Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to me: Mujahid narrated from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took hold of my shoulder and said: \"Be in this world as though you are a stranger or a wayfarer.\" He said: And Ibn 'Umar said to me: \"When you reach the morning, do not await the evening, and when you reach the evening, do not await the morning. And take from your good deeds [to atone] for your evil ones.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn al-Madini. [Sahih. al-Bukhari]"
"6513","(6513) It was narrated to us by Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak, rahimahullah, who informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever has wronged his brother in his honour or his wealth, let him settle it with him before the Day of Resurrection comes, when neither dinars nor dirhams will be accepted. If he has righteous deeds, they will be taken from him and given to his companion; and if he has no righteous deeds, the evil deeds of his companion will be taken and placed upon him.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas from Ibn Abi Dhi'b with the same meaning, except that he said: \"So let him seek his forgiveness from him today, before there will be neither dinars nor dirhams.\" [Sahih. al-Bukhari]"
"6514","(6514) It was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz, Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, and Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Fami, who all said: It was narrated to us by Abu al-'Abbas al-Asam: Abu 'Utbah Ahmad ibn al-Faraj al-Hijazi al-Himsi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to me: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam — (H) And it was narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn Furak, who informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubarak — (H) And it was narrated to us by Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz, who informed us: al-Hasan ibn Halim al-Marwazi informed us: Abu al-Muwajjah informed us: 'Abdan informed us: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam al-Ghassani narrated from Damrah ibn Habib, from Shaddad ibn Aws, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said: \"The prudent one is he who takes account of himself and works for what is after death, and the helpless one is he who follows his own desires and entertains hopes regarding Allah.\" This is the wording of the hadith of Muhammad ibn Himyar. And in the narration of Ibn al-Mubarak, he said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said.\" [Da'if. al-Tirmidhi]"
"6515","(6515) It was narrated to us by Abu Mansur Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Nuh, from the descendants of Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, in Kufa, who informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duham narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hazim ibn Abi Ghurzah informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad, meaning Ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated from Abu Raja' 'Abdullah ibn Waqid, from Muhammad ibn Malik, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at a funeral. When we reached the grave, he knelt upon it. I looked around and faced him, and he wept until the earth was moistened. Then he said: \"O my brothers, prepare for a day like this.\" [Da'if. Ibn Majah]"
"6516","(6516) It was narrated to us by Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Saqa, who informed us: Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad, meaning Ibn 'Abdus, narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Sijzi narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: al-Darawardi narrated to me: 'Amr ibn Abi 'Amr narrated from al-Mutalib, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever loves his worldly life harms his Hereafter, and whoever loves his Hereafter harms his worldly life. So prefer that which endures over that which perishes.\" [Da'if. Ahmad]"
"6517","(6517) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī narrated from Maʿn ibn Muḥammad, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \"Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, has closed the excuse for a servant whose term of life He has prolonged until he reaches seventy or sixty years of age.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Muṭahhar, from ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī, and he said: \"sixty years of age.\" He also said: It was followed by Abū Ḥāzim and Ibn ʿAjlān from al-Maqburī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6518","(6518) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Bajalī al-Muqriʾ in Kūfah informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Dārim narrated to us, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah al-Zubayrī narrated to us, Ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from his father, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \"Whoever Allah grants a lifespan of sixty years, He has indeed closed the excuse for him regarding his lifespan.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Ibn Ḥibbān, Aḥmad]"
"6519","(6519) It is narrated from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Whoever reaches sixty years of life, Allah the Most High has closed the excuse for him regarding his lifespan.\""
"6520","(6520) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Mihrān narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khuthaym, from Mujāhid, regarding the statement of Allah, the Mighty and Sublime: {Did We not grant you a life long enough for whoever would reflect to reflect therein, and the warner came to you?} [Fāṭir: 37] — Ibn ʿAbbās said: \"It is sixty years.\"\n\nThis is mawqūf. It was also narrated by Ibrāhīm ibn al-Faḍl al-Madanī, though he is not strong. [Ḥasan — al-Ṭabarī]"
"6521","(6521) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Fudayk narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn al-Faḍl, from Ibn Abī Ḥusayn al-Makkī, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"On the Day of Resurrection it will be said: 'Where are those who reached sixty years of age?' That is the lifespan regarding which Allah said: {Did We not grant you a life long enough for whoever would reflect to reflect therein, and the warner came to you?} [Fāṭir: 37].\"\n\nIbn Abī Fudayk said: And al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAṭiyyah narrated to me from one who narrated to him, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"By it he meant old age (al-shayb).\" [Very weak]"
"6522","(6522) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl al-Sāmirī in Baghdād narrated to us, Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah al-ʿAbdī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad al-Muḥāribī narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The lifespans of my Ummah are between sixty and seventy, and few among them will exceed that.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain — al-Tirmidhī]"
"6523","(6523) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Dārabijardī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān informed us, Ibn al-Mubārak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Hind narrated from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"There are two blessings of which many people are deprived: health and free time.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6524","(6524) Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us this ḥadīth with this same chain of narration (isnād) and this same text (matn). [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6525","(6525) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Yazīd narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated from Yūnus and Ḥumayd, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Kārizī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Ḥumayd, Yūnus, and Thābit, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Bakrah, that a man said: \"O Messenger of Allah, who among people is the best?\" He ﷺ said: \"The one whose lifespan is long and whose deeds are good.\" It was then asked: \"And who among people is the worst?\" He ﷺ said: \"The one whose lifespan is long and whose deeds are evil.\" [Ḥasan in other than this chain — al-Tirmidhī]"
"6526","(6526) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to me, Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from ʿAmr ibn Qays al-Kindī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Busr, who said: Two Bedouins came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him. One of them said: \"O Messenger of Allah, who among people is the best?\" He ﷺ said: \"The one whose lifespan is long and whose deeds are good.\" The other said: \"O Messenger of Allah, the ordinances of Islam have become too numerous for me, so tell me of a matter by which I may hold fast.\" He ﷺ said: \"Let your tongue remain moist with the remembrance of Allah.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain — al-Tirmidhī]"
"6527","(6527) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Mukram ibn Aḥmad al-Qāḍī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī informed us; Ayyūb ibn Sulaymān ibn Bilāl informed us; Abū Bakr narrated to me from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, who said: Zayd ibn Aslam said: Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Shall I not inform you of the best of you and the worst of you?\" They said: \"Yes, indeed.\" He said: \"The best of you are those who live the longest and are the best in deeds.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Ḥākim.]"
"6528","(6528) Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdān al-Naysābūrī and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzzakī both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Shall I not inform you of the best of you?\" They said: \"Yes, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"Those who live the longest and are the best in deeds.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains. Aḥmad.]"
"6529","(6529) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard ʿAmr ibn Maymūn narrating from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rubayʿah, who said: I heard ʿUbayd ibn Khālid say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) established brotherhood between two men. One of them was killed, and the other remained alive and then died. They performed the funeral prayer over him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"What did you say?\" They said: \"We supplicated to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, to forgive him, to have mercy on him, and to join him with his companion.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Then where are his prayers after his prayers, and where are his deeds after his deeds?\" — and I think he said: \"And where is his fasting after his fasting?\" — \"By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the distance between them is greater than that between the heavens and the earth.\" ʿAmr ibn Maymūn said: \"This hadith impressed me greatly because it was narrated to me with a connected chain.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd.]"
"6530","(6530) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us on a Friday, three days remaining or thereabouts in Shaʿbān, in the year two hundred and sixty-five; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Lahīʿah, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, and Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ, from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Usāmah ibn al-Hād, that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī narrated to him from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Taymī, who said: Two men from Balī came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and their acceptance of Islam was simultaneous. One of them was more diligent in striving than the other. The one who strove harder went out on a military expedition and was martyred. The other remained alive for a year after him and then passed away. Ṭalḥah said: \"While I was at the gate of Paradise in a dream, I saw the two of them. A gatekeeper emerged from Paradise and gave permission to enter to the one who had died last. Then he returned and gave permission to enter to the one who had been martyred. Then he turned to me and said: 'Go back, for your time has not yet come.'\" Ṭalḥah awoke and related this to the people, and they were astonished. This reached the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: \"What about this astonishes you?\" They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, this one was the more diligent of the two in striving, yet he was martyred in the cause of Allah, and the other entered Paradise before him.\" He said: \"Did he not remain alive for a year after him, and did he not reach Ramaḍān and fast it?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"And he performed such and such number of prostrations in the year.\" They said: \"Yes.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Then the distance between them is greater than that between the heavens and the earth.\" Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr corroborated it from Abū Salamah. [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Mājah.]"
"6531","(6531) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd, from ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, who said: \"I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and he was suffering from fever. I touched him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you are suffering from a severe fever.' He said: 'Yes, I suffer from fever as two of you suffer.' I said: 'That is because you have a double reward.' He said: 'Yes. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, no Muslim is afflicted by any harm from illness or otherwise except that Allah expiates his sins thereby, just as a tree sheds its leaves.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī.]"
"6532","(6532) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us; Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; al-Aʿmash narrated it with the same meaning and said: \"I placed my hand upon him.\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Abū Muʿāwiyah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through other chains from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim and al-Bukhārī.]"
"6533","(6533) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān al-Murādī and Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī narrated — al-Rabīʿ said: \"He narrated to us,\" and Baḥr said: \"He informed us\" — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb said: Hishām ibn Saʿd informed me from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was suffering from fever, with a blanket over him. He placed his hand upon him and felt the heat of the fever above the blanket. Abū Saʿīd said: \"How intense is the heat of your fever, O Messenger of Allah!\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"It is so with us — the trial is intensified for us, and the reward is multiplied for us.\" Then he said: \"O Messenger of Allah, who among people are tried the most severely?\" He said: \"The Prophets.\" He said: \"Then who?\" He said: \"Then the scholars.\" He said: \"Then who?\" He said: \"Then the righteous. One of them would be tried with poverty until he finds nothing but a cloak to wear, and would be tried with lice until it kills him. Yet none of them would be more joyful at receiving a trial than one of you would be at receiving a gift.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Reported by al-Ḥākim.]"
"6534","(6534) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah, Hishām, and Ḥammād ibn Salamah — all of them from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mūsā al-Ashyab narrated to us; Shaybān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from ʿĀṣim, from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waggāṣ, from his father, who said: \"I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): 'Who among people are tried the most severely?' He said: 'The Prophets, then the next best, then the next best. A man is tried according to the strength of his religion. If he is firm in his religion, his trial is intensified. If there is softness in his religion, he is tried according to the state of his religion. Trials do not cease to afflict a servant until they leave him walking upon the earth with no sin upon him.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains. al-Tirmidhī.]"
"6535","(6535) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us orally; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥuṣsin al-Sahmī said: I heard Muḥammad ibn Qays ibn Makhramah narrating from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"When the verse was revealed — 'Whoever does an evil deed will be recompensed for it' (al-Nisāʾ: 123) — it became difficult for the Muslims. They mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Seek nearness, be upright, and give glad tidings, for everything that befalls a Muslim is an expiation for him — even the thorn that pricks him or the injury that befalls him.'\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
"6536","(6536) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated from Sufyān, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Zuhayr, from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"O Messenger of Allah, how can there be salvation after this verse: 'Whoever does an evil deed will be recompensed for it' (al-Nisāʾ: 123)? Will we be recompensed for every evil we commit?\" He said: \"May Allah forgive you, O Abū Bakr\" — three times — \"Do you not fall ill? Do you not grieve? Do you not toil? Do you not suffer hardships?\" He said: \"I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'That is what you are recompensed for in this world.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains. al-Tirmidhī.]"
"6537","(6537) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Abī ʿAlī ibn al-Saqqā al-Isfarāʾīnī informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Aṣamm narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us — Abū Usāmah narrated to me — al-Walīd ibn Kathīr narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī and Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with both of them, that they both heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: \"No fatigue, disease, sorrow, or grief befalls a believer, even if it were the prick of a thorn, except that Allah expiates thereby some of his sins.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Abū Usāmah, and al-Bukhārī reported it through another chain from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6538","(6538) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī informed me. (Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — Abū Muḥammad al-Muzanī informed us — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us — Abū al-Yamān informed us — Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī — ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that ʿĀʾishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"No affliction befalls a Muslim except that Allah expiates thereby his sins, even the prick of a thorn.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6539","(6539) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān in Baghdad informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī. (Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to us — Yūnus narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"No affliction befalls a believer except that Allah expiates thereby his sins, even the prick of a thorn.\" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yūnus ibn Yazīd. In the narration of Maʿmar, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"No illness or pain befalls a believer except that it serves as an expiation for his sins, even the prick of a thorn that pricks him, or the stumble that trips him.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir, from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī reported it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6540","(6540) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr al-Muḥāribī in Kūfah narrated to us — Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Zuhrī al-Qāḍī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: \"No believer is pricked by a thorn or anything less except that Allah thereby erases a sin for him and raises him thereby in rank.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6541","(6541) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated. (Ḥ) He also said: And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"No believer is pricked by a thorn or anything less except that Allah thereby raises him in rank or erases thereby a sin for him.\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr and Isḥāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim, al-Tirmidhī]"
"6542","(6542) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us — Abū Ghassān narrated to us — Khālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us — Wāṣil, mawlā of Abī ʿUyaynah, narrated from Bashshār ibn Abī Sayf, from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿIyāḍ ibn Ghuṭayf, who said: We came to visit Abū ʿUbaydah, and his wife Ḥayfah was with him. We said: How did he spend the night? She said: He spent the night with reward (ajr). Abū ʿUbaydah said: I did not spend the night with reward. The people fell silent. He said: Will you not ask me about the matter? They said: What you said did not astonish us, so why should you ask us? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: \"Whoever spends an expenditure in the cause of Allah, it is multiplied for him seven hundred times; and whoever spends an expenditure upon his family or removes a harmful object from the path, a good deed is recorded for him as ten times its like; and fasting is a shield so long as one does not tear it; and whoever is afflicted with a trial in his body, his sins are thereby expiated.\" Khālid said, meaning: his sins are erased. [Ḍaʿīf — reported by Aḥmad]"
"6543","(6543) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us — Abū al-Azhar narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Trials continue to befall the believing man and the believing woman in themselves, their wealth, and their children, until they meet Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, with no sin upon them.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it — al-Tirmidhī]"
"6544","(6544) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated from Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd, who said: Jaʿfar ibn Rabīʿah narrated to me from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Sāʾib, that ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar narrated to him from his father, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The parable of the believer when fever or affliction befalls him is like that of a piece of iron placed in fire — its impurities are removed and its purity remains.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it — reported by al-Ḥākim]"
"6545","(6545) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī and Ibrāhīm ibn Mahdī al-Miṣṣīṣī, both with the same meaning, said — Abū al-Malīḥ narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Khālid narrated to us — Ibrāhīm al-Sulamī narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who had been a companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: \"When a servant has been allotted a station by Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, which he does not reach through his deeds, Allah afflicts him in his body, or in his wealth, or in his children.\" Ibn Nufayl added: \"And then he is patient upon that.\" Then they both agreed: \"Until he reaches the station that was previously allotted to him by Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it — Abū Dāwūd]"
"6546","(6546) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān in Baghdad informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Maʿmar narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd, from Kaythamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"When a servant is upon a righteous path of worship, and then falls ill, it is said to the angel entrusted with him: 'Write for him the like of his deeds when he was healthy, until I release him or take him to Myself.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than it — reported by Aḥmad]"
"6547","(6547) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us: al-ʿAwwām ibn Ḥawshab narrated to us: Abū Ismāʿīl Ibrāhīm al-Saksakī narrated to me that he heard Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā, and he and Yazīd ibn Abī Kabshah were companions on a journey. Yazīd used to fast, so Abū Burdah said to him: I heard Abā Mūsā say on numerous occasions that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When a servant falls ill or travels, there is written for him a reward equivalent to what he used to do while resident and in good health.\" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Maṭar ibn al-Faḍl, from Yazīd ibn Hārūn. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6548","(6548) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Bakr ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣayrafī narrated to me in Makkah: Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn Muḥammad ibn Zayd narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says: 'When I test My believing servant and he does not complain to Me about it to those who visit him, I release him from My bond, then I replace his flesh with flesh better than his flesh and his blood with blood better than his blood, and then he resumes his deeds.'\" Abū Ṣakhr Ḥumayd ibn Ziyād also narrated it from Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, as a mawqūf narration traced back to him. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Ḥākim]"
"6549","(6549) Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Mazzī informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣam, narrated to us: Baḥr, that is Ibn Naṣr, narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: Abū Ṣakhr Ḥumayd ibn Ziyād narrated that Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated to him, saying: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: \"I test My believing servant; and when he does not complain to Me about his deliverance, I release him from My bond, and I replace his blood with blood better than his blood and his flesh with flesh better than his flesh.\" Then He says to him: \"Resume your deeds.\" [Munkar — al-Ḥākim]"
"6550","(6550) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us by dictation: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar al-Salīṭī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated from Sufyān, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Sufyān, from Jābir, who said: A fever came seeking permission to visit the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: \"Who are you?\" It said: \"Fever.\" He said: \"Do you know the people of Qubāʾ?\" It said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Go to them.\" So it went to them, and they suffered severely from it. They complained about it to the Prophet (ﷺ), and he said: \"If you wish, I will supplicate Allah and He will remove it from you; and if you wish, it shall be an expiation and a purification.\" He said: \"Rather, let it be an expiation and a purification.\" [Munkar — Ibn Ḥibbān]"
"6551","(6551) Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated it from al-Aʿmash, mentioning the first narration from al-Aʿmash, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Anṣārī, from Umm Ṭāriq, the freedwoman of Saʿd, from the Prophet (ﷺ); and he mentioned the meaning of the second narration regarding their complaint, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Sufyān, from Jābir. Abū Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal informed us of that: Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Yaʿlā narrated it. [Munkar — Aḥmad]"
"6552","(6552) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, all saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: My father and Shuʿayb informed us, both saying: al-Layth informed us from Ibn al-Hād, from ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: 'When I test My beloved servant with his two beloved things [i.e., his eyes], and then he is patient, I shall compensate him with Paradise for them.'\" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6553","(6553) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Naṣrābādhī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Zuhayr ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mughrāʾ al-Dawsī narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The people of well-being will wish on the Day of Resurrection that their skins had been cut with scissors, on account of what they see of the reward of the people of affliction.\" [Ḍaʿīf — al-Tirmidhī]"
"6554","(6554) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Shaybān ibn Farrūkh narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us: Thābit narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from Ṣuhayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"For the believer, everything that befalls him is good, and that is for no one other than the believer. If prosperity befalls him and he is grateful to Allah, there is a reward for him; and if adversity befalls him and he is patient, there is a reward for him. Thus, every decree of Allah for the Muslim is good.\" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by Muslim]"
"6555","(6555) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar informed us, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-ʿAyzār ibn Ḥurayth, from ʿUmar ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"I am amazed at the believer: if good befalls him, he praises Allah and is grateful; and if a calamity befalls him, he praises Allah and is patient. The believer is rewarded in all his affairs, even in the morsel of food he raises to his wife's mouth.\" There are many narrations on this topic, and what we have mentioned is sufficient for one who is granted divine success. [Ḥasan — Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"6556","(6556) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī and ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā informed us, both saying: Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) set out for al-Shām. When he reached Sargh, it was conveyed to him that the plague had broken out in al-Shām. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had said: \"When you hear of it in a land, do not go to it; and if it breaks out in a land while you are in it, do not flee from it.\" So ʿUmar turned back from Sargh. Ibn Shihāb said: Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me from his father, Ibn ʿUmar, that ʿUmar only turned back the people on the basis of the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from al-Qaʿnabī and others, from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"6557","(6557) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd, who said: I heard Usāmah ibn Zayd narrating to Saʿd from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: \"When you hear that the plague has struck a land, do not enter it. And if it occurs in a land where you are, do not leave it.\" I said: Did you hear him narrating it to Saʿd without him denying it? He said: Yes."
"6558","(6558) Wahb ibn Jārīr narrated it from Shuʿbah, and he said in its text, from the Prophet ﷺ: \"This plague is a remnant of a punishment and chastisement by which a people were punished. So when it is in a land, do not descend upon it. And if it occurs in a land where you are, do not depart from it.\"\n\nAbū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jārīr narrated to us, and he mentioned it."
"6559","(6559) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Aḥmasī narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd from Saʿd ibn Mālik, Khuzaymah ibn Thābit, and Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Indeed, this plague is a punishment and a remnant of a chastisement by which a people were punished. So if it occurs in a land where you are, do not leave it fleeing from it. And if it occurs in a land where you are not, do not enter it.\""
"6560","(6560) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Muḥammad ibn Khallād al-Bāhilī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, Dāwūd ibn Abī al-Furāt narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah from Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmar from ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the plague. She said: The Prophet of Allah ﷺ told me: \"It is a punishment that Allah sends upon whom He wills, but He has made it a mercy for the believers. No servant remains in his land where the plague has struck, out of faith and expectation of reward, knowing that nothing will befall him except what Allah has decreed for him, except that he will have a reward equivalent to that of a martyr.\""
"6561","(6561) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān al-Ahwāzī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī in al-Ahwāz informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, who said: We visited Khabbāb to enquire after his health, and he had cauterised himself seven times. He said: Indeed, the Companions of our Prophet ﷺ who embraced Islam departed without their wealth diminishing their deeds at all. But we have acquired wealth for which we have found no place except the earth. Then we visited him again to enquire after his health, and he was building a wall for himself. He said: \"Indeed, a Muslim is rewarded for everything he spends, except for something he places in the earth.\" Were it not that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden us to supplicate for death, I would have supplicated for it."
"6562","(6562) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿImrān ibn Mūsā narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawārīrī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us, Ḥajjāj ibn al-Ṣawwāf narrated to us, Abū al-Zubayr narrated to us, and Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited Umm al-Sāʾib or Umm al-Musayyab while she was shivering. He said: \"What is the matter with you, O Umm al-Sāʾib — or O Umm al-Musayyab?\" She said: May Allah not bless it — the fever. He said: \"Do not curse fever, for it removes the sins of the children of Adam just as the bellows removes the impurities of iron.\""
"6563","(6563) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad al-Harawī informed us, ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us, Abū al-Yamān informed us, Shuʿayb informed us from al-Zuhrī, who informed me that Abū ʿUbayd, the freed slave of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, reported that Abū Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"No one will be admitted into Paradise by virtue of his deeds.\" They said: Not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: \"Not even me, unless Allah envelops me in His grace and mercy. So be upright and come close. Let none of you wish for death — if he is a doer of good, perhaps he may increase in good, and if he is a doer of evil, perhaps he may seek repentance.\""
"6564","(6564) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us orally, Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Let none of you wish for death, nor supplicate for it before it comes to him. Indeed, when one of you dies, his deeds are cut off from him, and indeed the lifespan of a believer only increases him in good.\""
"6565","(6565) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, Thābit al-Bunānī narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Let none of you wish for death due to some harm that has befallen him. And if he must do so, then let him say: 'O Allah, keep me alive as long as life is better for me, and take me in death when death is better for me.'\""
"6566","(6566) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, al-Aʿmash narrated — (ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā narrated from al-Aʿmash from Abī Sufyān from Jābir, who said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ, three days before his death, saying: \"Let none of you die except while having the best opinion of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.\""
"6567","(6567) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Saffar narrated to us, dictating: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Arim narrated to us: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us: Wasil, the freedman of Abu 'Uyaynah, narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say, three days before his death: \"None of you should die except while having a good opinion (husn al-zann) of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Sahih from Sulayman ibn Ma'bad, from 'Arim. [Sahih — Muslim]"
"6568","(6568) Abu Sa'id 'Uthman ibn 'Abdus ibn Mahfuz al-Faqih informed us: Abu Muhammad Yahya ibn Mansur narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Turk narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Nadr Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Faqih informed us: Muhammad ibn Nasr Abu 'Abdillah and Isma'il ibn Qutaybah Abu Ya'qub both said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Tamimi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad saying: 'A'ishah said: \"Woe to my head!\" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"That would be (a cause of concern) if it were to happen while I were alive, for I would seek forgiveness for you and supplicate for you.\" 'A'ishah said: \"Woe to me! By Allah, I think you love my death, and if that were so, you would have spent your last day in the bridal chamber with one of your wives.\" She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Rather, woe to my head! I have considered — or intended — to send for Abu Bakr and his son, and give a pledge, so that the speakers may say what they wish and the desirous may desire. Then I said: Allah refuses, and the believers reject — or: the believers reject, and Allah refuses.\"\n\nThe wording is from the hadith of Ja'far. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih — Bukhari]"
"6569","(6569) And Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came to visit me during an illness that had become severe during the time of the Farewell Pilgrimage. I said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what you see of affliction has befallen me, and I am a man of wealth.\" In another wording: \"The pain has become severe upon me.\"\n\nIbn 'Abdan reported it to us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us: Hisham ibn 'Ali and 'Uthman ibn 'Umar both said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz — he is Ibn Abi Salamah — narrated to us, from al-Zuhri: 'Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas informed me, from his father, and he mentioned it, saying: \"The pain has become severe upon me.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari extracted it from the hadith of 'Abd al-'Aziz, and Muslim extracted it from another route from al-Zuhri. [Sahih — Bukhari]"
"6570","(6570) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yahya narrated to us.\n\n(And) The faqih Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Ahmad al-Tusi narrated therein: Abu 'Ali al-Sawwaf narrated us: Muhammad ibn 'Uthman al-'Absi narrated us: 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Tamim ibn Salamah or Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, from 'Ubayd ibn Khalid al-Sulami, a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: Once he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and then on another occasion he said — from 'Ubayd ibn Khalid: \"Sudden death (al-faja'ah) is a seizure of grief.\"\n\n[Sahih — Abu Dawud]"
"6571","(6571) And Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrated it from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Tamim ibn Salamah, from 'Ubayd, without doubt, and he raised it (to the Prophet). Shu'bah said: \"Thus he narrated it to me, and he narrated it to me another time but did not raise it.\" Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us: Muhammad ibn Ghalib narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us: Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, and he mentioned it. Ibn Bashshar said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated this, as a mawquf (stopping at the Companion).\n\n[Sahih — its chain is mawquf]"
"6572","(6572) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Walid, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, who said: I asked 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) about sudden death — is it disliked? She said: \"What is there to dislike about it? I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) about that, and he said: 'It is a relief for the believer, and a seizure of grief for the sinner (al-fajir).'\"\n\nAnd Sufyan al-Thawri narrated it from 'Ubaydullah, as a mawquf (stopping) on 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her). [Very Weak — Ahmad]"
"6573","(6573) And Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili narrated to us: Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Mubarak narrated to us: Abu Shahab narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zubayd, from Abu al-Ahwas, from 'Abdullah and 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who both said: \"It is grief for the sinner and relief for the believer\" — meaning sudden death.\n\nAnd Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated it from al-A'mash, from Zubayd, from Murrah, from 'Abdullah, as his own statement. And al-Hajjaj narrated it from Zubayd, from Murrah, from 'Abdullah, as a marfu' (raised to the Prophet) narration. [Weak — Ibn Abi Shaybah]"
"6574","(6574) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi 'Isa and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-'Afsi both said: Ishaq ibn 'Isa narrated to us: Malik informed me.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abdillah informed us, and Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Shadhan narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Malik, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Halhalah al-Du'ili, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b, from Abu Qatadah ibn Rib'i, who said: A funeral procession passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: \"One at rest (mustarih), and one from whom rest is taken (mustarih minhu).\" They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what is the mustarih and what is the mustarih minhu?\" He said: \"The believing servant finds rest from the weariness and afflictions of the world and reaches the mercy of Allah. And the sinful servant — from him the people, the lands, the trees, and the beasts find rest.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Isma'il ibn Abi Uways, from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah. [Sahih — Bukhari]"
"6575","(6575) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih informed us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash and Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Feed the hungry, visit the sick, and free the captive (al-ani).\" Sufyan said: \"Al-ani means the prisoner.\" Isma'il said: In another place, Sufyan narrated to us from Mansur alone.\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Kathir, from Sufyan, from Mansur alone. [Sahih — Bukhari]"
"6576","(6576) And Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan: Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir narrated to me, from Abu Wa'il, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \"Free the captive, respond to the one who invites, and visit the sick.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad. [Sahih — Bukhari]"
"6577","(6577) Abu ʿAli al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us — Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us — Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated to us — al-Ashʿath ibn Sulaym narrated to us, saying: I heard Muʿāwiyah ibn Suwayd saying: I heard al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib saying: **Rasūlullāh ﷺ commanded us with seven things: He commanded us to visit the ill, to follow funeral processions, to respond to the one who sneezes, to return the greeting of salām, to accept the invitation of the one who invites, to fulfil the oath, and to aid the oppressed.**"
"6578","(6578) Abu al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us — Abu Jaʿfar ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — Abu ʿĀmir narrated to us — Yazīd ibn Ibrāhīm narrated from Qatādah — from Abū ʿĪsā al-Aswārī — from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī — from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: **\"Visit your sick and follow funeral processions, for they will remind you of the Hereafter.\"**"
"6579","(6579) Abu ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abu Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us by dictation — Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us — Hushaym narrated to us — from Khālid — from Abū Qilābah — from Abū Asmāʾ — from Thawbān, the freedman of Rasūlullāh ﷺ, who said: Rasūlullāh ﷺ said: **\"Whoever visits a sick person remains in the harvest of Paradise until he returns.\"** And Ayyūb said, from Abū Qilābah: \"In the makhrafah (orchard) of Paradise.\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā."
"6580","(6580) Abu ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me — Abu al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to me — Muḥammad ibn Rajāʾ ibn al-Sindī narrated to us — Abu al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us — from Ayyūb — from Abū Qilābah — from Abū Asmāʾ — from Thawbān, raising it to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: **\"The one who visits the sick is in the makhrafah of Paradise.\"** Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abu al-Rabīʿ. Wuhayb also narrated it from Ayyūb, saying: \"from the Prophet ﷺ,\" and added: \"until he returns.\" However, ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal differed from both of them regarding Abū Qilābah in its chain of narration."
"6581","(6581) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abu al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us. And Abu al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān in Baghdad informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān narrated to us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us — ʿĀṣim, meaning al-Aḥwal, narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd, meaning Abā Qilābah — from Abū al-Ashʿath al-Ṣanʿānī — from Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī — from Thawbān, who said: Rasūlullāh ﷺ said: **\"Whoever visits a sick person remains in the harvest of Paradise.\"** It was said: \"O Rasūlullāh, what is the harvest of Paradise?\" He ﷺ replied: **\"Its fruits.\"** This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Bashrān. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Yazīd ibn Hārūn, and also from Suwayd ibn Saʿīd, from Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah, from ʿĀṣim. And Ḥammād ibn Salamah similarly reported it from ʿĀṣim."
"6582","(6582) Shuʿbah and Thābit, Abū Zayd, differed from them both, saying: from ʿĀṣim — from Abū Qilābah — from Abū Asmāʾ — from Thawbān, that the Prophet ﷺ said: **\"The one who visits the sick is in the harvest of Paradise until he returns.\"** Abu Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Shuʿbah and Thābit, Abū Zayd, narrated it, but did not mention Abū al-Ashʿath in its chain. The narration of Yazīd and Marwān is more accurate. Abū Ghaṭfār also narrated it from Abū Qilābah — from Abū al-Ashʿath — from Abū Asmāʾ."
"6583","(6583) Abu al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār in Baghdad informed us — al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Mujashshir narrated to us — Hushaym narrated to us — from ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar al-Anṣārī — from Ibn Thawbān — from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: Rasūlullāh ﷺ said: **\"Whoever visits a sick person continues to wade in the Mercy until he sits; and when he sits, he is immersed therein.\"**"
"6584","(6584) Abu ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abu Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Abu Muʿāwiyah narrated to us — from al-Aʿmash — from al-Ḥakam — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylah, who said: Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī came to visit al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), so ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said to him: **\"Have you come to visit or to gloat?\"** He said: \"Rather, to visit.\" ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: \"Then indeed, I heard Rasūlullāh ﷺ saying: **'When a man comes to visit his brother who is ill, he walks in the orchards of Paradise until he sits; and when he sits, the Mercy envelops him. If he came in the morning, seventy thousand angels send blessings upon him until evening; and if he came in the evening, seventy thousand angels send blessings upon him until morning.'**\" Shuʿbah differed from him, narrating it from al-Ḥakam — from ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ — from ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), once as marfūʿ and once as mawqūf."
"6585","(6585) Abu Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us — Abu Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī in Makkah narrated to us — Abu Yaḥyā ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated to us — from al-Ḥakam — from ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ, who said: Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī came to visit al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), so ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said to him: **\"Have you come to visit or merely as a visitor?\"** Abū Mūsā said: \"I have come to visit.\" ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: Rasūlullāh ﷺ said: **\"Whoever visits a sick person in the morning, seventy thousand angels escort him, all of them seeking forgiveness for him until evening, and he will have a garden (kharīf) in Paradise. And if he visits him in the evening, seventy thousand angels escort him, all of them seeking forgiveness for him until morning, and he will have a garden in Paradise.\"** Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAdī similarly narrated it from Shuʿbah as marfūʿ, and Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated it from Shuʿbah as mawqūf."
"6586","(6586) Abu ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me — Abu Muḥammad al-Fākihī narrated to us — Abu Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masʿarah narrated to us, mentioning the ḥadīth with its like, and added: Ibn Abī Masʿarah said: Then al-Muqriʾ subsequently stopped it at ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) and did not mention the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"It has reached me that ʿAbd al-Malik al-Juddī stops it, and he is more memorious than I.\""
"6587","(6587) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When Saʿd ibn Muʿādh was wounded on the Day of the Trench, a man shot him with an arrow in the median vein of the arm. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had a tent pitched for him in the mosque so that he could visit him from nearby.\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr."
"6588","(6588) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal informed me, al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī narrated to us, Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated from his father, from Zayd ibn Arqam, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) visited me for an ailment that was in my eyes.\n\nSomething similar has also been narrated from Anas ibn Mālik from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). [Ḥasan for other narrators. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6589","(6589) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Bakr ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣayrafī in Marw narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us, Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, al-Juʿayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from ʿĀʾishah bint Saʿd that her father said: I fell ill in Makkah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came to visit me. He placed his hand on my forehead, then wiped my chest and my abdomen, and then said: \"O Allah, heal Saʿd and complete his emigration for him.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6590","(6590) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from al-Aʿmash. He said: I heard Abā al-Ḍuḥā narrating from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾishah, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) visited a sick person, he would wipe his face and his chest — or he said, he would wipe upon his chest — and say: \"Remove the harm, O Lord of the people, and grant healing, for You are the Healer. There is no healing except Your healing, a healing that leaves no illness behind.\"\n\nShe said: When the illness in which he (peace and blessings be upon him) passed away overtook him, I took his hand intending to place it upon his chest and to say those words, but he withdrew his hand from me and said: \"O Allah, admit me to al-Rafīq al-Aʿlā (the Highest Companion).\"\n\nMuslim narrated it from two routes from Shuʿbah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī from al-Aʿmash.\n\nJarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash: \"He wiped him with his right hand\" — and he conveyed its meaning. Al-Thawrī narrated from him, and Hushaym narrated from al-Aʿmash, and he said: \"He placed his hand where the pain was.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6591","(6591) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) went out to visit a companion of his who was suffering from a fever, and I was with him. He took hold of his hand and placed his own hand upon his forehead. He considered this to be part of the completeness of visiting the sick. Then he said: \"Indeed, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says: 'This is My fire, which I unleash upon My believing servant so that it may be his share of the Fire in the Hereafter.'\"\n\nAbū Usāmah narrated it from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and he said: from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ashʿarī, from Abū Hurayrah. [Jayyid. Ibn Mājah]"
"6592","(6592) Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated it from Ismāʿīl, from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ashʿarī, from Kaʿb al-Aḥbār, who said: \"Fever is a bellows of fire. Allah sends it upon His believing servant in this world, and it becomes his share of the Fire of Jahannam.\"\n\nAbū Ṭāhir informed us, Abū Ḥāmid informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Abū Mushir narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUbaydillāh, who said: \"I fell ill, so Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ashʿarī visited me and narrated to me from Kaʿb al-Aḥbār, and he mentioned it.\" [Jayyid. Al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"6593","(6593) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ, returning to the beginning, said: Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid al-Zāhid narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ziyād ibn Mihrān al-Simsār narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Kaʿb al-Anṭākī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn ʿUmayr narrated from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad ibn Yazīd, from ʿAbdullāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Treat your sick with charity, protect your wealth with zakāh, and prepare for tribulations with duʿāʾ (supplication).\"\n\nAbū ʿAbdillāh said: Mūsā ibn ʿUmayr is the sole narrator of this.\n\nThe Shaykh said: This text (matn) is only known from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), as a mursal narration. [Very weak. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr]"
"6594","(6594) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Fāriyābī narrated to us, and al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān al-Nasawī informed me — both said: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Mālik, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) arrived in Madīnah, Abū Bakr and Bilāl (may Allah be pleased with them both) fell ill with fever. She said: So I entered upon them and said: \"O my father, how do you find yourself?\" And I said to Bilāl: \"How do you find yourself?\"\n\nShe said: Whenever Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was seized by fever, he would recite:\n\n\"Every man wakes up among his family,\nwhile death is closer to him than the strap of his sandal.\"\n\nAnd whenever Bilāl (may Allah be pleased with him) recovered from it, he would recite:\n\n\"Would that I knew whether I shall spend a night\nin a valley with idhkhir and jalīl grass around me!\n\nShall I one day drink from the waters of Majannah?\nAnd shall Shāmah and Ṭafīl appear before me?\"\n\nʿĀʾishah said: So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and informed him. He said: \"O Allah, make Madīnah beloved to us as we love Makkah, or even more. Make it healthy, bless for us in its ṣāʿ and its mudd, and transfer its fever to al-Juḥfah.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6595","(6595) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, Muʿallā ibn Asad informed us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Mukhtār informed us, Khālid narrated from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) visited a Bedouin who was ill. He said: Whenever the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) entered upon a sick person to visit him, he would say to him: \"No harm, it is a purification, if Allah, the Most High, wills.\" The Bedouin said: \"Purification? No! Rather it is a fever that boils over — or rages — upon an old man, sending him to the graves.\" So the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Then yes, so it is.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muʿallā ibn Asad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6596","(6596) Abū Kāmil narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Mukhtār, and he added in the ḥadīth that he (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: \"No harm, it is a purification, if Allah wills.\" So the man said: \"Purification? No! Rather it is a fever that boils over.\"\n\nAbū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, ʿImrān ibn Mūsā informed us, Abū Kāmil narrated to us, and he mentioned it."
"6597","(6597) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Sulaym ibn Harb narrated to us; Hammad narrated from Thabit from Anas: that a young Jewish boy fell ill, so the Prophet (ﷺ) came to visit him. He sat near his head and presented Islam to him. His father said: \"Obey Abu al-Qasim.\" So he accepted Islam. The Prophet (ﷺ) stood up, saying: \"All praise is due to Allah, Who saved him through me from the Fire.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Sulaym ibn Harb, and Thabit narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he visited 'Abdullah ibn Ubayy, and before that he visited Abu Talib and presented Islam to him. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6598","(6598) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Nasrabadhi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Dhuhli narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated from Sulaym ibn Bilal from 'Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah from Yahya ibn 'Umarah from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Prompt your dying ones to say: 'There is no deity except Allah.'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Khalid ibn Makhlad from Sulaym, and he also narrated it from the hadith of Abu Hazim from Abu Hurayrah. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6599","(6599) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abdullah informed us; Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Raja' ibn al-Sindi narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us; Yazid ibn Kaysan narrated from Abu Hazim from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Prompt your dying ones to say: 'There is no deity except Allah.'\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Bakr and 'Uthman, the two sons of Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
"6600","(6600) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; 'Ubayd ibn 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Sharik narrated to us; Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us.\n\n(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us; Abu Ishaq al-Talaqani narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubarak narrated from Sulaym al-Taymi from Abu 'Uthman, other than al-Nahdi, from his father from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Recite it near your dying ones\" — meaning Surah Ya-Sin.\n\nThis is the hadith of Abu 'Abdullah, and in the narration of Ibn Bishran there is no mention of \"from his father.\" Abu Dawud narrated it in the Sunan from Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' and others from Ibn al-Mubarak, and he said: \"from his father.\" [Da'if. Abu Dawud]"
"6601","(6601) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us; 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated from al-A'mash from Shaqiq from Umm Salamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When you attend to the deceased, say good words, for the angels say 'Amen' to what you say.\" She said: When Abu Salamah died, I said: \"What shall I say, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Say: 'O Allah, forgive him and grant us a good successor after him.'\" She said: So Allah granted me a successor better than him — the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6602","(6602) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Khushaysh al-Muqri' in Kufah informed us; Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah al-Azdi, son of Abu al-'Aza'im, informed us; Ahmad ibn Hazim informed us; 'Ubaydullah informed us; al-A'mash narrated the same. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Abu Mu'awiyah from al-A'mash, and he said: \"When you attend to the sick or the deceased.\" [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6603","(6603) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Ishaq ibn al-Khurasani al-'Adl narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Haytham al-Qadi narrated to us; Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated from Ibn al-Had from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim from Abu Salamah from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: that when death approached him, he called for new garments and wore them, then said: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Indeed, the deceased will be raised in the garments in which he dies.'\" [Hasan. Abu Dawud]"
"6604","(6604) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad al-Sha'rani narrated to us; my grandfather narrated to us; Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us; 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated from Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah from his father from his father: that when the Prophet (ﷺ) arrived in Madinah, he asked about al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur. They said: \"He has passed away and bequeathed one-third of his wealth to you, O Messenger of Allah, and he instructed that he be turned toward the Qiblah when he was on his deathbed.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"He has attained the fitrah. I have returned his one-third to his children.\" Then he went and prayed over him and said: \"O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, admit him into Your Paradise, and You have done so.\" [Da'if. Narrated by al-Hakim]"
"6605","(6605) Abu Bakr al-Qadi informed us; Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad narrated to us; 'Abd al-Karim ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abu al-Yaman narrated to us; Shu'ayb narrated from al-Zuhri from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik in a story he mentioned, and he said: Al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur was the first to face the Qiblah both alive and dead. And it is a good mursal narration. (T) And it is narrated from al-Hasan, who said: 'Umar mentioned the Ka'bah and said: \"By Allah, it is nothing but stones that Allah has set as a Qiblah for our living, and we direct our dead toward it.\" [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn Sa'd]"
"6606","(6606) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us; Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari from Khalid al-Hadha' from Abu Qilabah from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered upon Abu Salamah when his eyes had become fixed (in death). He closed his eyes and said: \"Indeed, when the soul is taken, the eyes follow it.\" Some of his family members cried out, so he said: \"Do not supplicate against yourselves except with good, for the angels say 'Amen' to what you say.\" Then he said: \"O Allah, forgive Abu Salamah, raise his rank among the guided ones, and succeed him among those who remain. Forgive us and him, O Lord of the worlds. O Allah, expand his grave for him and illuminate it for him.\" [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6607","(6607) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us: Muʿāwiya ibn ʿAmr narrated it.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Muʿāwiya ibn ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6608","(6608) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yaʿqūb, who informed me that he heard Abū Hurayra say: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Do you not see that when a person dies, his eyes stare?\" They said: \"Yes, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"That is when his eyes follow his soul.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.\n\nIt is also narrated regarding the command to close the eyes (of the deceased) from Shaddād ibn Aws, from the Prophet ﷺ, and what we have mentioned suffices. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6609","(6609) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us. Both said: Saʿdān narrated to us: Muʿādh narrated to us: Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated to us, from Bakr ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: When you close the eyes of the deceased, say: \"Bismillāh wa ʿalā millati Rasūlillāh ﷺ\" (In the name of Allah and upon the way of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ). And when you carry him, say: \"Bismillāh\" (In the name of Allah), then glorify Allah (say \"Subḥānallāh\") for as long as you carry him. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
"6610","(6610) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me by way of ijāza (permission): Abū al-Walīd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUqba narrated to us: Abū al-Munīb narrated to us: Abū Khālid al-Madanī narrated to us, from ʿAbdillāh ibn Ādam, who said: A mawlā (freed slave) of Anas ibn Mālik died at the time of sunset, so Anas said: \"Place an iron object on his stomach.\"\n\nIt is narrated from al-Shaʿbī that he was asked about placing a sword on the stomach of the deceased. He said: \"It is placed only out of fear that the body may swell.\" Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: \"Some people of experience claim that it (the iron) hastens the swelling on the bedding.\" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6611","(6611) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ʿAbdillāh ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ashʿath narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād ibn Ādam narrated to us: Bakr ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to me, from ʿIkrima, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbbās, who said: When the preparation of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was completed on Tuesday, he was placed on his bed in his house ﷺ. [Ḍaʿīf — Ibn Mājah]"
"6612","(6612) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān informed us: Shuʿayb informed me, from al-Zuhrī, who informed me that Abū Salama ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed him that ʿĀʾisha, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, he was covered with a ḥibra (striped Yemeni cloth).\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6613","(6613) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdillāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salama, from ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was covered with a ḥibra cloth.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and others, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6614","(6614) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī said: It has reached me that it was said to Saʿd ibn Abī Wāqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him: \"Shall we not make for you something like a wooden chest?\" He said: \"Rather, do to me as you did to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — erect bricks over me and heap earth upon me.\" [Ḍaʿīf — narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
"6615","(6615) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutayba narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Miswarī narrated to us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad, from ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd, that Saʿd ibn Abī Wāqqāṣ said during the illness in which he died: \"Dig a laḥd (niche grave) for me and erect bricks over me in the same manner as was done for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6616","(6616) Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed me: My father informed me: al-Awzāʿī narrated to me: al-Zuhrī narrated to me, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Abū Hurayra, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"The rights of a Muslim upon another Muslim are five: returning the greeting of salām, visiting the sick, following the funeral processions, accepting invitations, and saying yarḥamukallāh to one who sneezes.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of al-Awzāʿī, and Muslim extracted it from two other routes from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6617","(6617) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl informed us; al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways informed us; my father informed me; al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Muḥammad al-Ḍabbī narrated from ʿUmara ibn Yaʿlā ibn Murra, from his father, who said: I travelled with the Prophet ﷺ on more than one occasion, and I never saw him pass by the corpse of a human being except that he ordered it to be buried, without asking whether the deceased was a Muslim or a disbeliever. [Very weak. Reported by al-Ḥākim]"
"6618","(6618) Others narrated from Ibn Abī Uways, with his chain of narration, from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaʿlā ibn Murra al-Thaqafī, from his father, who said: I heard Yaʿlā ibn Murra saying, and he mentioned it. Abū Bakr al-Aṣbahānī reported it to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Dāraquṭnī informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl al-Maḥāmilī informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Shabīb informed us; Ibn Abī Uways informed us, and he mentioned it. [Very weak. Reported by al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"6619","(6619) Al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿAbdillāh informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Zakariyyā informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muhallab informed us; Ibn Yūnus informed us; Layth, i.e. Ibn Saʿd, narrated from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbdullāh, i.e. Ibn ʿUmar, that he said: The people found, while they were returning from the Ḥajj, a dead woman at al-Baydāʾ. They passed by her without paying any attention to her, until a man from Banū Layth, known as Kalb the poor man, passed by her. He cast his garment over her, then sought help from those who could bury her. ʿUmar called ʿAbdullāh, i.e. his son, and said: Did you pass by this dead woman? He said: No. ʿUmar said: Had I been informed that you passed by her, I would have surely punished you. Then ʿUmar stood among the people and rebuked them regarding her, saying: Perhaps Allah will admit Kalb into Paradise because of his action towards her. While Kalb was performing wuḍūʾ near the mosque, Abū Luʾluʾa, the killer of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), came to him and slit his belly.\n\nNāfiʿ said: Abū Luʾluʾa killed, along with ʿUmar, seven other individuals.\n\nIt was also narrated by Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar from his father, with the same meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Ibn Ḥibbān]"
"6620","(6620) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; ʿAmr ibn Zurāra informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Muṭarrif al-Ruʾāsī, Abū Sufyān, and Aḥmad ibn Janāb said: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus informed us; Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Balawī narrated from ʿUrwa ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī, from his father, from Ḥuṣayn ibn Waḥwaḥ, that Ṭalḥa ibn al-Barāʾ fell ill, so the Prophet ﷺ came to visit him and said: \"I see that death has befallen Ṭalḥa. So inform me of his passing so that I may attend him and pray over him, and hasten his burial, for it is not befitting that the corpse of a Muslim be kept among his family.\" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdillāh. ʿAmr ibn Zurāra said it thus, and it was also said ʿUmar ibn Zurāra. A marfūʿ ḥadīth regarding giving the ghusl to a drowned person has been narrated, but nothing of its like is firmly established. It was narrated from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī regarding giving the ghusl to one struck by lightning. Al-Shāfiʿī used to recommend that until the person's death became evident. [Weak. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
"6621","(6621) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq; Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād, i.e. Ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, narrated to me from his father; from ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When they intended to perform the ghusl of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the people differed concerning it. Some of them said: Shall we undress the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of his garments as we undress our dead, or shall we wash him while he is clothed? Then Allah cast sleep upon them until there was not a single man among them whose chin was not resting upon his chest. Then a speaker from the corner of the house said—while they did not know who it was—\"Wash the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he is clothed.\" So they washed him while he was wearing his shirt, pouring water over it and rubbing it. ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: By Allah, had I known beforehand what I came to know afterwards, no one would have washed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except his wives. [Ḥasan. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
"6622","(6622) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; al-Nufaylī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Salama narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq; Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād narrated to me from his father ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, who said: I heard ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her) saying, and he mentioned the ḥadīth with its meaning, except that he said: So they washed him while he was wearing a shirt, pouring water over the shirt and rubbing it with the shirt, without using their hands. [Ḥasan. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
"6623","(6623) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us; Abū Muʿāwiya informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh said: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālūyah informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shayba informed us; Abū Muʿāwiya informed us. Abū Burda, i.e. Burayd ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Burda, narrated from ʿAlqama ibn Marthad, from Ibn Burayda, from his father, who said: When they began the ghusl of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a caller from inside called out: \"Do not remove the shirt from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" This Ibn Burayda is Sulaymān ibn Burayd. Others besides him named him as such from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shayba. [Ḥasan for other than its own chain. Reported by Ibn Mājah]"
"6624","(6624) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muthannā informed us; al-Qawārīrī informed us; Yazīd Abū Khālid al-Qurashī informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed me; Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamra, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: \"Do not expose your thigh, and do not look at the thigh of a living person or a dead one.\" [Ḥasan. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
"6625","(6625) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) washed the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ was wearing a shirt, and in the hand of ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) was a piece of cloth with which he would clean beneath the shirt. [Weak. Reported by Ibn Abī Shayba]"
"6626","(6626) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Musaddad informed us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I washed the Prophet ﷺ, and I looked to see what is ordinarily found with a deceased person, but I found nothing. He ﷺ was pure in life and pure in death. Those who took charge of his burial and preparation, to the exclusion of all others, were four: ʿAlī, al-ʿAbbās, al-Faḍl, and Ṣāliḥ, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A niche grave (laḥd) was dug for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and bricks were set in place over it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bazzāz]"
"6627","(6627) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us by dictation: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥamdān narrated to us in Hamadhān: Ibrāhīm ibn Naṣr al-Dārimī and Ibrāhīm ibn Dayzīl said: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"I washed the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and I began to look for what is ordinarily found in the deceased, but I found nothing. He (peace be upon him) was fragrant in life and in death.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bazzār.]"
"6628","(6628) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me by way of ijāzah: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us: Abū al-Munḍhir Yūsuf ibn ʬīyyah narrated to us: Junayd Abū Ḥāzim al-Taymī narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Bashīr, from Ibn Sīrīn, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"Whoever washes a deceased person, let him begin by pressing his abdomen.\" This is a mursal hadith, and its narrator is weak. [Very weak. Reported by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
"6629","(6629) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥadhāʾ — meaning Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr — informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ narrated — (ḥ) and Abū Bakr informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ, from Ḥafṣah, from Umm ʬīyyah, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said to them regarding the washing of his daughter: \"Begin with her right side and the places of ablution from her.\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6630","(6630) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Hushaym narrated from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ, from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from Umm ʬīyyah (may Allah be pleased with her), that when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) commanded her to wash his daughter, he said to her: \"Begin with her right side and the places of ablution.\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6631","(6631) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us: Mālik narrated from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Umm ʬīyyah al-Anṣāriyyah, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came to us when his daughter passed away and said: 'Wash her three times, or five times, or more than that if you deem it necessary, with water and leaves of the lote-tree (sidr), and put in the last washing camphor (kāfūr) or something of camphor. When you have finished, inform me.'\" She said: \"When we had finished, we informed him, and he gave us his waist-wrapper (ḥiqwah) and said: 'Wrap her with it'\" — meaning the izār (lower garment). Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah, both from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6632","(6632) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Hishām narrated from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from Umm ʬīyyah al-Anṣāriyyah, who said: \"One of the daughters of the Prophet (peace be upon him) passed away, and he came to us and said: 'Wash her with water and lote-tree leaves (sidr), and wash her an odd number of times — three, or five, or more than that if you deem it necessary. Put in the last washing camphor (kāfūr) or something of camphor. When you have finished, inform me.'\" She said: \"When we had finished, we informed him, and he threw to us his waist-wrapper (ḥiqwah) and said: 'Wrap her with it.'\" Umm ʬīyyah said: \"We braided her hair into three plaits, then placed them behind her — her front lock and her two plaits.\"\n\nAnd Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and Yazīd ibn Hārūn said: Hishām narrated, and he mentioned the hadith with a similar wording. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, from Yaḥyā, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAmr al-Nāqid, from Yazīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6633","(6633) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad and Ḥafṣah, from Umm ʬīyyah, who said: \"One of the daughters of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) passed away, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Wash her three times, or five times, or more than that if you deem it necessary, with water and lote-tree leaves (sidr). Put in the last washing camphor (kāfūr) or something of camphor. When you have finished, inform me.'\" When we had finished, we informed him, and he threw to us his waist-wrapper (ḥiqwah) and said: \"Wrap her with it.\" And Ayyūb narrated from Ḥafṣah, from Umm ʬīyyah: \"Three times, or five times, or seven times, or more than that if you deem it necessary.\" She said: \"And we made her hair into three plaits.\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, from Ḥammād, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar, from Ḥammād ibn Zayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim.]"
"6634","(6634) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us: Qatādah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, that he used to learn the method of washing from Umm ʬīyyah: she would wash with lote-tree leaves (sidr) twice, and the third time with water and camphor (kāfūr). And it is narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr that his father instructed him in his will, saying: \"O my son, when I die, wash me once with water.\" And ʬāʾ said: \"A single washing suffices for the deceased.\" And ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: \"There is nothing fixed (muʾaqqat) regarding it.\" And from Ibrāhīm: \"If he does not find lote-tree leaves (sidr), it does not harm him.\" And the companions of ʿAbd Allāh used to say: \"The deceased is washed an odd number of times, shrouded an odd number of times, and fumigated with incense an odd number of times.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"6635","(6635) The noble Imām Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Shurayḥī informed us: al-Baghawī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, from the companions of ʿAbd Allāh, who said: \"The deceased is washed an odd number of times, shrouded an odd number of times, and fumigated with incense an odd number of times.\" [Ḥasan. Reported by Ibn Mājah.]"
"6636","(6636) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us: Ibn Shihāb informed me: ʿUmar ibn Jāriyah al-Thaqafī, an ally of Banū Zuhrah and one of the companions of Abū Hurayrah, narrated: \"The Banū al-Ḥārith ibn ʿĀmir ibn Nawfal purchased Khubayb, and Khubayb (may Allah be pleased with him) was the one who had killed al-Ḥārith ibn ʿĀmir ibn Nawfal on the day of Badr. Khubayb remained with them as a prisoner until they resolved to kill him. He asked the daughter of al-Ḥārith for a razor to remove his pubic hair, and she lent it to him. One of her young children crawled to her while she was unaware, until he reached her, and she found him sitting on her thigh with the razor in his hand. She was alarmed by a fright that he recognized, and he said: 'Do you think I would kill him? I would never do that.'\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. If he did not have it done before his death, then al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah, the Most High, have mercy on him) said: \"Some of our companions said: I do not think that any hair should be cut from him after death, nor any nail clipped. And among them are those who saw no harm in that.\" The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It is narrated from al-Ḥasan and Ibn Sīrīn that they both said: \"No hair should be cut from him, nor any nail clipped.\" And it is narrated from Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ that he washed a deceased person and called for a razor, and in another narration that he clipped the pubic hair of a deceased person. And it is narrated from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: \"Why do you groom your deceased?\" — meaning, comb his hair — as though she disliked that if it were done with a fine-toothed comb. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6637","(6637) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us. And Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: We were with the Prophet (ﷺ) when a man fell from his camel while he was in the state of iḥrām, and his neck was broken, and he died. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in his two garments, and do not cover his head, for Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Sufyān. Likewise, Ibn Jurayj and al-Thawrī narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār — \"his two garments.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6638","(6638) As for the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd informed me; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Khashram narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj informed me; ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated to me, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A man came while in the state of iḥrām with the Prophet (ﷺ), and he fell from his camel, and his neck was broken, and he died. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and clothe him in his two garments, and do not cover his head, for he will come on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Khashram. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6639","(6639) As for the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī: Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated to me; ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated to me, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) was brought a man whose mount had thrown him, and he died while in the state of iḥrām. He said: \"Shroud him in his two garments, and wash him with water and lotus leaves, and do not cover his head, for Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6640","(6640) From Wakīʿ, from Sufyān, with the same meaning, except that he said: \"And do not cover his face or his head, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Shaybānī informed us; my father informed me; Abū Kurayb narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated, and he mentioned it with his addition.\n\nMuḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated it from Wakīʿ, similar to the narration of Muḥammad ibn Kathīr from Sufyān, and there is no mention of the face in it.\n\nḤammād ibn Zayd narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, and he was uncertain whether it was \"two garments\" or \"his two garments,\" and he did not mention his face, and he added: \"And do not apply perfume to him.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6641","(6641) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās: A man was standing with the Prophet (ﷺ) on his she-camel at ʿArafah when it threw him — or he said: it broke his neck — and he died. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in two garments — or he said: in his two garments — and do not apply perfume to him, and do not cover his head, for Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. And Ḥammād narrated it from Ayyūb and ʿAmr, and he said: \"two garments.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6642","(6642) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; Abū ʿAlī — al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār and Ayyūb, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: While a man was standing with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at ʿArafah, he fell from his mount. Ayyūb said: \"And it broke his neck\" — or he said: \"And it killed him.\" And ʿAmr said: \"And it threw him.\" That was mentioned to the Prophet (ﷺ), and he said: \"Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in two garments, and do not apply perfume to him, and do not cover his head.\" Ayyūb said: \"For Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\" And ʿAmr said: \"For Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ. And al-Bukhārī narrated it from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, from Ḥammād ibn Zayd, from Ayyūb alone. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6643","(6643) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us of it; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Abū Muslim narrated to us; Sulaymān narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated it with the wording of the ḥadīth of Ayyūb, except that he did not mention his statement: \"That was mentioned to the Prophet (ﷺ).\"\n\nʿAmr ibn Dīnār had said: \"in his two garments,\" and Ayyūb had said: \"in two garments.\" Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us of the correctness of that; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated to us, from ʿAmr and Ayyūb. Ayyūb said: \"in two garments,\" and ʿAmr said: \"in his two garments.\" And Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated it from Ayyūb, saying: \"I was informed from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6644","(6644) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; Abū Bishr narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Jubayr narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAbbās: A man was with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the state of iḥrām when his she-camel threw him, and he died. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in his two garments, and do not touch him with perfume, and do not cover his head, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And al-Bukhārī narrated it from Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī, from Hushaym. Likewise, Abū ʿAwānah narrated it from Abū Bishr, in agreement with Hushaym regarding the head and perfume, except that it was narrated from him: \"his two garments,\" and it was narrated: \"two garments.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6645","(6645) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah and Hushaym narrated to us, from Abū Bishr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās: A man was thrown by his mount and died while in the state of iḥrām. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in two garments, outside of his head, and do not touch him with perfume, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\" Thus narrated a group from Shuʿbah.\n\nThe Shaykh said: And I saw this ḥadīth in another copy with this isnād as \"in his two garments.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6646","(6646) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Qabbānī and Aḥmad ibn Sahl said: ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us. He said: I heard Abū Bishr narrating from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās narrating: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was in the state of iḥrām, and he fell from his she-camel, and it broke his neck. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded that he be washed with water and lotus leaves, that he be shrouded in two garments, and that he not be touched with perfume, outside of his head. Shuʿbah said: Then he narrated to me after that and said: \"His head and his face will be outside, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār and others, from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6647","(6647) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʪsā narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Manṣūr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A man was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when his she-camel threw him and he died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Wash him and do not bring perfume near him, and do not cover his face, for he will be raised (on the Day of Resurrection) saying the Talbiyah.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā, thus. And it is a narration containing an error from some of its narrators in both the isnād and the matn. And the authentic version is as previously mentioned. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6648","(6648) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd — Isḥāq said \"informed us\" and Qutaybah said \"narrated to us\" — Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A man's she-camel threw him while he was in iḥrām and killed him. He was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: \"Wash him and shroud him, and do not cover his head, and do not bring perfume near him, for he will be raised saying 'Labbayk.'\" And Isḥāq said: \"He will be raised saying the Talbiyah.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and this is the authentic version: Manṣūr from al-Ḥakam from Saʿīd, and in its matn is: \"Do not cover his head.\" The narration of the group (al-jamāʿah) mentions the head alone, and the mention of the face in it is unusual (gharīb).\n\nAbū al-Zubayr narrated it from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, and he mentioned the face with doubt in his matn. The narration of the group, who did not express doubt and who narrated the matn in the best manner, is more deserving of being preserved. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6649","(6649) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, and he mentioned the hadith in a manner similar to the narration of Ibn al-Madīnī from Sufyān, in abridged form. Al-Shāfiʿī said: Sufyān said, and Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ḥurrah added from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Cover his face and do not cover his head, and do not touch him with perfume, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection saying the Talbiyah.\"\n\nAl-Shāfiʿī said: Saʿīd ibn Sālim informed us from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihāb, that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) did something similar to that. [Munkar — narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
"6650","(6650) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Qutaybah narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from al-Zuhrī, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Walīd, the grandfather of Ayyūb ibn Salamah, died at al-Saqīyah during the time of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was in iḥrām, and his head was not covered. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6651","(6651) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Rāzī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Zāhir ibn Aḥmad informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Ziyād al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Haytham, meaning Ibn Jamīl, narrated to us: Sharīk narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from al-Ḍaḥḥāk, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he said: \"When a person in iḥrām dies, his head should not be covered so that he may meet Allah in the state of iḥrām.\" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6652","(6652) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdad: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʬṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Cover the faces of your dead, and do not resemble the Jews.\" And if this is authentic, it serves as evidence for the narration of Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ḥurrah regarding the command to cover the face.\n\nHowever, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to them: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: one of the Kūfans, namely ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ṣāliḥ, narrated this hadith with its like. ʿAbdullāh said: \"I narrated it to my father and he rejected it, saying: 'Ḥafṣ made an error in it by raising it (to the Prophet).' And he narrated to me from Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʬṭāʾ, as a mursal narration.\"\n\nThe Shaykh said: Likewise, al-Thawrī and others narrated it from Ibn Jurayj as mursal. It was also narrated from ʿAlī ibn ʬṣim from Ibn Jurayj as Ḥafṣ narrated it, and that is an error. And Allah knows best. [Munkar — al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"6653","(6653) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hārūn ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us. (H) And Abū Dāwūd said: Ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd, meaning al-Ṭayālisī, narrated to us. They both said: Ḥarb ibn Shaddād narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated to me: Bāb ibn ʿUmayr narrated to me: A man from the people of Madīnah narrated to me from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \"Do not follow the bier with raised voice or fire.\" Hārūn added: \"And do not walk before it.\"\n\nThe Shaykh said: He means — and Allah knows best — \"and do not walk before it with fire,\" just as it should not be followed with fire. [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi — Abū Dāwūd]"
"6654","(6654) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us: al-Muʿtamir said: I read to Fuḍayl ibn Mayṣarah, from Abū Ḥarīz, that Abū Burdah narrated to him, saying: Abū Mūsā made a bequest when death approached him. He said: \"When you take my bier, hasten my walking, and do not follow me with burning incense, and do not place anything upon my grave that would come between me and the soil, and do not build any structure over my grave. And I call you as witnesses that I am free of every woman who shaves (her head in mourning), or wails, or tears her garment.\" They said to him: \"Did you hear anything about this?\" He said: \"Yes, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\"\n\nThe Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And in the bequests of ʬʾishah, ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, Abū Hurayrah, Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, and Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them all) is the instruction that the bier should not be followed with fire. [Ḍaʿīf — Ibn Mājah]"
"6655","(6655) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī narrated to us in Baghdad: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Tarqafī narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ informed us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb informed us: Shuraḥbīl ibn Sharīk narrated from ʿAlī ibn Rābāḥ al-Lakhmī, who said: I heard Abū Rāfiʿ narrate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Whoever washes a Muslim and conceals (his faults), Allah will forgive him forty times. And whoever digs a grave for him and conceals him in it, he will receive a reward equivalent to that of providing a dwelling in which he resides until the Day of Resurrection. And whoever shrouds him, Allah will clothe him on the Day of Resurrection with silk and brocade of Paradise.\" [Jayyid — narrated by al-Ḥākim]"
"6656","(6656) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Aḥmad Ḥamzah ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥārith al-ʿAqabī informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Rūḥ al-Madāʾinī narrated to us: Sāwādah ibn Salamah ibn Nubayṭ narrated from his father Salamah ibn Nubayṭ, from Nubayṭ ibn Sharīṭ, from Sālim ibn ʿUbayd al-Ashjaʿī, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, the most distressed of all the people over him was ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him). He mentioned the hadith until he said: They said, meaning to Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): \"O Companion of the Messenger of Allah, has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died?\" He said: \"Yes, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died.\" They said: \"O Companion of the Messenger of Allah, who will wash him?\" He said: \"The men of his household, the nearest among them.\" They said: \"O Companion of the Messenger of Allah, where will you bury him?\" He said: \"Bury him in the place where Allah took his soul, for He did not take his soul except in the most beloved of places to Him.\" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6657","(6657) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, Abū Jaʿfar, say: The Prophet ﷺ was washed three times with sidr (lotus leaves), and he was washed while wearing a shirt, and he was washed from a well called al-Ghars, belonging to Saʿd ibn Khaythamah, from which the Prophet ﷺ used to drink. The lower part of his body was attended to by ʿAlī and al-Faḍl, who was cradling him, while al-ʿAbbās poured the water. Al-Faḍl kept saying: \"Give me relief, for you have cut my aorta, for I feel something dripping upon me.\"\n[Weak. ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
"6658","(6658) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿUthmān narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to them, saying: Salām ibn Abī Muṭīʿ narrated to us, from Jābir, from al-Shaʿbī, from Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Whoever takes charge of washing a deceased person and fulfils the trust therein — meaning, conceals what he sees at that time — his sins will be forgiven as on the day his mother bore him.\" She said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Let the one who washes him be the one closest to him, if he knows how; and if the closest one does not know, then someone whom you know to be pious and trustworthy.\"\n[Weak. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"6659","(6659) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿUrubah al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī Maʿshar al-Sulamī narrated to us in Ḥarrān: ʿAmr ibn Hishām and Aḥmad ibn Bakkār narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, from Yaʿqūb ibn ʿUtbah, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned one day from a funeral at al-Baqīʿ, and I was experiencing a headache in my head, and I was saying: \"Oh, my head!\" He said: \"Nay, ʿĀʾishah, oh my head!\" Then he said: \"What harm would it do you if you were to die before me, so that I could wash you, shroud you, pray over you, and then bury you?\" I said: \"It is as if, by Allah, you think that if you did that, you would return to your house and consummate the marriage with one of your other wives.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled. Then he was afflicted with the illness in which he passed away.\n[Ḥasan. Ibn Mājah]"
"6660","(6660) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Makhzūmī narrated to us: ʿAwn ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, from his mother Umm Jaʿfar, daughter of Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar — I think he also narrated from ʿUmarāh ibn al-Muhājir — from Umm Jaʿfar, that Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said: \"O Asmāʾ, when I die, you and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib should wash me.\" So ʿAlī and Asmāʾ washed her, may Allah be pleased with them both.\n[Weak. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym]"
"6661","(6661) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn ʿUmayr ibn Yūsuf al-Dimashqī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Ḥamzah al-Zubayrī, narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Mūsā, from ʿAwn ibn Muḥammad al-Hāshimī, from his mother, from Asmāʾ ibnat ʿUmays, that Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, bequeathed that her husband ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, should wash her. So he and Asmāʾ ibnat ʿUmays washed her.\n\nAl-Dārawardī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Mūsā, from ʿAwn ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, from ʿUmarāh ibn al-Muhājir, that Umm Jaʿfar, daughter of Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, said: Asmāʾ ibnat ʿUmays narrated to me, saying: \"I and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, washed Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal narrated to us: al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us: al-Nufaylī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Mūsā narrated to us, and he mentioned it.\n[Weak. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
"6662","(6662) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ḥaddāʾ narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Thābit narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Muhājir al-Bajalī narrated to us, from his father, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, that Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, washed his wife when she died.\n\nAnd through this chain, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, that he washed his wife when she died. And we have narrated regarding the husband washing his wife from ʿAlqamah, Jābir ibn Zayd, Abū Qilābah, and others among the Tābiʿīn. And it was narrated from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh, from Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"The man has more right to wash his wife.\"\n[Weak]"
"6663","(6663) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Baṭṭah al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rustah narrated to us: Abū Ayyūb Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Minqarī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn akhī al-Zuhrī narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, passed away on the night of Tuesday, the eighth of Jumādā al-Ūlā, in the year thirteen [AH]. He bequeathed that his wife Asmāʾ ibnat ʿUmays should wash him, but she was weak, so she sought the assistance of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.\n\nThis connected ḥadīth, even though its narrator Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar al-Wāqidī, the author of history and maghāzī, is not strong, has supporting narrations (marāsīl) from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, and from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, that Asmāʾ ibnat ʿUmays washed her husband Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Some of them mentioned that Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had bequeathed that.\n[Ḥasan li-ghayrih. al-Ḥākim]"
"6664","(6664) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Azdī narrated to us: al-Zuhrī narrated to me, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"May Allah have mercy on a man whose wife washed him and who was shrouded in his worn-out garments.\" She said: This was done with my father Abū Bakr — his wife Asmāʾ ibnat ʿUmays al-Ashjaʿiyyah washed him, and he was shrouded in the garments he used to wear.\nThis chain is weak. [Bāṭil]"
"6665","(6665) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Zurʿah ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAmr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Khālid narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: \"If I were to face what I have turned my back on regarding the matter, no one other than his wives would have washed the Prophet ﷺ.\"\n\nThe Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: She expressed regret over that, and one does not express regret except over something that is permissible.\n[Ḥasan. Narrated by Ibn Mājah]"
"6666","(6666) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdbārī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab al-Muqriʾ narrated to us in Wāsiṭ: Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Abī Isḥāq, from Nājiyah ibn Kaʿb, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: \"I came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'Your elderly misguided uncle has died' — meaning his father. He said: 'Go and bury him, and do not do anything until you come to me.' So I came to him, and he commanded me, so I washed him. Then he supplicated for me with supplications, the like of which nothing on earth would please me more.\"\n\nAbū Dāwūd narrated in the Marāsīl from ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān, from Baqiyyah, and from Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf, from Abī al-Mughīrah — both of them from Ṣafwān, from Abī al-Yamān al-Hawzanī, who said: When Abū Ṭālib died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out accompanying his funeral. Ibn ʿAwf said: He walked alongside it and said: \"May your ties of kinship be blessed, and may you be rewarded with good.\" And he did not stand over his grave. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad narrated it to us: Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī narrated to us: Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, and he mentioned it.\n[Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrih. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6667","(6667) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abū Sinān, from Saʿīb ibn Jubayr, who said: A man came to Ibn ʿAbbās and said, \"My father died as a Christian.\" He said, \"Wash him, shroud him, and apply perfume to him, then bury him.\" Then he recited: {It is not for the Prophet and those who have believed to ask forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they were relatives} [At-Tawbah: 113], the verse. [Ṣaḥīḥ. An-Nasāʾī]"
"6668","(6668) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us, from Sulaymān — meaning Ibn Bilāl — from ʿAmr, the freed slave of al-Muṭṭalib, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbāh, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: \"There is no obligation upon you regarding the washing of your deceased when you have washed him.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us in this regard from ʿAṭāʾ and Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās. And it has been narrated to us through another chain from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, in a marfūʿ narration: \"Do not consider your dead to be impure, for indeed the Muslim is not impure, whether alive or dead.\"\n\nAnd it has been narrated to us in this regard from Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, Ibn ʿUmar, Ibn Masʿud, and ʿĀʾishah. All of this has been discussed in the Book of Purification. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6669","(6669) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Abū ʿAlī Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Hārūn ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us; Abū Bakr — meaning Ibn ʿAyyāsh — narrated from Muḥammad ibn Abī Sahal, from Makhūl, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When a woman dies among men and there is no other woman with them, or a man dies among women and there is no other man with them, then both of them are to be given tayammum and buried, and they are in the position of one who does not find water.\" This is a mursal narration.\n\nAnd it has been narrated from Sinān ibn Gharafah, from the Prophet ﷺ, regarding a man who dies among women and a woman who dies among men, neither of whom has a maḥram with them: they are to be given tayammum with clean earth and are not to be washed. [Munkar. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6670","(6670) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; Yazīd — meaning Ibn Hārūn — informed us; Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated from Maṭar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, regarding a woman who dies among men and there is no other woman with them: he said, \"She is to be buried in her garments.\"\n\nAnd it is narrated from Ibn al-Musayyab that he said: \"She is to be given tayammum with clean earth.\"\n\nAnd from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī: \"Water is to be poured over her from above the garments.\" And ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ said the same. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6671","(6671) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-Faḍl al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-ʿAdl narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym al-Mulāʾī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated — all of them — from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three white Saḥūlī garments, in which there was neither a shirt nor a turban. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Mālik. And in the narration of al-Thawrī, she said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three Saḥūlī cotton garments, in which there was neither a shirt nor a turban.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym and from Ibn Abī Uways, from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6672","(6672) Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī informed us; Abū Sahal Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who informed him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three white Saḥūlī garments, in which there was neither a shirt nor a turban.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6673","(6673) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: When the illness of Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, became severe, I wept, and he lost consciousness. I said: \"The one whose tears were always restrained shall now be poured forth all at once.\" She said: Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, regained consciousness and said: \"It is not as you have said, my daughter, but {There came the stupor of death in truth: 'This is what you were trying to avoid.'}\" Then he said: \"On which day did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pass away?\" She said: I said, \"Monday.\" She said: He said, \"Then what day is this?\" I said, \"Monday.\" He said: \"Then I hope from Allah that it be between me and the night.\" She said: He died on Tuesday night and was buried before dawn. She said: And he said, \"In how many garments did you shroud the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" She said: \"We shrouded him in three new white Saḥūlī garments, in which there was neither a shirt nor a turban.\" She said: He said to me: \"Wash this garment of mine, on which there is saffron dye or a reddish stain, and add with it two new garments.\" ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I said, \"It is worn out.\" He said: \"The living have greater need of the new than the dead; it is only for the water (that comes out of the body).\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it with this meaning from the ḥadīth of Wahb, from Hishām, without what is at the beginning of it regarding the weeping of ʿĀʾishah, her statement, and her recitation of the verse. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6674","(6674) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad and Aḥmad ibn Sahal both said: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Yazīd ibn al-Hād, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abū Salamah, who said: I asked ʿĀʾishah, \"In how many garments was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shrouded?\" She said: \"In three Saḥūlī garments.\" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6675","(6675) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah both said: Ibn Idrīs narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three Najrānī garments — the ḥullah consists of two garments — and his shirt in which he died. And ʿUthmān said: \"In three garments: a red ḥullah and his shirt in which he died ﷺ.\"\n\nThis is how Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād narrated it from Miqsam, and with the same meaning al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī narrated it in a mursal form. [Munkar. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"6676","(6676) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in two white garments and a striped Yemeni cloak. This is how Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā narrated it.\n\nAnd with the same meaning ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated it in a mursal form. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"6677","(6677) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq: al-Zuhri narrated to me: from 'Ali ibn al-Husayn, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three garments — two Saḥārī garments and a striped Yemeni cloth (burda of ḥabra), in which he was wrapped. [Weak. Reported by Ibn Sa'd.]"
"6678","(6678) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam al-Warraq narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiya narrated —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abu Ṣalih ibn Abi Ṭahir informed us, and the wording is his: my grandfather Yahya ibn Manṣur narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salama narrated to us: Hannad ibn al-Sarri narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiya narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from his father, from 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three white Saḥūli garments of unbleached cotton (kursuf), in which there was neither a shirt (qamiṣ) nor a turban ('imama). As for the ḥulla (the Yemeni garment), the people were confused about it, for it had been purchased for him so that he might be shrouded in it, but the ḥulla was set aside. Then 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr took it and said: \"I shall keep it for myself so that I may be shrouded in it.\" Then he said: \"Had Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, been pleased with it for His Prophet ﷺ, He would have shrouded him in it.\" So he sold it and gave its price in charity.\n\nMuslim reported it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from Yahya ibn Yahya and others.\n\nAnd Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar reported it from Abu Mu'awiya with his isnad, that she said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in two Yemeni striped cloths (burdayn of ḥabra) that had belonged to 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, and he was wrapped in them, then they were removed from him, and he mentioned the hadith.\n\nIn this there is evidence that 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr only withheld them for himself because they had belonged to him. And the narration of 'Ali ibn Mushir from Hisham also indicates this. [Ṣaḥiḥ. Muslim.]"
"6679","(6679) And Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Shadhan narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Ḥujr narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Mushir narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from his father, from 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet ﷺ was wrapped in a Yemeni garment (ḥulla) that had belonged to 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, then it was removed from him, and he was shrouded in three white Yemeni Saḥūli garments, in which there was neither a turban nor a shirt. Then 'Abd Allah raised the ḥulla and said: \"I shall be shrouded in it.\" Then he said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not shrouded in it, and shall I be shrouded in it?\" So he gave it in charity.\n\nMuslim reported it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from 'Ali ibn Ḥujr. [Ṣaḥiḥ. Muslim.]"
"6680","(6680) And Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed me: Abu al-Walid al-Faqih and Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far informed us: they said: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyath, Ibn 'Uyayna, Ibn Idris, 'Abda, and Waki' — all of them — from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, and this is the wording of the hadith of Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyath, from Hisham, from his father, from 'A'isha: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three white Yemeni garments, in which there was neither a shirt nor a turban. She said: It was said to 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her: \"They claim that he was shrouded in a striped cloth (burda of ḥabra).\" 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: \"They brought a striped cloth, but they did not shroud him in it.\"\n\nMuslim reported it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba."
"6681","(6681) Abu al-Ḥasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawi informed us: Abu Ḥamid al-Sharqi informed us: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Dhuhli narrated to us: 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated from al-Awza'i, who said: al-Zuhri narrated to me: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was wrapped in a cloth of ḥabra, then it was removed from him.\n\nAl-Qasim said: The remnants of that cloth are still in our possession.\n\nThe shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: What 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr sold and gave its price in charity was the ḥulla, and the ḥulla among them consisted of two garments. And what al-Qasim said — that its remnants are in our possession — is the third garment in which they claimed he was shrouded. In this, 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, gave a clear explanation that the two garments that were brought — which they called the ḥulla — and the striped Yemeni cloth were not used for his shrouding. Rather, he was shrouded in three white garments of unbleached cotton (kursuf), in which there was neither a shirt nor a turban. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥiḥ. Reported by Abu Dawud.]"
"6682","(6682) Abu Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qadi informed us: Ḥajib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭusi informed us: Muhammad ibn Ḥammad narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiya narrated —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra' and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam al-Warraq said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiya narrated from al-A'mash, from Shaqiyq, from Khabbab ibn al-Aratt, who said: We emigrated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the way of Allah, seeking the pleasure of Allah, and our reward became incumbent upon Allah. Among us were those who passed away without having received anything of their reward — among them was Mu'ṣab ibn 'Umayr, who was killed on the day of Uḥud. Nothing was found for him to be shrouded in except a coarse cloth (namira). When we placed it over his head, his legs would become exposed, and when we placed it over his legs, his head would become exposed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Place it over his head, and put some idhkhir (fragrant rush) upon his legs.\" He said: And among us were those whose fruits had ripened, and they would pluck them.\n\nMuslim reported it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari reported it through other chains from al-A'mash. [Ṣaḥiḥ. Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
"6683","(6683) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Ḥibban narrated from Ibn al-Mubarak from Shu'ba, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from his father, that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf was brought food while he was fasting, and he said: \"Mu'ṣab ibn 'Umayr was killed, and he was better than me, and he was shrouded in a cloth — if his head was covered, his legs would become exposed, and if his legs were covered, his head would become exposed.\" He said: \"And I think he also said: And Ḥamza was killed, and he was better than me.\" Then the world was spread out before us — or he said: \"We were given of the world what we were given — and we feared that our good deeds had been hastened for us.\" And he wept until he left the food.\n\nAl-Bukhari reported it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from 'Abdān and others from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak. [Ṣaḥiḥ. al-Bukhari.]"
"6684","(6684) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us: Abu Sa'id al-A'rabi informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Mahdi narrated to us —\n\n(Ḥ) And Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qadi said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad said: Ibrahim ibn Mahdi narrated to us: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida said: Hisham ibn 'Urwa informed us, from his father, from al-Zubayr, who said: When the polytheists withdrew on the day of Uḥud, the Prophet ﷺ sat aside, and a woman came walking toward the slain. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"The woman, the woman!\" When I recognized her, she was my mother, Ṣafiyya. I said: \"O mother, go back!\" She struck me upon my chest and said: \"I will not be pleased for you.\" I said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is commanding you.\" She said: She gave me two garments and said: \"Shroud your brother in these two.\" We found beside Ḥamza a man of the Ansar who had no shroud. We felt a reluctance within ourselves — that we should shroud Ḥamza in two garments while the Ansarī beside him had no shroud. So we drew lots between them for the better of the two garments, and we shrouded each one of them in the garment that fell to him by lot. [Good (Jayyid). Reported by Aḥmad.]"
"6685","(6685) Abu al-Ḥusayn 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Ṣaffar and Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz said: Sa'dan ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyayna narrated from 'Amr, who heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the grave of 'Abd Allah ibn Ubayy after he had been placed in his grave. He commanded that he be brought out, so he was brought out. He placed him upon his knees — or his thighs — and blew upon him from his saliva, and clothed him with his shirt. And Allah knows best.\n\nAl-Bukhari reported it in his Ṣaḥiḥ from Malik ibn Isma'il and others, and Muslim reported it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba and others, from Sufyan. [Ṣaḥiḥ. al-Bukhari.]"
"6686","(6686) And Abu al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishran informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Ṣaffar and Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz said: Sa'dan ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from 'Amr, who heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah saying: When al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muṭṭalib was in Madina, the Ansar sought a garment to clothe him, but they could find no shirt that fit him except the shirt of 'Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, so they clothed him with it.\n\nAl-Bukhari reported it from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Musnadi, from Sufyan. And it has been said that the Prophet ﷺ intended by what he did to requite him for what he had done. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥiḥ. al-Bukhari.]"
"6687","(6687) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Ibn Nājiyah narrated to us, Isḥāq narrated to us, Sufyān narrated and mentioned the hadith. And Sufyān added: \"Perhaps the Prophet (ﷺ) clothed him with that shirt as a recompense.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6688","(6688) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: \"When ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl died, his son ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and asked him to give him his shirt so that he might shroud his father in it, and he gave it to him. Then he asked him to pray over him, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up to pray over him. Then ʿUmar stood and grabbed the garment of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, will you pray over him when Allah has forbidden you to pray over him?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Indeed, Allah has given me a choice, for He said: \"Ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them; even if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times, Allah will not forgive them\" [al-Tawbah: 80]. And I will exceed seventy.'\" He said: \"He is a hypocrite.\" He said: \"So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed over him, and then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: \"And never pray over any of them who dies, nor stand at his grave\" [al-Tawbah: 84].'\" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Mūsā and others from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6689","(6689) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī al-ʿAdl informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, who said: \"The deceased is to be dressed in a shirt (qamīṣ) and wrapped with an izār, and then wrapped in a third garment. If there is only one garment, he is to be shrouded in it.\" This is mawqūf. And it has been narrated to us from Nāfiʿ that a son of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar died, and Ibn ʿUmar shrouded him in five garments: a turban, a shirt, and three wrappings. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik]"
"6690","(6690) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, al-Masʿūdī narrated from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit and al-Ḥakam, from Maymūn ibn Abī Shabīb, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Wear white garments, for they are the purest and cleanest, and shroud your dead in them.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. See its extraction in 5969]"
"6691","(6691) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Saʿīd — who is Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — narrated from Ayyūb, from Abī Qilābah, from Abī al-Muhallab, from Samurah ibn Jundub, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he said: \"Adhere to white: let your living wear it and shroud your dead in it, for it is among the best of your garments.\" And we have narrated it from Ibn ʿAbbās from the Prophet (ﷺ) in the Book of Jumuʿah. [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. See the preceding]"
"6692","(6692) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Karīm narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAqīl ibn Maʿqil narrated to me from his father, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"When one of you dies, if something is found, let him be shrouded in a garment of ḥibarah (fine Yemeni cloth).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6693","(6693) Abū ʿAlī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me, Ibn Wahb narrated to me, Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated from Ḥātim ibn Abī Naṣr, from ʿUbadah ibn Nusayy, from his father, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"The best of shrouds is the ḥullah, and the best of sacrificial animals is the horned ram.\" The Shaykh said: \"The ḥullah is predominantly two red garments. And the hadiths that the Prophet (ﷺ) was shrouded in white garments and that he preferred white are more authentic. And success is through Allah.\" [Very weak. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6694","(6694) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh narrate from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he addressed the people one day and mentioned one of his Companions who had passed away and had been shrouded in an inadequate shroud, and had been buried at night. The Prophet (ﷺ) rebuked burying at night until the funeral prayer had been performed over the deceased, unless a person is compelled to do so. And the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you shrouds his brother, let him make his shroud beautiful.\" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6695","(6695) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Muḥāribī narrated to us, ʿAmr Abū Mālik al-Janbī narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khāliḍ, from ʿĀmir, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: \"Do not be extravagant in the shroud, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Do not be extravagant in the shroud, for it will be stripped away swiftly.'\" [Weak. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6696","(6696) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb al-ʿAdl informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from Ṣilah, who said: \"When death approached Ḥudhayfah, he said: 'Buy me a shroud.' So a ḥullah was brought to him, the price of which was three hundred and fifty dirhams. He said: 'I have no need of it. Buy me two white garments, for they will not be left upon me except for a short while, until I will be exchanged for something better than them or worse than them.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭabarānī]"
"6697","(6697) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Abū Yaʿlā and al-Manīʿī informed me, both saying: Abū Ibrāhīm al-Tarjumānī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from his father, from Sahl: that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ with a woven cloak (burdah) whose border was woven into it. Then he said, \"Do you know what a burdah is?\" They said, \"A shamlah (a type of garment).\" He said, \"Yes.\" She said, \"I wove this with my own hands, so that I might clothe you with it.\" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wore it, being in need of it, and he went out wearing it as his lower garment (izār) or his upper wrap (ridāʾ) — Abū Ibrāhīm was uncertain. Then a certain man, whom he named that day, touched it and said, \"How beautiful is this burdah! Will you clothe me with it?\" He said, \"Yes.\" When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered his home, he folded it and sent it to him. The people said to him, \"By Allah, you did not do well. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wore it out of need for it, and then you asked him for it, knowing that he does not refuse a petitioner.\" He said, \"By Allah, I only asked him for it so that it might be my shroud on the day I die.\" Sahl said: And it was indeed his shroud on the day he died.\n\nNarrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and he did not express uncertainty regarding the izār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6698","(6698) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb and ʿAmr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that a man was standing with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at ʿArafah when he fell from his mount. Ayyūb said: \"And it broke his neck,\" and ʿAmr said: \"And it killed him outright,\" and he died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in two garments. Do not apply perfume to him, and do not cover his head, for Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection saying 'Labbayk' (Here I am).\" ʿAmr said: \"Saying 'Labbayk.'\" Ismāʿīl said: \"Thus said Musaddad, but ʿĀrim and Sulaymān contradicted him, both agreeing that ʿAmr said: 'Yulabbī' (he will say 'Labbayk'), and that Ayyūb said: 'Mulabbīyan' (saying 'Labbayk').\"\n\nNarrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it as well. In it is evidence that the non-muhrim (one not in the state of iḥrām) is perfumed and his head is covered, and that the prohibition was specific to the state of iḥrām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its classification is as in 6637.]"
"6699","(6699) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rūḥ al-Madāʾinī narrated to us; Shabābah ibn Sawwār narrated to us; Khārijah ibn Muṣʿab narrated from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿUtayy, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When Ādam fell ill with the illness in which he died, he said to his children: 'O my children, I am ill, and I desire what a sick person desires, and I desire the fruits of Paradise. So seek for me from the fruits of Paradise.' So they went out rushing upon the earth, and the angels met them face to face. They said: 'O children of Ādam, where are you going?' They said: 'We are seeking for our father from the fruits of Paradise.' They said: 'Return, for the command has been given to take the soul of your father to Paradise.' So they took his soul while they looked on, and they shrouded him and perfumed him while they looked on, and they prayed over him while they looked on. Then they said: 'O children of Ādam, this is your practice among your dead.'\"\n\nKhārijah ibn Muṣʿab raised it (to the Prophet ﷺ), but Hushaym ibn Bashīr and others stopped it (as a mawqūf narration) from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, and some of them added: \"Then they dug a grave, then they buried him,\" and added: \"So do likewise.\" [Bāṭil. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
"6700","(6700) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb informed us; Surayj ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd narrated from al-Ḥasan; ʿUtayy al-Saʿdī narrated to me, saying: I heard Ubayy ibn Kaʿb narrating, saying: \"When Ādam was in his death throes…\" and he mentioned the hadith in its entirety, with the same meaning, as a mawqūf narration. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
"6701","(6701) Abū Ḥāziṃ al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah informed us; Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān narrated to us — he dictated it to us; Abū al-Naḍr Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah Shaybān narrated from Layth ibn Abī Sulaym, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Bashīr, from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from Umm Sulaym, mother of Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When a woman dies and you wish to wash her…\" Then he mentioned the hadith in its entirety, saying: \"Then stuff her lower portion (sāfilah) with cotton as much as you can, then moisten her cotton with her perfume. Then take a long washed cloth (sabaniyyah) and tie it around her waist as a waist-wrapper (niṭāq) is tied. Then fasten it between her thighs and press her thighs together. Then let the end of the sabaniyyah hang from near her waist down to near her knees — this is the covering of her lower portion. Then perfume her and shroud her.\"\n\n[Munkar. al-Ṭabarānī.]"
"6702","(6702) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā narrated to me; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated from Quṭbah ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, from al-Aʿmash, from Abī Sufyān, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When you perform istinjāʾ (cleansing) of the deceased, do so an odd number of times.\" And it is also narrated: \"Make the shroud of the deceased with three folds.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"6703","(6703) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad said: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, and I mentioned to him this hadith. Yaḥyā said: \"No one raised it (to the Prophet ﷺ) except Yaḥyā ibn Ādam.\" Yaḥyā said: \"And I do not think this hadith is anything but an error.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"6704","(6704) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr — may Allah be pleased with both of them — that she said to her family: \"Apply perfume to my garments when I die, then perfume me, but do not leave any perfume on my shroud, and do not follow me with fire (i.e., incense).\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik.]"
"6705","(6705) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Tawbah al-Ṣūfī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Ḥātim al-Āmulī informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Bishr ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated to me; Zāʾidah informed me, saying: I heard al-Nakhaʿī from ʿAlqamah, from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: \"Camphor (kāfūr) is placed on the places of prostration.\"\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
"6706","(6706) As for musk: Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us from his original manuscript; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Shuʿbah narrated from Khulayd ibn Jaʿfar and al-Mustamir al-Azdī, both saying: We heard Abā Naḍrah narrating from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned a woman from the Children of Israel and said: \"She filled her ring with musk, and musk is the best of perfumes.\"\n\nNarrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Yazīd ibn Hārūn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"6707","(6707) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Musa narrated to us; Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ru'asi narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Harun ibn Sa'd, from Abu Wa'il, who said: There was musk with 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and he instructed in his will that it be used as perfume for his body. And 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"It is from the surplus of the embalming perfume of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).\"\n\nAnd we have narrated something similar from Ibn 'Umar and Anas ibn Malik. [Good. Reported by al-Hakim]"
"6708","(6708) Imam Abu al-Fath al-'Umari narrated to us; Abu Muhammad al-Shurayhi narrated to us; Abu al-Qasim al-Baghawi narrated to us; Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Maslama narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Umayya narrated to us, from Nafi', who said: Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn 'Amr ibn Nufayl (may Allah be pleased with him) died, and he was a participant of Badr. Umm Sa'id said to 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): \"Will you embalm him with musk?\" He said: \"What perfume is better than musk? Bring me your musk.\" So she handed it to him. He said: \"It was not done as you do it now. We used to apply the embalming perfume to his armpits and the folds of his thighs.\" [Very Weak]"
"6709","(6709) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Muhammad al-Hasan ibn Hamshad, the brother of 'Ali, narrated to us; Abu Isma'il Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us; Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me; Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me; Humayd narrated to me, who said: When Anas ibn Malik died, musk was placed in his embalming perfume, which contained some of the perspiration of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). [Sahih in its own right. Reported by al-Tabrani]"
"6710","(6710) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib narrated to us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili narrated to me; Abu Ya'la narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Jamil al-Marwazi narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us; Ma'mar and Yunus narrated to us. Al-Zuhri said: And Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf informed me that 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him), informed him that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) came on a horse from his dwelling at Sunh, then he dismounted and entered the mosque. He did not speak to the people until he went in to 'A'ishah. He made his way to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who was covered with a striped Yemeni cloak. He uncovered his face, bent over him, kissed him, and wept. Then he said: \"May my father be sacrificed for you! By Allah, Allah will never bring upon you two deaths. As for the death that Allah had decreed for you, you have now experienced it.\"\n\nAl-Zuhri said: Abu Salamah informed me, saying: Ibn 'Abbas informed me that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) went out while 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was addressing the people. He said: \"Sit down.\" But 'Umar refused to sit. He said: \"Sit down.\" But he again refused to sit. So Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) pronounced the testimony of faith, and the people turned towards him and left 'Umar. He said: \"O people! Whoever among you used to worship Muhammad, then indeed Muhammad has died. And whoever among you used to worship Allah, then indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is Ever-Living and does not die.\" Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {Muhammad is not but a messenger. [Other] messengers have passed on before him. So if he was to die or be killed, would you turn back on your heels [to unbelief]?} [Aal 'Imran: 144] to {the grateful ones}. He said: \"By Allah, it was as if the people had not known that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, had revealed this verse until Abu Bakr recited it. The people received it from him, and there was no one who heard it except that he would recite it.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Bishr ibn Muhammad, from Ibn al-Mubarak. [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari]"
"6711","(6711) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; Ibn Milhan narrated to us; Yahya narrated, from al-Layth, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit al-Ansari informed me that Umm al-'Ala', a woman from the Ansar who had given her pledge of allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), informed him that they drew lots among the emigrants, and 'Uthman ibn Maz'un fell to their lot. We lodged him in our homes, and he fell ill with the illness in which he died. When he died, was washed, and was shrouded in three garments, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) entered. She said: I said: \"May Allah have mercy on you, Abu al-Sa'ib! My testimony about you is that Allah has honored you.\" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"How do you know that Allah has honored him?\" I said: \"May my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah! Whom then has Allah honored?\" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"As for him, by Allah, certainty has come to him. By Allah, I hope good for him. And by Allah, I do not know, even though I am the Messenger of Allah, what will be done to me.\" She said: \"By Allah, I will never declare anyone pure after him.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Bukayr, and he said: \"He was shrouded in his garments.\" And at the end of it she said: \"By Allah, I will never declare anyone pure after him.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari said: Nafi' ibn Yazid said, from 'Uqayl: \"What will be done to him.\" And Shu'ayb, 'Amr ibn Dinar, and Ma'mar followed him in this.\n\nIt is mentioned that al-Layth ibn Sa'd said this statement before the revelation of {Indeed, We have given you a clear victory} [al-Fath: 1].\n\n[Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari]"
"6712","(6712) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us; al-Thawri narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Asim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from 'A'ishah, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) entered upon 'Uthman ibn Maz'un while he was dead. He uncovered his face, then bent over him, kissed him, and wept until I saw the tears flowing down his cheeks. [Weak. Reported by al-Tirmidhi]"
"6713","(6713) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; al-Baghandi narrated to us; Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, who said: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say: When my father was killed on the day of Uhud, I began to weep and to remove the cloth from his face. The Companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him) kept forbidding me from doing that, but the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not forbid me from it. My aunt began to weep. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"Do not weep, or what makes you weep? The angels continued to shade him with their wings until they lifted him up.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Abu al-Walid, and Muslim reported it through another chain from Shu'bah. [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari]"
"6714","(6714) Abu al-Qasim Talhah ibn 'Ali ibn al-Saqr narrated to us in Baghdad; Ahmad ibn 'Uthman ibn Yahya al-Adami narrated to us; 'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us; Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us; Khalaf, that is Ibn Khalifah, narrated to us. He said: I heard my father say — I think he heard it from his freedman, and his freedman was Ma'qil ibn Yasar — that when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) placed Nu'aym ibn Mas'ud in the grave, he removed the ties from his face with his mouth.\n\nHis words \"I think\" — I reckon they are from the words of al-Duri.\n\nAbu Dawud narrated it in the Marasil from 'Abbad ibn Musa and Sulayman ibn Dawud al-'Atki, that Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to them from his father, who said: It reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) — and he mentioned it. [Very Weak. Reported by Ibn Abi Shaybah]"
"6715","(6715) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me by way of ijazah that Abu al-Walid informed them; Ibrahim ibn 'Ali narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from 'Uqbah ibn Yasar, who said: 'Uthman, the brother of Samurah, narrated to me, who said: A son of Samurah died, and he mentioned the hadith. He said: \"Take him to his grave, and when you place him in his niche, say: 'In the name of Allah and upon the way of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).' Then untie the knots of his head and the knots of his feet.\" [Weak. Reported by al-Harith]"
"6716","(6716) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad, Isma'il ibn Qutaybah, Muhammad ibn Hajjaj, and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam narrated to us. They said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Miswari narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad, from 'Amir ibn Sa'd, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas said during the illness in which he died: \"Dig a niche for me and set up bricks over me in the same manner as was done for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Reported by Muslim]"
"6717","(6717) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muhammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, saying: Abū al-'Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Ḥusayn ibn 'Abdillāh ibn 'Ubaydillāh ibn 'Abbās narrated to me from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: When they intended to dig the grave for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ used to dig the *shaqq* (trench-type grave) for the people of Makkah, and Abū Ṭalḥah Zayd ibn Sahl — may Allah be pleased with him — used to dig the *laḥd* (niche-type grave) for the people of Madinah. So al-'Abbās called two men and took hold of them by their necks and said: \"You go to Abū 'Ubaydah, and you go to Abū Ṭalḥah. O Allah, choose for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whichever of them comes first — let him dig his grave.\" The companion of Abū Ṭalḥah found Abū Ṭalḥah and brought him, but the companion of Abū 'Ubaydah did not find Abū 'Ubaydah. Thus a *laḥd* was prepared for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. This has also been narrated in an abridged form from Anas ibn Mālik. [Weak. Ibn Mājah]"
"6718","(6718) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ismā'īl narrated to us: Ḥakkām ibn Salm narrated from 'Alī ibn 'Abd al-A'lā, from his father, from Sa'īd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The *laḥd* is for us, and the *shaqq* is for others besides us.\" This has also also been narrated in a marfū' form from Jarīr ibn 'Abdillāh. [Hasan due to supporting narrations. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6719","(6719) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us: Sufyān narrated from Muslim ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān, from 'Uthmān ibn 'Umayr Abī al-Yaqẓān, from Zādhān, from Jarīr ibn 'Abdillāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The *laḥd* is for us, and the *shaqq* is for others besides us.\" 'Abd al-Razzāq narrated it thus from al-Thawrī. However, Wakī' and al-Firyābī and a group narrated it from Sufyān from 'Uthmān ibn 'Umayr, and they did not mention Muslim ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān in the chain. [Hasan due to supporting narrations. Ibn Mājah]"
"6720","(6720) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: 'Abdullāh ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from Abū Jamrah, who said: I heard Ibn 'Abbās say: A red velvet cloth (*qaṭīfah ḥamrā'*) was placed in the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6721","(6721) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismā'īl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Wakī' narrated from Shu'bah with the same meaning. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Wakī' from Shu'bah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6722","(6722) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Ḥusayn ibn 'Abdillāh narrated to me from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: Shuqrān, when he placed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in his grave, brought a velvet cloth that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to wear and spread out, and he buried it with him in the grave, saying: \"By Allah, no one shall wear it after you.\" So it was buried with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. In this narration, if it is established, there is an indication that they did not spread it out in the grave, in accordance with the Sunnah regarding that. It has been narrated from Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm, from Ibn 'Abbās, that he disliked placing a garment beneath the deceased in the grave. [Weak. Refer to the commentary of no. 6717]"
"6723","(6723) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī narrated to us by dictation: Abū Qalābah 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī narrated to us: Mu'ādh ibn Hāni' narrated to us: Ḥarb ibn Shaddād narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from 'Abd al-Ḥamīd ibn Sinān, from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from his father, who narrated to him — and he was one of the Companions — that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said during the Farewell Pilgrimage: \"Verily, the allies of Allah are those who perform the five prayers that have been made obligatory upon them, fast Ramadan seeking its reward believing it to be a duty upon them, give the zakāh of their wealth hoping for its reward, and avoid the major sins that Allah has forbidden.\" Then a man asked him and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what are the major sins?\" He ﷺ said: \"They are nine: associating partners with Allah, killing a believer without right, fleeing from the battlefield, consuming the wealth of the orphan, consuming usury, slandering a chaste woman, disobeying Muslim parents, and declaring the Sacred House — your qiblah in life and in death — lawful (for violation).\" Then he ﷺ said: \"No man dies without having committed these major sins, and he establishes the prayer and gives the zakāh, except that he will be with the Prophet ﷺ in a house whose doors are panels of gold.\" The word \"al-siḥr\" (sorcery) has dropped from my book or from my shaykh's book. [Weak. Al-Ḥākim narrated it]"
"6724","(6724) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us: al-'Abbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Marwarrūdhī narrated to us: Ayyūb narrated from Ṭaysalah ibn 'Alī, who said: I asked Ibn 'Umar while he was at the base of the *arāk* trees on the Day of 'Arafah, sprinkling water over his head and face, and I said to him: \"May Allah have mercy on you, narrate to me about the major sins.\" He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The major sins are associating partners with Allah and slandering a chaste woman.\" I said: \"What about killing a soul?\" He said: \"Yes, and despite your aversion — and killing a believing soul, fleeing from the battlefield, consuming the wealth of the orphan, disobeying Muslim parents, and deviating from the Sacred House — your qiblah in life and in death.\" We have narrated from al-Ḥasan, from 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — that he mentioned the Ka'bah and said: \"By Allah, it is nothing but stones that Allah has set as a qiblah for our living, and we direct our dead towards it.\" [Weak. Ibn Jarīr narrated it]"
"6725","(6725) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū 'Abdillāh al-Sūsī said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us: al-'Abbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed me: my father narrated to me: al-Awzā'ī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me: Abū Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān narrated to me: Abū Hurayrah narrated — and he mentioned the hadith regarding the sanctity of Makkah and the statement of the Prophet ﷺ: \"Its trees are not to be cut, and its thorns are not to be uprooted.\" He said: Then al-'Abbās ibn 'Abd al-Muṭṭalib said: \"O Messenger of Allah, except for the *idhkhir* (lemon grass), for we use it in our dwellings and our graves.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Except the *idhkhir*, except the *idhkhir*.\" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the two Ṣaḥīḥs from the hadith of al-Awzā'ī. Al-Bukhārī also narrated it from the hadith of Ibn 'Abbās and Ṣafiyyah bint Shaybah with the same meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it]"
"6726","(6726) 'Ubaydullāh ibn Zaḥr narrated from 'Alī ibn Yazīd, from al-Qāsim ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān, from Abī Umāmah, who said: When Umm Kulthūm, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, was placed in the grave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"{From it We created you, and into it We shall return you, and from it We shall bring you out once again} [Ṭā Hā: 55]. In the name of Allah, in the path of Allah, and upon the way of the Messenger of Allah.\" When her *laḥd* was built over her, he began throwing clods of earth at them and said: \"Fill the gaps between the bricks.\" Then he said: \"Verily, this is nothing, but it brings comfort to the soul of the living.\" This is a weak chain. Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Ja'far Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn 'Uthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Sahmī narrated to me: my father narrated to me: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated from 'Ubaydullāh ibn Zaḥr, and he mentioned it. It has also been narrated regarding filling the gaps with clay and his statement: \"Verily, it neither harms nor benefits, but it brings comfort to the eye of the living,\" from Makhūl, from the Prophet ﷺ, as a mursal narration. [Munkar. Aḥmad narrated it]"
"6727","(6727) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, the wife of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, narrated to me. Ibn Isḥāq said: She granted me access to her, so that I heard it directly from her. She narrated from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: \"By Allah, we did not learn of the burial of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) until we heard the sound of the shovels in the depths of Wednesday night.\" [Weak. Reported by Aḥmad.]"
"6728","(6728) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Thābit, from Anas, who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) fell ill with the illness in which he passed away, Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with him) leaned him against her chest. Distress overwhelmed him, and she said: 'O the distress of my father!' He said: 'Indeed, there shall be no distress for your father after this day.' When he passed away and was buried, Fāṭimah said to me: 'O Anas, did your souls find it agreeable that you should heap earth upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)?'\" Ḥammād said: What he preserved was \"did your souls find it agreeable that you should heap (earth).\" Reported by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb from Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6729","(6729) And in what Abū Dāwūd mentioned in al-Marāsīl, from Aḥmad ibn Manīʿ, from Ḥammād ibn Khālid, from Hishām ibn Saʿd, from Ziyād, from Abī al-Munḍhir: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) heaped earth into a grave three times.\" Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad narrated it to us, Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī narrated to us, Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us, and Abū Dāwūd narrated it, mentioning it. [Weak. al-Ṭabarānī.]"
"6730","(6730) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Ḥafṣ al-Madāʾinī narrated to us, al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, meaning al-ʿUmarī, narrated to us from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah, from his father, who said: \"I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) when ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (may Allah be pleased with him) was buried. He prayed over him and pronounced four takbīrs, and he heaped earth with his both hands, three handfuls of soil, while standing at the grave.\"\n\nIts chain of narration is weak, except that it has a corroborating witness from the narration of Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal hadith, and it is also narrated from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) as a marfūʿ hadith. And Allah knows best. [Very weak. Reported by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"6731","(6731) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ḥimyar narrated to me from Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Alhānī, from Abī Umāmah, who said: \"A man died and no good deed was recorded for him except three handfuls of earth that he had heaped into a grave, and his sins were forgiven.\" This is a mawqūf narration, sound in this chapter. [Weak. Sunan al-Kubrā.]"
"6732","(6732) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Mālik ibn Mighwal narrated to us from ʿUmayr ibn Saʿīd, who said: \"ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed over Yazīd ibn Mukaffif and pronounced four takbīrs. Then he heaped earth into his grave.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Sunan al-Kubrā.]"
"6733","(6733) He said: And Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, Sharīk narrated to us from Abī Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, from ʿUmayr ibn Saʿīd: \"He saw ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) at the grave of Ibn Mukaffif, heaping two or three handfuls (of earth).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains.]"
"6734","(6734) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Maʿmar narrated to us from ʿAlī ibn Zayd: \"When Ibn ʿAbbās buried Zayd ibn Thābit, he heaped earth upon him and then said: 'Thus is knowledge buried.' I narrated this to ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn, and he said: 'And Ibn ʿAbbās—by Allah—much knowledge was buried with him, by Allah.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Reported by al-Ḥākim.]"
"6735","(6735) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade that anything be built upon the grave, or that earth be added to it, or that it be plastered with gypsum.\"\n\n(T) And it was narrated by Abān ibn Abī ʿAyyāsh from al-Ḥasan and from Abī Naḍrah, from Jābir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \"Nor should earth be added to its excavation.\" The first hadith is sufficient. Abān is weak. And it was also narrated as such. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
"6736","(6736) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Kāmil narrated to us, al-Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān narrated to us from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father, from Jābir: \"The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was given a laḥd (niche grave), and bricks were set in place upon it.\" He mentioned the hadith and said: \"His grave was raised from the earth approximately a span, as I found it.\" [Munkar. Reported by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
"6737","(6737) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me by way of ijazah, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ahmad ibn 'Abdah narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father: that water was sprinkled over the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ), and pebbles from the pebbles of al-'Aṣrah were placed upon it, and his grave was raised to the extent of a hand-span. This is a mursal narration, and al-Waqidi narrated it with his own chain from Jabir, and that will be mentioned. [Hasan — Mursal]"
"6738","(6738) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi, Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, and Abu Sadiq ibn Abi al-Fawaris narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us, Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated from Thumamah ibn Shufayy, who said: We went out as raiders in the time of Mu'awiyah towards these desert paths, and Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd was our commander. A cousin of mine called Nafi' ibn 'Abd passed away. Fadalah stood at his grave, and when we had buried him, he said: \"Lighten the earth upon him, for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command us to level the graves.\" [Hasan — Narrated by Ahmad]"
"6739","(6739) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father: that the sprinkling of water upon the grave was practiced during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). [Da'if]"
"6740","(6740) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and others narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Rabi' informed us, al-Shafi'i informed us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father: that the Prophet (ﷺ) sprinkled water upon the grave of his son Ibrahim and placed pebbles upon it.\n\nAl-Shafi'i said: Pebbles do not remain firm except upon a leveled (flat) grave. [Very Da'if — Narrated by al-Shafi'i]"
"6741","(6741) In what Abu Dawud mentioned in al-Marasil, from 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah and others, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad, from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Umar from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sprinkled water upon the grave of Ibrahim, and that it was the first grave upon which water was sprinkled, and that when he had buried him and finished, he said: \"Salamun 'alaykum.\" And I do not know him to have said anything except that he cast earth upon it with his hand.\n\nAbu Bakr informed us, al-Fasawi narrated to us, al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated it and mentioned it. [Da'if]"
"6742","(6742) Abu 'Abd Allah informed me by way of ijazah, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar al-Ju'fi narrated to us, al-Darawardi narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Umar from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sprinkled water upon the grave of his son. He said: And I do not know him to have said anything except that he cast earth upon it with his hand. [Da'if]"
"6743","(6743) Muhammad ibn 'Umar al-Waqidi narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far, from Ibn Abi 'Awn, from Abu 'Atiq, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, who said: Water was sprinkled upon the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ) with sprinkling. He said: The one who sprinkled the water upon his grave was Bilal ibn Rabah, with a water-skin, beginning from the direction of his head on his right side until he reached his feet. Then he directed the water towards the wall, for he was not able to go around because of the wall.\n\nAbu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us of that, Abu 'Abd Allah al-Isbahani — that is, Ibn Battah — narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn al-Jahm narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, al-Waqidi narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated it and mentioned it. [Very Da'if — Ibn Sa'd]"
"6744","(6744) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us, Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Salim narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Yahya ibn al-Fadl al-Sijistani narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us with the same meaning, from Kathir ibn Zayd al-Madani from al-Muttalib, who said: When 'Uthman ibn Maz'un passed away, his bier was brought out and he was buried. The Prophet (ﷺ) ordered a man to bring him a stone, but he was unable to carry it. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up and rolled up his sleeves. Kathir said: al-Muttalib said — the one who informed me of that from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"It is as though I am looking at the whiteness of the forearms of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he rolled up his sleeves.\" Then he carried it and placed it at his head and said: \"So that thereby the grave of my brother may be known, and so that those of my family who die may be buried near him.\"\n\n[Ṣaḥīḥ by other than it — Narrated by Abu Dawud]"
"6745","(6745) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Abu al-Tahir and Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli said: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abu 'Abd Allah informed me, Abu Muhammad — 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl al-Dabbas — narrated to us in Makkah, Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Zayd al-Sa'ig narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Shabib ibn Sa'id narrated to us, my father narrated to me from Yunus from Ibn Shihab, who informed me that 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj informed him that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever attends the funeral until the prayer is performed over it shall have one qirat, and whoever attends it until it is buried shall have two qirats.\" It was said: \"And what are the two qirats?\" He said: \"Like the two great mountains.\"\n\nThe hadith of Shabib ends here. Ibn Wahb added in his narration from Yunus: Ibn Shihab said: Salim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar said: Ibn 'Umar used to pray over it and then depart. When the hadith of Abu Hurayrah reached him, he said: \"We have indeed forfeited many qirats.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Ahmad ibn Shabib, and Muslim narrated it from Abu al-Tahir, Haramlah, and Harun ibn Sa'id, and he mentioned the addition in the narration of Haramlah and Harun. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by al-Bukhari]"
"6746","(6746) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad, Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us, Isma'il al-Qadi informed us, Nasr ibn 'Ali informed us, 'Abd al-A'la informed us, Ma'mar narrated from al-Zuhri from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever prays over a funeral shall have one qirat, and whoever waits until it is finished shall have two qirats.\" They said: \"And what are the two qirats?\" He said: \"Like the two great mountains.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from 'Abd al-A'la, and he said: \"until it is finished from it.\"\n\n'Abd al-Razzaq narrated it from Ma'mar and said: \"until it is placed in the lahd.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhari]"
"6747","(6747) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Abī al-ʿAbbāz al-Zawzanī informed us; Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, and he mentioned it.\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. And ʿUqayl narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: \"Men from among the companions of Abū Hurayrah narrated to me, who said: 'And whoever follows it until it is buried.'\" And Abū Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated from Abū Hurayrah, who said: \"Until one is free from it (i.e., until the burial is completed).\" And likewise Abū Ṣāliḥ and al-Shaʿbī narrated from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Nasāʾī]"
"6748","(6748) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣiddīq, known as Khushnām, informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ informed us; Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ informed me; Abū Ṣakhrah narrated to me from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qusayyṭ, who narrated to him that Dāwūd ibn ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ narrated to him from his father, who said: He was sitting with ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar when Khabbāb, the owner of al-Maqṣūrah, came and said: \"O ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, do you not hear what Abū Hurayrah says? Indeed, he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Whoever goes out with a funeral procession from its house, then prays over it, then follows it until it is buried, he shall have two qīrāṭs of reward. And whoever prays over it and then returns, he shall have one qīrāṭ of reward, the size of Uḥud.'\" So Ibn ʿUmar sent Khabbāb to ʿĀʾishah to ask her about the statement of Abū Hurayrah, so that he might return to him and inform him of what ʿĀʾishah said. Ibn ʿUmar took a handful of pebbles from the mosque and turned them over in his hand until the messenger returned to him. He said: ʿĀʾishah said: \"Abū Hurayrah has spoken the truth.\" So Ibn ʿUmar struck the pebbles that were in his hand upon the ground and said: \"We have indeed neglected many qīrāṭs.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr from al-Muqriʾ.\n\nThe majority of narrations from Abū Hurayrah are upon the wording of \"completion\" (al-farāgh) and \"burial\" (al-dafn), except what we have narrated from ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar, although ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā contradicted him from Maʿmar. And the meaning of the narration of ʿAbd al-Razzāq has been narrated from another route from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6749","(6749) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed me; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbadī and Bishr ibn Hilāl al-Ṣawwāf both said: Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Saʿīd, narrated to us from Yazīd ibn Kaysān, who said: Abū Ḥāzim informed me from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever prays over a funeral, he shall have one qīrāṭ, and whoever follows it until it is placed in the grave, he shall have two qīrāṭs.\" He said: I said: \"O Abā Hurayrah, what is a qīrāṭ?\" He said: \"The size of Uḥud.\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. And Thawbān narrated it from the Prophet (ﷺ) with the meaning of the narration of the group. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6750","(6750) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us; ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us; Shuʿbah narrated to us from Qatādah from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd from Maʿdān ibn Abī Ṭalḥah from Thawbān, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever prays over a funeral, he shall have one qīrāṭ, and whoever witnesses its burial, he shall have two qīrāṭs. A qīrāṭ is the size of Uḥud.\" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah, Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, Hishām al-Dastawāʾī, and Abān ibn Yazīd, all from Qatādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6751","(6751) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl informed us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah informed us; Abū al-Naḍr informed us; Sulaymān, meaning Ibn al-Mughīrah, narrated to us from Ḥumayd, meaning Ibn Hilāl, from Hishām ibn ʿĀmir, who said: The Anṣār came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the day of Uḥud and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, we have suffered wounds and hardship. What do you command?\" He said: \"Dig wide and place two or three men in a single grave.\" They said: \"Who should be placed in the grave first?\" He said: \"The one who has memorized the most Qurʾān.\" He said: \"My father was placed ahead of two men\" — or he said, \"one man.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Abū Dāwūd]"
"6752","(6752) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us; Ibn ʿUthmān, meaning ʿAbdān, informed us; ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn al-Mubārak, informed us; Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah informed us, and he mentioned its isnād similarly. He said: The Anṣār suffered wounds and hardship on the day of Uḥud, and the Anṣār said: \"O Messenger of Allah, we have suffered wounds and hardship. What do you command us?\" He said: \"Dig wide.\" ʿAbd Allāh said: \"And I think he said: 'And dig deep.'\" Then ʿAbd Allāh said: \"Rather, it is thus.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6753","(6753) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us; Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah informed us; Sufyān narrated to us from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl from Hishām ibn ʿĀmir, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said regarding the martyrs of Uḥud: \"Dig deep and do well, and bury two and three in a single grave.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6754","(6754) Abū al-Ḥusayn informed us; ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Yaʿqūb informed us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyūb from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl from Saʿd ibn Hishām ibn ʿĀmir from his father, who said: The wounds became severe on the day of Uḥud, and that was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: \"Dig wide and do well, and bury two and three in a single grave.\" And likewise Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated it from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl from Saʿd ibn Hishām ibn ʿĀmir. Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us; Jarīr narrated to us; Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl narrated to us from Saʿd ibn Hishām ibn ʿĀmir. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Abū Dāwūd]"
"6755","(6755) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us; Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī narrated to us from Zāʾidah from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmī; my father narrated to me that a man from the Anṣār informed him.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Mundhir informed us; Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us; ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb narrated to us from his father from a man from the Anṣār, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for a funeral procession, and I was a boy with my father. He sat at the edge of the grave and gestured to the digger, saying: \"Widen it from the side of the head; widen it from the side of the feet. Perhaps he will have clusters of dates in Paradise.\" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū Ḥāzim.\n\nAnd in the narration of Abū ʿAbd Allāh, he said: \"I saw him sitting at the edge of the grave, and he said: 'Widen it from the side of his head. Perhaps he will have clusters of dates in Paradise.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Abū Dāwūd]"
"6756","In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.\n\nO my Lord, make it easy and assist me, O Generous. May the blessings and peace of Allah be upon Muḥammad and his family.\n\nThe pure and verified shaykh, Abū Bakr Abū al-Fatḥ Abū al-Qāsim Manṣūr ibn ʿAbd al-Munʿim al-Farāwī al-Naysābūrī, may Allah have mercy on him and on us, informed us — reciting to him from it — and he granted me permission for all his heard and authorized transmissions. He said: Abū al-Maʿālī Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Fārisī informed me, and he granted me permission for all his heard transmissions. He said: The Ḥāfiẓ Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī informed us. And more than one of my shaykhs informed me from Abū al-Qāsim Ṭāhir ibn Ṭāhir, who said: al-Bayhaqī informed us, who said:\n\n(6756) Abū Ṣāliḥ al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī informed us. And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Sarḥ informed us. Both said: Ibn Wahb informed us; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that Abū ʿAlī al-Hamdānī narrated to him, who said: We were with Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd in Rūdhūs, in the land of the Romans, when a companion of ours passed away. Faḍālah ordered that his grave be leveled, and then he said: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanding that they be leveled.\" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Rūdhbārī.\n\nAnd in the narration of Abū Ṣāliḥ: Thumāmah ibn Shufayy narrated to him, who said: We were with Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd in the land of the Romans, and the rest is the same. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr and Hārūn ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Aḥmad]"
"6757","(6757) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us, Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Sufyan narrated from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from Abu Wa'il from Abu Hayyaj al-Asadi who said: 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: \"I am sending you on the same mission on which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me: that you must not leave any raised grave without levelling it, and any image in a house without obliterating it.\"' Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the narration of al-Thawri. [Sahih. Narrated by Ahmad.]"
"6758","(6758) Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'quub narrated to us, Bahr ibn Nasr ibn Sabiq al-Khawlani narrated to us, it was recited to Abdullah ibn Wahb: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk al-Madani informed you from 'Amr ibn Hani' from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad who said: \"I visited 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: 'O Mother, show me the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ) and his two Companions.' She uncovered for me three graves — neither raised nor elevated — spread out on the reddish surface of the courtyard. I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) foremost, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, his head between the shoulders of the Prophet (ﷺ), and 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, his head at the feet of the Prophet (ﷺ).\" He is 'Amr ibn 'Uthman ibn Hani'; more than one narrated it from Ibn Abi Fudayk. [Da'if. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
"6759","(6759) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'quub narrated to us, al-Abbas al-Duri narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym — al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated from Abu al-Bada' who said: \"I entered with Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr the house in which was the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ), and I saw their graves flat and level.\" [Da'if.]"
"6760","(6760) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us, Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Imran al-Muqabiri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, Sufyan al-Tammar said: \"I saw the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ) raised in the shape of a camel's hump.\" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
"6761","(6761) Abu 'Amr informed us, Abu Bakr informed us, al-Hasan narrated to us, Hibban narrated from Ibn al-Mubarak, Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash informed us from Sufyan al-Tammar that he narrated to him that he saw the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ) raised in the shape of a camel's hump. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Muqatil from Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak. And whenever the narration of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad is authenticated — \"their graves were spread out on the surface of the courtyard\" — that indicates flattening (al-tashtih). And the sighting of Sufyan al-Tammar of the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ) as raised in the shape of a camel's hump is authenticated, as though it was changed from what it had been in earlier times, for its wall collapsed during the time of al-Walid ibn 'Abd al-Malik — and it was said during the time of 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz — and then it was repaired. The hadith of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad on this topic is more authentic and more deserving of being preserved. However, some of the scholars among our companions have recommended raising the grave (al-tasnim) in this era, since it is permissible by consensus, whereas flattening (al-tashtih) has become a hallmark of the people of innovation, so that it may not become a cause for tongues to wag and for them to be accused of the doctrines of the people of innovation, from which they are exalted. And with Allah is the granting of success. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
"6762","(6762) Abu Abdullah — Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed us, Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'quub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us, Hajjaj — meaning Ibn Muhammad — narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: \"I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) forbid that a man should sit upon a grave, or that it be plastered, or that anything be built upon it.\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Harun ibn Abdullah from Hajjaj ibn Muhammad. [Sahih. Muslim.]"
"6763","(6763) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us, Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Sulayman ibn Musa and from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir with this hadith, and he added: \"or that anything be added to it.\" And Sulayman ibn Musa added: \"or that anything be written upon it.\" And we have narrated from Abu Musa in his will: \"And do not place any structure upon my grave.\" And from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: \"And do not pitch a tent over me.\" And from Abu Hurayrah likewise. [Sahih. Abu Dawud.]"
"6764","(6764) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us, Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us. (H) And Abu Abdullah — Muhammad ibn Ya'quub — narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab and Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zurai' informed us from Ayyub from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Umm 'Ayyashah who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) came to us while we were washing his daughter, and he said: 'Wash her three times, or five times, or more than that if you see need, with water and sidr (lotus leaves), and in the last washing put camphor or some camphor. When you have finished, inform me.' She said: 'When we had finished, we informed him, and he threw his lower garment to us and said: \"Wrap her in it.\"'\" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it through other chains from Ayyub. [Sahih. Narrated by Al-Bukhari.]"
"6765","(6765) Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'quub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us, Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us. (H) And Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Ahmad — Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Hafiz — informed us, Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah — narrated to us, Mahmud ibn Ghaylan dictated it to us, Abu al-Nadr — Hisham ibn al-Qasim — narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah — Shayban — narrated from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir from Hafsah bint Sirin from Umm Sulaym, mother of Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When a woman has died and they wish to wash her, let them begin with her abdomen; let her abdomen be wiped gently if she is not pregnant. If she is pregnant, do not move her. When you wish to wash her, begin with her lower parts, and place a concealing cloth over her private parts. Then take a piece of cotton and wash her, and wash her well. Then insert your hand from beneath the cloth and wipe her with cotton three times, and wipe her well before performing her ablution. Then perform her ablution with water containing sidr. And let a woman pour the water while standing, not touching anything else, and the closest of her relatives should attend to her washing; otherwise, a pious woman. If she (the relative) is young or weak, then let another Muslim, pious woman wash her. When she has finished washing her lower parts with a thorough washing using water and sidr, this constitutes her ablution. Then wash her after that three times with water and sidr, beginning with her head before everything else, and cleanse each washing of the sidr with water. Do not comb her head with a comb. If any impurity emerges after the three washings, make it five. And if any impurity emerges after the five, make it seven. All of this should be in odd numbers with water and sidr, so that nothing makes you doubtful. When it is the last of the three washings or otherwise, put some camphor and some sidr in it. Then place that in a new jar. Then seat her and pour the water over her, beginning with her head until you reach her feet. When you have finished with her, place a clean cloth over her. Then insert your hand from behind the cloth and remove it from her.\" This is the explanation of the washing. \"Then stuff her lower parts with cotton as much as you can. Then wipe her cotton with her perfume. Then take a long washed cloth and tie it around her waist as a waist-wrapper is tied. Then fasten it between her thighs and press her thighs together. Then throw the end of the cloth from near her waist down towards her knees.\" This is the explanation of her lower parts. \"Then perfume her and shroud her, and braid her hair into three plaits — one short and two long — and do not make her resemble men. Her shroud should be five garments, one of which is that with which her thighs are wrapped. Do not remove any of her hair with lime or anything else. Whatever falls of her hair, wash it and return it to the hair of her head, or he said: 'insert it.' And perfume the hair of her head and perfume it well if you wish. And make everything concerning her in odd numbers, and do not forget that. If you wish to fumigate her bier, let it be a single fumigation so that it is an odd number.\" This is the explanation of her shroud and her head. \"And if she had smallpox, or henna dye, or anything similar, take a wide cloth and wash it with water.\" In another narration: \"Then immerse her in the water.\" And in our narration: \"And make sure to follow everything concerning her and do not move her, for I fear that something may gush from her that cannot be contained.\" This is the wording of Ibn Khuzaymah. The hadith of al-Saghani ends at his words: \"and let her shroud be five garments.\" Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi narrated it from Mahmud ibn Ghaylan, and added at his words: \"and perfume it well, and do not wash her with hot water, and fumigate her after you have shrouded her seven times if you wish.\" It is as though it fell from my shaykh's book. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Tabari.]"
"6766","(6766) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Adan informed us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn 'Asim informed us, Mahmud ibn Khalid narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Shayban — Abu Mu'awiyah — informed me, and he mentioned the hadith in its entirety in segments with its meaning, the wording differing, except that he said: \"When she has finished with the five (washings), let her place the camphor in the ears of the deceased.\" [Munkar. Al-Tabari.]"
"6767","(6767) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Usayd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Hishām, from Umm al-Hudhayl—meaning Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn—from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: \"A daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, so we asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: 'Wash her an odd number of times—three, five, or more than that if you see fit. And place camphor, or some camphor, in the last (washing). And when you have finished, inform me.'\" She said: \"So we informed him.\" She said: \"He then threw his waist-wrapper (ḥiqwah) towards us and said: 'Wrap her in it (as an undergarment).'\" She said: \"We braided her hair into three plaits—her forelock and her two side locks—and placed them behind her.\" al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the two Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Ḥassān. al-Bukhārī also narrated it from Qabīṣah, from Sufyān, in an abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6768","(6768) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb and Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: Yazīd ibn Zuraīʿ narrated to us, from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: \"We combed her hair into three plaits.\" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā thus. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
"6769","(6769) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to me, saying: Ḥarmalah narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me that Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah informed him, saying: \"I heard Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn say: Umm ʿAṭiyyah narrated to us that they divided the head of the daughter of the Prophet (ﷺ) into three plaits.\" He said: \"We undid it and washed it.\" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad, from Ibn Wahb, and he added: \"Then we made it into three plaits.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"6770","(6770) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, saying: Ḥarmalah narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me that Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah informed him, saying: \"I heard Ibn Sīrīn say: Umm ʿAṭiyyah narrated to us, saying: 'The Prophet (ﷺ) came to us while we were washing his daughter, and he said: \"Wash her three times, five times, or more than that if you see fit, with water and lote-tree leaves, and place camphor in the last (washing). And when you have finished, inform me.\"' When we had finished, he threw his waist-wrapper towards us and said: 'Wrap her in it (as an undergarment).'\" He (Ibn Jurayj) said: \"He did not add anything beyond that.\" He said: \"I do not know which of his daughters (it was).\" He claimed that al-ishʿār (wrapping) meant wrapping her in it. He said: \"And thus Ibn Sīrīn used to command that a woman be wrapped in a cloth (as an undergarment) and not be given an izar (lower garment).\" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6771","(6771) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir narrated to us, saying: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, saying: Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī narrated to me that he heard Ibn Sīrīn say: \"There was a woman from the Anṣār called Umm ʿAṭiyyah, among those who gave bayʿah to the Prophet (ﷺ). She came to Baṣrah seeking her son but did not find him. She narrated to us, and she mentioned the ḥadīth in a similar manner.\" He said: \"I said: 'What does his statement \"Wrap her in it (ashʿirnāhā)\" mean—is she to be given an izar (lower garment) with it?' He said: 'No, I think he only meant that she be wrapped in it.'\" Ayyūb said: \"And thus Ibn Sīrīn used to command that a woman be wrapped in a cloth (as an undergarment).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
"6772","(6772) Ibn Zanjawayh said, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: \"I said to Ayyūb: 'What does his statement \"Wrap her in it (ashʿirnāhā iyyāhu)\" mean—is she to be given an izar (lower garment) with it?' He said: 'I do not think so.' Ibn Sīrīn used to say: 'She is to be wrapped in a cloth beneath the chemise (dirʿ).' I do not think it means anything other than that.\" Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated it to us, saying: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, saying: al-Manīʿī narrated to us, saying: Ibn Zanjawayh narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ — ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
"6773","(6773) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, saying: Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, saying: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, saying: Nūḥ ibn Ḥakīm al-Thaqafī narrated to me—and he was a reciter of the Qurʾān—from a man from Banī ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd, whose name was Dāwūd, whose mother was Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ)—from Laylā bint Qānif al-Thaqafiyyah, who said: \"I was among those who washed Umm Kulthūm, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), at the time of her death. The first thing he gave us was the waist-wrapper (ḥiqw), then the chemise (dirʿ), then the head-covering (khimār), then the shrouding cloth (milḥafah), and then she was placed in the other garment. She said: 'And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was sitting at the door with her shroud, handing it to us garment by garment.'\" [Ḍaʿīf — Abū Dāwūd]"
"6774","(6774) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, saying: ʿAffān narrated to me, saying: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn Zayd and Thābit narrated to us, from Anas ibn Mālik, that Abū Ṭalḥah narrated the ḥadīth. In it he said: \"He embarked upon the sea and died, but they did not find an island (to bury him) until after seven days. So they buried him in it, and he had not changed (at all).\" It has been narrated from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī that he said: \"He is to be washed, shrouded, prayed over, and then cast into the sea.\" In another narration: \"He was placed in a basket and then thrown into the sea.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Ḥākim]"
"6775","(6775) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us, from Qatādah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"It would not please me to have gold equal to Mount Uḥud, which I would spend in the cause of Allah, and then die at the time of my death leaving behind ten awqiyyah (of gold), except for the price of a shroud or the repayment of a debt.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it — Supported by al-Bukhārī]"
"6776","(6776) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakkī narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, saying: al-Aʿmash narrated to us, from Shaqīq, from Khabbāb, who said: \"We emigrated with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), seeking the Countenance of Allah, and our reward was secured with Allah. Among us were those who passed away before us and did not consume anything of their reward—among them was Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, who was killed on the day of Uḥud and left behind nothing but a namirah (a striped cloth). When we covered his head with it, his feet became exposed, and when we covered his feet, his head became exposed. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Cover his head, and place some idhkhir (a type of fragrant grass) upon his feet.' And among us were those whose fruits have ripened, and he is now picking them.\" al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from various chains from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
"6777","(6777) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Ziyad ibn bint Ahmad ibn Ibrahim informed me; my grandfather informed us; Abu Marwan al-'Uthmani informed us; Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: Ibn 'Awf — meaning 'Abd al-Rahman — was brought food, and he said: \"Mus'ab ibn 'Umayr was killed, and he was better than me, yet nothing was found for him except a single cloak in which he could be shrouded. And Hamza — or another man — was killed, and he was better than me, yet nothing was found for him except a single cloak in which he could be shrouded. I do not think but that our good deeds have been hastened to us in the life of this world.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Makki from Ibrahim. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6778","(6778) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Ayyub al-Sibghi informed us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Ziyad informed us; Ibn Abi Uways informed us; Husayn ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Dumayrah narrated from his father, from his grandfather, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: \"The shroud is to be provided from the capital estate.\" [Batil. Lisan al-Mizan]"
"6779","(6779) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dassah informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated from Khalid, from Yunus, from Ziyad ibn Jubayr, from his father, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, who said — and I think the family of Ziyad informed me that he raised it to the Prophet (ﷺ) — who said: \"The rider shall walk behind the funeral procession, and the one on foot shall walk behind it, in front of it, to its right, and to its left, close to it. The stillborn child shall be prayed over, and its parents shall be supplicated for with forgiveness and mercy.\" [Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
"6780","(6780) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Ali ibn Hamshadh al-'Adl informed us; Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib informed us; Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us; Abu Hammam Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan informed us; Yunus ibn 'Ubayd narrated it with the same meaning, except that he said: \"With well-being and mercy,\" and he did not mention regarding the one on foot \"behind it and in front of it.\"\n\nIbrahim ibn Abi Talib said: The statement of Yunus ibn 'Ubayd — \"and some of his family informed me that he raised it to the Prophet (ﷺ)\" — is a narration of Yunus ibn 'Ubayd from Sa'id ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn Jubayr ibn Hayyah. The Sheikh said: [Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
"6781","(6781) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed him; Ahmad ibn Salman ibn al-Hasan al-Faqih informed us; al-Hasan ibn Mukram informed us; Ruh ibn 'Ubadah informed us; Sa'id ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn Jubayr ibn Hayyah informed me; my uncle Ziyad ibn Jubayr ibn Hayyah informed me; my father Jubayr ibn Hayyah al-Thaqafi informed me that he heard al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah say: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'The rider shall be behind the funeral procession, and the one on foot shall be close to it, and the infant child shall be prayed over.'\" [Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
"6782","(6782) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas — al-Asam — informed us; Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated from 'Ata', from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: \"When the infant cries out (at birth), it inherits and is prayed over.\" [Mawquf. Hasan li-ghayrihi. Sharh al-Ma'ani]"
"6783","(6783) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn al-Husayn al-Qadi in Marw informed us; al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah informed us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Isma'il al-Makki narrated from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When the infant cries out (at birth), it inherits and is prayed over.\"\n\nIsma'il ibn Muslim al-Makki — others besides him are more reliable than him — and it has been narrated through other chains from Abu al-Zubayr, raised (to the Prophet). [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Ibn Majah]"
"6784","(6784) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih in Baghdad informed us; Hilal ibn al-'Ala' al-Raqqi informed us; my father informed us; Baqiyyah narrated from al-Awza'i, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When the newborn cries out, it shall be prayed over, it shall inherit, and it shall be inherited from.\" [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Al-Tirmidhi]"
"6785","(6785) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Lakhmi informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim al-Dibaji informed us; Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Khalaf al-Baghdadi informed us; Ishaq al-Azraq informed us; Sufyan narrated from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"When the infant cries out (at birth), it shall inherit, it shall be inherited from, and it shall be prayed over.\"\n\nSulayman said: None narrated it from Sufyan except Ishaq.\n\nThe Sheikh said: And al-Mughirah ibn Salih narrated it from Abu al-Zubayr, raised (to the Prophet), and we have narrated it in the book of al-Fara'id from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, raised (to the Prophet). [Sahih li-ghayrihi. See before it]"
"6786","(6786) Abu al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Ma'ruf al-Faqih informed us; Abu Sa'id 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Razi in Naysabur informed us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Muslim informed us; Hisham informed us; Qatadah narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"Pray over your children, for they are more deserving of those whom you pray over.\"\n\nAnd this has been narrated through another chain, raised (to the Prophet). [Da'if]"
"6787","(6787) Abū Manṣūr — Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh, of the descendants of Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī, in al-Kūfah — informed us: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us: al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, from Layth, from ʿĀṣim, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The most deserving of those over whom you perform the funeral prayer are your children.\" [Munkar. Aḥmad]"
"6788","(6788) Abū ʿAbd Allāl-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Abū Umayyah narrated to us: al-Aswad ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us, from Isrāʾīl, from Jābir, from ʿĀmir, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed the funeral prayer over his son Ibrāhīm, who died at the age of sixteen months, and he said: \"Indeed, he has in Paradise one who will complete his nursing, and he was truthful.\" [Munkar. Aḥmad]"
"6789","(6789) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hannād al-Sarī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, from Wāʾil ibn Dāwūd, who said: I heard al-Bahīyy say: When Ibrāhīm, the son of the Prophet (ﷺ), died, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed the funeral prayer over him in the Maqāʿid. [Munkar. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6790","(6790) And Abū ʿAlī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, and he said: I read before Saʿīd ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ṭālaqānī: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to you, from Yaʿqūb ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ, from ʬAṭāʾ, that the Prophet (ﷺ) performed the funeral prayer over his son Ibrāhīm when he was seventy nights old. [Munkar. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6791","(6791) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl informed me, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed the funeral prayer over his son Ibrāhīm when he died.\n\nThese narrations, even though they are mursal, strengthen the connected narration that precedes them, and some of them reinforce others. They have established the funeral prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) over his son Ibrāhīm, and this is more deserving than the narration of the one who reported that he did not perform the prayer over him. [Munkar. Ṭabaqāt Saʿd]"
"6792","(6792) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬAṭāʾ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿUmarī narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he would not perform the funeral prayer over a miscarried fetus until it cried out. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6793","(6793) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām narrated to us: Shādhān informed us: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd and Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that he performed the funeral prayer over a newborn who had drawn breath, and then he said: \"O Allah, protect it from the punishment of the grave.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6794","(6794) Abū Naṣr — ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah — informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us: Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Kātib narrated to us: Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Maʿmar, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, from Abū Hurayrah, that he would perform the funeral prayer over a newborn who had committed no sin whatsoever, and he would say: \"O Allah, make him for us a forerunner, a predecessor, and a treasure.\"\n\nNuʿaym said: It was said to some of them: \"Do you perform the funeral prayer over a newborn who has committed no sin whatsoever?\" He said: \"The funeral prayer was performed over the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he was forgiven, occupying the status of one who never disobeyed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.\" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6795","(6795) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Asfāṭī narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Abū Khalīfah narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, that Jābir informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to combine two men from among the martyrs of Uḥud in a single garment and would ask: \"Which of them had memorized more of the Qurʾān?\" When one of them was indicated, he would place him forward in the grave and say: \"I shall be a witness over these on the Day of Resurrection.\" And he ordered that they be buried in their blood, and no funeral prayer was performed over them, and they were not washed. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū Khalīfah. al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from al-Layth in its entirety, and from Abū al-Walīd in abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"6796","(6796) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Qutaybah — Salm ibn al-Faḍl al-Ādamī, in Makkah — narrated to us: Jaʿfar al-Fāriyābī narrated to us: Qutaybah narrated to us: al-Layth narrated it with the same isnād and text, except that he said: Then he would say: \"Which of them had memorized more of the Qurʾān?\" And he said: \"I am a witness.\" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd.\n\nAnd likewise a group narrated it from Layth ibn Saʿ from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī.\n\nBut Usāmah ibn Zayd contradicted him, narrating it from al-Zuhrī from Anas ibn Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6797","(6797) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, that Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakim informed us, that Ibn Wahb informed me, that Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me that Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri narrated to him that Anas ibn Malik narrated to him: The martyrs of Uhud were not given the ritual washing (ghusl), and they were buried in their blood, and no funeral prayer (Salat al-Janazah) was offered over them. [Hasan. Abu Dawud]"
"6798","(6798) And Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-'Adl in Baghdad narrated to us, that 'Abdullah ibn Rawh al-Mada'ini narrated to us, that 'Uthman ibn 'Umar said — and Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that 'Abdullah ibn al-Husayn al-Qadi in Marw narrated to us, that al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah narrated to us, that Ruh ibn 'Badah narrated to us — both saying: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Anas, who said: On the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by Hamzah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him), who had been mutilated and his body disfigured. He said: \"Were it not that Safiyyah would be grieved, I would have left him until Allah resurrects him from the bellies of birds and beasts of prey.\" So he shrouded him in a striped cloak; when his head was covered, his legs became visible, and when his legs were covered, his head became visible. So he covered his head. He did not offer the funeral prayer over any of the martyrs except him. Then he said: \"I am a witness over you today.\" He used to place two or three in a single grave and would ask which of them had memorized the most Qur'an, and would place that one foremost in the grave. He would shroud two or three in a single cloth.\n\nAbu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us that 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz said: The wording \"He did not offer the funeral prayer over any of the martyrs except him\" is not preserved (as authentic).\n\nAbu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi said in his book al-'Ilal: I asked Muhammad (i.e., al-Bukhari) about this hadith, meaning its chain of narration, and he said: The hadith of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah is a hasan hadith. And the hadith of Usamah ibn Zayd is not preserved; Usamah ibn Zayd made an error in it.\n\nThe Sheikh said: It has also been narrated from al-Zuhri from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b from his father. [Hasan. Abu Dawud]"
"6799","(6799) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Abu Bakr al-Qattan narrated to us that Abu al-Azhar narrated to us that Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Ansari narrated to me that al-Zuhri narrated to me from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from his father: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on the day of Uhud: \"Has anyone seen where Hamzah was killed?\" A man who was unarmed said: \"I saw where he was killed.\" He said: \"Then go and show it to us.\" So he went out until he stood over Hamzah and saw that his belly had been split open and he had been mutilated. He said: \"O Messenger of Allah, he has been mutilated!\" By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) disliked looking at him. Then he stood among the slain and said: \"I am a witness over these people. Wrap them in their blood. Indeed, no one is wounded in the cause of Allah — and Allah knows best who is wounded in His cause — except that his wound will come on the Day of Resurrection flowing with blood: its color will be the color of blood, and its fragrance will be the fragrance of musk.\" And he said: \"Place the one who has memorized the most Qur'an foremost in the grave.\"\n\nIn this narration there are additions not found in the narration of al-Layth, and in the narration of al-Layth there is an addition not found in this narration. It is therefore possible that both his narrations — from him (al-Zuhri) from Jabir and from him from his father — are sound, even though they differ. Al-Layth ibn Sa'd (may Allah have mercy on him) was an Imam and a masterful narrator, and his narration is more deserving of acceptance. And Allah knows best.\n\nIt has also been narrated from al-Zuhri from Ibn Abi Su'ayr from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal (disconnected) narration in abbreviated form. [Weak. Ibn Abi Shaybah]"
"6800","(6800) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us that Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri in Makkah narrated to us that al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sabah al-Za'faran narrated to us that Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Ibn Abi Su'ayr: The Prophet (ﷺ) looked out over the slain of Uhud and said: \"Indeed, I am a witness over these people. Wrap them in their blood and their wounds.\"\n\nSufyan ibn 'Uyaynah said: Ma'mar corroborated me in this hadith. And it has also been narrated from him from Jabir. [Sahih. Ahmad]"
"6801","(6801) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Hajib ibn Ahmad al-Tusi narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Hammad al-Abiwardi narrated to us that 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us from Ma'mar from al-Zuhri from Ibn Abi Su'ayr from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, who said: On the day of Uhud, the Prophet (ﷺ) looked out over the martyrs who had been killed that day and said: \"Wrap them in their blood, for indeed I am a witness over them.\" He used to bury one man, two men, or three men in a single grave, and he would ask: \"Which of them had memorized the most Qur'an?\" and they would place that one foremost. Jabir said: My father and my uncle were buried that day in a single grave. [Sahih. Narrated by Ahmad]"
"6802","(6802) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us that Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us that Abu Mus'ab narrated to us that Malik narrated — and Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus narrated to us that 'Uthman ibn Sa'id narrated to us that al-Qa'nabi narrated, reciting to Malik — from Abu al-Zinad from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, no one is wounded in the cause of Allah — and Allah knows best who is wounded in His cause — except that he will come on the Day of Resurrection with his wound flowing blood: the color will be the color of blood, and the fragrance will be the fragrance of musk.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Ibn 'Uyaynah from Abu al-Zinad. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6803","(6803) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us that Adam narrated to us that Shu'bah narrated to us from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, who said: I heard Abu Malik al-Ghifari say: The slain of Uhud were brought in groups of nine, with Hamzah being the tenth among them. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would offer the funeral prayer over them, and then they would be carried away. Then another group of nine would be brought, and he would offer the funeral prayer over them, with Hamzah remaining in his place, until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had offered the funeral prayer over them (all). [Munkar. Abu Dawud]"
"6804","(6804) And Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah narrated to us that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Khalid al-Isbahani narrated to us by dictation that Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Ishaq al-Anmati narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Mani' narrated to us that Abu Yusuf narrated to us that Husayn narrated to us from Abu Malik al-Ghifari that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) offered the funeral prayer over the slain of Uhud in groups of ten, with Hamzah being the tenth in every group of ten, until he offered the funeral prayer over him seventy times.\n\nThis is the most authentic narration in this chapter, though it is mursal (disconnected). Abu Dawud narrated it in al-Marasil with the same meaning, saying: Hannad narrated to us from al-Ahwaz from 'Ata' from al-Sha'bi, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) offered the funeral prayer over Hamzah seventy times, beginning with Hamzah and praying over him, then calling for the martyrs and praying over them while Hamzah remained in his place. This narration is also munqati' (disconnected). And the hadith of Jabir is musnad (connected), and his father was among the martyrs of Uhud. [Munkar. Ibn Majah]"
"6805","(6805) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Qatadah informed us that Abu 'Ali Hamid ibn Muhammad al-Raffa' informed us that 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us that Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad from Miqsam from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: When Hamzah was killed on the day of Uhud, Safiyyah came seeking him, not knowing what had befallen him. She met 'Ali and al-Zubayr. 'Ali said to al-Zubayr: \"Inform your mother.\" Al-Zubayr said: \"No, rather you inform your aunt.\" She said: \"What has happened to Hamzah?\" They gave her the impression that they did not know. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) came and said: \"I fear for her sanity.\" So he placed his hand upon her chest and supplicated for her. She said: \"I seek refuge in Allah\" (istirja') and wept. Then he came and stood over Hamzah, who had been mutilated, and said: \"Were it not for the distress of the women, I would have left him until he is resurrected from the bellies of beasts of prey and the craws of birds.\" Then he commanded regarding the slain, and he began to offer the funeral prayer over them. Nine would be placed with Hamzah, and he would make seven takbirs over them. They would be raised and carried away, and Hamzah would be left. Then another nine would be brought, and he would make seven takbirs over them, until he finished with them.\n\nI do not preserve this hadith except from the narration of Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, and both of them were not strong in retention (hifz). [Munkar. Ibn Majah]"
"6806","(6806) And Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Isbahani informed us that Abu 'Amr ibn Hamdan informed us that al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us that Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us that Ibn Fudayl narrated to us from Yazid from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) offered the funeral prayer over Hamzah and made nine takbirs over him.\n\nThis is more deserving of being considered preserved (mahfuz), though it is munqati' (disconnected). [Munkar. Ibn Abi Shaybah]"
"6807","(6807) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq: a man from my companions narrated to me from Miqsam — and he had met him — from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed over Ḥamzah and pronounced seven takbīrs over him. No slain person was brought except that he prayed over him alongside Ḥamzah, until he had prayed over him seventy-two funeral prayers.\"\n\nAnd this is weak. Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yāsār, when he does not name the one from whom he narrated, one does not find comfort in his narration.\n\nAl-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah narrated from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed over the martyrs of Uḥud. And al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah is weak; his narration cannot be used as a proof. [Munkar. Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
"6808","(6808) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Aḥmasī narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥumayd ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd said: Shuʿbah said to me: \"Go to Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim and say to him: 'It is not permissible for you to narrate from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah, for he is a liar.'\" Maḥmūd said: \"So I said to Abū Dāwūd: 'How is that?' He said: 'I said to Shuʿbah: \"What is the sign of his lying?\" He said: \"He narrated from al-Ḥakam things for which I could not find any basis.\" I said to al-Ḥakam: \"Did the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) pray over the martyrs of Uḥud?\" He said: \"No.\" And al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah said: al-Ḥakam narrated to me from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed over the martyrs of Uḥud.' And I said to al-Ḥakam: 'What do you say regarding the children of zinā?' He said: 'They are to be freed.' I said: 'On whose authority?' He said: 'It is narrated from the Baṣran narrators from ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him).' And al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah said: al-Ḥakam narrated to me from Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār from ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) that they are to be freed.'\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Khaṭīb]"
"6809","(6809) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Sharḥabīl informed us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Khayr from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) went out one day and prayed over the people of Uḥud the prayer of the deceased. Then he turned to the minbar and said: 'I am your predecessor, and I am a witness over you. By Allah, I am now looking at my Ḥawḍ, and I have been given the treasures of the keys of the earth — or the keys of the earth — and by Allah, I do not fear for you that you will associate partners with me after me, but I fear for you that you will compete over it (i.e., the world).'\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Sharḥabīl and others, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6810","(6810) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm narrated to us: Zakariyyā ibn ʿAdī informed us: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Ḥaywah from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Khayr from ʿUqbah — that is, Ibn ʿĀmir — who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed over the martyrs of Uḥud after eight years, like one bidding farewell to the living and the dead. Then he ascended the minbar and said: 'I am your predecessor, and I am a witness over you, and your appointed place is the Ḥawḍ. I am looking at it from this place of mine. I do not fear for you that you will associate partners, but I fear for you the world, that you will compete over it.'\" He said: \"And that was the last glance I cast upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm. And Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated it from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb with the meaning of al-Layth's narration, and he said at the end of it: ʿUqbah said: \"That was the last time I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) upon the minbar.\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6811","(6811) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥātim al-Dāraburdī in Marw narrated to us: ʿAbdān ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Maʿn ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir, who said: \"A man was struck in his chest or in his throat and died, and he was shrouded as he was in his garments, and we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).\" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6812","(6812) Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim informed us: ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded regarding the martyrs of Uḥud that the iron and leather be removed from them, and that they be buried in their garments with their blood. And there has preceded regarding the concession to shroud him in garments other than those in which he was killed the narration concerning Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr (may Allah be pleased with them both).\" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6813","(6813) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Shawdhab al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Daqīqī narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd informed us: my father narrated from his father from his grandfather, who said: \"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān was brought food, and he said: 'Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr ibn Hāshim was killed, and nothing was found to shroud him in except a cloak, and he was better than me. And Ḥamzah — or another man — was killed, and nothing was found to shroud him in except a cloak.'\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it from Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6814","(6814) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Yaḥyā al-Umawī narrated to me: my father said: Ibn Isḥāq said: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me from his father from his grandfather, regarding the account of Uḥud and the killing of Shaddād ibn al-Aswad, who was called Ibn Shaʿūb — that is, Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī ʿĀmir — he said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Your companion is being washed by the angels, so ask his wife.' She said: 'He went out in a state of janābah when he heard the clamor.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'That is why the angels washed him.'\" Thus he said with this chain. [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Ibn Ḥibbān, al-Ḥākim]"
"6815","(6815) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Your companion is being washed by the angels\" — meaning Ḥanẓalah — \"so ask his people about his condition.\" So his wife was asked, and she said: \"He went out in a state of janābah when he heard the clamor.\" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"That is why the angels washed him.\"\n\nYūnus said: Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us from ʿĀmir, who said: \"Ḥamzah was killed on the day of Uḥud, and Ḥanẓalah ibn al-Rāhib was killed on the day of Uḥud, and he is the one whom the angels purified. Both narrations are mursal, and they are well-known among the people of the maghāzī.\" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym]"
"6816","(6816) Abū Shaybah narrated from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) looked at Ḥanẓalah al-Rāhib and Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib being washed by the angels.\"\n\nAbū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated it to us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Manṣūr informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-ʿAnbar narrated to us: Manṣūr ibn Abī Mazāḥim narrated to us: Abū Shaybah narrated it. (J) And Abū Shaybah is weak. [Very weak. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr]"
"6817","(6817) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadabadi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us: Abd al-Razzak informed me: Ibn Jurayj informed me: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me from Ibn Abi Ammar, who was informed by Shaddad ibn al-Had: that a man from the Bedouins came to the Prophet (peace be upon him), believed in him, and followed him. He said, \"I will emigrate with you.\" The Prophet (peace be upon him) entrusted him to some of his Companions. When the expedition of Khaybar took place, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) acquired some spoils, distributed them, and allocated a share for him. His Companions gave him what had been allotted to him. He had been tending their mounts. When he came, they handed it to him. He said, \"What is this?\" They said, \"A share that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) has allotted for you.\" He took it and brought it to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and said, \"What is this, O Muhammad?\" He said, \"A share that I have allotted for you.\" He said, \"It is not for this that I followed you. Rather, I followed you so that I would be shot with an arrow right here\" — and he pointed to his throat — \"and die and enter Paradise.\" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, \"If you are truthful to Allah, He will be truthful to you.\" Then they rose to engage the enemy in battle. He was brought to the Prophet (peace be upon him), carried, having been struck by an arrow exactly where he had pointed. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, \"Is it he?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"He was truthful to Allah, so Allah proved him truthful.\" The Prophet (peace be upon him) shrouded him in his own garment, then placed him forward and prayed over him. Among what was apparent from the Prophet's (peace be upon him) funeral prayer was: \"O Allah, this is Your slave who went out as an emigrant in Your cause; he was killed as a martyr, and I am a witness over him.\"\n\nAta' said: They claimed that he did not pray over the people of Uhud.\n\nThe Sheikh said: Ibn Jurayj narrates it from Ata'.\n\nAnd it is possible that this man remained alive until the war ended, then died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prayed over him. And those over whom he did not pray at Uhud died before the conclusion of the battle. And Allah knows best. [Hasan. An-Nasa'i]"
"6818","(6818) Abu Abd al-Hafiz Abu Abdullah narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi narrated to me from Abu al-Haytham, who said: His father narrated to him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say during his march toward Khaybar to Amir ibn al-Akwa' — and the name of al-Akwa' was Sinan: \"Dismount, O son of al-Akwa', and sing for us some of your verses.\" So he dismounted, chanting verses in praise of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), saying:\n\n\"By Allah, were it not for you, we would not have been guided,\nNor would we have given charity, nor prayed.\nSo send down tranquility upon us,\nAnd make our feet firm if we meet the enemy.\nIndeed, the children of the disbelievers have transgressed against us,\nAnd if they seek to tempt us, we refuse.\"\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"May your Lord have mercy on you.\" Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: \"It has become incumbent — by Allah, had you let us enjoy his company longer.\" He was killed on the day of Khaybar as a martyr. His killing, as it has reached me, was that his own sword recoiled upon him and struck him a severe wound while he fought, and he died from it. The Muslims were doubtful about him and said: \"He was killed only by his own weapon.\" Until his nephew Salamah ibn Amr asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and informed him of what the people were saying about him. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"He is indeed a martyr.\" So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prayed over him, and the Muslims prayed as well. [Da'if. Reported by Ibn Ishaq]"
"6819","(6819) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Malik narrated from Nafi' from Ibn Umar: that Umar ibn al-Khattab was washed, shrouded, and prayed over. Ubayd Allah ibn Umar added in it, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: and he was embalmed. [Sahih. Reported by Malik]"
"6820","(6820) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Sa'id al-Thaqafi narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Shabib al-Ma'mari narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Hisab narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated from Thabit from Abu Rafi', who said: Abu Lu'lu'ah belonged to al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, and he mentioned the hadith. He said: He fashioned for him a dagger with two points. When he (Umar) pronounced the takbir, he struck him on his shoulder, then struck him in another place, and plunged the dagger into his side, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) fell.\n\nAnd it has passed in the authentic hadith from Husayn from Amr ibn Maymun in the story of the killing of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) when he stabbed him: The polytheist flew at him with the double-edged dagger; he did not pass anyone to his right or left without stabbing him. And in this is an indication that he was killed with a sharp weapon. Then he was washed, shrouded, and prayed over. [Hasan. Reported by Ibn Hibban]"
"6821","(6821) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us: Ahmad ibn Yunus informed us: Isra'il informed us from Abu Ishaq: that al-Hasan prayed over Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both). [Hasan for other than him]"
"6822","(6822) And Abu al-Husayn informed us: Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us: Ya'qub narrated to us: Sa'id narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim informed us: Ayyub informed us from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, who said: I visited Asma' bint Abi Bakr after the killing of Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr. He said: A letter came from Abd al-Malik instructing that he be handed over to his family. I brought it to Asma'. She washed him, shrouded him, embalmed him, and then buried him. Ayyub said: And I think he said: She did not live after that except three days, then she died. Others added in it: And she prayed over him. [Sahih. Reported by Ibn Abi Shaybah]"
"6823","(6823) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr both narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu al-Qasim Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Hashimi in Halab narrated to us: Adam narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, who said: I heard Qays ibn Abi Hazim say: Ammar said: \"Bury me in my garments, for I am one who disputes (in argument).\" [Sahih. Reported by Ibn Abi Asim]"
"6824","(6824) And Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us: Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us: Abu Nu'aym and Qabisah both narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Mikhwar from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, who said: Zayd ibn Suhhan said: \"Do not wash any blood from me, and do not remove any garment from me except the two leather socks. And bury me in the earth, for I am a man who disputes (in argument).\" Abu Nu'aym added: \"I will dispute on the Day of Resurrection.\" Thus said Ammar and Zayd ibn Suhhan. [Sahih. Reported by Abd al-Razzaq]"
"6825","(6825) And Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us: Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us: Abu Isma'il al-Tirmidhi informed us: Abu Ghassan narrated to us: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated from Ash'ath, who informed them from al-Sha'bi: that Ali prayed over Ammar ibn Yasir and Hashim ibn Utbah. He placed Ammar closer to him and Hashim in front of him. When he placed them in the grave, he put Ammar in front of him and Hashim closer to him. [Da'if. al-Tabrani in al-Kabir]"
"6826","(6826) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr narrated to us: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Some of our companions narrated to us from Thawr ibn Yazid from Khalid ibn Ma'dan: that Abu Ubaydah prayed over Ru'us.\n\nAl-Shafi'i said: And it has reached us that a bird cast a hand in Makkah during the Battle of the Camel, and they recognized it by its ring. So they washed it and prayed over it. [Da'if. Reported by al-Shafi'i]"
"6827","(6827) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us that al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr that Usāmah ibn Zayd informed him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rode a donkey upon a saddle pad of Fadakī cloth, and he seated Usāmah ibn Zayd behind him, while he was going to visit Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah before the Battle of Badr. He passed by a gathering in which was ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl — and this was before ʿAbd Allāh had accepted Islam. In the gathering were a mixture of Muslims, polytheists who worshipped idols, and Jews. Also present in the gathering was ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah. When the dust of the mount enveloped them, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy covered his nose with his cloak and said, \"Do not raise dust upon us.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ greeted them with salām and stopped. He then dismounted and called them to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and recited the Qurʾān to them, and he mentioned the hadith.\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6828","(6828) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr that Usāmah ibn Zayd informed him: The Prophet ﷺ passed by a gathering containing a mixture of Muslims, Jews, and polytheists who worshipped idols, and he greeted them with salām.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"6829","(6829) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Hishām narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr that Abū Qilābah narrated to him from Abī al-Muhallab from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn: A woman from Juhaynah came to the Prophet ﷺ while she was pregnant from zinā (unlawful sexual intercourse). He ﷺ instructed her guardian to treat her well, saying, \"When she delivers her child, bring her to me.\" He did so. He ﷺ then commanded regarding her, and her garments were bound upon her. Then he commanded regarding her and she was stoned. Then he prayed over her. ʿUmar said, \"O Messenger of Allah, will you pray over her when she has committed zinā?\" He said, \"She has repented with such a repentance that, if it were distributed among the people of Madīnah, it would suffice them. Have you found anything better than that she gave up her life?\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Hishām al-Dastawāʾī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"6830","(6830) And we have narrated in the hadith of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah from his father, regarding the story of the Ghāmidī woman who was stoned for zinā, that the Prophet ﷺ said: \"By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, she has repented with such a repentance that if a tax collector were to repent with it, he would be forgiven.\" Then he commanded regarding her, and she was prayed over and buried.\n\nAbū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us that Muʿādh ibn Najdah narrated to us that Khallād ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that Bashīr ibn Muhājir narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah from his father, and he mentioned its meaning.\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Bashīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"6831","(6831) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us that Muʿallā ibn Mahdī narrated to us that Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Abī Bishr who said: A number of the people of Baṣrah narrated to me from Abū Burdah who said: The Prophet ﷺ did not pray over Māʿiz ibn Mālik, nor did he forbid praying over him.\n\nAnd we have narrated from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālli — may Allah be pleased with him — that when he stoned Shurāḥah al-Hamadāniyyah, he said, \"Do with her as you do with your dead.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"6832","(6832) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh al-Aṣbahānī informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū Rūq Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated in Baṣrah that Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us that Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith from Makḥūl from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Pray behind every righteous and sinful person, and pray over every righteous and sinful person, and wage jihād alongside every righteous and sinful person.\"\n\nʿAlī said: Makḥūl did not hear from Abū Hurayrah, and those below him are trustworthy.\n\nThe Shaykh said: Narrations have been transmitted regarding praying over every righteous and sinful person, and praying over whoever says \"Lā ilāha illā Allāh,\" all of them are weak, at the utmost degree of weakness. The most authentic narration in this regard is the hadith of Makḥūl from Abū Hurayrah, and Abū Dāwūd narrated it in Kitāb al-Sunan, except that it contains an irsāl (broken chain), as mentioned by al-Dāraquṭnī — may Allah have mercy on him. As for the hadith which…\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Its meaning is referenced in the section on al-Faṭ.]"
"6833","(6833) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me that Aḥmad ibn Sahl al-Bukhārī narrated to us that Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥabīb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that ʿAwn ibn Salām narrated to us. Both said: Zuhayr narrated from Simāk from Jābir ibn Samurah who said: A man who had killed himself with arrows was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and he did not pray over him.\n\nThis is the wording of the hadith of ʿAwn ibn Salām.\n\nIn the hadith of Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, he said: A man fell ill and people cried out over him. A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, \"He has died.\" He said, \"How do you know?\" He said, \"People cried out over him.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"He has not died.\" Then the man departed and saw that he had slaughtered himself with arrows. He went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him that he had died. He said, \"How do you know?\" He said, \"I saw that he had slaughtered himself with arrows.\" He said, \"Then I shall not pray over him.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAwn ibn Salām in an abridged form.\n\nAnd we have narrated from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥanẓalī that he ﷺ said that only to warn the people, by refraining from praying over him, so that they would not commit what he had committed. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"6834","(6834) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Manṣūr from ʿUbayd ibn Nisṭās from Abī ʿUbaydah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd — may Allah be pleased with him: When one of you follows a funeral, let him take hold of the four sides of the bier, then let him volunteer further or leave it, for that is from the Sunnah.\n\n[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī.]"
"6835","(6835) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Nūḥ ibn al-Haytham al-ʿAsqalānī narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated from his father from his grandfather who said: I saw Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ at the funeral of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, standing between the two front pillars, carrying the bier upon his shoulder. This is the wording of the hadith of al-Shāfiʿī, and the hadith of al-ʿAsqalānī has the same meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm.]"
"6836","(6836) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that a trustworthy one among our companions narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ṭalḥah narrated from his uncle ʿĪsā ibn Ṭalḥah who said: I saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān — may Allah be pleased with him — carrying between the two pillars of his mother's bier, and he did not leave it until he set it down.\n\n[Very weak. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
"6837","(6837) And with its isnad, he said: Al-Shafi'i informed us, some of our companions informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, that he saw Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) standing at the funeral of Rafi', positioned between the two upright supports of the bier. [Sahih. Narrated by Al-Shafi'i in his Musnad]"
"6838","(6838) And with its isnad, he said: Al-Shafi'i informed us, some of our companions informed us, from Abdullah ibn Thabit, from his father, who said: I saw Abu Hurayrah carrying the bier of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas between the two upright supports. [Da'if. Narrated by Al-Shafi'i]"
"6839","(6839) And with its isnad, he said: Al-Shafi'i informed us, some of our companions informed us, from Shurahbil ibn Abi 'Awn, from his father, who said: I saw Ibn al-Zubayr carrying the bier of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah between the two upright supports. [Da'if. Narrated by Al-Shafi'i]"
"6840","(6840) Al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us, Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us, Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, Ma'n narrated to us, Harun, the freedman of Quraysh, said: I saw al-Muttalib carrying the bier of Jabir ibn Abdullah between the two upright supports. Ya'qub said: We had it as \"Kharijah,\" but Hisham said: \"Jabir.\" [Da'if]"
"6841","(6841) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us verbally, Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri informed us in Makkah, al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \"Hasten with the funeral. If it is righteous, then it is goodness that you are advancing toward; and if it is other than that, then it is an evil that you are removing from your necks.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from 'Ali, and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr and Zuhayr, all of them from Sufyan. [Sahih. Narrated by Al-Bukhari]"
"6842","(6842) Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us, Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Ahmad al-Isbahani informed us, Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym al-'Adawi, from Abu Barzah al-Aslami, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was on one of his military expeditions. When he had finished the fighting, he said: \"Do you miss anyone?\" They said: \"By Allah, we miss so-and-so, and so-and-so, and so-and-so.\" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Look, do you miss anyone?\" They said: \"We miss so-and-so and so-and-so.\" He said: \"But I miss Julaybib.\" They found him beside seven men whom he had killed, and then they killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was brought and informed, so he went to him and said: \"He killed seven, and then they killed him. He is of me, and I am of him. He killed seven, and then they killed him. He is of me, and I am of him.\" He said this two or three times. Then he indicated with his two forearms, spreading them out, and the body was placed upon the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him) forearms until a grave was dug for him. He had no bier except the forearms of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) until he was buried. He (the narrator) said: And no mention of ghusl was made. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn 'Umar ibn Salit, from Hammad ibn Salamah. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6843","(6843) And in what Abu Dawud narrated in the Marasil, from Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Hajjaj, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muhammad ibn 'Ali informed me that the funeral of Ibrahim, the son of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), was carried on the saddle-cloth of a horse. Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Muhammad informed us, Abu al-Husayn al-Fasawi informed us, Abu 'Ali al-Lu'lu'i informed us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Da'if]"
"6844","(6844) Abu Muhammad Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us verbally, Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri informed us in Makkah, al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: \"Hasten with the funeral. If it is righteous, then it is goodness that you are advancing it toward; and if it is other than that, then it is an evil that you are removing from your necks.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from 'Ali, and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr and Zuhayr, all of them from Sufyan. [Sahih. Narrated by Al-Bukhari]"
"6845","(6845) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us, Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us, Sa'dan ibn Nasr informed us, Yazid ibn Harun informed us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahran, that Abu Hurayrah, at the time of his death, instructed that no tent be erected over his grave, that no incense be carried behind him, and that he be hastened with — hastened with — for he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: \"When the believing person is placed upon his bier, he says: 'Advance me, advance me.' And when the disbeliever is placed upon his bier, he says: 'Woe unto me! Where are you taking me?'\" [Hasan. Narrated by Ahmad]"
"6846","(6846) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us, Ahmad ibn Ibrahim informed us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from his father, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"When the funeral is placed and the men carry it upon their necks, if it is righteous, it says: 'Advance me, advance me.' And if it is not righteous, it says: 'Woe unto me! Where are you taking me?' Its voice is heard by everything except a human being. And if a human being were to hear it, he would collapse unconscious.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Abdullah ibn Yusuf and others, from al-Layth. [Sahih. Narrated by Al-Bukhari]"
"6847","(6847) Abu Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us, Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, 'Uyaynah — meaning Ibn 'Abd al-Rahman — from his father, who said: I was present at the funeral of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah when Ziyad and some of his freedmen were walking on their heels before the bier, saying: \"Gently, gently, may Allah bless you.\" He said: Abu Bakrah caught up with them in one of the lanes of al-Mirbad, drove his mule toward them, and struck them with his whip, saying: \"Make way! By the One who honored the face of Abu al-Qasim (ﷺ), I indeed saw us during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) almost running with it (the bier) at a quick pace.\"\n\nIsma'il ibn Ibrahim, Yahya ibn Sa'id, Waki', ibn al-Harith, and 'Isa ibn Yunus also narrated it from 'Uyaynah. However, Shu'bah differed from them on the authority of 'Uyaynah, saying: \"At the funeral of 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As.\" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tayalisi]"
"6848","(6848) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us, Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Shu'bah from 'Uyaynah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from his father, that he was present at the funeral of 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As, and we were walking at a light pace. Abu Bakrah caught up with us and raised his whip, saying: \"I indeed saw us, while we were with the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ), running at a quick pace.\" [Sahih. Narrated by Abu Dawud]"
"6849","(6849) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf informed us, Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Yahya al-Jabir from Abu Majidah from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: We asked our Prophet (ﷺ) about walking with the funeral. He said: \"The pace should be quicker than a leisurely walk. If the deceased is righteous, hasten him toward that (which is good for him). And if it is other than that, then woe to the people of the Fire. The funeral is followed, not followed — there is no one who goes ahead of it.\"\n\nThis hadith is weak. Yahya ibn 'Abdullah al-Jabir is weak, and Abu Majidah — also said to be Abu Majid — is unknown. What has preceded is sufficient. It is also narrated from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri that when he was on his deathbed, Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas came to him, and he said to them: \"When you carry me, hasten with me, hasten with me.\" [Weak. Narrated by Abu Dawud]"
"6850","(6850) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Abu 'Amr Ahmad ibn Hazim ibn Abi Gharzah al-Kufi narrated to us, Ja'far — meaning Ibn 'Awn — from Ibn Jurayj from 'Ata', who said: We were present with Ibn 'Abbas at the funeral of Maymunah, the wife of the Prophet (may Allah pray over him, his family, and grant them peace), at Sarif. Ibn 'Abbas said: \"This is Maymunah. When you lift her bier, do not shake it and do not jolt it, and be gentle, for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had nine wives, and he would divide (his time) among eight and would not divide for one.\"\n\nAl-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6851","(6851) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us, 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Zaidah from Layth from Abu Burdah from Abu Musa, that the Prophet (ﷺ) passed by a funeral that was being rushed along, being shaken as a skin vessel is churned. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"You should observe moderation in walking with your funerals.\"\n\nIt has also been narrated from Abu Musa that he gave instructions saying: \"When you set out with my funeral, hasten my pace.\" This indicates that what has been narrated here, if established, refers to the dislike of excessive haste. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Tayalisi]"
"6852","(6852) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Hasan and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab both said: Abu Nu'aym al-Mula'i narrated to us, Malik ibn Mihwal narrated to us.\n\n(Additionally,) Abu 'Abdullah informed us, Abu 'Amr informed me, al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from Malik ibn Mihwal from Simak ibn Harb from Jabir ibn Samurah, who said: A horse without a saddle was brought to the Prophet (ﷺ) when he returned from the funeral of Ibn al-Dahdah, and we were walking around him.\n\nMuslim narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6853","(6853) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir informed us, my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Muhammad — meaning Ibn Ja'far — narrated to us, Shu'bah from Simak from Jabir ibn Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed over Ibn al-Dahdah, and a horse without a saddle was brought. A man hobbled it, and he rode it, prancing upon it, and we followed him, running behind him. A man of the people said: \"Indeed the Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'How many a hanging cluster of grapes there is for Ibn al-Dahdah in Paradise.'\" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn Bashshar. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
"6854","(6854) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from Thawban, that the Prophet (ﷺ) accompanied a funeral. A mount was brought to him, but he refused to ride it. When he departed, a mount was brought to him and he rode it. He was asked about that, and he said: \"The angels were walking, and I would not ride while they were walking. When they departed — or he said: when they ascended — I rode.\" [Munkar. Narrated by Abu Dawud]"
"6855","(6855) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Abu 'Utbah narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Rashid ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he went out for a funeral and saw people going out mounted on their animals, riding. Thawban said to them: \"Are you not ashamed? The angels of Allah are on their feet, and you are riding.\"\n\nThis is the preserved version with this chain, as a mawquf narration (stopping at Thawban).\n\n'Isa ibn Yunus also narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam from Rashid ibn Sa'd from Thawban, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out for a funeral and saw people riding, and he said: 'Are you not ashamed? Indeed the angels of Allah are on their feet, and you are on the backs of animals.'\" [Weak]"
"6856","(6856) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, and he mentioned it. A group also narrated it from 'Isa.\n\nThawr ibn Yazid narrated it from Rashid ibn Sa'd as a mawquf narration stopping at Thawban. This indicates that the mawquf version is more correct, and al-Bukhari also said so. [Munkar. Narrated by Ibn Majah]"
"6857","(6857) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī informed us.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us. They all said: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from his father, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, walking ahead of the funeral procession. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Tirmidhī]"
"6858","(6858) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Madanī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar walking ahead of the funeral procession, so I stood before him and said: O Abū Muḥammad, indeed Maʿmar and Ibn Jurayj contradict you in this, meaning that they transmit the hadith from the Prophet ﷺ as mursal. He said: al-Zuhrī confirmed it; I heard it from his mouth as he repeated it and narrated it from Sālim, from his father. I said to him: O Abū Muḥammad, Maʿmar and Ibn Jurayj both mention ʿUthmān in it. He said: He confirmed their narration and said: Perhaps he said it and I did not record it, for at that time I inclined toward the Shīʿah.\n\nThe Shaykh said: There has been disagreement regarding Ibn Jurayj and Maʿmar on the connection (waṣl) of this hadith. Each of them has been reported as narrating the hadith in connected (mawṣūl) form, and it has also been reported as mursal. It has also been narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Ziyād ibn Saʿd from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Tirmidhī]"
"6859","(6859) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Dharr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the grandson of Abū al-Qāsim al-Mudhakkir, both informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Darābjirdī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: Hammām narrated from Sufyān, meaning Ibn ʿUyaynah, and Manṣūr, and Ziyād, and Bukr — all of them mentioned that they heard from al-Zuhrī that Sālim informed him that his father informed him: He saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān walking in front of the funeral procession.\n\nHowever, Bukr did not mention ʿUthmān. Hammām is alone in reporting this, and he is thiqah (trustworthy).\n\nDisagreement has occurred regarding ʿUqayl and Yūnus ibn Yazīd in this hadith. Each of them has been reported as narrating it from al-Zuhrī in connected (mawṣūl) form, and it has also been reported as mursal.\n\nWhoever connected it and established its connection without differing upon it — and that is Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah — is a thiqah (trustworthy) authority. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Nasāʾī]"
"6860","(6860) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Rabiʿah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Hudayr: He saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, leading the people ahead of the funeral procession of Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, may Allah be pleased with her. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik]"
"6861","(6861) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Shawdhb al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdī, from Abū Ḥāzim, who said: I saw Abū Hurayrah and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī walking ahead of the funeral procession. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
"6862","(6862) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Aḥmasī narrated to us: al-Muḥāribī narrated from Saʿd ibn Ṭāriq al-Ashjaʿī, who said: I said to Abū Ḥāzim: Do you recall a funeral in which a group of fuqahāʾ walked ahead of it? He said: Yes, I saw ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, and Ibn al-Zubayr walking ahead of it until it was set down. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"6863","(6863) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from ʿUbayd, the mawlā of al-Sāʾib, who said: I saw Ibn ʿUmar and ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr walking ahead of the funeral procession. They went ahead and sat down and conversed. When the funeral drew level with them, they stood up. [Ḥasan for other than it. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
"6864","(6864) Abū Zakariyyā and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Ṣāliḥ, the mawlā of al-Tawʾamah: He saw Abū Hurayrah, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, Abū Usayd al-Sāʿidī, and Abū Qatādah, may Allah be pleased with them, walking ahead of the funeral procession. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
"6865","(6865) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Bakr Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah, from Ziyād ibn Qays al-Ashʿarī, who said: I came to Madīnah and I saw the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from among the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār walking ahead of the funeral procession. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"6866","(6866) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Qabīṣah narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from Ziyād ibn Jubayr, from his father, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who said — and Qabīṣah expressed doubt as to whether he raised it (to the Prophet) — he said: The rider travels behind the funeral procession, and the walker walks behind it, in front of it, and to its left and right. The miscarried child is prayed over, and his parents are supplicated for with well-being and mercy. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
"6867","(6867) Abū Muḥammad — Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Muḥārībī in al-Kūfah — informed us: Abū Ja'far ibn Duḥaym — informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim — informed us: Abū Ghassān — informed us: Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Yaḥyā al-Jābir, from Abī Mājid, from 'Abd Allāh ibn Mas'ūd, who said: We asked our Prophet ﷺ about walking with the funeral procession, and he said: \"The walking shall be at a pace less than a trot. If the deceased is good, he will be hastened to that; and if it is otherwise, then woe be to the people of the Fire. The funeral is followed, and it is not to be followed; there is no one who should go ahead of it.\"\n\nAbī Mājid is unknown (majhūl), and Yaḥyā al-Jābir was declared weak (ḍa'īf) by a group of scholars of transmission. And Allah knows best. [Weak. Ibn Ḥibbān]"
"6868","(6868) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh — informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ghālib — informed me: 'Amr ibn Marzūq — informed us: Shu'bah — from Abī Farwah al-Juhanī, who said: I heard Zā'idah narrating from Ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā, from his father, that Abū Bakr and 'Umar — may Allah be pleased with them both — used to walk in front of the funeral procession, while 'Alī — may Allah be pleased with him — used to walk behind it. It was said to 'Alī — may Allah be pleased with him: \"They are walking in front of it.\" He replied: \"They both know that walking behind it is superior to walking in front of it, just as the prayer of a man in congregation is superior to his prayer alone. But they are making things easy for the people.\"\n\nZā'idah here is Ibn Khirāsh, and it is also said Ibn Aws ibn Khirāsh al-Kindī, who narrates from Sa'īd ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā.\n\nThis hadith and the reports (āthār) concerning walking in front of the funeral are more authentic and more numerous. And success is by Allah. [Weak]"
"6869","(6869) Abū Muḥammad — 'Abd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī — informed us: Abū Sa'īd — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Za'farānī — informed us: Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Umar, from his father, from 'Āmir ibn Rabi'ah, reaching the Prophet ﷺ, who said: \"When you see the funeral procession, stand until it passes you by or is set down.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Alī, and Muslim narrated it from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, and a group — all of them from Sufyān.\n\nAnd Layth ibn Sa'd narrated it from Nāfi', adding in it: \"Even if one is not walking with it.\" [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6870","(6870) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Abū al-'Abbās — Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb — informed us: al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān al-Murādī — informed us: Shu'ayb ibn Layth ibn Sa'd — informed us: al-Layth — mentioned it with his chain of narration from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6871","(6871) And Abū Ṣāliḥ al-'Anbarī — informed us: my grandfather, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr — informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah — informed us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd — informed us: al-Layth, from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar, from 'Āmir ibn Rabi'ah al-'Adawī, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: \"When you see the funeral procession, stand for it until it passes you by or is set down — even if you are not walking with it.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it from Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd from al-Layth. [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6872","(6872) Abū 'Abd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq in Baghdad — informed us: 'Abd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Ayyūb al-Bazzāz — informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn 'Abd Allāh al-Baṣrī — informed us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm — informed us: Hishām — informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abī Salamah, from Abī Sa'īd, that the Prophet ﷺ said: \"When you see the funeral procession, stand. And whoever follows it, let him not sit until it is set down.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm. And Muslim reported it through another route from Hishām al-Dustawā'ī. [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6873","(6873) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad — 'Abd Allāh ibn Yūsuf — both said: Abū 'Abd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb — informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā — informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus — informed us: Ibn Abī Dh'ib, from Sa'īd ibn Abī Sa'īd al-Maqburī, from his father, who said: We were at a funeral procession when Abū Hurayrah took Marwān by the hand, and they both sat down before the bier was set down. Then Abī Sa'īd came and took Marwān by the hand and said: \"Stand up! By Allah, this one certainly knows that the Prophet ﷺ forbade that.\" Abū Hurayrah said: \"He has spoken the truth.\"\n\nAl-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī]"
"6874","(6874) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-'Alawī — informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī — informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ ibn 'Abd Allāh and 'Abd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Farrā' and Qaṭan ibn Ibrāhīm — they said: Ḥafṣ ibn 'Abd Allāh — informed me: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān, from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ.\n\n(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me: Abū Bakr ibn 'Abd Allāh — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān — informed us: 'Uthmān ibn Abī Shaybah — informed us: Jarīr, from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, from Abī Sa'īd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When you follow a funeral procession, do not sit until it is set down.\" [Authentic. Muslim]"
"6875","(6875) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan — informed us: Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad — informed us: 'Abd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb — informed us: Jarīr, from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, from Abī Sa'īd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When you follow a funeral procession, do not sit until it is set down.\" Suhayl said: \"And I saw Abī Ṣāliḥ not sitting until it was set down from the shoulders of the men.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it from 'Uthmān ibn Abī Shaybah from Jarīr, without the statement of Suhayl.\n\nAbū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr — said: Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī said: al-Thawrī narrated this hadith from Suhayl, from his father, from Abī Hurayrah, and in it he said: \"Until it is set down on the ground.\"\n\nAnd Abū Mu'āwiyah narrated from Suhayl, saying: \"Until it is set down in the grave.\"\n\nAnd Sufyān is more precise (aḥfaẓ) than Abī Mu'āwiyah. [Authentic. Muslim]"
"6876","(6876) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdān — informed us: Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī — informed us: al-Ma'marī and Aḥmad ibn Shu'ayb — Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Nasā'ī — both said: 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Ādharamī — informed us: Qāsim ibn Yazīd al-Jurmī — informed us: al-Thawrī, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abī Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When one of you follows a funeral procession, let him not sit until it is set down on the ground.\" [Authentic. Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Awsaṭ]"
"6877","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Tamim al-QanTari narrated to us; Abu Qilabah narrated to us; Mu'adh ibn FaDaLah narrated to us; Hisham narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Miqsam from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by a funeral procession, so he stood up for it. We said, \"O Messenger of Allah, it is the funeral of a Jew.\" He said, \"Indeed, death is a fright, so when you see a funeral, stand up for it.\" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6878","Abu 'Amr al-Adib narrated to us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili narrated to us; Hamid ibn Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; Surayj narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Hisham al-Dastawa'i narrated it with his chain of narration, except that he said: \"So he stood up for it, and we stood up with him.\" And he said: \"A Jewish woman,\" and he said: \"Stand up.\" He did not say \"for it.\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Mu'adh ibn FaDaLah, and Muslim narrated it through another route from Hisham. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
"6879","Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari narrated to us; Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadabadi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to me; Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up for a funeral procession that passed by him until it disappeared from sight. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi' from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6880","And Abu 'Ali narrated to us; Abu Tahir narrated to us; Ahmad narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to me; Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say: The Prophet (ﷺ) and his Companions stood up for the funeral of a Jew until it disappeared from sight. Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn Rafi' from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6881","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to me; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to me; Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us; Adam narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Murrah narrated to us; he said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla say: Sahl ibn Hunayf and Qays ibn Sa'd were sitting in al-Qadisiyyah when a funeral procession passed by them, so they both stood up. It was said to them, \"He is from the people of the land,\" or \"from the people of the dhimmah.\" They both said, \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by a funeral procession and stood up for it. It was said to him, 'It is the funeral of a Jew.' He said, 'Is it not a soul?'\" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas, and Muslim narrated it through another route from Shu'bah. [Sahih. Narrated by Al-Bukhari]"
"6882","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbubi narrated to us in Marw; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Tarsusi narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us; Rabi'ah ibn Sayf al-Ma'afiri narrated to me from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Habli from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As that he said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying, \"O Messenger of Allah, the funeral of a disbeliever passes by us, so should we stand up for it?\" He said, \"Yes, stand up for it, for you are not standing up for it; rather, you are standing up in veneration of the One Who takes the souls.\" And it has been narrated to us from Anas ibn Malik from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said, \"I only stood up for the angel.\" And from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"Rather, we stand up for the angels who are with it.\" [Sahih due to other narrations. Ahmad]"
"6883","Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us from Abu Ishaq from Abu Malik al-Ash'ari from Abu Hazim said: I walked with Abu Hurayrah, Ibn 'Umar, Ibn al-Zubayr, and al-Hasan ibn 'Ali in front of the funeral procession until we reached the graveyard. They stood until it was placed down, then they sat. I said to one of them, and he said, \"The one who stands is like the one who carries.\" [Sahih. Abu Ishaq]"
"6884","Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi narrated among others; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' narrated to us; al-Shafi'i narrated to us; Malik narrated to us from Yahya ibn Sa'id — And Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir narrated to us; my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id al-Thaqafi narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Yahya ibn Sa'id from Waqid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Sa'd ibn Mu'adh — and in the hadith of Malik: Waqid ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Mu'adh — from Nafi' ibn Jubayr from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) that the matter of standing for the funeral until it was placed down was mentioned, and 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood, then he sat. And in the narration of Malik, he said on the authority of 'Ali ibn Abi Talib: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to stand for funerals, then he sat afterwards. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Qutaybah and Muhammad ibn Rumh, except that he attributed the wording to Ibn Rumh and said: Waqid ibn 'Amr. And Ibn Bukayr also said it from al-Layth: Waqid ibn 'Amr. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6885","And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both narrated to us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us from his father. He mentioned the hadith in a manner similar to the narration of Qutaybah, and added, connected to the hadith, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he saw a funeral, would stand up for it, then he abandoned standing and would not stand up for a funeral when he saw it. And 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi and Ibn Abi Za'idah and others narrated it from Yahya ibn Sa'id, and they said in the hadith something similar to the narration of Qutaybah from al-Layth, and in the chain of narration: Waqid ibn 'Amr. [Sahih. Muslim]"
"6886","And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan both narrated to us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to me; Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me that Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah narrated to him from Waqid ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Mu'adh from Nafi' ibn Jubayr from Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam al-Zurqi from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood with the funeral processions until they were placed down, and the people stood with him. Then after that he sat and commanded them to sit. And others narrated something to that effect from Muhammad ibn 'Amr regarding the command to sit. [Sahih]"
"6887","(6887) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Muqriʾ informed us in Baghdad: Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik, meaning Ibn Muḥammad, informed us: Bishr ibn ʿUmar informed us: Shuʿbah narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir from Masʿūd ibn al-Ḥakam from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stood, so we stood; and he sat, so we sat.\" I said: \"Was it regarding a funeral procession that passed by?\" He said: \"It was regarding a funeral procession that passed by.\"\n\nMuslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from two routes from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"6888","(6888) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammādābādhī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah informed me from Qays ibn Masʿūd from his father that he accompanied ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) in Kūfah, and he saw ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) find the people standing, waiting for the funeral bier to be placed down. So he signaled to them with a whip or a switch that was with him to sit down, saying: \"Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sat after he used to stand.\" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
